const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A street.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a company of mutinous citizens, with staves, clubs, and other weapons.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Before we proceed any further, hear me speak."],"id":2,"m":"Before we go any further, listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Speak, speak."],"id":3,"m":"Speak, speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["You are all resolved rather to die than to famish?"],"id":4,"m":"Are you all determined to die rather than starve?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Resolved, resolved."],"id":5,"m":"Determined, determined."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["First, you know Caius Marcius is chief enemy to the people."],"id":6,"m":"First of all, you know Caius Marcius is the people's worst enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["We know't, we know't."],"id":7,"m":"We know it, we know it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Let us kill him, and we'll have corn at our own price. Is't a","verdict?"],"id":8,"m":"Let's kill him, and then we'll have grain at a price we set ourselves. Are we agreed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["No more talking on't; let it be done: away, away!"],"id":9,"m":"No more talk about it; let's do it. Come on, come on!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["One word, good citizens."],"id":10,"m":"Just one word, good citizens."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["We are accounted poor citizens; the patricians good.","What authority surfeits on would relieve us; if they would yield","us but the superfluity, while it were wholesome, we might guess","they relieved us humanely; but they think we are too dear: the","leanness that afflicts us, the object of our misery, is as an","inventory to particularize their abundance; our sufferance is a","gain to them.—Let us revenge this with our pikes ere we become","rakes: for the gods know I speak this in hunger for bread, not in","thirst for revenge."],"id":11,"m":"We're counted the poor citizens; the patricians are the \"good\" ones. What the rulers stuff themselves with would be enough to feed us. If they gave us only their leftovers while they were still fresh, we might think they were helping us out of humanity; but they think we cost too much. Our thinness, the sight of our misery, works like a list that spells out how rich they are; our suffering is their profit. Let's get revenge with our pikes before we're as thin as rakes. The gods know I say this because I'm hungry for bread, not thirsty for revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Would you proceed especially against Caius Marcius?"],"id":12,"m":"Do you mean to go after Caius Marcius in particular?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Against him first: he's a very dog to the commonalty."],"id":13,"m":"Him first. He's a real dog to the common people."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Consider you what services he has done for his country?"],"id":14,"m":"Have you thought about what he has done for his country?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Very well; and could be content to give him good report for't,","but that he pays himself with being proud."],"id":15,"m":"Very much, and I'd gladly praise him for it, except that he pays himself back by being proud."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Nay, but speak not maliciously."],"id":16,"m":"Come on, don't speak out of spite."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["I say unto you, what he hath done famously he did it to that end:","though soft-conscienced men can be content to say it was for his","country, he did it to please his mother, and to be partly proud;","which he is, even to the altitude of his virtue."],"id":17,"m":"I tell you, the famous things he has done he did for that reason. Soft-hearted people may say it was for his country, but he did it to please his mother and partly out of pride, and his pride is as great as his courage."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["What he cannot help in his nature you account a vice in him. You","must in no way say he is covetous."],"id":18,"m":"You're calling something he can't help in his own nature a vice. At least you can't say he's greedy."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["If I must not, I need not be barren of accusations; he hath","faults, with surplus, to tire in repetition. [Shouts within]","What shouts are these? The other side o' the city is risen: why","stay we prating here? to the Capitol!"],"id":19,"m":"If I can't say that, I'm still not short of accusations; he has faults enough to tire you out listing them. (Shouts offstage) What's that shouting? The other side of the city has risen. Why are we standing here chattering? To the Capitol!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Come, come."],"id":20,"m":"Come on, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Soft! who comes here?"],"id":21,"m":"Wait! Who's this coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Worthy Menenius Agrippa; one that hath always loved the people."],"id":22,"m":"The worthy Menenius Agrippa, a man who has always loved the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["He's one honest enough; would all the rest were so!"],"id":23,"m":"He's honest enough. I wish all the rest were like him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MENENIUS AGRIPPA.","id":24},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What work's, my countrymen, in hand? where go you","With bats and clubs? the matter? speak, I pray you."],"id":25,"m":"What work have you got in hand, my countrymen? Where are you going with bats and clubs? What's the matter? Tell me, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Our business is not unknown to the senate; they have had inkling","this fortnight what we intend to do, which now we'll show 'em in","deeds. They say poor suitors have strong breaths; they shall know","we have strong arms too."],"id":26,"m":"The senate knows all about our business; they've had a hint of what we mean to do for two weeks, and now we'll show them in action. They say poor petitioners have strong breath; they'll find out we have strong arms too."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Why, masters, my good friends, mine honest neighbours,","Will you undo yourselves?"],"id":27,"m":"Why, sirs, my good friends, my honest neighbours, do you want to ruin yourselves?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["We cannot, sir; we are undone already."],"id":28,"m":"We can't, sir; we're ruined already."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I tell you, friends, most charitable care","Have the patricians of you. For your wants,","Your suffering in this dearth, you may as well","Strike at the heaven with your staves as lift them","Against the Roman state; whose course will on","The way it takes, cracking ten thousand curbs","Of more strong link asunder than can ever","Appear in your impediment: for the dearth,","The gods, not the patricians, make it; and","Your knees to them, not arms, must help. Alack,","You are transported by calamity","Thither where more attends you; and you slander","The helms o' th' state, who care for you like fathers,","When you curse them as enemies."],"id":29,"m":"I tell you, friends, the patricians look after you with the kindest care. As for your needs and your suffering in this famine, you might as well hit the sky with your sticks as raise them against the Roman state. The state will go on its way, snapping ten thousand chains far stronger than any you could put in its path. As for the famine, the gods make it, not the patricians, and you need to kneel to the gods, not take up arms. Oh dear, your misery is carrying you towards worse trouble, and you slander the men who steer the state, who care for you like fathers, when you curse them as enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Care for us! True, indeed! They ne'er cared for us yet. Suffer us","to famish, and their storehouses crammed with grain; make edicts","for usury, to support usurers; repeal daily any wholesome act","established against the rich, and provide more piercing statutes","daily to chain up and restrain the poor. If the wars eat us not","up, they will; and there's all the love they bear us."],"id":30,"m":"Care for us! Oh yes, really! They've never cared for us yet. They let us starve while their storehouses are crammed with grain; they make laws for moneylenders' interest to support the moneylenders; every day they repeal some good law made against the rich, and every day they bring in harsher laws to chain up and restrain the poor. If the wars don't eat us up, they will; and that's all the love they have for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Either you must","Confess yourselves wondrous malicious,","Or be accus'd of folly. I shall tell you","A pretty tale: it may be you have heard it;","But, since it serves my purpose, I will venture","To stale't a little more."],"id":31,"m":"You must either admit you're amazingly malicious or be accused of foolishness. I'll tell you a pretty story. Maybe you've heard it, but since it suits my purpose I'll risk making it a little staler."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Well, I'll hear it, sir; yet you must not think to fob off our","disgrace with a tale: but, an't please you, deliver."],"id":32,"m":"Well, I'll listen, sir; but don't think you can brush off our wrongs with a story. Go on, if you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["There was a time when all the body's members","Rebell'd against the belly; thus accus'd it:—","That only like a gulf it did remain","I' the midst o' the body, idle and unactive,","Still cupboarding the viand, never bearing","Like labour with the rest; where th' other instruments","Did see and hear, devise, instruct, walk, feel,","And, mutually participate, did minister","Unto the appetite and affection common","Of the whole body. The belly answered,—"],"id":33,"m":"Once upon a time all the parts of the body rebelled against the belly and accused it like this: that it just sat like a pit in the middle of the body, idle and doing nothing, always storing away the food, never doing its share of the work like the rest, while the other organs saw and heard, planned, taught, walked, felt, and, working together, served the desires and needs of the whole body. The belly answered—"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Well, sir, what answer made the belly?"],"id":34,"m":"Well, sir, what answer did the belly give?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Sir, I shall tell you.—With a kind of smile,","Which ne'er came from the lungs, but even thus,—","For, look you, I may make the belly smile","As well as speak,—it tauntingly replied","To the discontented members, the mutinous parts","That envied his receipt; even so most fitly","As you malign our senators for that","They are not such as you."],"id":35,"m":"I'll tell you, sir. With a sort of smile that didn't come from the lungs, just like this (for you see, I can make the belly smile as well as speak), it answered mockingly to the discontented parts, the rebels who envied what it took in, exactly as you resent our senators because they aren't like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Your belly's answer? What!","The kingly crowned head, the vigilant eye,","The counsellor heart, the arm our soldier,","Our steed the leg, the tongue our trumpeter,","With other muniments and petty helps","Is this our fabric, if that they,—"],"id":36,"m":"The belly's answer? What! The royal crowned head, the watchful eye, the heart that advises, the arm that's our soldier, the leg that's our horse, the tongue our trumpeter, and all the other defences and little helpers in our body, if they—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What then?—","'Fore me, this fellow speaks!—what then? what then?"],"id":37,"m":"What then? Goodness me, this fellow can talk! What then? What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Should by the cormorant belly be restrain'd,","Who is the sink o' the body,—"],"id":38,"m":"Should be held back by the greedy belly, the sewer of the body—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Well, what then?"],"id":39,"m":"Well, what then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["The former agents, if they did complain,","What could the belly answer?"],"id":40,"m":"If those working parts complained, what could the belly say back?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I will tell you;","If you'll bestow a small,—of what you have little,—","Patience awhile, you'll hear the belly's answer."],"id":41,"m":"I'll tell you. If you'll spare me a little of what you're short of, patience, for a while, you'll hear the belly's answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["You are long about it."],"id":42,"m":"You're taking a long time about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Note me this, good friend;","Your most grave belly was deliberate,","Not rash like his accusers, and thus answer'd:","'True is it, my incorporate friends,' quoth he,","'That I receive the general food at first","Which you do live upon; and fit it is,","Because I am the storehouse and the shop","Of the whole body: but, if you do remember,","I send it through the rivers of your blood,","Even to the court, the heart,—to the seat o' the brain;","And, through the cranks and offices of man,","The strongest nerves and small inferior veins","From me receive that natural competency","Whereby they live: and though that all at once","You, my good friends,'—this says the belly,—mark me,—"],"id":43,"m":"Take note of this, my good friend. Your very serious belly took its time, unlike its rash accusers, and answered like this: \"It's true, my friends who make up one body with me,\" it said, \"that I take in all the food first, which you live on, and that's right, because I'm the storehouse and the workshop of the whole body. But remember, I send it through the rivers of your blood all the way to the court, which is the heart, and to the throne, which is the brain; and through all the winding passages and rooms of the body, the strongest sinews and smallest veins get from me the natural supply they live on. And though all of you at once, my good friends\"—this is the belly speaking, listen to me—"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Ay, sir; well, well."],"id":44,"m":"Yes, sir; well, well."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["'Though all at once cannot","See what I do deliver out to each,","Yet I can make my audit up, that all","From me do back receive the flour of all,","And leave me but the bran.' What say you to't?"],"id":45,"m":"\"Though you can't all see at once what I hand out to each of you, I can prove by my accounts that you all get the best flour from me and leave me only the bran.\" What do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["It was an answer: how apply you this?"],"id":46,"m":"It was an answer. How do you apply it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["The senators of Rome are this good belly,","And you the mutinous members; for, examine","Their counsels and their cares; digest things rightly","Touching the weal o' the common; you shall find","No public benefit which you receive","But it proceeds or comes from them to you,","And no way from yourselves.—What do you think,","You, the great toe of this assembly?"],"id":47,"m":"The senators of Rome are this good belly, and you are the rebellious parts. Examine their discussions and their worries, think properly about the common welfare, and you'll find there's no public benefit you receive that doesn't come from them to you, and none from yourselves. What do you think, you, the big toe of this crowd?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["I the great toe? why the great toe?"],"id":48,"m":"Me, the big toe? Why the big toe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["For that, being one o' the lowest, basest, poorest,","Of this most wise rebellion, thou go'st foremost:","Thou rascal, that art worst in blood to run,","Lead'st first to win some vantage.—","But make you ready your stiff bats and clubs:","Rome and her rats are at the point of battle;","The one side must have bale.—"],"id":49,"m":"Because, though you're one of the lowest, meanest, poorest members of this very wise rebellion, you go first. You scoundrel, the worst-bred to run with the pack, you lead the way to get some advantage for yourself. Still, get your stiff bats and clubs ready: Rome and her rats are about to fight, and one side must come to harm."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CAIUS MARCIUS.","id":50},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Hail, noble Marcius!"],"id":51,"m":"Greetings, noble Marcius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Thanks.—What's the matter, you dissentious rogues","That, rubbing the poor itch of your opinion,","Make yourselves scabs?"],"id":52,"m":"Thanks. What's going on, you quarrelsome rascals, scratching the poor itch of your opinions until you turn yourselves into scabs?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["We have ever your good word."],"id":53,"m":"We always get kind words from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["He that will give good words to thee will flatter","Beneath abhorring.—What would you have, you curs,","That like nor peace nor war? The one affrights you,","The other makes you proud. He that trusts to you,","Where he should find you lions, finds you hares;","Where foxes, geese: you are no surer, no,","Than is the coal of fire upon the ice,","Or hailstone in the sun. Your virtue is","To make him worthy whose offence subdues him,","And curse that justice did it. Who deserves greatness","Deserves your hate; and your affections are","A sick man's appetite, who desires most that","Which would increase his evil. He that depends","Upon your favours swims with fins of lead,","And hews down oaks with rushes. Hang ye! Trust ye!","With every minute you do change a mind;","And call him noble that was now your hate,","Him vile that was your garland. What's the matter,","That in these several places of the city","You cry against the noble senate, who,","Under the gods, keep you in awe, which else","Would feed on one another?—What's their seeking?"],"id":54,"m":"Anyone who gives you kind words is a disgusting flatterer. What do you want, you dogs, who like neither peace nor war? War frightens you, peace makes you arrogant. Whoever trusts you finds hares where he wanted lions, geese where he wanted foxes. You're no more reliable than hot coals on ice or a hailstone in the sun. Your great skill is to honour a criminal brought down by his crime and curse the justice that did it. Whoever deserves greatness deserves your hatred, and your desires are like a sick man's appetite, wanting most what will make him worse. Anyone who depends on your favour is swimming with lead fins and chopping down oaks with reeds. Hang you! Trust you! You change your minds every minute, calling a man noble who was just now hateful to you, and calling the man you crowned with garlands vile. What's the matter, that all over the city you're shouting against the noble senate, who, under the gods, keep you in check, when otherwise you'd eat each other? What are they after?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["For corn at their own rates; whereof they say","The city is well stor'd."],"id":55,"m":"Grain at prices they set, and they say the city has plenty."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Hang 'em! They say!","They'll sit by th' fire and presume to know","What's done i' the Capitol; who's like to rise,","Who thrives and who declines; side factions, and give out","Conjectural marriages; making parties strong,","And feebling such as stand not in their liking","Below their cobbled shoes. They say there's grain enough!","Would the nobility lay aside their ruth","And let me use my sword, I'd make a quarry","With thousands of these quarter'd slaves, as high","As I could pick my lance."],"id":56,"m":"Hang them! \"They say\"! They sit by the fire and claim to know what goes on in the Capitol: who's likely to rise, who's thriving and who's in decline. They take sides, gossip about who'll marry whom, build up one party and run down those they don't like until they're lower than their patched shoes. \"They say there's grain enough\"! If the nobility would put aside their pity and let me use my sword, I'd make a heap of these chopped-up slaves as high as I could throw my lance."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Nay, these are almost thoroughly persuaded;","For though abundantly they lack discretion,","Yet are they passing cowardly. But, I beseech you,","What says the other troop?"],"id":57,"m":"No, this lot are almost won round, because though they're badly short of sense, they're extremely cowardly. But please tell me, what does the other crowd say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["They are dissolved: hang 'em!","They said they were an-hungry; sigh'd forth proverbs,—","That hunger broke stone walls, that dogs must eat,","That meat was made for mouths, that the gods sent not","Corn for the rich men only:—with these shreds","They vented their complainings; which being answer'd,","And a petition granted them,—a strange one,","To break the heart of generosity,","And make bold power look pale,—they threw their caps","As they would hang them on the horns o' the moon,","Shouting their emulation."],"id":58,"m":"They've broken up, hang them! They said they were hungry and sighed out proverbs: that hunger breaks through stone walls, that dogs must eat, that food was made for mouths, that the gods didn't send grain for the rich alone. With these scraps they let out their complaints. When they were answered and given what they asked, a strange demand fit to break the heart of the nobility and make power go pale, they threw their caps up as if to hang them on the horns of the moon, shouting to outdo each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What is granted them?"],"id":59,"m":"What have they been given?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Five tribunes, to defend their vulgar wisdoms,","Of their own choice: one's Junius Brutus,","Sicinius Velutus, and I know not.—'Sdeath!","The rabble should have first unroof'd the city","Ere so prevail'd with me: it will in time","Win upon power, and throw forth greater themes","For insurrection's arguing."],"id":60,"m":"Five tribunes of their own choosing, to stand up for their common wisdom. One is Junius Brutus, one Sicinius Velutus, and I don't know who else. Damn it! The mob should have torn the roofs off the city before they got that from me. In time it will eat away at power and throw up bigger causes for rebellion to argue over."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["This is strange."],"id":61,"m":"This is strange."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Go get you home, you fragments!"],"id":62,"m":"Go home, you scraps!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a MESSENGER, hastily.","id":63},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Where's Caius Marcius?"],"id":64,"m":"Where's Caius Marcius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Here: what's the matter?"],"id":65,"m":"Here. What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The news is, sir, the Volsces are in arms."],"id":66,"m":"The news is, sir, that the Volscians are up in arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I am glad on't: then we shall ha' means to vent","Our musty superfluity.—See, our best elders."],"id":67,"m":"I'm glad of it. Now we'll have a way to get rid of our mouldy surplus. Look, here come our chief elders."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter COMINIUS, TITUS LARTIUS, and other SENATORS; JUNIUS BRUTUS and SICINIUS VELUTUS.","id":68},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Marcius, 'tis true that you have lately told us:—","The Volsces are in arms."],"id":69,"m":"Marcius, what you told us recently is true: the Volscians are in arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["They have a leader,","Tullus Aufidius, that will put you to't.","I sin in envying his nobility;","And were I anything but what I am,","I would wish me only he."],"id":70,"m":"They have a leader, Tullus Aufidius, who will test you. It's a sin, but I envy his nobility, and if I were anyone but myself, I'd wish to be him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["You have fought together."],"id":71,"m":"You've fought each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Were half to half the world by the ears, and he","Upon my party, I'd revolt, to make","Only my wars with him: he is a lion","That I am proud to hunt."],"id":72,"m":"If half the world were fighting the other half and he were on my side, I'd change sides just to fight him. He's a lion I'm proud to hunt."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Then, worthy Marcius,","Attend upon Cominius to these wars."],"id":73,"m":"Then, worthy Marcius, go with Cominius to these wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["It is your former promise."],"id":74,"m":"You promised to before."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Sir, it is;","And I am constant.—Titus Lartius, thou","Shalt see me once more strike at Tullus' face.","What, art thou stiff? stand'st out?"],"id":75,"m":"Yes, sir, I did, and I keep my word. Titus Lartius, you'll see me strike at Tullus's face once more. What, are you stiff? Will you stay out of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["No, Caius Marcius;","I'll lean upon one crutch and fight with the other","Ere stay behind this business."],"id":76,"m":"No, Caius Marcius. I'd lean on one crutch and fight with the other before I'd be left out of this."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["O, true bred!"],"id":77,"m":"Oh, a true thoroughbred!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Your company to the Capitol; where, I know,","Our greatest friends attend us."],"id":78,"m":"Come with us to the Capitol, where I know our chief friends are waiting."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Lead you on.","Follow, Cominius; we must follow you;","Right worthy your priority."],"id":79,"m":"(To Cominius) You lead on. (To Marcius) Follow Cominius; we must follow you, you who fully deserve to go first."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Noble Marcius!"],"id":80,"m":"Noble Marcius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Hence to your homes; be gone!"],"id":81,"m":"Go to your homes; be off!"},{"t":"sd","text":"To the Citizens.","id":82},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Nay, let them follow:","The Volsces have much corn; take these rats thither","To gnaw their garners.—Worshipful mutineers,","Your valour puts well forth: pray follow."],"id":83,"m":"No, let them come along. The Volscians have plenty of grain; take these rats there to gnaw their granaries. Honourable rebels, your courage is blooming nicely. Please, follow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Senators, COM., MAR, TIT., and MENEN. Citizens steal away.","id":84},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Was ever man so proud as is this Marcius?"],"id":85,"m":"Was there ever a man as proud as this Marcius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He has no equal."],"id":86,"m":"He has no equal."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["When we were chosen tribunes for the people,—"],"id":87,"m":"When we were chosen as tribunes for the people—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Mark'd you his lip and eyes?"],"id":88,"m":"Did you see his lip and his eyes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Nay, but his taunts!"],"id":89,"m":"Yes, but his taunts!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Being mov'd, he will not spare to gird the gods."],"id":90,"m":"When he's angry, he won't hesitate to mock the gods."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Bemock the modest moon."],"id":91,"m":"To sneer at the chaste moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The present wars devour him: he is grown","Too proud to be so valiant."],"id":92,"m":"These wars will swallow him up; he's grown too proud to be so brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Such a nature,","Tickled with good success, disdains the shadow","Which he treads on at noon: but I do wonder","His insolence can brook to be commanded","Under Cominius."],"id":93,"m":"A nature like his, flattered by success, scorns the very shadow he walks on at noon. But I'm amazed his arrogance can stand being under Cominius's command."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Fame, at the which he aims,—","In whom already he is well grac'd,—cannot","Better be held, nor more attain'd, than by","A place below the first: for what miscarries","Shall be the general's fault, though he perform","To th' utmost of a man; and giddy censure","Will then cry out of Marcius 'O, if he","Had borne the business!'"],"id":94,"m":"Fame is what he's after, and he's already well endowed with it. He can't keep it better, or win more, than by being second in command. Whatever goes wrong will be the general's fault, even if the general does all a man can, and fickle public opinion will cry out about Marcius, \"Oh, if only he had been in charge!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Besides, if things go well,","Opinion, that so sticks on Marcius, shall","Of his demerits rob Cominius."],"id":95,"m":"Besides, if things go well, public opinion, which is so attached to Marcius, will rob Cominius of the credit he deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come:","Half all Cominius' honours are to Marcius,","Though Marcius earn'd them not; and all his faults","To Marcius shall be honours, though, indeed,","In aught he merit not."],"id":96,"m":"Come on: half of Cominius's honours will go to Marcius, though Marcius didn't earn them, and all Cominius's faults will turn into honours for Marcius, even if he doesn't deserve them at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Let's hence and hear","How the dispatch is made; and in what fashion,","More than in singularity, he goes","Upon this present action."],"id":97,"m":"Let's go and hear how the orders are drawn up and in what manner, apart from his usual style, he goes off on this campaign."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Let's along."],"id":98,"m":"Let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":99},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Corioli. The Senate House.","id":100},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TULLUS AUFIDIUS and certain SENATORS.","id":101},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["So, your opinion is, Aufidius,","That they of Rome are enter'd in our counsels","And know how we proceed."],"id":102,"m":"So your view, Aufidius, is that the Romans have got into our secret plans and know what we're doing."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Is it not yours?","What ever have been thought on in this state,","That could be brought to bodily act ere Rome","Had circumvention! 'Tis not four days gone","Since I heard thence; these are the words: I think","I have the letter here; yes, here it is:"],"id":103,"m":"Isn't that your view too? What has ever been planned in this state that Rome didn't block before it could be carried out? It's not four days since I heard from there. These are the words; I think I have the letter here. Yes, here it is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Reads.","id":104},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","cont":1,"lines":["'They have pressed a power, but it is not known","Whether for east or west: the dearth is great;","The people mutinous: and it is rumour'd,","Cominius, Marcius your old enemy,—","Who is of Rome worse hated than of you,—","And Titus Lartius, a most valiant Roman,","These three lead on this preparation","Whither 'tis bent: most likely 'tis for you:","Consider of it.'"],"id":105,"m":"\"They have raised an army, but nobody knows whether it's for east or west. The famine is severe, the people rebellious, and there's a rumour that Cominius, Marcius your old enemy (who is more hated in Rome than by you), and Titus Lartius, a very brave Roman, these three lead this force wherever it's heading. Most likely it's against you. Think about it.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Our army's in the field:","We never yet made doubt but Rome was ready","To answer us."],"id":106,"m":"Our army is in the field. We never doubted Rome would be ready to answer us."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Nor did you think it folly","To keep your great pretences veil'd till when","They needs must show themselves; which in the hatching,","It seem'd, appear'd to Rome. By the discovery","We shall be shorten'd in our aim; which was,","To take in many towns ere, almost, Rome","Should know we were afoot."],"id":107,"m":"And you didn't think it foolish to keep your great plans hidden until they had to come out, yet it seems Rome saw them while they were still hatching. Because they've been discovered, we'll fall short of our aim, which was to capture many towns before Rome even knew we were on the move."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Noble Aufidius,","Take your commission; hie you to your bands;","Let us alone to guard Corioli:","If they set down before's, for the remove","Bring up your army; but I think you'll find","They've not prepared for us."],"id":108,"m":"Noble Aufidius, take your command and hurry to your troops. Leave it to us to guard Corioli. If they lay siege to us, bring up your army to lift it; but I think you'll find they haven't prepared for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["O, doubt not that;","I speak from certainties. Nay, more,","Some parcels of their power are forth already,","And only hitherward. I leave your honours.","If we and Caius Marcius chance to meet,","'Tis sworn between us we shall ever strike","Till one can do no more."],"id":109,"m":"Oh, don't doubt that; I speak from what I know for certain. More than that, some of their forces are already out and heading only this way. I take my leave of you. If Caius Marcius and I happen to meet, we've sworn to keep striking until one of us can't go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["The gods assist you!"],"id":110,"m":"May the gods help you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["And keep your honours safe!"],"id":111,"m":"And keep you safe, my lords!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Farewell."],"id":112,"m":"Farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Farewell."],"id":113,"m":"Farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Farewell."],"id":114,"m":"Farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":115},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"Rome. An apartment in Marcius' house.","id":116},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter VOLUMNIA and VIRGILIA; they sit down on two low stools and sew.","id":117},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I pray you, daughter, sing, or express yourself in a more","comfortable sort; if my son were my husband, I should freelier","rejoice in that absence wherein he won honour than in the","embracements of his bed where he would show most love. When yet","he was but tender-bodied, and the only son of my womb; when youth","with comeliness pluck'd all gaze his way; when, for a day of","kings' entreaties, a mother should not sell him an hour from her","beholding; I,—considering how honour would become such a person;","that it was no better than picture-like to hang by th' wall if","renown made it not stir;—was pleased to let him seek danger","where he was to find fame. To a cruel war I sent him; from whence","he returned his brows bound with oak. I tell thee, daughter, I","sprang not more in joy at first hearing he was a man-child than","now in first seeing he had proved himself a man."],"id":118,"m":"Please, daughter, sing, or at least cheer up a bit. If my son were my husband, I'd be happier about him being away winning honour than about him embracing me in bed, where he'd show most love. When he was still a soft-bodied boy, my only son, when his youth and good looks drew every eye to him, when a mother wouldn't let him out of her sight for an hour even if kings begged her for a whole day, I thought how honour would suit a man like him, that without fame to bring him to life he'd be no better than a picture hanging on the wall. So I was glad to let him look for danger where he'd find fame. I sent him to a cruel war, and he came back with an oak-leaf wreath on his brow. I tell you, daughter, I didn't leap for joy more when I first heard I'd given birth to a boy than I did when I first saw he'd proved himself a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["But had he died in the business, madam? how then?"],"id":119,"m":"But if he'd died doing it, madam? What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Then his good report should have been my son; I therein","would have found issue. Hear me profess sincerely,—had I a dozen","sons, each in my love alike, and none less dear than thine and my","good Marcius, I had rather had eleven die nobly for their country","than one voluptuously surfeit out of action."],"id":120,"m":"Then his good name would have been my son; I would have found my descendants in that. Let me tell you honestly: if I had a dozen sons, all loved equally, none less dear than your Marcius and mine, I'd rather eleven died nobly for their country than one stuffed himself with pleasure, doing nothing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a GENTLEWOMAN.","id":121},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEWOMAN","lines":["Madam, the Lady Valeria is come to visit you."],"id":122,"m":"Madam, Lady Valeria has come to visit you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Beseech you, give me leave to retire myself."],"id":123,"m":"Please, let me go to my room."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Indeed you shall not.","Methinks I hear hither your husband's drum;","See him pluck Aufidius down by the hair;","As children from a bear, the Volsces shunning him:","Methinks I see him stamp thus, and call thus:—","'Come on, you cowards! you were got in fear","Though you were born in Rome:' his bloody brow","With his mail'd hand then wiping, forth he goes,","Like to a harvest-man that's tasked to mow","Or all, or lose his hire."],"id":124,"m":"You certainly won't. I think I can hear your husband's drum from here. I can see him dragging Aufidius down by the hair, the Volscians running from him like children from a bear. I can see him stamping like this and shouting like this: \"Come on, you cowards! You were conceived in fear, even if you were born in Rome!\" Then, wiping his bloody brow with his armoured hand, he goes forward like a harvester who has to mow the whole field or lose his pay."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["His bloody brow! O Jupiter, no blood!"],"id":125,"m":"His bloody brow! Oh Jupiter, no blood!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Away, you fool! It more becomes a man","Than gilt his trophy: the breasts of Hecuba,","When she did suckle Hector, looked not lovelier","Than Hector's forehead when it spit forth blood","At Grecian swords contending.—Tell Valeria","We are fit to bid her welcome."],"id":126,"m":"Get away, you fool! Blood suits a man better than gold suits his monument. Hecuba's breasts, when she was nursing Hector, didn't look lovelier than Hector's forehead spitting blood as he fought Greek swords. Tell Valeria we're ready to welcome her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit GENTLEWOMAN.","id":127},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Heavens bless my lord from fell Aufidius!"],"id":128,"m":"Heavens protect my husband from cruel Aufidius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["He'll beat Aufidius' head below his knee,","And tread upon his neck."],"id":129,"m":"He'll beat Aufidius's head down below his knee and stamp on his neck."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter GENTLEWOMAN, with VALERIA and her Usher.","id":130},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["My ladies both, good-day to you."],"id":131,"m":"Good day to you both, my ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Sweet madam."],"id":132,"m":"Dear madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["I am glad to see your ladyship."],"id":133,"m":"I'm glad to see your ladyship."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["How do you both? you are manifest housekeepers. What are","you sewing here? A fine spot, in good faith.—How does your","little son?"],"id":134,"m":"How are you both? You're real stay-at-homes. What are you sewing? A pretty pattern, honestly. How is your little son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["I thank your ladyship; well, good madam."],"id":135,"m":"Thank you, your ladyship; he's well, good madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["He had rather see the swords and hear a drum than look upon his","schoolmaster."],"id":136,"m":"He'd rather see swords and hear a drum than look at his schoolmaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["O' my word, the father's son: I'll swear 'tis a very pretty boy.","O' my troth, I looked upon him o' Wednesday, half an hour","together: has such a confirmed countenance. I saw him run after a","gilded butterfly; and when he caught it he let it go again; and","after it again; and over and over he comes, and up again; catched","it again; or whether his fall enraged him, or how 'twas, he did","so set his teeth and tear it; O, I warrant, how he mammocked it!"],"id":137,"m":"Upon my word, he's his father's son. I swear he's a very pretty boy. Honestly, I watched him on Wednesday for half an hour on end; he has such a determined face. I saw him run after a golden butterfly; when he caught it he let it go, and ran after it again, and fell head over heels, and got up again, and caught it again. And whether the fall made him angry or what, he gritted his teeth and tore it apart. Oh, I promise you, how he shredded it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["One on's father's moods."],"id":138,"m":"One of his father's moods."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["Indeed, la, 'tis a noble child."],"id":139,"m":"Really, he's a noble child."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["A crack, madam."],"id":140,"m":"A little rascal, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["Come, lay aside your stitchery; I must have you play the idle","huswife with me this afternoon."],"id":141,"m":"Come, put away your needlework. I want you to play the idle housewife with me this afternoon."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["No, good madam; I will not out of doors."],"id":142,"m":"No, good madam, I won't go out of the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["Not out of doors!"],"id":143,"m":"Not go out of the house!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["She shall, she shall."],"id":144,"m":"She will, she will."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Indeed, no, by your patience; I'll not over the threshold till my","lord return from the wars."],"id":145,"m":"Really, no, if you'll excuse me. I won't cross the doorstep until my husband comes back from the wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["Fie, you confine yourself most unreasonably; come, you must go","visit the good lady that lies in."],"id":146,"m":"Shame on you, you shut yourself up quite unreasonably. Come, you must visit the good lady who's just had her baby."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["I will wish her speedy strength, and visit her with my prayers;","but I cannot go thither."],"id":147,"m":"I'll wish her a quick recovery and visit her with my prayers, but I can't go there."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Why, I pray you?"],"id":148,"m":"Why not, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["'Tis not to save labour, nor that I want love."],"id":149,"m":"It's not to save effort, and it's not that I don't care."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["You would be another Penelope; yet they say all the yarn she spun","in Ulysses' absence did but fill Ithaca full of moths. Come; I","would your cambric were sensible as your finger, that you might","leave pricking it for pity.—Come, you shall go with us."],"id":150,"m":"You want to be another Penelope; yet they say all the yarn she spun while Ulysses was away only filled Ithaca with moths. Come on. I wish your linen could feel like your finger, so you'd stop pricking it out of pity. Come, you're coming with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["No, good madam, pardon me; indeed I will not forth."],"id":151,"m":"No, good madam, forgive me; I really won't go out."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["In truth, la, go with me; and I'll tell you excellent news","of your husband."],"id":152,"m":"Honestly, come with me, and I'll tell you excellent news about your husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["O, good madam, there can be none yet."],"id":153,"m":"Oh, good madam, there can't be any yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["Verily, I do not jest with you; there came news from him last","night."],"id":154,"m":"Truly, I'm not joking; news came from him last night."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Indeed, madam?"],"id":155,"m":"Really, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["In earnest, it's true; I heard a senator speak it. Thus it","is:—the Volsces have an army forth; against whom Cominius the","general is gone, with one part of our Roman power: your lord and","Titus Lartius are set down before their city Corioli; they","nothing doubt prevailing, and to make it brief wars. This is","true, on mine honour; and so, I pray, go with us."],"id":156,"m":"Seriously, it's true; I heard a senator say it. It's like this: the Volscians have an army out, and the general Cominius has gone against it with part of our Roman forces. Your husband and Titus Lartius are besieging their city of Corioli. They have no doubt they'll win and make it a short war. This is true, on my honour; so please, come with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Give me excuse, good madam; I will obey you in everything","hereafter."],"id":157,"m":"Excuse me, good madam; I'll do what you say in everything else from now on."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Let her alone, lady; as she is now, she will but disease our","better mirth."],"id":158,"m":"Leave her alone, lady. In her present mood she'd only spoil our fun."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["In troth, I think she would.—Fare you well, then.—Come,","good sweet lady.—Pr'ythee, Virgilia, turn thy solemness out o'","door and go along with us."],"id":159,"m":"Honestly, I think she would. Goodbye, then. Come, dear lady. Please, Virgilia, throw your gloom out of the door and come along with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["No, at a word, madam; indeed I must not. I wish you much mirth."],"id":160,"m":"No, once and for all, madam, I really mustn't. I hope you have a lot of fun."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["Well then, farewell."],"id":161,"m":"Well then, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":162},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"Before Corioli.","id":163},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, with drum and colours, MARCIUS, TITUS LARTIUS, Officers, and soldiers.","id":164},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Yonder comes news:—a wager they have met."],"id":165,"m":"Here comes news. I bet they've met in battle."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["My horse to yours, no."],"id":166,"m":"My horse against yours that they haven't."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["'Tis done."],"id":167,"m":"It's a deal."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Agreed."],"id":168,"m":"Agreed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":169},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Say, has our general met the enemy?"],"id":170,"m":"Tell me, has our general met the enemy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["They lie in view; but have not spoke as yet."],"id":171,"m":"They're in sight of each other but haven't engaged yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["So, the good horse is mine."],"id":172,"m":"So the good horse is mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I'll buy him of you."],"id":173,"m":"I'll buy him from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["No, I'll nor sell nor give him: lend you him I will","For half a hundred years.—Summon the town."],"id":174,"m":"No, I won't sell him or give him away; but I'll lend him to you for fifty years. Call on the town to talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["How far off lie these armies?"],"id":175,"m":"How far off are these armies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Within this mile and half."],"id":176,"m":"Within a mile and a half."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Then shall we hear their 'larum, and they ours.—","Now, Mars, I pr'ythee, make us quick in work,","That we with smoking swords may march from hence","To help our fielded friends!—Come, blow thy blast."],"id":177,"m":"Then we'll hear their call to arms and they'll hear ours. Now, Mars, please make us quick at our work, so we can march from here with smoking swords to help our friends in the field! Come on, blow your trumpet."},{"t":"sd","text":"They sound a parley. Enter, on the Walls, some Senators and others.","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["Tullus Aufidius, is he within your walls?"],"id":179,"m":"Is Tullus Aufidius inside your walls?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["No, nor a man that fears you less than he,","That's lesser than a little."],"id":180,"m":"No, and there's no one here who fears you less than he does, and that's less than a little."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum afar off.","id":181},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","cont":1,"lines":["Hark, our drums","Are bringing forth our youth! we'll break our walls","Rather than they shall pound us up: our gates,","Which yet seem shut, we have but pinn'd with rushes;","They'll open of themselves."],"id":182,"m":"Listen, our drums are calling out our young men! We'll break down our own walls rather than let you pen us in like animals. Our gates, which look shut, are only fastened with reeds; they'll open by themselves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum far off.","id":183},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","cont":1,"lines":["Hark you far off!","There is Aufidius; list what work he makes","Amongst your cloven army."],"id":184,"m":"Listen out there! That's Aufidius; hear what damage he's doing to your split army."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["O, they are at it!"],"id":185,"m":"Oh, they're fighting!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Their noise be our instruction.—Ladders, ho!"],"id":186,"m":"Let their noise be our signal. Ladders, now!"},{"t":"sd","text":"The Volsces enter and pass over.","id":187},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["They fear us not, but issue forth their city.","Now put your shields before your hearts, and fight","With hearts more proof than shields.—Advance, brave Titus:","They do disdain us much beyond our thoughts,","Which makes me sweat with wrath.—Come on, my fellows:","He that retires, I'll take him for a Volsce,","And he shall feel mine edge."],"id":188,"m":"They're not afraid of us; they're coming out of their city. Now put your shields in front of your hearts, and fight with hearts tougher than shields. Forward, brave Titus. They scorn us far more than we thought, which makes me sweat with rage. Come on, my men: anyone who retreats I'll treat as a Volscian, and he'll feel my blade."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums, and exeunt Romans and Volsces fighting. Romans are beaten back to their trenches. Re-enter MARCIUS.","id":189},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["All the contagion of the south light on you,","You shames of Rome!—you herd of—Boils and plagues","Plaster you o'er, that you may be abhorr'd","Farther than seen, and one infect another","Against the wind a mile! You souls of geese","That bear the shapes of men, how have you run","From slaves that apes would beat! Pluto and hell!","All hurt behind; backs red, and faces pale","With flight and agued fear! Mend, and charge home,","Or, by the fires of heaven, I'll leave the foe","And make my wars on you: look to't: come on;","If you'll stand fast we'll beat them to their wives,","As they us to our trenches."],"id":190,"m":"May all the diseases of the south fall on you, you disgraces to Rome! You herd of— May boils and plagues cover you so people loathe you before they even see you, and you infect each other a mile upwind! You souls of geese in the shape of men, how you've run from slaves that apes could beat! Pluto and hell! All wounded in the back, backs red and faces white with running and shivering fear! Pull yourselves together and charge hard, or by the fires of heaven I'll leave the enemy and make war on you. Watch out! Come on: if you stand firm, we'll beat them back to their wives, as they beat us back to our trenches."},{"t":"sd","text":"Another alarum. The Volsces and Romans re-enter, and the fight is renewed. The Volsces retire into Corioli, and MARCIUS follows them to the gates.","id":191},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["So, now the gates are ope:—now prove good seconds:","'Tis for the followers fortune widens them,","Not for the fliers: mark me, and do the like."],"id":192,"m":"There, now the gates are open. Now be good backup. Fortune opens them for those who follow, not for those who run away. Watch me, and do the same."},{"t":"sd","text":"He enters the gates.","id":193},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Fool-hardiness: not I."],"id":194,"m":"That's madness. Not me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SOLDIER","lines":["Nor I."],"id":195,"m":"Nor me."},{"t":"sd","text":"MARCIUS is shut in.","id":196},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["See, they have shut him in."],"id":197,"m":"Look, they've shut him in."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["To th' pot, I warrant him."],"id":198,"m":"He's in the cooking pot, I'll bet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum continues.","id":199},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter TITUS LARTIUS.","id":200},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["What is become of Marcius?"],"id":201,"m":"What's happened to Marcius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Slain, sir, doubtless."],"id":202,"m":"Killed, sir, no doubt."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Following the fliers at the very heels,","With them he enters; who, upon the sudden,","Clapp'd-to their gates: he is himself alone,","To answer all the city."],"id":203,"m":"He was chasing the runaways right at their heels and went in with them, and they suddenly slammed their gates shut. He's all alone, taking on the whole city."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["O noble fellow!","Who sensible, outdares his senseless sword,","And when it bows stands up! Thou art left, Marcius:","A carbuncle entire, as big as thou art,","Were not so rich a jewel. Thou wast a soldier","Even to Cato's wish, not fierce and terrible","Only in strokes; but with thy grim looks and","The thunder-like percussion of thy sounds","Thou mad'st thine enemies shake, as if the world","Were feverous and did tremble."],"id":204,"m":"Oh noble man! He can feel, yet he's bolder than his unfeeling sword, and he stands firm when it bends! You're lost, Marcius. A flawless ruby as big as you would not be so rich a jewel. You were a soldier just as Cato would have wished, not only fierce and terrible in your blows, but with your grim looks and the thunderous crash of your voice you made your enemies shake as if the world had a fever and trembled."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter MARCIUS, bleeding, assaulted by the enemy.","id":205},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Look, sir."],"id":206,"m":"Look, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["O, 'tis Marcius!","Let's fetch him off, or make remain alike."],"id":207,"m":"Oh, it's Marcius! Let's rescue him, or stay there with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight, and all enter the city.","id":208},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"V","loc":"Within Corioli. A street.","id":209},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain Romans, with spoils.","id":210},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST ROMAN","lines":["This will I carry to Rome."],"id":211,"m":"I'll take this to Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND ROMAN","lines":["And I this."],"id":212,"m":"And I'll take this."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD ROMAN","lines":["A murrain on't! I took this for silver."],"id":213,"m":"A plague on it! I thought this was silver."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum continues still afar off.","id":214},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MARCIUS and TITUS LARTIUS with a trumpet.","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["See here these movers that do prize their hours","At a crack'd drachma! Cushions, leaden spoons,","Irons of a doit, doublets that hangmen would","Bury with those that wore them, these base slaves,","Ere yet the fight be done, pack up:—down with them!—","And hark, what noise the general makes!—To him!—","There is the man of my soul's hate, Aufidius,","Piercing our Romans; then, valiant Titus, take","Convenient numbers to make good the city;","Whilst I, with those that have the spirit, will haste","To help Cominius."],"id":216,"m":"Look at these looters, who value their time at a cracked penny! Cushions, lead spoons, iron scraps worth a farthing, jackets a hangman would bury along with the men who wore them: these low slaves are packing up before the fight is even over. Cut them down! And listen to the noise the general's making! Go to him! There's the man my soul hates, Aufidius, cutting through our Romans. So, brave Titus, take enough men to hold the city, while I hurry with those who have the spirit to help Cominius."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Worthy sir, thou bleed'st;","Thy exercise hath been too violent","For a second course of fight."],"id":217,"m":"Worthy sir, you're bleeding. You've worked too hard already to go into a second round of fighting."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Sir, praise me not;","My work hath yet not warm'd me: fare you well;","The blood I drop is rather physical","Than dangerous to me: to Aufidius thus","I will appear, and fight."],"id":218,"m":"Sir, don't praise me. My work hasn't even warmed me up yet. Farewell. The blood I'm losing is more like a healthy bloodletting than a danger to me. This is how I'll show myself to Aufidius and fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Now the fair goddess, Fortune,","Fall deep in love with thee; and her great charms","Misguide thy opposers' swords! Bold gentleman,","Prosperity be thy page!"],"id":219,"m":"Now may the lovely goddess Fortune fall deeply in love with you, and may her powerful spells turn your enemies' swords aside! Bold gentleman, may success follow you like a page!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Thy friend no less","Than those she placeth highest!—So farewell."],"id":220,"m":"And may she be no less your friend than she is to those she raises highest! So, farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Thou worthiest Marcius!—"],"id":221,"m":"You most worthy Marcius!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit MARCIUS.","id":222},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Go, sound thy trumpet in the market-place;","Call thither all the officers o' the town,","Where they shall know our mind: away!"],"id":223,"m":"Go and sound your trumpet in the market square. Call all the town's officials there, where they'll learn what we've decided. Go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":224},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"VI","loc":"Near the camp of Cominius.","id":225},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter COMINIUS and Forces, retreating.","id":226},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Breathe you, my friends: well fought; we are come off","Like Romans, neither foolish in our stands","Nor cowardly in retire: believe me, sirs,","We shall be charg'd again. Whiles we have struck,","By interims and conveying gusts we have heard","The charges of our friends. The Roman gods,","Lead their successes as we wish our own,","That both our powers, with smiling fronts encountering,","May give you thankful sacrifice!—"],"id":227,"m":"Catch your breath, my friends. Well fought. We've come out of it like Romans, not foolish in holding our ground or cowardly in retreating. Believe me, sirs, we'll be attacked again. While we were fighting, we heard our friends' charges now and then on the wind. Roman gods, lead them to success as we wish for our own, so both our forces can meet with smiling faces and offer you thankful sacrifice!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter A MESSENGER.","id":228},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Thy news?"],"id":229,"m":"What's your news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The citizens of Corioli have issued,","And given to Lartius and to Marcius battle:","I saw our party to their trenches driven,","And then I came away."],"id":230,"m":"The citizens of Corioli came out and attacked Lartius and Marcius. I saw our side driven back to their trenches, and then I left."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Though thou speak'st truth,","Methinks thou speak'st not well. How long is't since?"],"id":231,"m":"Even if you're telling the truth, I don't think you're telling it well. How long ago was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Above an hour, my lord."],"id":232,"m":"More than an hour, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["'Tis not a mile; briefly we heard their drums:","How couldst thou in a mile confound an hour,","And bring thy news so late?"],"id":233,"m":"It's not a mile away; we heard their drums a little while ago. How could you waste an hour covering a mile and bring your news so late?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Spies of the Volsces","Held me in chase, that I was forc'd to wheel","Three or four miles about; else had I, sir,","Half an hour since brought my report."],"id":234,"m":"Volscian scouts chased me, so I had to go three or four miles round; otherwise, sir, I'd have brought my report half an hour ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Who's yonder,","That does appear as he were flay'd? O gods!","He has the stamp of Marcius; and I have","Before-time seen him thus."],"id":235,"m":"Who's that over there, looking as if he'd been skinned? Oh gods! He has the look of Marcius, and I've seen him like this before."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["[Within] Come I too late?"],"id":236,"m":"(Offstage) Am I too late?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["The shepherd knows not thunder from a tabor","More than I know the sound of Marcius' tongue","From every meaner man."],"id":237,"m":"A shepherd can tell thunder from a drum no better than I can tell Marcius's voice from any lesser man's."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MARCIUS.","id":238},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Come I too late?"],"id":239,"m":"Am I too late?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Ay, if you come not in the blood of others,","But mantled in your own."],"id":240,"m":"Yes, if you come covered not in other men's blood but in your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["O! let me clip ye","In arms as sound as when I woo'd; in heart","As merry as when our nuptial day was done,","And tapers burn'd to bedward."],"id":241,"m":"Oh, let me hug you in arms as strong as when I courted my wife, with a heart as happy as when our wedding day was over and the candles were lit for bed!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Flower of warriors,","How is't with Titus Lartius?"],"id":242,"m":"Flower of warriors, how is Titus Lartius doing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["As with a man busied about decrees:","Condemning some to death and some to exile;","Ransoming him or pitying, threat'ning the other;","Holding Corioli in the name of Rome,","Even like a fawning greyhound in the leash,","To let him slip at will."],"id":243,"m":"Like a man busy making decrees: sentencing some to death and some to exile, letting one man pay a ransom or pitying him, threatening another; holding Corioli for Rome like a fawning greyhound on a leash, ready to let it go when he likes."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Where is that slave","Which told me they had beat you to your trenches?","Where's he? call him hither."],"id":244,"m":"Where's that wretch who told me they'd beaten you back to your trenches? Where is he? Call him here."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Let him alone;","He did inform the truth: but for our gentlemen,","The common file,—a plague!—tribunes for them!—","The mouse ne'er shunned the cat as they did budge","From rascals worse than they."],"id":245,"m":"Leave him alone; he was telling the truth. But our gentlemen, the common soldiers (a plague on them! Tribunes for them!), never did a mouse run from a cat the way they ran from scoundrels worse than themselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["But how prevail'd you?"],"id":246,"m":"But how did you win?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Will the time serve to tell? I do not think.","Where is the enemy? are you lords o' the field?","If not, why cease you till you are so?"],"id":247,"m":"Is there time to tell it? I don't think so. Where's the enemy? Are you masters of the field? If not, why stop before you are?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Marcius,","We have at disadvantage fought, and did","Retire, to win our purpose."],"id":248,"m":"Marcius, we fought at a disadvantage and retreated in order to gain our purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["How lies their battle? know you on which side","They have placed their men of trust?"],"id":249,"m":"How is their army drawn up? Do you know on which side they've put their most trusted men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["As I guess, Marcius,","Their bands in the vaward are the Antiates,","Of their best trust; o'er them Aufidius,","Their very heart of hope."],"id":250,"m":"As I guess, Marcius, the troops in their front line are men of Antium, the ones they trust most, and over them is Aufidius, the very heart of their hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I do beseech you,","By all the battles wherein we have fought,","By the blood we have shed together, by the vows","We have made to endure friends, that you directly","Set me against Aufidius and his Antiates;","And that you not delay the present, but,","Filling the air with swords advanc'd and darts,","We prove this very hour."],"id":251,"m":"I beg you, by all the battles we've fought, by the blood we've shed together, by the vows we've made to stay friends forever, put me straight up against Aufidius and his men of Antium. And don't let this moment slip; let's fill the air with raised swords and spears and try our luck this very hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Though I could wish","You were conducted to a gentle bath,","And balms applied to you, yet dare I never","Deny your asking: take your choice of those","That best can aid your action."],"id":252,"m":"Though I'd rather you were taken to a soothing bath and had ointment put on your wounds, I'd never dare refuse what you ask. Take your pick of the men who can best help you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Those are they","That most are willing.—If any such be here,—","As it were sin to doubt,—that love this painting","Wherein you see me smear'd; if any fear","Lesser his person than an ill report;","If any think brave death outweighs bad life,","And that his country's dearer than himself;","Let him alone, or so many so minded,","Wave thus [waving his hand], to express his disposition,","And follow Marcius."],"id":253,"m":"The best men are the most willing. If there are any here (and it would be a sin to doubt it) who love this paint you see me smeared with, if any fear for their bodies less than for a bad reputation, if any think a brave death outweighs a shameful life and their country is dearer than themselves, let him alone, or all who think the same, wave like this (waving his hand) to show their feelings, and follow Marcius."},{"t":"sd","text":"They all shout and wave their swords; take him up in their arms and cast up their caps.","id":254},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["O, me alone! Make you a sword of me?","If these shows be not outward, which of you","But is four Volsces? none of you but is","Able to bear against the great Aufidius","A shield as hard as his. A certain number,","Though thanks to all, must I select from all: the rest","Shall bear the business in some other fight,","As cause will be obey'd. Please you to march;","And four shall quickly draw out my command,","Which men are best inclin'd."],"id":255,"m":"Oh, me alone! Are you making a sword of me? If this isn't just for show, which of you isn't worth four Volscians? Every one of you can hold up a shield against the great Aufidius as hard as his own. Thanking you all, I must still choose a certain number; the rest will do their part in some other fight, as duty requires. Please march on, and four of you will quickly pick out the men best suited for my command."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["March on, my fellows;","Make good this ostentation, and you shall","Divide in all with us."],"id":256,"m":"March on, my comrades. Make good on this show of spirit and you'll share in everything with us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":257},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"VII","loc":"The gates of Corioli.","id":258},{"t":"sd","text":"TITUS LARTIUS, having set a guard upon Corioli, going with drum and trumpet toward COMINIUS and CAIUS MARCIUS, enters with a LIEUTENANT, a party of Soldiers, and a Scout.","id":259},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["So, let the ports be guarded: keep your duties","As I have set them down. If I do send, despatch","Those centuries to our aid; the rest will serve","For a short holding: if we lose the field","We cannot keep the town."],"id":260,"m":"So, guard the gates, and carry out your duties as I've set them out. If I send for them, rush those companies to help us; the rest will do for a short defence. If we lose the battle, we can't keep the town."},{"t":"sp","who":"LIEUTENANT","lines":["Fear not our care, sir."],"id":261,"m":"Don't doubt we'll take care, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Hence, and shut your gates upon's.—","Our guider, come; to the Roman camp conduct us."],"id":262,"m":"Go, and shut your gates behind us. Our guide, come; lead us to the Roman camp."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":263},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"VIII","loc":"A field of battle between the Roman and the Volscian camps.","id":264},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter, from opposite sides, MARCIUS and AUFIDIUS.","id":265},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I'll fight with none but thee, for I do hate thee","Worse than a promise-breaker."],"id":266,"m":"I'll fight no one but you, because I hate you more than a man who breaks his word."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["We hate alike:","Not Afric owns a serpent I abhor","More than thy fame and envy. Fix thy foot."],"id":267,"m":"We hate each other equally. There's no snake in Africa I loathe more than your fame, which I envy. Stand your ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Let the first budger die the other's slave,","And the gods doom him after!"],"id":268,"m":"Let whoever moves first die as the other's slave, and then let the gods damn him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["If I fly, Marcius,","Halloo me like a hare."],"id":269,"m":"If I run, Marcius, hunt me down like a hare."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Within these three hours, Tullus,","Alone I fought in your Corioli walls,","And made what work I pleas'd: 'tis not my blood","Wherein thou seest me mask'd: for thy revenge","Wrench up thy power to the highest."],"id":270,"m":"Within these last three hours, Tullus, I fought alone inside your walls of Corioli and did whatever damage I liked. It's not my own blood you see me covered in. Strain your strength to the highest to get your revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Wert thou the Hector","That was the whip of your bragg'd progeny,","Thou shouldst not scape me here.—"],"id":271,"m":"Even if you were Hector, the scourge of the Greeks and pride of your boasted Trojan ancestors, you wouldn't escape me here."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight, and certain Volsces come to the aid of AUFIDIUS.","id":272},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Officious, and not valiant,—you have sham'd me","In your condemned seconds."],"id":273,"m":"Meddling, not brave! You've shamed me with your damned help."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt fighting, driven in by MAR.","id":274},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IX","loc":"The Roman camp.","id":275},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. A retreat is sounded. Flourish. Enter, at one side, COMINIUS and Romans; at the other side, MARCIUS, with his arm in a scarf, and other Romans.","id":276},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["If I should tell thee o'er this thy day's work,","Thou't not believe thy deeds: but I'll report it","Where senators shall mingle tears with smiles;","Where great patricians shall attend, and shrug,","I' the end admire; where ladies shall be frighted","And, gladly quak'd, hear more; where the dull tribunes,","That, with the fusty plebeians, hate thine honours,","Shall say, against their hearts 'We thank the gods","Our Rome hath such a soldier.'","Yet cam'st thou to a morsel of this feast,","Having fully dined before."],"id":277,"m":"If I told you all over again what you did today, you wouldn't believe your own deeds. But I'll report it where senators will mix tears with smiles, where great patricians will listen, shrug, and finally admire; where ladies will be frightened and, happily trembling, ask to hear more; where the dull tribunes, who along with the mouldy plebeians hate your honours, will say against their will, \"We thank the gods that our Rome has such a soldier.\" And yet you came to this feast for just a bite, having already eaten your fill."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TITUS LARTIUS, with his power, from the pursuit.","id":278},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["O general,","Here is the steed, we the caparison:","Hadst thou beheld,—"],"id":279,"m":"Oh general, here's the horse, and we're just its trappings. If you had seen—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Pray now, no more: my mother,","Who has a charter to extol her blood,","When she does praise me grieves me. I have done","As you have done,—that's what I can; induced","As you have been,—that's for my country:","He that has but effected his good will","Hath overta'en mine act."],"id":280,"m":"Please, no more. My mother, who has a right to praise her own child, upsets me when she praises me. I've done what you've done, that is, what I can; driven as you were, that is, by love of my country. Anyone who has carried out his good intentions has done more than I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["You shall not be","The grave of your deserving; Rome must know","The value of her own: 'twere a concealment","Worse than a theft, no less than a traducement,","To hide your doings; and to silence that","Which, to the spire and top of praises vouch'd,","Would seem but modest: therefore, I beseech you,—","In sign of what you are, not to reward","What you have done,—before our army hear me."],"id":281,"m":"You won't be the grave of your own merit. Rome must know the worth of her own men. It would be a concealment worse than theft, as bad as slander, to hide what you've done and keep quiet about deeds that would seem modestly described even if praised to the skies. So I beg you, as a sign of what you are and not as a reward for what you've done, hear me in front of the army."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I have some wounds upon me, and they smart","To hear themselves remember'd."],"id":282,"m":"I have some wounds on me, and they sting to hear themselves mentioned."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Should they not,","Well might they fester 'gainst ingratitude,","And tent themselves with death. Of all the horses,—","Whereof we have ta'en good, and good store,—of all","The treasure in this field achiev'd and city,","We render you the tenth; to be ta'en forth","Before the common distribution at","Your only choice."],"id":283,"m":"If they didn't hear it, they might well fester from ingratitude and be dressed only by death. Of all the horses we've captured, and there are good ones and plenty, and all the treasure won in this field and in the city, we give you a tenth, to be taken out before the general share-out, entirely your choice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I thank you, general,","But cannot make my heart consent to take","A bribe to pay my sword: I do refuse it;","And stand upon my common part with those","That have beheld the doing."],"id":284,"m":"Thank you, general, but I can't bring my heart to take a bribe as payment for my sword. I refuse it, and I'll take only my ordinary share with those who saw the fighting."},{"t":"sd","text":"A long flourish. They all cry 'Marcius, Marcius!', cast up their caps and lances. COMINIUS and LARTIUS stand bare.","id":285},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["May these same instruments which you profane","Never sound more! When drums and trumpets shall","I' the field prove flatterers, let courts and cities be","Made all of false-fac'd soothing.","When steel grows soft as the parasite's silk,","Let him be made a coverture for the wars.","No more, I say! for that I have not wash'd","My nose that bled, or foil'd some debile wretch,—","Which, without note, here's many else have done,—","You shout me forth in acclamations hyperbolical;","As if I loved my little should be dieted","In praises sauc'd with lies."],"id":286,"m":"May these same instruments that you're abusing never sound again! When drums and trumpets on the battlefield turn into flatterers, then courts and cities may as well be made entirely of two-faced smooth talk. When steel grows as soft as a flatterer's silk, let silk be made into armour for war. No more, I say! Just because I haven't washed my bleeding nose or have knocked down some feeble wretch, which plenty of others here have done without being noticed, you cheer me with exaggerated shouts, as if I liked my small deeds to be fed on praise flavoured with lies."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Too modest are you;","More cruel to your good report than grateful","To us that give you truly; by your patience,","If 'gainst yourself you be incens'd, we'll put you,—","Like one that means his proper harm,—in manacles,","Then reason safely with you.—Therefore be it known,","As to us, to all the world, that Caius Marcius","Wears this war's garland: in token of the which,","My noble steed, known to the camp, I give him,","With all his trim belonging; and from this time,","For what he did before Corioli, call him,","With all the applause—and clamour of the host,","'Caius Marcius Coriolanus.'—","Bear the addition nobly ever!"],"id":287,"m":"You're too modest, more cruel to your own reputation than grateful to us who are being truthful about you. With your permission, if you're angry with yourself, we'll put you in handcuffs like a man who means to harm himself, and then argue with you safely. So let it be known to the whole world, as it is to us, that Caius Marcius wins this war's garland. As a token of it I give him my noble horse, known throughout the camp, with all its trappings; and from now on, for what he did at Corioli, call him, with all the army's applause and cheering, \"Caius Marcius Coriolanus.\" Wear that title nobly forever!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Trumpets sound, and drums.","id":288},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Caius Marcius Coriolanus!"],"id":289,"m":"Caius Marcius Coriolanus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I will go wash;","And when my face is fair you shall perceive","Whether I blush or no: howbeit, I thank you;—","I mean to stride your steed; and at all times","To undercrest your good addition","To the fairness of my power."],"id":290,"m":"I'll go and wash, and when my face is clean you'll see whether I'm blushing or not. Still, I thank you. I'll ride your horse, and always try to live up to the honourable name you've given me as well as I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["So, to our tent;","Where, ere we do repose us, we will write","To Rome of our success.—You, Titus Lartius,","Must to Corioli back: send us to Rome","The best, with whom we may articulate","For their own good and ours."],"id":291,"m":"So, to our tent, where before we rest we'll write to Rome about our success. You, Titus Lartius, must go back to Corioli. Send us their leading men to Rome, so we can make terms with them for their good and ours."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["I shall, my lord."],"id":292,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["The gods begin to mock me. I, that now","Refus'd most princely gifts, am bound to beg","Of my lord general."],"id":293,"m":"The gods are starting to mock me. I've just refused princely gifts, and now I have to beg from my lord general."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Take't: 'tis yours.—What is't?"],"id":294,"m":"Take it, it's yours. What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I sometime lay here in Corioli","At a poor man's house; he used me kindly:","He cried to me; I saw him prisoner;","But then Aufidius was within my view,","And wrath o'erwhelmed my pity: I request you","To give my poor host freedom."],"id":295,"m":"Once, here in Corioli, I stayed at a poor man's house. He treated me kindly. He called out to me; I saw he'd been taken prisoner. But just then I caught sight of Aufidius, and anger swamped my pity. I ask you to set my poor host free."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["O, well begg'd!","Were he the butcher of my son, he should","Be free as is the wind. Deliver him, Titus."],"id":296,"m":"Oh, well asked! Even if he had butchered my son, he'd be as free as the wind. Release him, Titus."},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["Marcius, his name?"],"id":297,"m":"Marcius, what's his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["By Jupiter, forgot:—","I am weary; yea, my memory is tir'd.—","Have we no wine here?"],"id":298,"m":"By Jupiter, I've forgotten. I'm worn out; yes, even my memory's tired. Haven't we any wine here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Go we to our tent:","The blood upon your visage dries; 'tis time","It should be look'd to: come."],"id":299,"m":"Let's go to our tent. The blood on your face is drying; it's time it was seen to. Come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":300},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"X","loc":"The camp of the Volsces.","id":301},{"t":"sd","text":"A flourish. Cornets. Enter TULLUS AUFIDIUS, bloody, with two or three soldiers.","id":302},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["The town is ta'en."],"id":303,"m":"The town has been taken."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["'Twill be delivered back on good condition."],"id":304,"m":"We'll get it back on good terms."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Condition!","I would I were a Roman; for I cannot,","Being a Volsce, be that I am.—Condition?","What good condition can a treaty find","I' the part that is at mercy?—Five times, Marcius,","I have fought with thee; so often hast thou beat me;","And wouldst do so, I think, should we encounter","As often as we eat.—By the elements,","If e'er again I meet him beard to beard,","He's mine or I am his: mine emulation","Hath not that honour in't it had; for where","I thought to crush him in an equal force,—","True sword to sword,—I'll potch at him some way,","Or wrath or craft may get him."],"id":305,"m":"Terms! I wish I were a Roman, because as a Volscian I can't be what I really am. Terms? What good terms can a treaty give the side that's at the other's mercy? Five times, Marcius, I've fought you, and every time you've beaten me, and I think you would even if we met as often as we eat. By the elements, if I ever meet him face to face again, either he's mine or I'm his. My rivalry has lost the honour it had, because where I once meant to crush him in a fair fight, sword against sword, now I'll stab at him somehow; either anger or cunning will get him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["He's the devil."],"id":306,"m":"He's the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Bolder, though not so subtle. My valour's poisoned","With only suffering stain by him; for him","Shall fly out of itself: nor sleep nor sanctuary,","Being naked, sick; nor fane nor Capitol,","The prayers of priests nor times of sacrifice,","Embarquements all of fury, shall lift up","Their rotten privilege and custom 'gainst","My hate to Marcius: where I find him, were it","At home, upon my brother's guard, even there,","Against the hospitable canon, would I","Wash my fierce hand in's heart. Go you to the city;","Learn how 'tis held; and what they are that must","Be hostages for Rome."],"id":307,"m":"Bolder, though not so cunning. My courage is poisoned just by being stained with defeat by him; it will abandon its own nature to get him. Neither sleep nor holy refuge, nor his being unarmed or sick, nor temple nor Capitol, nor priests' prayers or times of sacrifice, all of which hold back fury, shall raise their worn-out rights and customs against my hatred of Marcius. Wherever I find him, even at home under my brother's protection, there, against the laws of hospitality, I'd wash my fierce hand in his heart. Go to the city; find out how it's being held and who must be given as hostages to Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Will not you go?"],"id":308,"m":"Aren't you coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I am attended at the cypress grove: I pray you,—","'Tis south the city mills,—bring me word thither","How the world goes, that to the pace of it","I may spur on my journey."],"id":309,"m":"I'm expected at the cypress grove. Please (it's south of the city mills) bring me word there of how things stand, so I can set the pace of my journey to match."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["I shall, sir."],"id":310,"m":"I will, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":311},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A public place.","id":312},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MENENIUS, SICINIUS, and BRUTUS.","id":313},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["The augurer tells me we shall have news tonight."],"id":314,"m":"The fortune-teller says we'll have news tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Good or bad?"],"id":315,"m":"Good or bad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Not according to the prayer of the people, for they love not","Marcius."],"id":316,"m":"Not the kind the people are praying for, since they don't love Marcius."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Nature teaches beasts to know their friends."],"id":317,"m":"Nature teaches animals to know their friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Pray you, who does the wolf love?"],"id":318,"m":"Tell me, whom does the wolf love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["The lamb."],"id":319,"m":"The lamb."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Ay, to devour him, as the hungry plebeians would the noble","Marcius."],"id":320,"m":"Yes, to eat it, as the hungry plebeians would like to eat the noble Marcius."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He's a lamb indeed, that baas like a bear."],"id":321,"m":"He's a lamb all right, one that bleats like a bear."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["He's a bear indeed, that lives like a lamb. You two are old men:","tell me one thing that I shall ask you."],"id":322,"m":"He's a bear all right, one that lives like a lamb. You two are old men; answer me one question."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["Well, sir."],"id":323,"m":"Well, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["In what enormity is Marcius poor in, that you two have not","in abundance?"],"id":324,"m":"What vice is Marcius short of that you two don't have plenty of?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He's poor in no one fault, but stored with all."],"id":325,"m":"He's short of no fault at all; he's stocked with every one."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Especially in pride."],"id":326,"m":"Especially pride."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["And topping all others in boasting."],"id":327,"m":"And he's ahead of everyone at boasting."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["This is strange now: do you two know how you are censured here in","the city, I mean of us o' the right-hand file? Do you?"],"id":328,"m":"Now this is odd. Do you two know what people here in the city think of you, I mean people of our upper class? Do you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["Why, how are we censured?"],"id":329,"m":"Why, what do they think of us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Because you talk of pride now,—will you not be angry?"],"id":330,"m":"Because you're talking about pride now, you won't be angry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["Well, well, sir, well."],"id":331,"m":"Well, well, sir, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Why, 'tis no great matter; for a very little thief of occasion","will rob you of a great deal of patience: give your dispositions","the reins, and be angry at your pleasures; at the least, if you","take it as a pleasure to you in being so. You blame Marcius for","being proud?"],"id":332,"m":"Why, it doesn't matter much, since the smallest excuse will rob you of a great deal of patience. Give your tempers free rein and be angry as much as you like, at least if you enjoy being angry. You blame Marcius for being proud?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["We do it not alone, sir."],"id":333,"m":"We're not the only ones, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I know you can do very little alone; for your helps are many, or","else your actions would grow wondrous single: your abilities are","too infant-like for doing much alone. You talk of pride: O that","you could turn your eyes toward the napes of your necks, and make","but an interior survey of your good selves! O that you could!"],"id":334,"m":"I know you can do very little on your own; you have plenty of helpers, or else your doings would be remarkably thin. Your abilities are too babyish to do much alone. You talk about pride: oh, if only you could turn your eyes round to the backs of your necks and take a good look inside your own good selves! Oh, if only you could!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["What then, sir?"],"id":335,"m":"What then, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Why, then you should discover a brace of unmeriting, proud,","violent, testy magistrates,—alias fools,—as any in Rome."],"id":336,"m":"Why, then you'd find a pair of undeserving, proud, violent, bad-tempered magistrates, otherwise known as fools, as any in Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Menenius, you are known well enough too."],"id":337,"m":"Menenius, you're pretty well known too."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I am known to be a humorous patrician, and one that loves a cup","of hot wine with not a drop of allaying Tiber in't; said to","be something imperfect in favouring the first complaint, hasty","and tinder-like upon too trivial motion; one that converses more","with the buttock of the night than with the forehead of the","morning. What I think I utter, and spend my malice in my breath.","Meeting two such wealsmen as you are,—I cannot call you","Lycurguses,—if the drink you give me touch my palate adversely,","I make a crooked face at it. I cannot say your worships have","delivered the matter well when I find the ass in compound with","the major part of your syllables; and though I must be content to","bear with those that say you are reverend grave men, yet they lie","deadly that tell you have good faces. If you see this in the map","of my microcosm, follows it that I am known well enough too? What","harm can your bisson conspectuities glean out of this character,","if I be known well enough too?"],"id":338,"m":"I'm known as a whimsical patrician who loves a cup of hot wine without a drop of Tiber water to dilute it; said to be rather at fault for siding with whoever complains first, quick to flare up like tinder over trifles; one who keeps company with the backside of the night more than the brow of the morning. I say what I think, and use up my spite in talking. When I meet two statesmen like you (I can't call you great lawgivers like Lycurgus), if the drink you give me tastes bad, I pull a face at it. I can't say your worships have argued well when I find most of what you say is asinine; and though I have to put up with people who call you serious, respectable men, those who tell you you have good faces are lying through their teeth. If you can read this in the map of my little world, does it follow that I'm well known too? What harm can your blinkered sight pick out of this description, if I'm well known too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come, sir, come, we know you well enough."],"id":339,"m":"Come, sir, come, we know you well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You know neither me, yourselves, nor anything. You are ambitious","for poor knaves' caps and legs; you wear out a good wholesome","forenoon in hearing a cause between an orange-wife and a","fosset-seller, and then rejourn the controversy of threepence","to a second day of audience.—When you are hearing a matter","between party and party, if you chance to be pinched with the","colic, you make faces like mummers, set up the bloody flag","against all patience, and, in roaring for a chamber-pot, dismiss","the controversy bleeding, the more entangled by your hearing: all","the peace you make in their cause is calling both the parties","knaves. You are a pair of strange ones."],"id":340,"m":"You know neither me, nor yourselves, nor anything. You're greedy for poor wretches to doff their caps and bow to you. You waste a whole good morning hearing a case between an orange-seller and a tap-maker, and then adjourn the threepenny dispute to a second day of hearing. When you're hearing a case between two parties, if you happen to get a stomach ache, you pull faces like clowns, raise the red flag of war against all patience, and, bellowing for a chamber pot, dismiss the case still bleeding, more tangled than before you heard it. The only peace you make between them is calling both sides scoundrels. You're a strange pair."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come, come, you are well understood to be a perfecter giber","for the table than a necessary bencher in the Capitol."],"id":341,"m":"Come, come, everyone knows you're better as a joker at the dinner table than as a useful judge in the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Our very priests must become mockers if they shall encounter such","ridiculous subjects as you are. When you speak best unto the","purpose, it is not worth the wagging of your beards; and your","beards deserve not so honourable a grave as to stuff a botcher's","cushion or to be entombed in an ass's pack-saddle. Yet you must","be saying, Marcius is proud; who, in a cheap estimation, is worth","all your predecessors since Deucalion; though peradventure some","of the best of 'em were hereditary hangmen. God-den to your","worships: more of your conversation would infect my brain, being","the herdsmen of the beastly plebeians: I will be bold to take my","leave of you."],"id":342,"m":"Even our priests would have to turn mockers if they met such ridiculous creatures as you. When you speak most to the point, it isn't worth the wagging of your beards, and your beards don't deserve so honourable a grave as stuffing a tailor's cushion or being buried in a donkey's saddle. Yet you have to keep saying Marcius is proud, a man who, at the lowest estimate, is worth all your ancestors since the Flood, though maybe some of the best of them were hangmen by family trade. Good evening to your worships. Any more of your conversation would infect my brain, since you're the herdsmen of those beastly plebeians. I'll make so bold as to take my leave of you."},{"t":"sd","text":"BRUTUS and SICINIUS retire.","id":343},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter VOLUMNIA, VIRGILIA, VALERIA, &c.","id":344},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","cont":1,"lines":["How now, my as fair as noble ladies,—and the moon, were she","earthly, no nobler,—whither do you follow your eyes so fast?"],"id":345,"m":"Well now, my ladies, as beautiful as you are noble (and the moon, if she lived on earth, would be no nobler), where are you hurrying off to so fast?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Honourable Menenius, my boy Marcius approaches; for the love of","Juno, let's go."],"id":346,"m":"Honourable Menenius, my boy Marcius is coming. For the love of Juno, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Ha! Marcius coming home!"],"id":347,"m":"Ha! Marcius coming home!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Ay, worthy Menenius, and with most prosperous approbation."],"id":348,"m":"Yes, worthy Menenius, and with the greatest success and acclaim."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Take my cap, Jupiter, and I thank thee.—Hoo! Marcius coming","home!"],"id":349,"m":"Take my cap, Jupiter, and thank you! Hurrah! Marcius coming home!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA AND VIRGILIA","lines":["Nay, 'tis true."],"id":350,"m":"Yes, it's true."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Look, here's a letter from him: the state hath another,","his wife another; and I think there's one at home for you."],"id":351,"m":"Look, here's a letter from him. The state has another, his wife another, and I think there's one at home for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I will make my very house reel to-night.—A letter for me?"],"id":352,"m":"I'll make my whole house shake with celebration tonight. A letter for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Yes, certain, there's a letter for you; I saw it."],"id":353,"m":"Yes, certainly, there's a letter for you; I saw it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["A letter for me! It gives me an estate of seven years'","health; in which time I will make a lip at the physician: the","most sovereign prescription in Galen is but empiricutic, and, to","this preservative, of no better report than a horse-drench. Is he","not wounded? he was wont to come home wounded."],"id":354,"m":"A letter for me! It gives me seven years of good health, during which I'll sneer at the doctor. The best prescription in Galen is just quackery and, next to this tonic, no better than horse medicine. Isn't he wounded? He usually comes home wounded."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["O, no, no, no."],"id":355,"m":"Oh no, no, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["O, he is wounded, I thank the gods for't."],"id":356,"m":"Oh, he's wounded, and I thank the gods for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["So do I too, if it be not too much.—Brings a victory in","his pocket?—The wounds become him."],"id":357,"m":"So do I, as long as it's not too badly. Is he bringing a victory in his pocket? The wounds suit him."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["On's brows: Menenius, he comes the third time home with the oaken","garland."],"id":358,"m":"On his brows, Menenius. He's coming home for the third time with the oak garland."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Has he disciplined Aufidius soundly?"],"id":359,"m":"Has he taught Aufidius a good lesson?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Titus Lartius writes,—they fought together, but Aufidius","got off."],"id":360,"m":"Titus Lartius writes that they fought each other, but Aufidius got away."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["And 'twas time for him too, I'll warrant him that: an he","had stayed by him, I would not have been so fidiused for all the","chests in Corioli and the gold that's in them. Is the Senate","possessed of this?"],"id":361,"m":"And it was high time he did, I promise you. If he'd stayed to face him, I wouldn't have wanted to be Aufidius'd like that for all the chests in Corioli and the gold in them. Has the Senate been told?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Good ladies, let's go.—Yes, yes, yes; the Senate has letters","from the general, wherein he gives my son the whole name of the","war: he hath in this action outdone his former deeds doubly."],"id":362,"m":"Good ladies, let's go. Yes, yes, yes; the Senate has letters from the general in which he gives my son full credit for the war. In this campaign he has doubled his earlier deeds."},{"t":"sp","who":"VALERIA","lines":["In troth, there's wondrous things spoke of him."],"id":363,"m":"Honestly, people are saying wonderful things about him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Wondrous! ay, I warrant you, and not without his true purchasing."],"id":364,"m":"Wonderful! Yes, I'm sure, and not without his having truly earned them."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["The gods grant them true!"],"id":365,"m":"May the gods make them true!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["True! pow, wow."],"id":366,"m":"True! Pooh, nonsense."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["True! I'll be sworn they are true. Where is he wounded?—[To the","TRIBUNES, who come forward.] God save your good worships! Marcius","is coming home; he has more cause to be proud.—Where is he","wounded?"],"id":367,"m":"True! I'll swear they're true. Where is he wounded? (To the Tribunes, who come forward) God save your worships! Marcius is coming home; he has more reason to be proud. Where is he wounded?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I' the shoulder and i' the left arm; there will be large","cicatrices to show the people when he shall stand for his place.","He received in the repulse of Tarquin seven hurts i' the body."],"id":368,"m":"In the shoulder and the left arm. There'll be big scars to show the people when he stands for office. When Tarquin was driven out, he got seven wounds in the body."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["One i' the neck and two i' the thigh,—there's nine that I","know."],"id":369,"m":"One in the neck and two in the thigh; that's nine that I know of."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["He had, before this last expedition, twenty-five wounds upon him."],"id":370,"m":"Before this last campaign, he had twenty-five wounds on him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Now it's twenty-seven: every gash was an enemy's grave."],"id":371,"m":"Now it's twenty-seven; every gash was an enemy's grave."},{"t":"sd","text":"A shout and flourish.","id":372},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Hark! the trumpets."],"id":373,"m":"Listen! The trumpets."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["These are the ushers of Marcius: before him","He carries noise, and behind him he leaves tears;","Death, that dark spirit, in's nervy arm doth lie;","Which, being advanc'd, declines, and then men die."],"id":374,"m":"These are Marcius's heralds. He carries noise in front of him and leaves tears behind him. Death, that dark spirit, lies in his muscular arm, which rises, falls, and then men die."},{"t":"sd","text":"A sennet. Trumpets sound. Enter COMINIUS and TITUS LARTIUS; between them, CORIOLANUS, crowned with an oaken garland; with CAPTAINS and Soldiers and a HERALD.","id":375},{"t":"sp","who":"HERALD","lines":["Know, Rome, that all alone Marcius did fight","Within Corioli gates: where he hath won,","With fame, a name to Caius Marcius; these","In honour follows Coriolanus:—","Welcome to Rome, renowned Coriolanus!"],"id":376,"m":"Know, Rome, that Marcius fought all alone inside the gates of Corioli, where along with fame he has won a new name for Caius Marcius; in honour, Coriolanus follows those names. Welcome to Rome, famous Coriolanus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish.","id":377},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Welcome to Rome, renowned Coriolanus!"],"id":378,"m":"Welcome to Rome, famous Coriolanus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["No more of this, it does offend my heart;","Pray now, no more."],"id":379,"m":"No more of this; it hurts my heart. Please, no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Look, sir, your mother!"],"id":380,"m":"Look, sir, your mother!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["O,","You have, I know, petition'd all the gods","For my prosperity!"],"id":381,"m":"Oh, I know you have begged all the gods for my success!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels.","id":382},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Nay, my good soldier, up;","My gentle Marcius, worthy Caius, and","By deed-achieving honour newly nam'd,—","What is it?—Coriolanus must I call thee?","But, O, thy wife!"],"id":383,"m":"No, my good soldier, get up. My gentle Marcius, worthy Caius, newly named by honour won through deeds— what is it? Must I call you Coriolanus? But oh, your wife!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["My gracious silence, hail!","Wouldst thou have laugh'd had I come coffin'd home,","That weep'st to see me triumph? Ah, my dear,","Such eyes the widows in Corioli wear,","And mothers that lack sons."],"id":384,"m":"My dear silent one, greetings! Would you have laughed if I'd come home in a coffin, since you cry to see me in triumph? Ah, my dear, the widows in Corioli have eyes like yours, and so do mothers who have lost their sons."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Now the gods crown thee!"],"id":385,"m":"Now may the gods crown you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["And live you yet? [To VALERIA]—O my sweet lady, pardon."],"id":386,"m":"And you're still alive? (To Valeria) Oh my sweet lady, forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I know not where to turn.—O, welcome home;—and welcome,","general;—and you are welcome all."],"id":387,"m":"I don't know which way to turn. Oh, welcome home; and welcome, general; and you're all welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["A hundred thousand welcomes.—I could weep","And I could laugh; I am light and heavy.—Welcome:","A curse begin at very root on's heart","That is not glad to see thee!—You are three","That Rome should dote on: yet, by the faith of men,","We have some old crab trees here at home that will not","Be grafted to your relish. Yet welcome, warriors.","We call a nettle but a nettle; and","The faults of fools but folly."],"id":388,"m":"A hundred thousand welcomes. I could cry and I could laugh; I'm light-hearted and heavy-hearted. Welcome! May a curse strike right at the root of the heart of anyone who isn't glad to see you! You are three men Rome should dote on; and yet, I swear, we have some old crab-apple trees here at home who won't be grafted to your taste. Still, welcome, warriors. We call a nettle just a nettle, and the faults of fools just foolishness."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Ever right."],"id":389,"m":"Always right."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Menenius ever, ever."],"id":390,"m":"Always Menenius, always."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERALD","lines":["Give way there, and go on!"],"id":391,"m":"Make way there, and move on!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["[To his wife and mother] Your hand, and yours:","Ere in our own house I do shade my head,","The good patricians must be visited;","From whom I have receiv'd not only greetings,","But with them change of honours."],"id":392,"m":"(To his wife and mother) Your hand, and yours. Before I rest my head under our own roof, I must visit the good patricians, from whom I've received not only greetings but new honours with them."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I have lived","To see inherited my very wishes,","And the buildings of my fancy; only","There's one thing wanting, which I doubt not but","Our Rome will cast upon thee."],"id":393,"m":"I've lived to see my dearest wishes come true and my imagined castles built. There's only one thing missing, and I'm sure our Rome will give it to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Know, good mother,","I had rather be their servant in my way","Than sway with them in theirs."],"id":394,"m":"Understand, good mother, I'd rather be their servant my way than rule with them their way."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["On, to the Capitol."],"id":395,"m":"On to the Capitol."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Cornets. Exeunt in state, as before. The tribunes remain.","id":396},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["All tongues speak of him and the bleared sights","Are spectacled to see him: your prattling nurse","Into a rapture lets her baby cry","While she chats him: the kitchen malkin pins","Her richest lockram 'bout her reechy neck,","Clamb'ring the walls to eye him: stalls, bulks, windows,","Are smother'd up, leads fill'd and ridges hors'd","With variable complexions; all agreeing","In earnestness to see him: seld-shown flamens","Do press among the popular throngs, and puff","To win a vulgar station: our veil'd dames","Commit the war of white and damask, in","Their nicely gawded cheeks, to the wanton spoil","Of Phoebus' burning kisses; such a pother,","As if that whatsoever god who leads him","Were slily crept into his human powers,","And gave him graceful posture."],"id":397,"m":"Everyone's talking about him, and people with bleary eyes put on spectacles to see him. The chattering nurse lets her baby scream itself into fits while she gossips about him. The kitchen maid pins her best linen round her grimy neck and climbs the walls to look at him. Stalls, shop fronts and windows are packed, roofs filled and rooftops straddled by all sorts of people, all equally eager to see him. Priests who rarely show themselves push into the crowds and puff and pant to get a place among the common people. Our veiled ladies let the war of white and red in their carefully made-up cheeks be ruined by the sun's burning kisses. Such a fuss, as if whatever god guides him had slipped secretly into his human body and given him grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["On the sudden","I warrant him consul."],"id":398,"m":"I'm sure he'll be made consul straight away."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Then our office may","During his power go sleep."],"id":399,"m":"Then our office can go to sleep while he's in power."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["He cannot temp'rately transport his honours","From where he should begin and end; but will","Lose those he hath won."],"id":400,"m":"He can't carry his honours calmly from where he starts to where he should finish; he'll lose the ones he's won."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["In that there's comfort."],"id":401,"m":"There's comfort in that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Doubt not the commoners, for whom we stand,","But they, upon their ancient malice will forget,","With the least cause these his new honours; which","That he will give them make as little question","As he is proud to do't."],"id":402,"m":"Don't doubt that the common people we represent, because of their long-standing hatred, will forget these new honours of his at the slightest excuse; and there's as little doubt he'll give them one as there is that he's proud enough to do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I heard him swear,","Were he to stand for consul, never would he","Appear i' the market-place, nor on him put","The napless vesture of humility;","Nor, showing, as the manner is, his wounds","To the people, beg their stinking breaths."],"id":403,"m":"I heard him swear that if he stood for consul, he'd never appear in the market square or put on the threadbare gown of humility, or show his wounds to the people, as the custom is, to beg for their stinking breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["'Tis right."],"id":404,"m":"That's right."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["It was his word: O, he would miss it rather","Than carry it but by the suit of the gentry to him,","And the desire of the nobles."],"id":405,"m":"That's what he said. Oh, he'd rather lose it than win it except by the request of the gentry and the wishes of the nobles."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["I wish no better","Than have him hold that purpose, and to put it","In execution."],"id":406,"m":"I couldn't wish for anything better than for him to stick to that plan and carry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["'Tis most like he will."],"id":407,"m":"He very likely will."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["It shall be to him then, as our good wills,","A sure destruction."],"id":408,"m":"Then it will be what we want: his certain ruin."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["So it must fall out","To him or our authorities. For an end,","We must suggest the people in what hatred","He still hath held them; that to's power he would","Have made them mules, silenc'd their pleaders, and","Dispropertied their freedoms; holding them,","In human action and capacity,","Of no more soul nor fitness for the world","Than camels in their war; who have their provand","Only for bearing burdens, and sore blows","For sinking under them."],"id":409,"m":"It has to turn out that way, for him or for our authority. To that end, we must remind the people how much he has always hated them: that if he'd had the power he'd have made them mules, silenced their spokesmen and stripped them of their freedoms; thinking of them, as human beings, as having no more soul or use in the world than camels in war, who get their food only for carrying loads and get hard blows when they sink under them."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["This, as you say, suggested","At some time when his soaring insolence","Shall touch the people,—which time shall not want,","If it be put upon't; and that's as easy","As to set dogs on sheep,—will be his fire","To kindle their dry stubble; and their blaze","Shall darken him for ever."],"id":410,"m":"If this is suggested, as you say, at some moment when his soaring arrogance offends the people (and such a moment won't be lacking if he's provoked into it, which is as easy as setting dogs on sheep), it will be the fire that sets their dry stubble alight, and the blaze will blacken him forever."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter A MESSENGER.","id":411},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":412,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["You are sent for to the Capitol. 'Tis thought","That Marcius shall be consul:","I have seen the dumb men throng to see him, and","The blind to hear him speak: matrons flung gloves,","Ladies and maids their scarfs and handkerchers,","Upon him as he pass'd; the nobles bended","As to Jove's statue; and the commons made","A shower and thunder with their caps and shouts:","I never saw the like."],"id":413,"m":"You're called to the Capitol. They think Marcius will be made consul. I've seen the dumb crowd to see him and the blind to hear him speak. Married women threw gloves at him as he passed, ladies and girls their scarves and handkerchiefs; the nobles bowed as if to Jupiter's statue, and the common people made a storm of thunder with their caps and shouts. I've never seen anything like it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Let's to the Capitol;","And carry with us ears and eyes for the time,","But hearts for the event."],"id":414,"m":"Let's go to the Capitol, and take ears and eyes for what's happening now, but our hearts set on how it will end."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Have with you."],"id":415,"m":"I'm with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":416},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Rome. The Capitol.","id":417},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two OFFICERS, to lay cushions.","id":418},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["Come, come; they are almost here. How many stand for consulships?"],"id":419,"m":"Come on, come on; they're nearly here. How many are standing for the consulship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OFFICER","lines":["Three, they say; but 'tis thought of every one Coriolanus will","carry it."],"id":420,"m":"Three, they say; but everyone thinks Coriolanus will win it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["That's a brave fellow; but he's vengeance proud and loves not the","common people."],"id":421,"m":"He's a fine fellow, but he's terribly proud and doesn't love the common people."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OFFICER","lines":["Faith, there have been many great men that have flattered the","people, who ne'er loved them; and there be many that they have","loved, they know not wherefore; so that, if they love they know","not why, they hate upon no better a ground: therefore, for","Coriolanus neither to care whether they love or hate him","manifests the true knowledge he has in their disposition; and,","out of his noble carelessness, lets them plainly see't."],"id":422,"m":"Well, there have been plenty of great men who flattered the people without ever loving them, and plenty whom the people have loved without knowing why. So if they love without knowing why, they hate for no better reason. So Coriolanus not caring whether they love or hate him shows he truly understands their nature, and his noble indifference lets them see it plainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["If he did not care whether he had their love or no, he waved","indifferently 'twixt doing them neither good nor harm; but he","seeks their hate with greater devotion than they can render it","him; and leaves nothing undone that may fully discover him their","opposite. Now to seem to affect the malice and displeasure of the","people is as bad as that which he dislikes,—to flatter them for","their love."],"id":423,"m":"If he didn't care whether he had their love or not, he'd sit neutrally between doing them good and doing them harm. But he goes looking for their hatred more eagerly than they can return it, and does everything he can to show he's against them. Seeming to court the people's ill will and anger is as bad as what he dislikes, flattering them for their love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND OFFICER","lines":["He hath deserved worthily of his country: and his ascent is not","by such easy degrees as those who, having been supple and","courteous to the people, bonnetted, without any further deed to","have them at all, into their estimation and report: but he hath","so planted his honours in their eyes, and his actions in their","hearts, that for their tongues to be silent, and not confess","so much, were a kind of ingrateful injury; to report otherwise","were a malice that, giving itself the lie, would pluck reproof","and rebuke from every ear that heard it."],"id":424,"m":"He has served his country well, and his rise hasn't come by easy steps like those who were smooth and polite to the people and doffed their caps their way into the people's good opinion without doing anything else. He has planted his honours so firmly in their eyes and his deeds in their hearts that for their tongues to stay silent and not admit it would be an ungrateful wrong. To say otherwise would be malice that proves itself a liar and earns rebuke from everyone who heard it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST OFFICER","lines":["No more of him; he is a worthy man: make way, they are coming."],"id":425,"m":"No more about him; he's a worthy man. Make way, they're coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"A sennet. Enter, with Lictors before them, COMINIUS the Consul, MENENIUS, CORIOLANUS, Senators, SICINIUS and BRUTUS. The Senators take their places; the Tribunes take theirs also by themselves.","id":426},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Having determined of the Volsces, and","To send for Titus Lartius, it remains,","As the main point of this our after-meeting,","To gratify his noble service that","Hath thus stood for his country: therefore please you,","Most reverend and grave elders, to desire","The present consul, and last general","In our well-found successes, to report","A little of that worthy work perform'd","By Caius Marcius Coriolanus; whom","We met here both to thank and to remember","With honours like himself."],"id":427,"m":"Having settled what to do about the Volscians and to send for Titus Lartius, it remains, as the main point of this further meeting, to reward the noble service of the man who has stood up for his country in this way. So may it please you, most reverend and serious elders, to ask the present consul, our general in these well-won victories, to tell us a little of the worthy work done by Caius Marcius Coriolanus, whom we've met here both to thank and to honour as he deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Speak, good Cominius:","Leave nothing out for length, and make us think","Rather our state's defective for requital","Than we to stretch it out.—Masters o' the people,","We do request your kindest ears; and, after,","Your loving motion toward the common body,","To yield what passes here."],"id":428,"m":"Speak, good Cominius. Leave nothing out to save time, and make us think our state lacks the means to reward him rather than that we lack the will. Representatives of the people, we ask you to listen kindly, and afterwards to use your influence with the common people to agree to what's decided here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["We are convented","Upon a pleasing treaty; and have hearts","Inclinable to honour and advance","The theme of our assembly."],"id":429,"m":"We've been called together for a pleasant purpose, and our hearts are inclined to honour and support the subject of this meeting."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Which the rather","We shall be bless'd to do, if he remember","A kinder value of the people than","He hath hereto priz'd them at."],"id":430,"m":"Which we'll be all the happier to do if he values the people more kindly than he has up to now."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["That's off, that's off;","I would you rather had been silent. Please you","To hear Cominius speak?"],"id":431,"m":"That's beside the point, beside the point. I wish you'd kept quiet. Will you please hear Cominius speak?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Most willingly.","But yet my caution was more pertinent","Than the rebuke you give it."],"id":432,"m":"Very willingly. But my warning was more to the point than your rebuke."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["He loves your people;","But tie him not to be their bedfellow.—","Worthy Cominius, speak."],"id":433,"m":"He loves your people, but don't insist he sleep with them. Worthy Cominius, speak."},{"t":"sd","text":"CORIOLANUS rises, and offers to go away.","id":434},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Nay, keep your place."],"id":435,"m":"No, stay in your seat."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Sit, Coriolanus; never shame to hear","What you have nobly done."],"id":436,"m":"Sit down, Coriolanus. Never be ashamed to hear about what you've nobly done."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Your Honours' pardon:","I had rather have my wounds to heal again","Than hear say how I got them."],"id":437,"m":"Forgive me, your honours. I'd rather have my wounds to heal all over again than hear how I got them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Sir, I hope","My words disbench'd you not."],"id":438,"m":"Sir, I hope my words didn't drive you from your seat."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["No, sir; yet oft,","When blows have made me stay, I fled from words.","You sooth'd not, therefore hurt not: but your people,","I love them as they weigh."],"id":439,"m":"No, sir; but often, when blows made me stand my ground, I've run from words. You didn't flatter me, so you didn't hurt me. But your people, I love them as much as they're worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Pray now, sit down."],"id":440,"m":"Please, sit down."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I had rather have one scratch my head i' the sun","When the alarum were struck, than idly sit","To hear my nothings monster'd."],"id":441,"m":"I'd rather have someone scratch my head in the sun when the call to arms was sounded than sit idly listening to my trifles blown up into monsters."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":442},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Masters o' the people,","Your multiplying spawn how can he flatter,—","That's thousand to one good one,—when you now see","He had rather venture all his limbs for honour","Than one on's ears to hear it?—Proceed, Cominius."],"id":443,"m":"Representatives of the people, how can he flatter your swarming spawn (there's one good one in a thousand) when you see he'd rather risk all his limbs for honour than one of his ears to hear about it? Go on, Cominius."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I shall lack voice: the deeds of Coriolanus","Should not be utter'd feebly.—It is held","That valour is the chiefest virtue, and","Most dignifies the haver: if it be,","The man I speak of cannot in the world","Be singly counterpois'd. At sixteen years,","When Tarquin made a head for Rome, he fought","Beyond the mark of others; our then dictator,","Whom with all praise I point at, saw him fight,","When with his Amazonian chin he drove","The bristled lips before him: he bestrid","An o'erpress'd Roman and i' the consul's view","Slew three opposers: Tarquin's self he met,","And struck him on his knee: in that day's feats,","When he might act the woman in the scene,","He proved best man i' the field, and for his meed","Was brow-bound with the oak. His pupil age","Man-enter'd thus, he waxed like a sea;","And in the brunt of seventeen battles since","He lurch'd all swords of the garland. For this last,","Before and in Corioli, let me say,","I cannot speak him home: he stopp'd the fliers;","And by his rare example made the coward","Turn terror into sport: as weeds before","A vessel under sail, so men obey'd,","And fell below his stem: his sword,—death's stamp,—","Where it did mark, it took; from face to foot","He was a thing of blood, whose every motion","Was timed with dying cries: alone he enter'd","The mortal gate of the city, which he painted","With shunless destiny; aidless came off,","And with a sudden re-enforcement struck","Corioli like a planet. Now all's his:","When, by and by, the din of war 'gan pierce","His ready sense; then straight his doubled spirit","Re-quick'ned what in flesh was fatigate,","And to the battle came he; where he did","Run reeking o'er the lives of men, as if","'Twere a perpetual spoil: and till we call'd","Both field and city ours he never stood","To ease his breast with panting."],"id":444,"m":"I won't have voice enough; the deeds of Coriolanus shouldn't be told feebly. It's said that courage is the greatest virtue and brings most honour to whoever has it. If so, the man I'm speaking of has no equal in the world. At sixteen, when Tarquin raised an army against Rome, he fought better than anyone. Our dictator then, whom I name with all praise, saw him fight, when with his beardless, Amazon-like chin he drove bearded men before him. He stood over a Roman who'd been knocked down and, in the consul's sight, killed three attackers. He met Tarquin himself and struck him to his knee. In that day's deeds, when he was young enough to play a woman's part on stage, he proved the best man on the field, and as his reward he was crowned with oak leaves. Having entered manhood like that in his boyhood, he grew like a rising sea, and in the shock of seventeen battles since then he has robbed every other sword of the garland. As for this last one, at and in Corioli, let me say I can't praise him enough. He stopped the men who were running away and by his rare example made cowards treat terror as a game. Like weeds before a ship under sail, men gave way and fell beneath his prow. His sword, death's seal, took whatever it marked. From head to foot he was a thing of blood, every movement keeping time with dying cries. Alone he entered the deadly city gate, which he painted with the blood of men who couldn't escape their fate; he got out without help, and with sudden reinforcements struck Corioli like a planet's evil influence. Now it's all his. Then, when the noise of battle reached his alert ears, his doubled spirit revived his tired body, and he came to the battle, where he ran steaming over men's lives as if it would be endless slaughter. Until we could call both field and city ours, he never stopped to let his chest rest from panting."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Worthy man!"],"id":445,"m":"Worthy man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["He cannot but with measure fit the honours","Which we devise him."],"id":446,"m":"He's fully equal to whatever honours we plan for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Our spoils he kick'd at;","And looked upon things precious as they were","The common muck of the world: he covets less","Than misery itself would give; rewards","His deeds with doing them; and is content","To spend the time to end it."],"id":447,"m":"He kicked away our loot and looked on precious things as if they were the common dirt of the world. He wants less than poverty itself would give. He rewards his deeds by doing them, and is happy to spend his time just to fill it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["He's right noble:","Let him be call'd for."],"id":448,"m":"He's truly noble. Let him be called in."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Call Coriolanus."],"id":449,"m":"Call Coriolanus."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["He doth appear."],"id":450,"m":"Here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter CORIOLANUS.","id":451},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["The Senate, Coriolanus, are well pleas'd","To make thee consul."],"id":452,"m":"Coriolanus, the Senate is pleased to make you consul."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I do owe them still","My life and services."],"id":453,"m":"I still owe them my life and my services."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["It then remains","That you do speak to the people."],"id":454,"m":"Then it only remains for you to speak to the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I do beseech you","Let me o'erleap that custom; for I cannot","Put on the gown, stand naked, and entreat them,","For my wounds' sake to give their suffrage: please you","That I may pass this doing."],"id":455,"m":"I beg you, let me skip that custom. I can't put on the gown, stand there bare, and beg them to vote for me because of my wounds. Please let me be spared doing that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Sir, the people","Must have their voices; neither will they bate","One jot of ceremony."],"id":456,"m":"Sir, the people must have their votes; nor will they give up one bit of the ceremony."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Put them not to't:—","Pray you, go fit you to the custom; and","Take to you, as your predecessors have,","Your honour with your form."],"id":457,"m":"Don't push them on it. Please go and follow the custom, and take your honour together with its formalities, as those before you did."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["It is a part","That I shall blush in acting, and might well","Be taken from the people."],"id":458,"m":"It's a part I'll blush to act, and it could well be taken away from the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Mark you that?"],"id":459,"m":"Did you hear that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["To brag unto them,—thus I did, and thus;—","Show them the unaching scars which I should hide,","As if I had receiv'd them for the hire","Of their breath only!"],"id":460,"m":"To boast to them, \"I did this, and this\"; to show them healed scars I ought to hide, as if I'd got them only to buy their votes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Do not stand upon't.—","We recommend to you, tribunes of the people,","Our purpose to them;—and to our noble consul","Wish we all joy and honour."],"id":461,"m":"Don't insist on it. Tribunes of the people, we leave our proposal to you to put to them; and to our noble consul we all wish joy and honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SENATORS","lines":["To Coriolanus come all joy and honour!"],"id":462,"m":"May all joy and honour come to Coriolanus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt all but SICINIUS and BRUTUS.","id":463},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You see how he intends to use the people."],"id":464,"m":"You see how he means to treat the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["May they perceive's intent! He will require them","As if he did contemn what he requested","Should be in them to give."],"id":465,"m":"I hope they see what he intends! He'll ask them as if he despised the idea that it's in their power to give what he asks."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come, we'll inform them","Of our proceedings here: on the market-place","I know they do attend us."],"id":466,"m":"Come, we'll tell them what happened here. I know they're waiting for us in the market square."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":467},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Rome. The Forum.","id":468},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter several citizens.","id":469},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Once, if he do require our voices, we ought not to deny him."],"id":470,"m":"Once and for all, if he asks for our votes, we shouldn't refuse him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["We may, sir, if we will."],"id":471,"m":"We can, sir, if we want to."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["We have power in ourselves to do it, but it is a power that we","have no power to do: for if he show us his wounds and tell us his","deeds, we are to put our tongues into those wounds and speak for","them; so, if he tell us his noble deeds, we must also tell him","our noble acceptance of them. Ingratitude is monstrous: and for","the multitude to be ingrateful were to make a monster of the","multitude; of the which we being members, should bring ourselves","to be monstrous members."],"id":472,"m":"We have the power to do it in ourselves, but it's a power we have no power to use. If he shows us his wounds and tells us his deeds, we have to put our tongues into those wounds and speak for them. So if he tells us his noble deeds, we must tell him we nobly accept them. Ingratitude is monstrous, and for the crowd to be ungrateful would make a monster of the crowd; and since we're members of it, we'd turn ourselves into monstrous members."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["And to make us no better thought of, a little help will serve;","for once we stood up about the corn, he himself stuck not to call","us the many-headed multitude."],"id":473,"m":"And it won't take much to make us thought no better of; because once, when we rose up over the grain, he didn't hesitate to call us the many-headed mob."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["We have been called so of many; not that our heads are some","brown, some black, some auburn, some bald, but that our wits are","so diversely coloured; and truly I think if all our wits were to","issue out of one skull, they would fly east, west, north, south;","and their consent of one direct way should be at once to all the","points o' the compass."],"id":474,"m":"Lots of people have called us that, not because some of our heads are brown, some black, some auburn, some bald, but because our wits are so many different colours. And honestly, I think if all our wits came out of one skull, they'd fly east, west, north and south, and the only direction they'd agree on would be all points of the compass at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Think you so? Which way do you judge my wit would fly?"],"id":475,"m":"You think so? Which way do you reckon my wit would fly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Nay, your wit will not so soon out as another man's will,—'tis","strongly wedged up in a block-head; but if it were at liberty","'twould, sure, southward."],"id":476,"m":"Well, your wit wouldn't get out as quickly as another man's; it's jammed tight in a blockhead. But if it were free, it would surely go south."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Why that way?"],"id":477,"m":"Why that way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["To lose itself in a fog; where being three parts melted away with","rotten dews, the fourth would return for conscience' sake, to","help to get thee a wife."],"id":478,"m":"To lose itself in a fog, where, once three-quarters of it had melted away in rotten dews, the fourth part would come back out of conscience to help you get a wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["You are never without your tricks:—you may, you may."],"id":479,"m":"You're always up to your tricks. Go on, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Are you all resolved to give your voices? But that's no matter,","the greater part carries it. I say, if he would incline to the","people, there was never a worthier man. Here he comes, and in the","gown of humility. Mark his behaviour. We are not to stay all","together, but to come by him where he stands, by ones, by twos,","and by threes. He's to make his requests by particulars, wherein","every one of us has a single honour, in giving him our own voices","with our own tongues; therefore follow me, and I'll direct you","how you shall go by him."],"id":480,"m":"Are you all determined to give your votes? But it doesn't matter; the majority decides. I say, if he were well disposed to the people, there was never a worthier man. Here he comes, in the gown of humility. Watch how he behaves. We're not to stay all together but go up to him where he stands in ones, twos and threes. He has to make his requests one by one, so each of us gets the honour of giving him our own vote with our own tongue. So follow me, and I'll show you how to go up to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Content, content."],"id":481,"m":"Agreed, agreed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":482},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS and MENENIUS.","id":483},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["O sir, you are not right; have you not known","The worthiest men have done't!"],"id":484,"m":"Oh sir, you've got it wrong. Don't you know the worthiest men have done it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What must I say?—","'I pray, sir'—Plague upon't! I cannot bring","My tongue to such a pace.—'Look, sir,—my wounds;—","I got them in my country's service, when","Some certain of your brethren roar'd, and ran","From the noise of our own drums.'"],"id":485,"m":"What must I say? \"Please, sir\"? A plague on it! I can't get my tongue to go at that pace. \"Look, sir, my wounds. I got them serving my country, when some of your brothers roared and ran away from the noise of our own drums.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["O me, the gods!","You must not speak of that: you must desire them","To think upon you."],"id":486,"m":"Oh my, the gods! You mustn't mention that. You must ask them to think well of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Think upon me! Hang 'em!","I would they would forget me, like the virtues","Which our divines lose by 'em."],"id":487,"m":"Think of me! Hang them! I wish they'd forget me, like the moral lessons our priests waste on them."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You'll mar all:","I'll leave you. Pray you speak to 'em, I pray you,","In wholesome manner."],"id":488,"m":"You'll ruin everything. I'll leave you. Please speak to them, I beg you, in a decent way."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Bid them wash their faces","And keep their teeth clean."],"id":489,"m":"Tell them to wash their faces and keep their teeth clean."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit MENENIUS.","id":490},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["So, here comes a brace:"],"id":491,"m":"So, here comes a pair."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter two citizens.","id":492},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["You know the cause, sirs, of my standing here."],"id":493,"m":"You know why I'm standing here, sirs."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["We do, sir; tell us what hath brought you to't."],"id":494,"m":"We do, sir. Tell us what brought you to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Mine own desert."],"id":495,"m":"My own merit."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Your own desert?"],"id":496,"m":"Your own merit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Ay, not mine own desire."],"id":497,"m":"Yes, not my own desire."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["How! not your own desire!"],"id":498,"m":"What! Not your own desire!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["No, sir, 'twas never my desire yet to trouble the poor with","begging."],"id":499,"m":"No, sir; it was never my desire to trouble the poor by begging."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["You must think, if we give you anything, we hope to gain by you."],"id":500,"m":"You must realize that if we give you anything, we hope to get something from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Well then, I pray, your price o' the consulship?"],"id":501,"m":"Well then, tell me, what's your price for the consulship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["The price is to ask it kindly."],"id":502,"m":"The price is to ask for it nicely."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Kindly! sir, I pray, let me ha't: I have wounds to show you,","which shall be yours in private.—Your good voice, sir; what","say you?"],"id":503,"m":"Nicely! Sir, please, let me have it. I have wounds to show you, which you can see in private. Your good vote, sir; what do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["You shall ha' it, worthy sir."],"id":504,"m":"You'll have it, worthy sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["A match, sir.—There's in all two worthy voices begg'd.—I have","your alms: adieu."],"id":505,"m":"It's a deal, sir. That's two worthy votes begged in all. I have your charity. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["But this is something odd."],"id":506,"m":"But this is rather odd."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["An 'twere to give again,—but 'tis no matter."],"id":507,"m":"If it were to do over again— but it doesn't matter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt two citizens.","id":508},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter other two citizens.","id":509},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Pray you now, if it may stand with the tune of your voices that I","may be consul, I have here the customary gown."],"id":510,"m":"Please, if it suits the tune of your voices that I should be consul, I have the customary gown on here."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["You have deserved nobly of your country, and you have not","deserved nobly."],"id":511,"m":"You've served your country nobly, and you haven't served it nobly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Your enigma?"],"id":512,"m":"What's your riddle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["You have been a scourge to her enemies; you have been a rod to","her friends: you have not indeed loved the common people."],"id":513,"m":"You've been a scourge to her enemies, and a rod to her friends. You haven't really loved the common people."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["You should account me the more virtuous, that I have not been","common in my love. I will, sir, flatter my sworn brother, the","people, to earn a dearer estimation of them; 'tis a condition","they account gentle: and since the wisdom of their choice is","rather to have my hat than my heart, I will practise the","insinuating nod and be off to them most counterfeitly: that is,","sir, I will counterfeit the bewitchment of some popular man","and give it bountifully to the desirers. Therefore, beseech you,","I may be consul."],"id":514,"m":"You should think me all the more virtuous for not being common with my love. Sir, I'll flatter my sworn brother, the people, to win a higher opinion from them; that's a quality they think gentlemanly. And since in their wisdom they'd rather have my hat than my heart, I'll practise the ingratiating nod and doff my cap to them as falsely as possible; that is, sir, I'll imitate the charm of some popular man and hand it out generously to those who want it. So I beg you, let me be consul."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["We hope to find you our friend; and therefore give you our voices","heartily."],"id":515,"m":"We hope you'll be our friend, so we give you our votes gladly."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["You have received many wounds for your country."],"id":516,"m":"You've received many wounds for your country."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I will not seal your knowledge with showing them. I will make","much of your voices, and so trouble you no further."],"id":517,"m":"I won't confirm what you know by showing them. I'll value your votes highly, and so trouble you no further."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH CITIZENS","lines":["The gods give you joy, sir, heartily!"],"id":518,"m":"The gods give you joy, sir, truly!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt citizens.","id":519},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Most sweet voices!—","Better it is to die, better to starve,","Than crave the hire which first we do deserve.","Why in this wolvish toge should I stand here,","To beg of Hob and Dick that do appear,","Their needless vouches? custom calls me to't:—","What custom wills, in all things should we do't,","The dust on antique time would lie unswept,","And mountainous error be too highly heap'd","For truth to o'erpeer. Rather than fool it so,","Let the high office and the honour go","To one that would do thus.—I am half through;","The one part suffer'd, the other will I do.","Here come more voices."],"id":520,"m":"Sweetest voices! It's better to die, better to starve, than beg for the reward we've already earned. Why should I stand here in this wolfish gown begging every Tom, Dick and Harry who comes along for their unnecessary approval? Custom makes me do it. If we did whatever custom wanted in everything, the dust of ancient times would lie unswept, and mountains of error would pile up too high for truth to see over them. Rather than play the fool like this, let the high office and the honour go to someone who would. I'm halfway through; I've suffered one half, I'll do the other. Here come more voices."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter other three citizens.","id":521},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["Your voices: for your voices I have fought;","Watch'd for your voices; for your voices bear","Of wounds two dozen odd; battles thrice six","I have seen and heard of; for your voices have","Done many things, some less, some more: your voices:","Indeed, I would be consul."],"id":522,"m":"Your votes! For your votes I have fought; for your votes I've kept watch; for your votes I carry two dozen or so wounds; I've seen and heard of eighteen battles; for your votes I've done many things, some small, some great. Your votes! Truly, I'd like to be consul."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIFTH CITIZEN","lines":["He has done nobly, and cannot go without any honest man's voice."],"id":523,"m":"He has acted nobly, and no honest man can refuse him his vote."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIXTH CITIZEN","lines":["Therefore let him be consul: the gods give him joy, and make him","good friend to the people!"],"id":524,"m":"So let him be consul. The gods give him joy and make him a good friend to the people!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL THREE CITIZENS","lines":["Amen, amen.—God save thee, noble consul!"],"id":525,"m":"Amen, amen. God save you, noble consul!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":526},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Worthy voices!"],"id":527,"m":"Worthy votes!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter MENENIUS, with BRUTUS and SICINIUS.","id":528},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You have stood your limitation; and the tribunes","Endue you with the people's voice:—remains","That, in the official marks invested, you","Anon do meet the senate."],"id":529,"m":"You've stood the required time, and the tribunes give you the people's vote. All that remains is for you to put on the robes of office and meet the senate shortly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Is this done?"],"id":530,"m":"Is this done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["The custom of request you have discharg'd:","The people do admit you; and are summon'd","To meet anon, upon your approbation."],"id":531,"m":"You've carried out the custom of asking. The people accept you and are called to meet soon to confirm their approval."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Where? at the senate-house?"],"id":532,"m":"Where? At the senate house?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["There, Coriolanus."],"id":533,"m":"There, Coriolanus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["May I change these garments?"],"id":534,"m":"May I change these clothes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["You may, sir."],"id":535,"m":"You may, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["That I'll straight do; and, knowing myself again,","Repair to the senate-house."],"id":536,"m":"I'll do that at once, and when I'm myself again, I'll go to the senate house."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I'll keep you company.—Will you along?"],"id":537,"m":"I'll go with you. (To the Tribunes) Are you coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["We stay here for the people."],"id":538,"m":"We're staying here for the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Fare you well."],"id":539,"m":"Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CORIOLANUS and MENENIUS.","id":540},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","cont":1,"lines":["He has it now; and by his looks methinks","'Tis warm at his heart."],"id":541,"m":"He has it now, and from his looks I think he's glowing inside."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["With a proud heart he wore his humble weeds.","Will you dismiss the people?"],"id":542,"m":"He wore his humble clothes with a proud heart. Will you dismiss the people?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter citizens.","id":543},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["How now, my masters! have you chose this man?"],"id":544,"m":"Well, my friends! Have you chosen this man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["He has our voices, sir."],"id":545,"m":"He has our votes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["We pray the gods he may deserve your loves."],"id":546,"m":"We pray the gods he'll deserve your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Amen, sir:—to my poor unworthy notice,","He mocked us when he begg'd our voices."],"id":547,"m":"Amen, sir. In my poor, humble opinion, he was mocking us when he begged for our votes."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Certainly;","He flouted us downright."],"id":548,"m":"Certainly. He openly sneered at us."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["No, 'tis his kind of speech,—he did not mock us."],"id":549,"m":"No, it's just his way of talking. He didn't mock us."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Not one amongst us, save yourself, but says","He us'd us scornfully: he should have show'd us","His marks of merit, wounds received for's country."],"id":550,"m":"Not one of us except you doesn't say he treated us with scorn. He should have shown us the marks of his merit, the wounds he got for his country."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Why, so he did, I am sure."],"id":551,"m":"Why, I'm sure he did."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["No, no; no man saw 'em."],"id":552,"m":"No, no; nobody saw them."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["He said he had wounds, which he could show in private;","And with his hat, thus waving it in scorn,","'I would be consul,' says he; 'aged custom","But by your voices, will not so permit me;","Your voices therefore:' when we granted that,","Here was, 'I thank you for your voices,—thank you,—","Your most sweet voices:—now you have left your voices","I have no further with you:'—was not this mockery?"],"id":553,"m":"He said he had wounds he could show in private, and waving his hat scornfully like this, \"I'd like to be consul,\" he says; \"ancient custom won't allow me unless I have your votes; so, your votes.\" When we agreed, it was, \"Thank you for your votes, thank you, your sweetest votes. Now that you've handed over your votes, I've nothing more to do with you.\" Wasn't that mockery?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Why either were you ignorant to see't?","Or, seeing it, of such childish friendliness","To yield your voices?"],"id":554,"m":"Why, were you too ignorant to see it? Or, seeing it, so childishly friendly as to give him your votes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Could you not have told him,","As you were lesson'd,—when he had no power,","But was a petty servant to the state,","He was your enemy; ever spake against","Your liberties, and the charters that you bear","I' the body of the weal: and now, arriving","A place of potency and sway o' the state,","If he should still malignantly remain","Fast foe to the plebeii, your voices might","Be curses to yourselves? You should have said,","That as his worthy deeds did claim no less","Than what he stood for, so his gracious nature","Would think upon you for your voices, and","Translate his malice towards you into love,","Standing your friendly lord."],"id":555,"m":"Couldn't you have told him, as you'd been taught, that when he had no power and was only a minor servant of the state, he was your enemy, always speaking against your liberties and the rights you hold in the commonwealth; and that now he's reaching a position of power and control in the state, if he stayed spitefully your firm enemy, your votes might turn into curses on yourselves? You should have said that, just as his worthy deeds deserved nothing less than what he was asking for, so his gracious nature should remember you for your votes and turn his malice towards you into love, as your friendly lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Thus to have said,","As you were fore-advis'd, had touch'd his spirit","And tried his inclination; from him pluck'd","Either his gracious promise, which you might,","As cause had call'd you up, have held him to;","Or else it would have gall'd his surly nature,","Which easily endures not article","Tying him to aught; so, putting him to rage,","You should have ta'en the advantage of his choler","And pass'd him unelected."],"id":556,"m":"Saying that, as you'd been advised, would have tested his spirit and tried his intentions. It would have dragged out of him either a gracious promise, which you could have held him to when the need came, or else it would have irritated his surly nature, which can't easily bear any condition that binds him. Then, having put him in a rage, you could have taken advantage of his anger and left him unelected."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Did you perceive","He did solicit you in free contempt","When he did need your loves; and do you think","That his contempt shall not be bruising to you","When he hath power to crush? Why, had your bodies","No heart among you? Or had you tongues to cry","Against the rectorship of judgment?"],"id":557,"m":"Didn't you see that he asked for your votes with open contempt when he needed your love? And don't you think his contempt will crush you when he has the power to crush? Why, didn't your bodies have a single heart among them? Or did you have tongues only to cry out against good sense?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Have you","Ere now denied the asker, and now again,","Of him that did not ask but mock, bestow","Your su'd-for tongues?"],"id":558,"m":"Have you refused people who asked before now, and now give your sought-after votes to a man who didn't ask but mocked?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["He's not confirm'd: we may deny him yet."],"id":559,"m":"He isn't confirmed yet; we can still refuse him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["And will deny him:","I'll have five hundred voices of that sound."],"id":560,"m":"And we will refuse him. I'll get five hundred votes to say so."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["I twice five hundred, and their friends to piece 'em."],"id":561,"m":"I'll get a thousand, and their friends to add to them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Get you hence instantly; and tell those friends","They have chose a consul that will from them take","Their liberties, make them of no more voice","Than dogs, that are as often beat for barking","As therefore kept to do so."],"id":562,"m":"Go at once and tell those friends they've chosen a consul who'll take their liberties away and give them no more voice than dogs, which are beaten for barking as often as they're kept to bark."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Let them assemble;","And, on a safer judgment, all revoke","Your ignorant election: enforce his pride","And his old hate unto you: besides, forget not","With what contempt he wore the humble weed;","How in his suit he scorn'd you: but your loves,","Thinking upon his services, took from you","Th' apprehension of his present portance,","Which, most gibingly, ungravely, he did fashion","After the inveterate hate he bears you."],"id":563,"m":"Let them assemble and, on second thoughts, all cancel your ignorant choice. Stress his pride and his old hatred of you. And don't forget how scornfully he wore the humble gown, how he despised you while asking. But your love, thinking of his services, stopped you noticing how he was behaving now, which he shaped, in the most mocking and frivolous way, out of his deep-rooted hatred of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Lay","A fault on us, your tribunes; that we labour'd,—","No impediment between,—but that you must","Cast your election on him."],"id":564,"m":"Put the blame on us, your tribunes: say that we worked hard, letting nothing stand in the way, to make sure you voted for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Say you chose him","More after our commandment than as guided","By your own true affections; and that your minds,","Pre-occupied with what you rather must do","Than what you should, made you against the grain","To voice him consul. Lay the fault on us."],"id":565,"m":"Say you chose him more because we told you to than guided by your own true feelings, and that your minds, full of what you had to do rather than what you should do, made you vote him consul against your will. Put the blame on us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Ay, spare us not. Say we read lectures to you,","How youngly he began to serve his country,","How long continued: and what stock he springs of—","The noble house o' the Marcians; from whence came","That Ancus Marcius, Numa's daughter's son,","Who, after great Hostilius, here was king;","Of the same house Publius and Quintus were,","That our best water brought by conduits hither;","And Censorinus, darling of the people,","And nobly nam'd so, twice being censor,","Was his great ancestor."],"id":566,"m":"Yes, don't spare us. Say we lectured you on how young he was when he started serving his country, how long he has continued, and what family he comes from: the noble house of the Marcii, which produced that Ancus Marcius, grandson of Numa, who was king here after great Hostilius; Publius and Quintus, who brought our best water here by aqueducts, were of the same house; and Censorinus, the people's darling, so nobly named because he was censor twice, was his great ancestor."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["One thus descended,","That hath beside well in his person wrought","To be set high in place, we did commend","To your remembrances: but you have found,","Scaling his present bearing with his past,","That he's your fixed enemy, and revoke","Your sudden approbation."],"id":567,"m":"We recommended to you a man descended like this, who had besides done enough personally to be given high office. But you've found, weighing how he behaves now against his past, that he's your fixed enemy, and so you withdraw your hasty approval."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Say you ne'er had done't,—","Harp on that still,—but by our putting on:","And presently when you have drawn your number,","Repair to the Capitol."],"id":568,"m":"Say you'd never have done it except that we urged you (keep harping on that), and as soon as you've gathered your numbers, go to the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["We will so; almost all","Repent in their election."],"id":569,"m":"We'll do that. Almost everyone regrets their choice."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":570},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Let them go on;","This mutiny were better put in hazard","Than stay, past doubt, for greater:","If, as his nature is, he fall in rage","With their refusal, both observe and answer","The vantage of his anger."],"id":571,"m":"Let them go on. It's better to risk this riot now than wait for a certain greater one later. If, as is his nature, he flies into a rage at their refusal, we must watch for and use the advantage his anger gives us."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["To the Capitol,","Come: we will be there before the stream o' the people;","And this shall seem, as partly 'tis, their own,","Which we have goaded onward."],"id":572,"m":"To the Capitol, come. We'll get there before the flood of people, and this will look like their own doing, as it partly is, though we've driven it on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":573},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A street.","id":574},{"t":"sd","text":"Cornets. Enter CORIOLANUS, MENENIUS, COMINIUS, TITUS LARTIUS, Senators, and Patricians.","id":575},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Tullus Aufidius, then, had made new head?"],"id":576,"m":"So Tullus Aufidius had raised a new army?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["He had, my lord; and that it was which caus'd","Our swifter composition."],"id":577,"m":"He had, my lord, and that's why we made peace terms so quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["So then the Volsces stand but as at first;","Ready, when time shall prompt them, to make road","Upon's again."],"id":578,"m":"So the Volscians are just where they were at first, ready to raid us again when the time's right."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["They are worn, lord consul, so","That we shall hardly in our ages see","Their banners wave again."],"id":579,"m":"They're so worn out, lord consul, that we'll hardly see their banners wave again in our lifetimes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Saw you Aufidius?"],"id":580,"m":"Did you see Aufidius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["On safeguard he came to me; and did curse","Against the Volsces, for they had so vilely","Yielded the town; he is retir'd to Antium."],"id":581,"m":"He came to me under a safe-conduct and cursed the Volscians for giving up the town so shamefully. He has withdrawn to Antium."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Spoke he of me?"],"id":582,"m":"Did he speak of me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["He did, my lord."],"id":583,"m":"He did, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["How? What?"],"id":584,"m":"How? What did he say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["How often he had met you, sword to sword;","That of all things upon the earth he hated","Your person most; that he would pawn his fortunes","To hopeless restitution, so he might","Be call'd your vanquisher."],"id":585,"m":"How often he had met you sword to sword; that of everything on earth he hated you most; that he would pledge all his fortunes beyond hope of recovery just to be called your conqueror."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["At Antium lives he?"],"id":586,"m":"He lives in Antium?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LARTIUS","lines":["At Antium."],"id":587,"m":"In Antium."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I wish I had a cause to seek him there,","To oppose his hatred fully.—Welcome home. [To LARTIUS]"],"id":588,"m":"I wish I had a reason to go and find him there, to meet his hatred head on. (To Lartius) Welcome home."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SICINIUS and BRUTUS.","id":589},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["Behold! these are the tribunes of the people;","The tongues o' the common mouth. I do despise them,","For they do prank them in authority,","Against all noble sufferance."],"id":590,"m":"Look! These are the tribunes of the people, the tongues of the common mouth. I despise them, because they show off their authority beyond what any noble person can stand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Pass no further."],"id":591,"m":"Go no further."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Ha! what is that?"],"id":592,"m":"Ha! What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["It will be dangerous to go on: no further."],"id":593,"m":"It will be dangerous to go on. No further."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What makes this change?"],"id":594,"m":"What has caused this change?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["The matter?"],"id":595,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Hath he not pass'd the noble and the commons?"],"id":596,"m":"Hasn't he been approved by the nobles and the commoners?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Cominius, no."],"id":597,"m":"No, Cominius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Have I had children's voices?"],"id":598,"m":"Have I had children's votes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Tribunes, give way; he shall to the market-place."],"id":599,"m":"Tribunes, make way; he's going to the market square."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The people are incens'd against him."],"id":600,"m":"The people are furious with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Stop,","Or all will fall in broil."],"id":601,"m":"Stop, or everything will fall into chaos."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Are these your herd?—","Must these have voices, that can yield them now,","And straight disclaim their tongues?—What are your offices?","You being their mouths, why rule you not their teeth?","Have you not set them on?"],"id":602,"m":"Are these your herd? Must these people have votes, who give them one moment and take them back the next? What's your job? Since you're their mouths, why don't you control their teeth? Didn't you set them on?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Be calm, be calm."],"id":603,"m":"Calm down, calm down."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["It is a purpos'd thing, and grows by plot,","To curb the will of the nobility:","Suffer't, and live with such as cannot rule,","Nor ever will be rul'd."],"id":604,"m":"It's deliberate, a growing plot to curb the will of the nobility. Put up with it, and you'll live with people who can't rule and will never be ruled."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Call't not a plot:","The people cry you mock'd them; and of late,","When corn was given them gratis, you repin'd;","Scandal'd the suppliants for the people,—call'd them","Time-pleasers, flatterers, foes to nobleness."],"id":605,"m":"Don't call it a plot. The people say you mocked them, and recently, when grain was given to them free, you complained and slandered the people's representatives, calling them time-servers, flatterers and enemies of nobility."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Why, this was known before."],"id":606,"m":"Why, this was known before."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Not to them all."],"id":607,"m":"Not to all of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Have you inform'd them sithence?"],"id":608,"m":"Have you told them since?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["How! I inform them!"],"id":609,"m":"What! Me tell them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["You are like to do such businesss."],"id":610,"m":"It's just the kind of thing you'd do."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Not unlike,","Each way, to better yours."],"id":611,"m":"Not unlikely; and every way better than you'd do yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Why, then, should I be consul? By yond clouds,","Let me deserve so ill as you, and make me","Your fellow tribune."],"id":612,"m":"Then why should I be consul? By those clouds, let me be as worthless as you, and make me your fellow tribune."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["You show too much of that","For which the people stir: if you will pass","To where you are bound, you must inquire your way,","Which you are out of, with a gentler spirit;","Or never be so noble as a consul,","Nor yoke with him for tribune."],"id":613,"m":"You're showing too much of what the people are angry about. If you want to get where you're going, you must ask the way, which you've lost, in a gentler spirit; or else you'll never be so noble as a consul, nor even be paired with him as a tribune."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Let's be calm."],"id":614,"m":"Let's keep calm."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["The people are abus'd; set on. This palt'ring","Becomes not Rome; nor has Coriolanus","Deserv'd this so dishonour'd rub, laid falsely","I' the plain way of his merit."],"id":615,"m":"The people have been deceived; they've been stirred up. This trickery doesn't suit Rome, and Coriolanus hasn't deserved this shameful obstacle, falsely placed on the smooth road his merit has earned."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Tell me of corn!","This was my speech, and I will speak't again,—"],"id":616,"m":"Talk to me about grain! This was what I said, and I'll say it again—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Not now, not now."],"id":617,"m":"Not now, not now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Not in this heat, sir, now."],"id":618,"m":"Not in this heat, sir, not now."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Now, as I live, I will.—My nobler friends,","I crave their pardons:","For the mutable, rank-scented many, let them","Regard me as I do not flatter, and","Therein behold themselves: I say again,","In soothing them we nourish 'gainst our senate","The cockle of rebellion, insolence, sedition,","Which we ourselves have plough'd for, sow'd, and scatter'd,","By mingling them with us, the honour'd number,","Who lack not virtue, no, nor power, but that","Which they have given to beggars."],"id":619,"m":"Now, as I live, I will. My nobler friends, I ask your pardon. As for the fickle, foul-smelling mob, let them look at me, since I don't flatter, and see themselves in what I say. I say again: by humouring them we're growing the weeds of rebellion, insolence and riot against our senate, which we ourselves ploughed for, sowed and scattered by mixing them with us, the honoured few, who lack no virtue, no, nor power either, except what we've given away to beggars."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Well, no more."],"id":620,"m":"Well, that's enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["No more words, we beseech you."],"id":621,"m":"No more words, we beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["How! no more!","As for my country I have shed my blood,","Not fearing outward force, so shall my lungs","Coin words till their decay against those measles","Which we disdain should tetter us, yet sought","The very way to catch them."],"id":622,"m":"What! No more! Just as I've shed my blood for my country, not fearing enemies abroad, so my lungs will keep making words until they give out, against these scabs we'd hate to be covered with, yet we went looking for the very way to catch them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You speak o' the people","As if you were a god, to punish, not","A man of their infirmity."],"id":623,"m":"You speak of the people as if you were a god sent to punish them, not a man as weak as they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["'Twere well","We let the people know't."],"id":624,"m":"It would be good to let the people know that."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What, what? his choler?"],"id":625,"m":"What, what? His temper?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Choler!","Were I as patient as the midnight sleep,","By Jove, 'twould be my mind!"],"id":626,"m":"Temper! If I were as patient as midnight sleep, by Jove, I'd still think the same!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["It is a mind","That shall remain a poison where it is,","Not poison any further."],"id":627,"m":"It's a way of thinking that will stay a poison where it is, and poison nothing further."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Shall remain!—","Hear you this Triton of the minnows? mark you","His absolute 'shall'?"],"id":628,"m":"\"Will stay\"! Do you hear this Triton of the minnows? Did you notice his commanding \"will\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["'Twas from the canon."],"id":629,"m":"It was out of order."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["'Shall'!","O good, but most unwise patricians! why,","You grave but reckless senators, have you thus","Given Hydra leave to choose an officer,","That with his peremptory 'shall,' being but","The horn and noise o' the monster, wants not spirit","To say he'll turn your current in a ditch,","And make your channel his? If he have power,","Then vail your ignorance: if none, awake","Your dangerous lenity. If you are learn'd,","Be not as common fools; if you are not,","Let them have cushions by you. You are plebeians,","If they be senators: and they are no less","When, both your voices blended, the great'st taste","Most palates theirs. They choose their magistrate;","And such a one as he, who puts his 'shall,'","His popular 'shall,' against a graver bench","Than ever frown'd in Greece. By Jove himself,","It makes the consuls base: and my soul aches","To know, when two authorities are up,","Neither supreme, how soon confusion","May enter 'twixt the gap of both and take","The one by the other."],"id":630,"m":"\"Will\"! Oh good but most unwise patricians! Why, you serious but careless senators, have you let the many-headed Hydra choose an official who, with his imperious \"will\", though he's only the horn and noise of the monster, has the nerve to say he'll divert your stream into a ditch and make your channel his own? If he has power, then bow down in your ignorance; if he has none, wake up from your dangerous softness. If you're wise, don't act like common fools; if you're not, let them sit on the senate cushions beside you. You're plebeians if they're senators, and they're no less than senators when, with both your voices mixed, the strongest flavour is theirs. They choose their own magistrate, and one like this, who sets his \"will\", his mob's \"will\", against a more dignified senate than ever frowned in Greece. By Jove himself, it makes the consuls worthless, and it pains my soul to know that when two authorities are set up and neither is supreme, chaos can very soon slip into the gap between them and use one to destroy the other."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Well, on to the market-place."],"id":631,"m":"Well, on to the market square."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Whoever gave that counsel, to give forth","The corn o' the storehouse gratis, as 'twas us'd","Sometime in Greece,—"],"id":632,"m":"Whoever gave the advice to hand out the grain from the storehouse free, as they used to do in Greece—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Well, well, no more of that."],"id":633,"m":"Well, well, no more of that."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Though there the people had more absolute power,—","I say they nourish'd disobedience, fed","The ruin of the state."],"id":634,"m":"Even though the people there had more absolute power— I say they nourished disobedience and fed the ruin of the state."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why shall the people give","One that speaks thus their voice?"],"id":635,"m":"Why should the people give their vote to someone who talks like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I'll give my reasons,","More worthier than their voices. They know the corn","Was not our recompense, resting well assur'd","They ne'er did service for't; being press'd to the war,","Even when the navel of the state was touch'd,","They would not thread the gates,—this kind of service","Did not deserve corn gratis: being i' the war,","Their mutinies and revolts, wherein they show'd","Most valour, spoke not for them. The accusation","Which they have often made against the senate,","All cause unborn, could never be the motive","Of our so frank donation. Well, what then?","How shall this bisson multitude digest","The senate's courtesy? Let deeds express","What's like to be their words:—'We did request it;","We are the greater poll, and in true fear","They gave us our demands:'—Thus we debase","The nature of our seats, and make the rabble","Call our cares fears; which will in time","Break ope the locks o' the senate and bring in","The crows to peck the eagles.—"],"id":636,"m":"I'll give my reasons, which are worth more than their votes. They know the grain wasn't a reward from us, since they're well aware they never did any service for it. When they were called up for war, even when the state was struck at its heart, they wouldn't go out through the gates. That kind of service didn't deserve free grain. In the war, their mutinies and revolts, where they showed most courage, didn't speak well for them. The accusations they've often made against the senate, completely groundless, could never have been the reason for our so generous gift. Well, what then? How will this blind mob take the senate's kindness? Let their actions show what they're likely to say: \"We demanded it; we're the majority, and they gave us what we wanted out of real fear.\" This is how we cheapen the nature of our office and let the rabble call our care fear, which will in time break open the senate's locks and let in the crows to peck at the eagles."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Come, enough."],"id":637,"m":"Come, enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Enough, with over-measure."],"id":638,"m":"Enough, and more than enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["No, take more:","What may be sworn by, both divine and human,","Seal what I end withal!—This double worship,—","Where one part does disdain with cause, the other","Insult without all reason; where gentry, title, wisdom,","Cannot conclude but by the yea and no","Of general ignorance—it must omit","Real necessities, and give way the while","To unstable slightness: purpose so barr'd, it follows,","Nothing is done to purpose. Therefore, beseech you,—","You that will be less fearful than discreet;","That love the fundamental part of state","More than you doubt the change on't; that prefer","A noble life before a long, and wish","To jump a body with a dangerous physic","That's sure of death without it,—at once pluck out","The multitudinous tongue; let them not lick","The sweet which is their poison: your dishonour","Mangles true judgment, and bereaves the state","Of that integrity which should become't;","Not having the power to do the good it would,","For the ill which doth control't."],"id":639,"m":"No, take more. May everything one can swear by, divine and human, confirm what I end with! This divided rule, where one side scorns with reason and the other insults without any reason at all, where birth, title and wisdom can't decide anything without the yes and no of common ignorance, must neglect real needs and give way to fickle trifles. With purpose blocked like that, it follows that nothing purposeful gets done. So I beg you, you who'd rather be wise than fearful, who love the foundations of the state more than you fear changing them, who prefer a noble life to a long one, and would risk a dangerous medicine on a body that will certainly die without it: pull out the mob's tongue at once. Don't let them lick the sweet that is their poison. Your dishonour mangles true judgment and robs the state of the integrity that should belong to it, leaving it without the power to do the good it wants because of the evil that controls it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Has said enough."],"id":640,"m":"He has said enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Has spoken like a traitor, and shall answer","As traitors do."],"id":641,"m":"He has spoken like a traitor, and he'll answer for it as traitors do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Thou wretch, despite o'erwhelm thee!—","What should the people do with these bald tribunes?","On whom depending, their obedience fails","To the greater bench: in a rebellion,","When what's not meet, but what must be, was law,","Then were they chosen; in a better hour","Let what is meet be said it must be meet,","And throw their power i' the dust."],"id":642,"m":"You wretch, may contempt crush you! What should the people do with these bald tribunes? By depending on them, they fail to obey the higher authority. They were chosen in a rebellion, when what had to be, not what was right, was law. In a better time, let what's right be declared right, and throw their power into the dust."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Manifest treason!"],"id":643,"m":"Open treason!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["This a consul? no."],"id":644,"m":"This a consul? No."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The aediles, ho!—Let him be apprehended."],"id":645,"m":"The aediles, here! Let him be arrested."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Go call the people [Exit BRUTUS]; in whose name myself","Attach thee as a traitorous innovator,","A foe to the public weal. Obey, I charge thee,","And follow to thine answer."],"id":646,"m":"Go and call the people. (Exit Brutus) In their name I arrest you myself as a treasonous revolutionary, an enemy of the public good. I order you to obey and come to answer the charge."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Hence, old goat!"],"id":647,"m":"Get away, you old goat!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SENATORS AND PATRICIANS","lines":["We'll surety him."],"id":648,"m":"We'll stand bail for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Aged sir, hands off."],"id":649,"m":"Old man, take your hands off."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Hence, rotten thing! or I shall shake thy bones","Out of thy garments."],"id":650,"m":"Get away, you rotten thing, or I'll shake your bones out of your clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Help, ye citizens!"],"id":651,"m":"Help, citizens!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Brutus, with the AEDILES and a rabble of Citizens.","id":652},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["On both sides more respect."],"id":653,"m":"More respect on both sides."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Here's he that would take from you all your power."],"id":654,"m":"Here's the man who would take all your power away from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Seize him, aediles."],"id":655,"m":"Seize him, aediles."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLEBEIANS","lines":["Down with him! down with him!"],"id":656,"m":"Down with him! Down with him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Weapons, weapons, weapons!"],"id":657,"m":"Weapons, weapons, weapons!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They all bustle about CORIOLANUS.","id":658},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","cont":1,"lines":["Tribunes! patricians! citizens!—What, ho!—","Sicinius, Brutus, Coriolanus, Citizens!"],"id":659,"m":"Tribunes! Patricians! Citizens! Stop! Sicinius, Brutus, Coriolanus, citizens!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Peace, peace, peace; stay, hold, peace!"],"id":660,"m":"Peace, peace, peace! Stop, hold on, peace!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What is about to be?—I am out of breath;","Confusion's near: I cannot speak.—You tribunes","To the people,—Coriolanus, patience:—","Speak, good Sicinius."],"id":661,"m":"What's about to happen? I'm out of breath. Chaos is close; I can't speak. You tribunes, speak to the people. Coriolanus, be patient. Speak, good Sicinius."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Hear me, people: peace!"],"id":662,"m":"Listen to me, people. Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Let's hear our tribune: peace!—","Speak, speak, speak."],"id":663,"m":"Let's hear our tribune. Quiet! Speak, speak, speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["You are at point to lose your liberties;","Marcius would have all from you; Marcius,","Whom late you have nam'd for consul."],"id":664,"m":"You're about to lose your liberties. Marcius would take everything from you, Marcius, whom you've just named as consul."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Fie, fie, fie!","This is the way to kindle, not to quench."],"id":665,"m":"Shame, shame, shame! This is the way to light the fire, not put it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["To unbuild the city, and to lay all flat."],"id":666,"m":"To pull the city down and flatten everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["What is the city but the people?"],"id":667,"m":"What is the city but the people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["True,","The people are the city."],"id":668,"m":"True, the people are the city."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["By the consent of all, we were establish'd","The people's magistrates."],"id":669,"m":"With everyone's agreement, we were made the people's magistrates."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["You so remain."],"id":670,"m":"And so you remain."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["And so are like to do."],"id":671,"m":"And are likely to."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["That is the way to lay the city flat;","To bring the roof to the foundation,","And bury all which yet distinctly ranges,","In heaps and piles of ruin."],"id":672,"m":"That's the way to flatten the city, to bring the roof down to the foundations and bury everything that still stands in its proper order under heaps and piles of ruins."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["This deserves death."],"id":673,"m":"This deserves death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Or let us stand to our authority,","Or let us lose it.—We do here pronounce,","Upon the part o' the people, in whose power","We were elected theirs, Marcius is worthy","Of present death."],"id":674,"m":"Either let's stand by our authority or lose it. We declare here, on behalf of the people, by whose power we were elected their officers, that Marcius deserves immediate death."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Therefore lay hold of him;","Bear him to the rock Tarpeian, and from thence","Into destruction cast him."],"id":675,"m":"So seize him. Take him to the Tarpeian rock and throw him off it to his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Aediles, seize him!"],"id":676,"m":"Aediles, seize him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Yield, Marcius, yield!"],"id":677,"m":"Surrender, Marcius, surrender!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Hear me one word;","Beseech you, tribunes, hear me but a word."],"id":678,"m":"Let me say one word. I beg you, tribunes, hear just one word."},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILES","lines":["Peace, peace!"],"id":679,"m":"Quiet, quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Be that you seem, truly your country's friends,","And temperately proceed to what you would","Thus violently redress."],"id":680,"m":"Be what you seem to be, true friends of your country, and go about calmly what you want to set right with such violence."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Sir, those cold ways,","That seem like prudent helps, are very poisonous","Where the disease is violent.—Lay hands upon him","And bear him to the rock."],"id":681,"m":"Sir, those cool methods, which look like sensible remedies, are deadly poison when the disease is violent. Seize him and take him to the rock."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["No; I'll die here. [Draws his sword]","There's some among you have beheld me fighting;","Come, try upon yourselves what you have seen me."],"id":682,"m":"No; I'll die here. (Draws his sword) Some of you have seen me fighting. Come and try on yourselves what you've seen me do."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Down with that sword!—Tribunes, withdraw awhile."],"id":683,"m":"Put that sword down! Tribunes, step back for a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Lay hands upon him."],"id":684,"m":"Seize him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Help Marcius, help,","You that be noble; help him, young and old!"],"id":685,"m":"Help Marcius, help, all of you who are noble; help him, young and old!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Down with him, down with him!"],"id":686,"m":"Down with him, down with him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"In this mutiny the TRIBUNES, the AEDILES, and the people are beat in.","id":687},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Go, get you to your house; be gone, away!","All will be nought else."],"id":688,"m":"Go, get to your house. Go, away! Otherwise everything will be ruined."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Get you gone."],"id":689,"m":"Go."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Stand fast;","We have as many friends as enemies."],"id":690,"m":"Stand firm. We have as many friends as enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Shall it be put to that?"],"id":691,"m":"Must it come to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["The gods forbid:","I pr'ythee, noble friend, home to thy house;","Leave us to cure this cause."],"id":692,"m":"The gods forbid! Please, noble friend, go home to your house. Leave us to heal this matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["For 'tis a sore upon us","You cannot tent yourself; be gone, beseech you."],"id":693,"m":"Because it's a wound in all of us that you can't treat yourself. Go, I beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Come, sir, along with us."],"id":694,"m":"Come, sir, come with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I would they were barbarians,—as they are,","Though in Rome litter'd,—not Romans,—as they are not,","Though calv'd i' the porch o' the Capitol."],"id":695,"m":"I wish they were barbarians, which they are, though born in Rome, and not Romans, which they're not, though they were dropped like calves in the doorway of the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Be gone;","Put not your worthy rage into your tongue;","One time will owe another."],"id":696,"m":"Go. Don't put your worthy rage into words. There'll be another time to settle this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["On fair ground","I could beat forty of them."],"id":697,"m":"On fair ground I could beat forty of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I could myself","Take up a brace o' the best of them; yea, the two tribunes."],"id":698,"m":"I could take on a pair of the best of them myself; yes, the two tribunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["But now 'tis odds beyond arithmetic;","And manhood is call'd foolery when it stands","Against a falling fabric.—Will you hence,","Before the tag return? whose rage doth rend","Like interrupted waters, and o'erbear","What they are used to bear."],"id":699,"m":"But now the odds are beyond counting, and courage is called foolishness when it stands against a falling building. Will you go before the mob comes back? Their rage tears like water held back by a dam and overwhelms what it usually holds up."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Pray you be gone:","I'll try whether my old wit be in request","With those that have but little: this must be patch'd","With cloth of any colour."],"id":700,"m":"Please go. I'll see if my old wits are in demand with those who have very few. This must be patched up with cloth of any colour."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Nay, come away."],"id":701,"m":"Come away now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CORIOLANUS, COMINIUS, and others.","id":702},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST PATRICIAN","lines":["This man has marr'd his fortune."],"id":703,"m":"This man has ruined his fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["His nature is too noble for the world:","He would not flatter Neptune for his trident,","Or Jove for's power to thunder. His heart's his mouth:","What his breast forges, that his tongue must vent;","And, being angry, does forget that ever","He heard the name of death."],"id":704,"m":"His nature is too noble for the world. He wouldn't flatter Neptune for his trident or Jove for his power to thunder. His heart is his mouth: whatever his chest forges, his tongue has to let out; and when he's angry he forgets he ever heard the word death."},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise within.","id":705},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Here's goodly work!"],"id":706,"m":"Here's a fine mess!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND PATRICIAN","lines":["I would they were a-bed!"],"id":707,"m":"I wish they were in bed!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I would they were in Tiber!","What the vengeance, could he not speak 'em fair?"],"id":708,"m":"I wish they were in the Tiber! What the devil, couldn't he speak to them politely?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter BRUTUS and SICINIUS, with the rabble.","id":709},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Where is this viper","That would depopulate the city and","Be every man himself?"],"id":710,"m":"Where's this snake who would empty the city of people and be every man himself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You worthy tribunes,—"],"id":711,"m":"You worthy tribunes—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["He shall be thrown down the Tarpeian rock","With rigorous hands: he hath resisted law,","And therefore law shall scorn him further trial","Than the severity of the public power,","Which he so sets at nought."],"id":712,"m":"He'll be thrown down the Tarpeian rock by stern hands. He has resisted the law, and so the law will deny him any further trial than the severity of the people's power, which he sets at nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["He shall well know","The noble tribunes are the people's mouths,","And we their hands."],"id":713,"m":"He'll learn that the noble tribunes are the people's mouths, and we are their hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["He shall, sure on't."],"id":714,"m":"He will, for sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Sir, sir,—"],"id":715,"m":"Sir, sir—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Peace!"],"id":716,"m":"Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Do not cry havoc, where you should but hunt","With modest warrant."],"id":717,"m":"Don't give the signal for total slaughter when you should only hunt within proper limits."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Sir, how comes't that you","Have holp to make this rescue?"],"id":718,"m":"Sir, how is it that you helped him escape?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Hear me speak:—","As I do know the consul's worthiness,","So can I name his faults,—"],"id":719,"m":"Let me speak. Just as I know the consul's worth, I can also name his faults—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Consul!—what consul?"],"id":720,"m":"Consul! What consul?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["The consul Coriolanus."],"id":721,"m":"The consul Coriolanus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He consul!"],"id":722,"m":"Him, consul!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["No, no, no, no, no."],"id":723,"m":"No, no, no, no, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["If, by the tribunes' leave, and yours, good people,","I may be heard, I would crave a word or two;","The which shall turn you to no further harm","Than so much loss of time."],"id":724,"m":"If, with the tribunes' permission and yours, good people, I may be heard, I'd ask for a word or two, which will cost you nothing more than a little lost time."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Speak briefly, then;","For we are peremptory to dispatch","This viperous traitor: to eject him hence","Were but one danger; and to keep him here","Our certain death: therefore it is decreed","He dies to-night."],"id":725,"m":"Speak briefly, then, because we're determined to get rid of this snake of a traitor. Banishing him would be just one danger, and keeping him here our certain death; so it's decided he dies tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Now the good gods forbid","That our renowned Rome, whose gratitude","Towards her deserved children is enroll'd","In Jove's own book, like an unnatural dam","Should now eat up her own!"],"id":726,"m":"Now may the good gods forbid that our famous Rome, whose gratitude to her deserving children is written in Jove's own book, should now, like an unnatural mother animal, eat up her own young!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["He's a disease that must be cut away."],"id":727,"m":"He's a disease that has to be cut out."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["O, he's a limb that has but a disease;","Mortal, to cut it off; to cure it, easy.","What has he done to Rome that's worthy death?","Killing our enemies, the blood he hath lost,—","Which I dare vouch is more than that he hath","By many an ounce,—he dropt it for his country;","And what is left, to lose it by his country","Were to us all, that do't and suffer it","A brand to the end o' the world."],"id":728,"m":"Oh, he's a limb that only has a disease: fatal to cut it off, easy to cure it. What has he done to Rome that deserves death? For killing our enemies? The blood he has lost, which I'd swear is more than he has left by many an ounce, he shed for his country. And if his country made him lose what's left, it would be a brand of shame to the end of the world on all of us who do it or allow it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["This is clean kam."],"id":729,"m":"This is completely backwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Merely awry: when he did love his country,","It honour'd him."],"id":730,"m":"Totally wrong. When he loved his country, it honoured him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["The service of the foot,","Being once gangren'd, is not then respected","For what before it was."],"id":731,"m":"Once a foot has gangrene, its service isn't valued for what it did before."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["We'll hear no more.—","Pursue him to his house, and pluck him thence;","Lest his infection, being of catching nature,","Spread further."],"id":732,"m":"We'll hear no more. Go after him to his house and drag him out, in case his infection, which is catching, spreads further."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["One word more, one word.","This tiger-footed rage, when it shall find","The harm of unscann'd swiftness, will, too late,","Tie leaden pounds to's heels. Proceed by process;","Lest parties,—as he is belov'd,—break out,","And sack great Rome with Romans."],"id":733,"m":"One more word, just one. This tiger-footed rage, when it sees the harm of rushing ahead without thinking, will tie lead weights to its heels, too late. Proceed by legal process, in case factions break out, since he's loved, and great Rome is sacked by Romans."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["If it were so,—"],"id":734,"m":"If that were so—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["What do ye talk?","Have we not had a taste of his obedience?","Our aediles smote? ourselves resisted?—come,—"],"id":735,"m":"What are you talking about? Haven't we had a taste of how obedient he is? Our aediles struck, ourselves resisted? Come on—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Consider this:—he has been bred i' the wars","Since 'a could draw a sword, and is ill school'd","In bolted language; meal and bran together","He throws without distinction. Give me leave,","I'll go to him and undertake to bring him","Where he shall answer, by a lawful form,","In peace, to his utmost peril."],"id":736,"m":"Consider this: he has been brought up in the wars since he could draw a sword, and he's badly trained in refined language; he throws out the flour and the bran together without sorting them. Let me go to him, and I'll undertake to bring him where he'll answer peacefully, by lawful procedure, at the risk of the harshest penalty."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Noble tribunes,","It is the humane way: the other course","Will prove too bloody; and the end of it","Unknown to the beginning."],"id":737,"m":"Noble tribunes, that is the humane way. The other course will be too bloody, and how it ends can't be known at the beginning."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Noble Menenius,","Be you then as the people's officer.—","Masters, lay down your weapons."],"id":738,"m":"Noble Menenius, then act as the people's officer. Friends, put down your weapons."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Go not home."],"id":739,"m":"Don't go home."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Meet on the market-place.—We'll attend you there:","Where, if you bring not Marcius, we'll proceed","In our first way."],"id":740,"m":"Meet in the market square. We'll wait for you there, and if you don't bring Marcius, we'll proceed as we first intended."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I'll bring him to you.—","[To the SENATORS] Let me desire your company: he must come,","Or what is worst will follow."],"id":741,"m":"I'll bring him to you. (To the Senators) Please come with me. He must come, or the worst will follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Pray you let's to him."],"id":742,"m":"Please, let's go to him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":743},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Rome. A room in Coriolanus's house.","id":744},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS and Patricians.","id":745},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Let them pull all about mine ears; present me","Death on the wheel, or at wild horses' heels;","Or pile ten hills on the Tarpeian rock,","That the precipitation might down stretch","Below the beam of sight; yet will I still","Be thus to them."],"id":746,"m":"Let them pull everything down around my ears; threaten me with death on the wheel or dragged at wild horses' heels; or pile ten hills on the Tarpeian rock so the drop falls further than the eye can see; I'll still be like this towards them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST PATRICIAN","lines":["You do the nobler."],"id":747,"m":"That's the nobler way."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I muse my mother","Does not approve me further, who was wont","To call them woollen vassals, things created","To buy and sell with groats; to show bare heads","In congregations, to yawn, be still, and wonder,","When one but of my ordinance stood up","To speak of peace or war."],"id":748,"m":"I'm surprised my mother doesn't approve of me more. She used to call them slaves in wool, creatures made to buy and sell with pennies, to stand bareheaded in meetings and gape, keep quiet and marvel, when a man of my rank stood up to speak about peace or war."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter VOLUMNIA.","id":749},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["I talk of you: [To Volumnia]","Why did you wish me milder? Would you have me","False to my nature? Rather say, I play","The man I am."],"id":750,"m":"(To Volumnia) I'm talking about you. Why did you want me to be milder? Would you have me false to my own nature? Say rather that I play the man I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["O, sir, sir, sir,","I would have had you put your power well on","Before you had worn it out."],"id":751,"m":"Oh, sir, sir, sir, I would have liked you to put your power on properly before you wore it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Let go."],"id":752,"m":"Let it go."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["You might have been enough the man you are","With striving less to be so: lesser had been","The thwartings of your dispositions, if","You had not show'd them how ye were dispos'd,","Ere they lack'd power to cross you."],"id":753,"m":"You could have been the man you are without trying so hard to be. Your wishes would have been blocked less if you hadn't shown them how you felt before they lost the power to stop you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Let them hang."],"id":754,"m":"Let them hang."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Ay, and burn too."],"id":755,"m":"Yes, and burn too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MENENIUS with the SENATORS.","id":756},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Come, come, you have been too rough, something too rough;","You must return and mend it."],"id":757,"m":"Come, come, you've been too rough, rather too rough. You must go back and make amends."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["There's no remedy;","Unless, by not so doing, our good city","Cleave in the midst, and perish."],"id":758,"m":"There's no other cure, unless, by not doing it, our good city splits in two and dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Pray be counsell'd;","I have a heart as little apt as yours,","But yet a brain that leads my use of anger","To better vantage."],"id":759,"m":"Please take advice. My heart is no more willing than yours, but I have a brain that directs my anger to better advantage."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Well said, noble woman!","Before he should thus stoop to the herd, but that","The violent fit o' the time craves it as physic","For the whole state, I would put mine armour on,","Which I can scarcely bear."],"id":760,"m":"Well said, noble lady! Rather than have him bow like this to the herd, I'd put on my armour, which I can hardly carry, except that the violent sickness of the times needs it as medicine for the whole state."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What must I do?"],"id":761,"m":"What must I do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Return to the tribunes."],"id":762,"m":"Go back to the tribunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Well, what then? what then?"],"id":763,"m":"Well, what then? What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Repent what you have spoke."],"id":764,"m":"Take back what you said."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["For them?—I cannot do it to the gods;","Must I then do't to them?"],"id":765,"m":"For them? I can't do it for the gods; must I do it for them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["You are too absolute;","Though therein you can never be too noble","But when extremities speak. I have heard you say","Honour and policy, like unsever'd friends,","I' the war do grow together: grant that, and tell me","In peace what each of them by th' other lose","That they combine not there."],"id":766,"m":"You're too unbending, though you could never be too noble in that, except when things are desperate. I've heard you say that honour and cunning grow together in war like inseparable friends. Admit that, and tell me what either of them loses by the other in peace that they shouldn't join together there too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Tush, tush!"],"id":767,"m":"Tush, tush!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["A good demand."],"id":768,"m":"A good question."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["If it be honour in your wars to seem","The same you are not,—which for your best ends","You adopt your policy,—how is it less or worse","That it shall hold companionship in peace","With honour as in war; since that to both","It stands in like request?"],"id":769,"m":"If it's honourable in your wars to seem what you're not, which you adopt as a strategy for your best ends, how is it any less or worse for it to keep company with honour in peace as in war, since it's equally needed in both?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Why force you this?"],"id":770,"m":"Why are you pressing this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Because that now it lies you on to speak","To the people; not by your own instruction,","Nor by the matter which your heart prompts you,","But with such words that are but rooted in","Your tongue, though but bastards and syllables","Of no allowance, to your bosom's truth.","Now, this no more dishonours you at all","Than to take in a town with gentle words,","Which else would put you to your fortune and","The hazard of much blood.","I would dissemble with my nature where","My fortunes and my friends at stake requir'd","I should do so in honour: I am in this","Your wife, your son, these senators, the nobles;","And you will rather show our general louts","How you can frown, than spend a fawn upon 'em","For the inheritance of their loves and safeguard","Of what that want might ruin."],"id":771,"m":"Because now it's your duty to speak to the people, not as you'd choose yourself or with what your heart prompts, but with words that are rooted only in your tongue, illegitimate syllables that your heart's truth doesn't stand behind. Now this dishonours you no more than capturing a town with gentle words, which otherwise would force you to trust to luck and risk much bloodshed. I'd disguise my true nature if my fortunes and my friends were at stake and honour required it. I speak in this for your wife, your son, these senators, the nobles; and you'd rather show our common oafs how you can frown than give them one fawning look to win their love and protect what lacking it could ruin."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Noble lady!—","Come, go with us; speak fair: you may salve so,","Not what is dangerous present, but the loss","Of what is past."],"id":772,"m":"Noble lady! Come, go with us; speak politely. That way you can heal not just the present danger but the loss of what's already happened."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I pr'ythee now, my son,","Go to them with this bonnet in thy hand;","And thus far having stretch'd it,—here be with them,—","Thy knee bussing the stones,—for in such business","Action is eloquence, and the eyes of the ignorant","More learned than the ears,—waving thy head,","Which often, thus correcting thy stout heart,","Now humble as the ripest mulberry","That will not hold the handling: or say to them","Thou art their soldier, and, being bred in broils,","Hast not the soft way which, thou dost confess,","Were fit for thee to use, as they to claim,","In asking their good loves; but thou wilt frame","Thyself, forsooth, hereafter theirs, so far","As thou hast power and person."],"id":773,"m":"Please now, my son, go to them with this cap in your hand, holding it out this far, like this, be with them; your knee kissing the stones (because in such business action is eloquence, and the eyes of ignorant people are more learned than their ears), nodding your head, and so often correcting your proud heart, which is now as humble as the ripest mulberry that falls apart when it's handled. Or tell them you're their soldier, and being brought up in fighting you don't have the gentle manner which, you admit, it would be right for you to use and for them to expect when you ask for their love; but that from now on you'll make yourself theirs, indeed, as far as you have power and person."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["This but done","Even as she speaks, why, their hearts were yours:","For they have pardons, being ask'd, as free","As words to little purpose."],"id":774,"m":"If he only did that, just as she says, their hearts would be yours; because when asked, they give pardons as freely as idle words."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Pr'ythee now,","Go, and be rul'd; although I know thou had'st rather","Follow thine enemy in a fiery gulf","Than flatter him in a bower."],"id":775,"m":"Please now, go, and do as you're told; though I know you'd rather follow your enemy into a fiery pit than flatter him in a garden."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter COMINIUS.","id":776},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","cont":1,"lines":["Here is Cominius."],"id":777,"m":"Here's Cominius."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I have been i' the market-place; and, sir, 'tis fit","You make strong party, or defend yourself","By calmness or by absence: all's in anger."],"id":778,"m":"I've been to the market square, and, sir, you'd better raise a strong party or protect yourself by calmness or by going away. Everyone's furious."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Only fair speech."],"id":779,"m":"Only fair words."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I think 'twill serve, if he","Can thereto frame his spirit."],"id":780,"m":"I think that will do, if he can bring his spirit to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["He must, and will.—","Pr'ythee now, say you will, and go about it."],"id":781,"m":"He must, and he will. Please now, say you will, and get on with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Must I go show them my unbarb'd sconce? must I","With my base tongue, give to my noble heart","A lie, that it must bear? Well, I will do't:","Yet, were there but this single plot to lose,","This mould of Marcius, they to dust should grind it,","And throw't against the wind.—To the market-place:—","You have put me now to such a part which never","I shall discharge to the life."],"id":782,"m":"Must I go and show them my bare head? Must I make my base tongue give my noble heart a lie it has to put up with? Well, I'll do it. Yet if there were only this one piece of earth to lose, this body of Marcius, they could grind it to dust and throw it into the wind. To the market square! You've given me a part I'll never play convincingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Come, come, we'll prompt you."],"id":783,"m":"Come, come, we'll prompt you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I pr'ythee now, sweet son,—as thou hast said","My praises made thee first a soldier, so,","To have my praise for this, perform a part","Thou hast not done before."],"id":784,"m":"Please now, sweet son: as you've said my praise first made you a soldier, so, to have my praise for this, play a part you've never played before."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Well, I must do't:","Away, my disposition, and possess me","Some harlot's spirit! My throat of war be turn'd,","Which quired with my drum, into a pipe","Small as an eunuch, or the virgin voice","That babies lulls asleep! the smiles of knaves","Tent in my cheeks; and school-boys' tears take up","The glasses of my sight! a beggar's tongue","Make motion through my lips; and my arm'd knees,","Who bow'd but in my stirrup, bend like his","That hath receiv'd an alms!—I will not do't;","Lest I surcease to honour mine own truth,","And by my body's action teach my mind","A most inherent baseness."],"id":785,"m":"Well, I have to do it. Away with my true nature, and let some prostitute's spirit possess me! Let my war-trained throat, which sang in chorus with my drum, turn into a pipe as thin as a eunuch's or the girl's voice that lulls babies to sleep! Let villains' smiles camp in my cheeks, and schoolboys' tears fill my eyes! Let a beggar's tongue move between my lips, and my armoured knees, which have only bent in my stirrup, bow like a man who's been given charity! I won't do it, in case I stop honouring my own integrity and, by what my body does, teach my mind a deep-rooted baseness."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["At thy choice, then:","To beg of thee, it is my more dishonour","Than thou of them. Come all to ruin: let","Thy mother rather feel thy pride than fear","Thy dangerous stoutness; for I mock at death","With as big heart as thou. Do as thou list.","Thy valiantness was mine, thou suck'dst it from me;","But owe thy pride thyself."],"id":786,"m":"Have it your own way, then. It's more dishonour for me to beg from you than for you to beg from them. Let everything go to ruin. Let your mother suffer your pride rather than fear your dangerous stubbornness, because I laugh at death as boldly as you. Do as you like. Your courage was mine; you sucked it from me. But your pride you owe to yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Pray, be content:","Mother, I am going to the market-place;","Chide me no more. I'll mountebank their loves,","Cog their hearts from them, and come home belov'd","Of all the trades in Rome. Look, I am going.","Commend me to my wife. I'll return consul;","Or never trust to what my tongue can do","I' the way of flattery further."],"id":787,"m":"Please, be calm. Mother, I'm going to the market square. Don't scold me any more. I'll win their love like a quack doctor, cheat their hearts out of them, and come home loved by all the tradesmen in Rome. Look, I'm going. Give my love to my wife. I'll come back consul, or never trust my tongue's skill at flattery again."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Do your will."],"id":788,"m":"Do as you wish."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":789},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Away! The tribunes do attend you: arm yourself","To answer mildly; for they are prepar'd","With accusations, as I hear, more strong","Than are upon you yet."],"id":790,"m":"Come on! The tribunes are waiting for you. Prepare to answer mildly, because I hear they're ready with accusations even stronger than the ones you face already."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["The word is, mildly.—Pray you let us go:","Let them accuse me by invention, I","Will answer in mine honour."],"id":791,"m":"The word is \"mildly\". Please let's go. Let them accuse me with made-up charges; I'll answer honourably."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Ay, but mildly."],"id":792,"m":"Yes, but mildly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Well, mildly be it then; mildly."],"id":793,"m":"Well, mildly it is, then. Mildly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":794},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Rome. The Forum.","id":795},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SICINIUS and BRUTUS.","id":796},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["In this point charge him home, that he affects","Tyrannical power: if he evade us there,","Enforce him with his envy to the people;","And that the spoil got on the Antiates","Was ne'er distributed."],"id":797,"m":"Press him hard on this point, that he's aiming at tyrannical power. If he gets out of that, hit him with his hatred of the people and the fact that the loot taken from the men of Antium was never shared out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter an AEDILE.","id":798},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["What, will he come?"],"id":799,"m":"Well, is he coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["He's coming."],"id":800,"m":"He's coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["How accompanied?"],"id":801,"m":"Who's with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["With old Menenius, and those senators","That always favour'd him."],"id":802,"m":"Old Menenius, and the senators who have always supported him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Have you a catalogue","Of all the voices that we have procur'd,","Set down by the poll?"],"id":803,"m":"Do you have a list of all the votes we've secured, set down by head?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["I have; 'tis ready."],"id":804,"m":"I have; it's ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Have you collected them by tribes?"],"id":805,"m":"Have you grouped them by tribes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["I have."],"id":806,"m":"I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Assemble presently the people hither:","And when they hear me say 'It shall be so","I' the right and strength o' the commons,' be it either","For death, for fine, or banishment, then let them,","If I say fine, cry 'Fine!'- if death, cry 'Death;'","Insisting on the old prerogative","And power i' the truth o' the cause."],"id":807,"m":"Gather the people here at once. And when they hear me say, \"It shall be so, by the right and power of the commons\", whether it's for death, a fine or banishment, then, if I say fine, let them shout \"Fine!\", if death, \"Death!\", insisting on their ancient right and their power in the justice of the cause."},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["I shall inform them."],"id":808,"m":"I'll tell them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["And when such time they have begun to cry,","Let them not cease, but with a din confus'd","Enforce the present execution","Of what we chance to sentence."],"id":809,"m":"And once they've started shouting, don't let them stop; let them use the confused din to force the immediate carrying out of whatever sentence we pass."},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["Very well."],"id":810,"m":"Very well."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Make them be strong, and ready for this hint,","When we shall hap to give't them."],"id":811,"m":"Make them steadfast and ready for this cue whenever we give it to them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Go about it."],"id":812,"m":"Go and see to it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit AEDILE.","id":813},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Put him to choler straight: he hath been us'd","Ever to conquer, and to have his worth","Of contradiction; being once chaf'd, he cannot","Be rein'd again to temperance; then he speaks","What's in his heart; and that is there which looks","With us to break his neck."],"id":814,"m":"Make him angry straight away. He has always been used to winning and to having his full share of contradicting others. Once he's provoked, he can't be reined back into calm; then he says what's in his heart, and what's there will help us break his neck."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Well, here he comes."],"id":815,"m":"Well, here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS, MENENIUS, COMINIUS, Senators, and Patricians.","id":816},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Calmly, I do beseech you."],"id":817,"m":"Calmly, I beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Ay, as an ostler, that for the poorest piece","Will bear the knave by the volume.—The honoured gods","Keep Rome in safety, and the chairs of justice","Supplied with worthy men! plant love among's!","Throng our large temples with the shows of peace,","And not our streets with war!"],"id":818,"m":"Yes, like a stable boy who, for the smallest tip, will put up with being called a rogue over and over. May the honoured gods keep Rome safe and the seats of justice filled with worthy men! Plant love among us! Fill our great temples with the ceremonies of peace, and not our streets with war!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Amen, amen!"],"id":819,"m":"Amen, amen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["A noble wish."],"id":820,"m":"A noble wish."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter the AEDILE, with Citizens.","id":821},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Draw near, ye people."],"id":822,"m":"Come closer, you people."},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["List to your tribunes; audience: peace, I say!"],"id":823,"m":"Listen to your tribunes. Attention! Quiet, I say!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["First, hear me speak."],"id":824,"m":"First, hear me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["Well, say.—Peace, ho!"],"id":825,"m":"Well, speak. Quiet, there!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Shall I be charg'd no further than this present?","Must all determine here?"],"id":826,"m":"Will I be charged with nothing beyond what's charged now? Must everything be settled here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["I do demand,","If you submit you to the people's voices,","Allow their officers, and are content","To suffer lawful censure for such faults","As shall be proved upon you."],"id":827,"m":"I ask whether you submit yourself to the people's votes, accept their officers, and are willing to suffer lawful punishment for such faults as are proved against you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I am content."],"id":828,"m":"I am willing."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Lo, citizens, he says he is content:","The warlike service he has done, consider; think","Upon the wounds his body bears, which show","Like graves i' the holy churchyard."],"id":829,"m":"Look, citizens, he says he's willing. Consider the service he has done in war; think about the wounds on his body, which look like graves in a holy churchyard."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Scratches with briers,","Scars to move laughter only."],"id":830,"m":"Scratches from brambles, scars that would only make you laugh."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Consider further,","That when he speaks not like a citizen,","You find him like a soldier: do not take","His rougher accents for malicious sounds,","But, as I say, such as become a soldier,","Rather than envy you."],"id":831,"m":"Consider too that when he doesn't speak like a citizen, you'll find he speaks like a soldier. Don't take his rougher tone for malice, but, as I say, as suits a soldier, rather than as hatred of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Well, well, no more."],"id":832,"m":"Well, well, no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What is the matter,","That being pass'd for consul with full voice,","I am so dishonour'd that the very hour","You take it off again?"],"id":833,"m":"What's the matter, that after being passed as consul with full approval, I'm so disgraced that in the same hour you take it away again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Answer to us."],"id":834,"m":"You answer to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Say then: 'tis true, I ought so."],"id":835,"m":"Go on, then. It's true, I should."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["We charge you that you have contriv'd to take","From Rome all season'd office, and to wind","Yourself into a power tyrannical;","For which you are a traitor to the people."],"id":836,"m":"We charge you with having schemed to remove all established offices from Rome and to work your way into tyrannical power, for which you're a traitor to the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["How! traitor!"],"id":837,"m":"What! Traitor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Nay, temperately; your promise."],"id":838,"m":"No, calmly; your promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["The fires i' the lowest hell fold in the people!","Call me their traitor!—Thou injurious tribune!","Within thine eyes sat twenty thousand deaths,","In thy hands clutch'd as many millions, in","Thy lying tongue both numbers, I would say,","Thou liest unto thee with a voice as free","As I do pray the gods."],"id":839,"m":"May the fires of deepest hell swallow the people! Call me their traitor! You insulting tribune! If there were twenty thousand deaths sitting in your eyes, as many millions clutched in your hands, and both numbers on your lying tongue, I would still say \"You lie\" to your face, as freely as I pray to the gods."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Mark you this, people?"],"id":840,"m":"Do you hear this, people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["To the rock, to the rock, with him!"],"id":841,"m":"To the rock, to the rock with him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Peace!","We need not put new matter to his charge:","What you have seen him do and heard him speak,","Beating your officers, cursing yourselves,","Opposing laws with strokes, and here defying","Those whose great power must try him; even this,","So criminal and in such capital kind,","Deserves the extremest death."],"id":842,"m":"Quiet! We don't need to add new charges. What you've seen him do and heard him say, beating your officers, cursing you, resisting the law with blows, and here defying those whose great power must judge him, this, so criminal and so capital, deserves the harshest death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["But since he hath","Serv'd well for Rome,—"],"id":843,"m":"But since he has served Rome well—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What do you prate of service?"],"id":844,"m":"What are you chattering about service for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I talk of that that know it."],"id":845,"m":"I'm talking about what I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["You?"],"id":846,"m":"You?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Is this the promise that you made your mother?"],"id":847,"m":"Is this the promise you made your mother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Know, I pray you,—"],"id":848,"m":"Understand, please—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I'll know no further:","Let them pronounce the steep Tarpeian death,","Vagabond exile, flaying, pent to linger","But with a grain a day, I would not buy","Their mercy at the price of one fair word,","Nor check my courage for what they can give,","To have't with saying Good-morrow."],"id":849,"m":"I'll understand nothing more. Let them sentence me to death from the steep Tarpeian rock, a wanderer's exile, flaying, being locked up to linger on a grain a day; I wouldn't buy their mercy at the price of one polite word, or hold back my spirit for anything they can give, even if all it took was saying \"Good morning\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["For that he has,—","As much as in him lies,—from time to time","Envied against the people, seeking means","To pluck away their power; as now at last","Given hostile strokes, and that not in the presence","Of dreaded justice, but on the ministers","That do distribute it;—in the name o' the people,","And in the power of us the tribunes, we,","Even from this instant, banish him our city,","In peril of precipitation","From off the rock Tarpeian, never more","To enter our Rome gates: I' the people's name,","I say it shall be so."],"id":850,"m":"Because he has, as far as he could, shown hatred against the people time and again, looking for ways to take away their power, and now at last has struck hostile blows, and not just in the presence of feared justice but against the officers who administer it: in the name of the people and by the power of us, the tribunes, we banish him from our city from this moment, on pain of being thrown off the Tarpeian rock, never to enter Rome's gates again. In the people's name, I say it shall be so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["It shall be so, it shall be so; let him away;","He's banished, and it shall be so."],"id":851,"m":"It shall be so, it shall be so! Let him go! He's banished, and it shall be so."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Hear me, my masters and my common friends,—"],"id":852,"m":"Hear me, my masters and my common friends—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["He's sentenc'd; no more hearing."],"id":853,"m":"He has been sentenced. No more hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Let me speak:","I have been consul, and can show for Rome","Her enemies' marks upon me. I do love","My country's good with a respect more tender,","More holy and profound, than mine own life,","My dear wife's estimate, her womb's increase,","And treasure of my loins; then if I would","Speak that,—"],"id":854,"m":"Let me speak. I have been consul, and I can show Rome's enemies' marks on me. I love my country's good with a more tender, holy and deep regard than my own life, my dear wife's honour, her children, the treasure of my body. So if I were to say that—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["We know your drift. Speak what?"],"id":855,"m":"We know where you're going. Say what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["There's no more to be said, but he is banish'd,","As enemy to the people and his country:","It shall be so."],"id":856,"m":"There's nothing more to say except that he's banished as an enemy of the people and his country. It shall be so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["It shall be so, it shall be so."],"id":857,"m":"It shall be so, it shall be so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["You common cry of curs! whose breath I hate","As reek o' the rotten fens, whose loves I prize","As the dead carcasses of unburied men","That do corrupt my air,—I banish you;","And here remain with your uncertainty!","Let every feeble rumour shake your hearts!","Your enemies, with nodding of their plumes,","Fan you into despair! Have the power still","To banish your defenders; till at length","Your ignorance,—which finds not till it feels,—","Making but reservation of yourselves,—","Still your own foes,—deliver you, as most","Abated captives to some nation","That won you without blows! Despising,","For you, the city, thus I turn my back:","There is a world elsewhere."],"id":858,"m":"You common pack of curs, whose breath I hate like the stink of rotten swamps, whose love I value like the dead bodies of unburied men that poison my air: I banish you! Stay here with your fickleness! May every feeble rumour make your hearts shake! May your enemies, nodding their plumed helmets, fan you into despair! Keep the power to banish your defenders, until at last your ignorance, which understands nothing until it feels it, keeps only yourselves, always your own enemies, and hands you over as the most humbled prisoners to some nation that conquered you without a blow! Despising the city because of you, I turn my back on it like this. There is a world elsewhere."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CORIOLANUS, COMINIUS, MENENIUS, Senators, and Patricians.","id":859},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["The people's enemy is gone, is gone!"],"id":860,"m":"The people's enemy is gone, he's gone!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Our enemy is banish'd, he is gone! Hoo! hoo!"],"id":861,"m":"Our enemy is banished, he's gone! Hurrah! Hurrah!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Shouting, and throwing up their caps.","id":862},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Go, see him out at gates, and follow him,","As he hath follow'd you, with all despite;","Give him deserv'd vexation. Let a guard","Attend us through the city."],"id":863,"m":"Go and see him out of the gates, and follow him with every insult, as he has pursued you. Give him the torment he deserves. Let a guard go with us through the city."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Come, come, let's see him out at gates; come.","The gods preserve our noble tribunes! Come."],"id":864,"m":"Come on, come on, let's see him out of the gates. Come! The gods preserve our noble tribunes! Come on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":865},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. Before a gate of the city.","id":866},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS, VOLUMNIA, VIRGILIA, MENENIUS, COMINIUS, and several young Patricians.","id":867},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Come, leave your tears; a brief farewell:—the beast","With many heads butts me away.—Nay, mother,","Where is your ancient courage? you were us'd","To say extremities was the trier of spirits;","That common chances common men could bear;","That when the sea was calm all boats alike","Show'd mastership in floating; fortune's blows,","When most struck home, being gentle wounded, craves","A noble cunning; you were us'd to load me","With precepts that would make invincible","The heart that conn'd them."],"id":868,"m":"Come, stop crying; a short goodbye. The beast with many heads butts me out. No, mother, where's your old courage? You used to say extreme trouble tests the spirit; that ordinary men can bear ordinary luck; that when the sea is calm, all boats look equally skilled at floating; that when fortune's blows strike deepest, staying gentle under the wound takes noble skill. You used to load me with rules that would make the heart that learned them unbeatable."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["O heavens! O heavens!"],"id":869,"m":"Oh heavens! Oh heavens!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Nay, I pr'ythee, woman,—"],"id":870,"m":"No, please, woman—"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Now the red pestilence strike all trades in Rome,","And occupations perish!"],"id":871,"m":"Now may the red plague strike every trade in Rome and all its occupations die!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What, what, what!","I shall be lov'd when I am lack'd. Nay, mother,","Resume that spirit when you were wont to say,","If you had been the wife of Hercules,","Six of his labours you'd have done, and sav'd","Your husband so much sweat.—Cominius,","Droop not; adieu.—Farewell, my wife,—my mother:","I'll do well yet.—Thou old and true Menenius,","Thy tears are salter than a younger man's,","And venomous to thine eyes.—My sometime general,","I have seen thee stern, and thou hast oft beheld","Heart-hard'ning spectacles; tell these sad women","'Tis fond to wail inevitable strokes,","As 'tis to laugh at 'em.—My mother, you wot well","My hazards still have been your solace: and","Believe't not lightly,—though I go alone,","Like to a lonely dragon, that his fen","Makes fear'd and talk'd of more than seen,—your son","Will or exceed the common or be caught","With cautelous baits and practice."],"id":872,"m":"What, what, what! They'll love me when they miss me. No, mother, take up again the spirit you had when you used to say that if you'd been Hercules's wife, you'd have done six of his labours and saved your husband all that sweat. Cominius, don't be downcast. Goodbye. Farewell, my wife, my mother. I'll do well yet. You old and faithful Menenius, your tears are saltier than a younger man's and poison to your eyes. My former general, I've seen you stern, and you've often seen sights that harden the heart. Tell these sad women it's as foolish to wail at blows that can't be avoided as it is to laugh at them. Mother, you know well my dangers have always been your comfort; and believe me, though I go alone, like a solitary dragon whose swamp is feared and talked about more than seen, your son will either outdo ordinary men or be caught by crafty bait and trickery."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["My first son,","Whither wilt thou go? Take good Cominius","With thee awhile: determine on some course","More than a wild exposture to each chance","That starts i' the way before thee."],"id":873,"m":"My first-born son, where will you go? Take good Cominius with you for a while. Decide on some plan, rather than wildly exposing yourself to every chance that springs up in front of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["O the gods!"],"id":874,"m":"Oh, the gods!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I'll follow thee a month, devise with thee","Where thou shalt rest, that thou mayst hear of us,","And we of thee: so, if the time thrust forth","A cause for thy repeal, we shall not send","O'er the vast world to seek a single man;","And lose advantage, which doth ever cool","I' the absence of the needer."],"id":875,"m":"I'll go with you for a month and work out with you where you'll settle, so you can hear from us and we from you. Then, if time brings a reason to recall you, we won't have to send all over the vast world to look for one man and lose our advantage, which always cools when the man who needs it isn't there."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Fare ye well:","Thou hast years upon thee; and thou art too full","Of the wars' surfeits to go rove with one","That's yet unbruis'd: bring me but out at gate.—","Come, my sweet wife, my dearest mother, and","My friends of noble touch; when I am forth,","Bid me farewell, and smile. I pray you, come.","While I remain above the ground, you shall","Hear from me still; and never of me aught","But what is like me formerly."],"id":876,"m":"Farewell. You're old, and you've had too much of war to go wandering with someone who's still unbruised. Just see me out of the gate. Come, my sweet wife, my dearest mother, and my noble friends. When I'm outside, say goodbye to me and smile. Please, come. While I'm still above ground, you'll keep hearing from me, and never anything about me but what's like what I've been before."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["That's worthily","As any ear can hear.—Come, let's not weep.—","If I could shake off but one seven years","From these old arms and legs, by the good gods,","I'd with thee every foot."],"id":877,"m":"That's as worthy as anything anyone could hear. Come, let's not cry. If I could shake just seven years off these old arms and legs, by the good gods, I'd go with you every step."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Give me thy hand:—","Come."],"id":878,"m":"Give me your hand. Come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":879},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Rome. A street near the gate.","id":880},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SICINIUS, BRUTUS, and an AEDILE.","id":881},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Bid them all home; he's gone, and we'll no further.—","The nobility are vex'd, whom we see have sided","In his behalf."],"id":882,"m":"Send them all home. He's gone, and we'll go no further. The nobility are angry; we can see they've taken his side."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Now we have shown our power,","Let us seem humbler after it is done","Than when it was a-doing."],"id":883,"m":"Now that we've shown our power, let's seem humbler now it's done than while it was being done."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Bid them home:","Say their great enemy is gone, and they","Stand in their ancient strength."],"id":884,"m":"Send them home. Tell them their great enemy is gone, and they keep their old strength."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Dismiss them home."],"id":885,"m":"Dismiss them to their homes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit AEDILE.","id":886},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes his mother."],"id":887,"m":"Here comes his mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Let's not meet her."],"id":888,"m":"Let's not meet her."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why?"],"id":889,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["They say she's mad."],"id":890,"m":"They say she's mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["They have ta'en note of us: keep on your way."],"id":891,"m":"They've seen us. Keep walking."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter VOLUMNIA, VIRGILIA, and MENENIUS.","id":892},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["O, you're well met: the hoarded plague o' the gods","Requite your love!"],"id":893,"m":"Oh, well met! May the gods' stored-up plagues repay your love!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Peace, peace, be not so loud."],"id":894,"m":"Quiet, quiet, don't be so loud."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["If that I could for weeping, you should hear,—","Nay, and you shall hear some.—[To BRUTUS] Will you be gone?"],"id":895,"m":"If I could speak for crying, you'd hear— No, you'll hear some anyway. (To Brutus) Are you leaving?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["You shall stay too[To SICINIUS]: I would I had the power","To say so to my husband."],"id":896,"m":"(To Sicinius) You'll stay too. I wish I had the power to say that to my husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Are you mankind?"],"id":897,"m":"Are you turning into a man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Ay, fool; is that a shame?—Note but this, fool.—","Was not a man my father? Hadst thou foxship","To banish him that struck more blows for Rome","Than thou hast spoken words?—"],"id":898,"m":"Yes, fool; is that shameful? Just note this, fool: wasn't my father a man? Did you have the foxy cunning to banish the man who struck more blows for Rome than you've spoken words?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["O blessed heavens!"],"id":899,"m":"Oh blessed heavens!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Moe noble blows than ever thou wise words;","And for Rome's good.—I'll tell thee what;—yet go;—","Nay, but thou shalt stay too:—I would my son","Were in Arabia, and thy tribe before him,","His good sword in his hand."],"id":900,"m":"More noble blows than you've ever spoken wise words, and for Rome's good. I'll tell you what— no, go. No, you'll stay too. I wish my son were in the Arabian desert, and your tribe in front of him, with his good sword in his hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["What then?"],"id":901,"m":"What then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["What then!","He'd make an end of thy posterity."],"id":902,"m":"What then! He'd wipe out all your descendants."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Bastards and all.—","Good man, the wounds that he does bear for Rome!"],"id":903,"m":"Bastards and all. Good man, the wounds he carries for Rome!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Come, come, peace."],"id":904,"m":"Come, come, quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["I would he had continu'd to his country","As he began, and not unknit himself","The noble knot he made."],"id":905,"m":"I wish he had carried on with his country as he began, and not untied the noble knot he made himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I would he had."],"id":906,"m":"I wish he had."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["I would he had! 'Twas you incens'd the rabble;—","Cats, that can judge as fitly of his worth","As I can of those mysteries which heaven","Will not have earth to know."],"id":907,"m":"\"I wish he had\"! It was you who stirred up the mob: cats, who can judge his worth as well as I can judge the mysteries heaven won't let earth know."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Pray, let us go."],"id":908,"m":"Please, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Now, pray, sir, get you gone:","You have done a brave deed. Ere you go, hear this,—","As far as doth the Capitol exceed","The meanest house in Rome, so far my son,—","This lady's husband here; this, do you see?—","Whom you have banish'd does exceed you all."],"id":909,"m":"Now, please, sir, be off. You've done a fine deed. Before you go, hear this: as far as the Capitol towers above the meanest house in Rome, so far my son, this lady's husband here, do you see, whom you have banished, towers above all of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Well, well, we'll leave you."],"id":910,"m":"Well, well, we'll leave you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Why stay we to be baited","With one that wants her wits?"],"id":911,"m":"Why should we stay to be baited by a woman who's lost her wits?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Take my prayers with you.—"],"id":912,"m":"Take my prayers with you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt TRIBUNES.","id":913},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","cont":1,"lines":["I would the gods had nothing else to do","But to confirm my curses! Could I meet 'em","But once a day, it would unclog my heart","Of what lies heavy to't."],"id":914,"m":"I wish the gods had nothing else to do but carry out my curses! If I could meet them just once a day, it would unburden my heart of what weighs on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You have told them home,","And, by my troth, you have cause. You'll sup with me?"],"id":915,"m":"You've told them straight, and honestly, you have reason to. Will you have supper with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Anger's my meat; I sup upon myself,","And so shall starve with feeding.—Come, let's go:","Leave this faint puling and lament as I do,","In anger, Juno-like. Come, come, come."],"id":916,"m":"Anger is my food. I feed on myself, and so I'll starve by eating. Come, let's go. (To Virgilia) Stop this feeble whimpering and grieve as I do, in anger, like Juno. Come, come, come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":917},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Fie, fie, fie!"],"id":918,"m":"Shame, shame, shame!"},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"A highway between Rome and Antium.","id":919},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a ROMAN and a VOLSCE, meeting.","id":920},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["I know you well, sir, and you know me; your name, I think,","is Adrian."],"id":921,"m":"I know you well, sir, and you know me. Your name, I think, is Adrian."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["It is so, sir: truly, I have forgot you."],"id":922,"m":"It is, sir. Honestly, I've forgotten you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["I am a Roman; and my services are, as you are, against 'em: know","you me yet?"],"id":923,"m":"I'm a Roman, and I work, as you do, against the Romans. Do you know me now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["Nicanor? no!"],"id":924,"m":"Nicanor? No!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["The same, sir."],"id":925,"m":"The same, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["You had more beard when I last saw you; but your favour is","well approved by your tongue. What's the news in Rome? I have a","note from the Volscian state, to find you out there; you have","well saved me a day's journey."],"id":926,"m":"You had more beard when I last saw you, but your voice confirms your face. What's the news in Rome? I have orders from the Volscian state to find you there; you've saved me a good day's journey."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["There hath been in Rome strange insurrections: the people","against the senators, patricians, and nobles."],"id":927,"m":"There have been strange uprisings in Rome: the people against the senators, patricians and nobles."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["Hath been! is it ended, then? Our state thinks not so;","they are in a most warlike preparation, and hope to come upon","them in the heat of their division."],"id":928,"m":"Have been! Is it over, then? Our state doesn't think so. They're preparing hard for war and hope to attack them while they're hotly divided."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["The main blaze of it is past, but a small thing would make it","flame again; for the nobles receive so to heart the banishment","of that worthy Coriolanus that they are in a ripe aptness to take","all power from the people, and to pluck from them their tribunes","for ever. This lies glowing, I can tell you, and is almost mature","for the violent breaking out."],"id":929,"m":"The main blaze is past, but a small thing would make it flare up again, because the nobles take the banishment of that worthy Coriolanus so much to heart that they're ripe to take all power away from the people and abolish their tribunes forever. It's glowing under the surface, I can tell you, and almost ready to break out violently."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["Coriolanus banished!"],"id":930,"m":"Coriolanus banished!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["Banished, sir."],"id":931,"m":"Banished, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["You will be welcome with this intelligence, Nicanor."],"id":932,"m":"You'll be welcome with this news, Nicanor."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["The day serves well for them now. I have heard it said the","fittest time to corrupt a man's wife is when she's fallen out","with her husband. Your noble Tullus Aufidius will appear well in","these wars, his great opposer, Coriolanus, being now in no","request of his country."],"id":933,"m":"The moment's just right for them now. I've heard it said the best time to seduce a man's wife is when she has fallen out with her husband. Your noble Tullus Aufidius will shine in these wars, now that his great opponent, Coriolanus, is no longer wanted by his country."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["He cannot choose. I am most fortunate thus accidentally to","encounter you; you have ended my business, and I will merrily","accompany you home."],"id":934,"m":"He's bound to. I'm very lucky to have met you by chance like this. You've finished my business for me, and I'll happily go home with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["I shall between this and supper tell you most strange things","from Rome; all tending to the good of their adversaries. Have you","an army ready, say you?"],"id":935,"m":"Between now and supper I'll tell you the strangest things from Rome, all of them good for Rome's enemies. You have an army ready, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["A most royal one; the centurions and their charges, distinctly","billeted, already in the entertainment, and to be on foot at an","hour's warning."],"id":936,"m":"A splendid one. The centurions and their companies, each quartered separately, are already on the payroll and can march at an hour's notice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["I am joyful to hear of their readiness, and am the man, I think,","that shall set them in present action. So, sir, heartily well","met, and most glad of your company."],"id":937,"m":"I'm glad to hear they're ready, and I think I'm the man to set them going at once. So, sir, very well met, and I'm very glad of your company."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLSCE","lines":["You take my part from me, sir; I have the most cause to be","glad of yours."],"id":938,"m":"You're taking my line, sir; I have the most reason to be glad of yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROMAN","lines":["Well, let us go together."],"id":939,"m":"Well, let's go together."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":940},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Antium. Before Aufidius's house.","id":941},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS, in mean apparel, disguised and muffled.","id":942},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["A goodly city is this Antium. City,","'Tis I that made thy widows: many an heir","Of these fair edifices 'fore my wars","Have I heard groan and drop: then know me not.","Lest that thy wives with spits and boys with stones,","In puny battle slay me."],"id":943,"m":"This Antium is a fine city. City, it's I who made your widows. I've heard many an heir of these fine buildings groan and fall in my wars. So don't recognize me, or your wives with roasting spits and your boys with stones will kill me in a petty fight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a CITIZEN.","id":944},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["Save you, sir."],"id":945,"m":"God keep you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZEN","lines":["And you."],"id":946,"m":"And you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Direct me, if it be your will,","Where great Aufidius lies; is he in Antium?"],"id":947,"m":"Please tell me, if you will, where the great Aufidius lives. Is he in Antium?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZEN","lines":["He is, and feasts the nobles of the state","At his house this night."],"id":948,"m":"He is, and he's feasting the nobles of the state at his house tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Which is his house, beseech you?"],"id":949,"m":"Which is his house, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZEN","lines":["This, here, before you."],"id":950,"m":"This one, here, in front of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Thank you, sir; farewell."],"id":951,"m":"Thank you, sir. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit CITIZEN.","id":952},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["O world, thy slippery turns! Friends now fast sworn,","Whose double bosoms seems to wear one heart,","Whose hours, whose bed, whose meal and exercise","Are still together, who twin, as 'twere, in love","Unseparable, shall within this hour,","On a dissension of a doit, break out","To bitterest enmity; so fellest foes,","Whose passions and whose plots have broke their sleep","To take the one the other, by some chance,","Some trick not worth an egg, shall grow dear friends","And interjoin their issues. So with me:—","My birthplace hate I, and my love's upon","This enemy town.—I'll enter; if he slay me,","He does fair justice; if he give me way,","I'll do his country service."],"id":953,"m":"Oh world, how slippery your turns are! Friends sworn true now, whose two breasts seem to hold one heart, who always spend their hours, their bed, their meals and their exercise together, who are like twins in their inseparable love, will within the hour break out into the bitterest hatred over a quarrel about a penny. In the same way, the deadliest enemies, whose passions and schemes to catch each other have kept them awake, will by some chance, some trick not worth an egg, become dear friends and join their children in marriage. So it is with me. I hate my birthplace, and my love has turned to this enemy town. I'll go in. If he kills me, he does fair justice; if he takes me in, I'll serve his country."},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"Antium. A hall in Aufidius's house.","id":954},{"t":"sd","text":"Music within. Enter A SERVANT.","id":955},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Wine, wine, wine! What service is here!","I think our fellows are asleep."],"id":956,"m":"Wine, wine, wine! What service is this? I think our fellow servants are asleep."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":957},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a second SERVANT.","id":958},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Where's Cotus? my master calls for him.—Cotus!"],"id":959,"m":"Where's Cotus? My master's calling for him. Cotus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":960},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS.","id":961},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["A goodly house: the feast smells well; but I","Appear not like a guest."],"id":962,"m":"A fine house. The feast smells good; but I don't look like a guest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter the first SERVANT.","id":963},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["What would you have, friend? whence are you? Here's no place for","you: pray go to the door."],"id":964,"m":"What do you want, friend? Where are you from? There's no place for you here. Please go to the door."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I have deserv'd no better entertainment","In being Coriolanus."],"id":965,"m":"I've deserved no better welcome by being Coriolanus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter second SERVANT.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Whence are you, sir? Has the porter his eyes in his head that he","gives entrance to such companions? Pray, get you out."],"id":967,"m":"Where are you from, sir? Has the porter got eyes in his head, letting in fellows like this? Please, get out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Away!"],"id":968,"m":"Go away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Away? Get you away."],"id":969,"m":"\"Go away\"? You go away."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Now thou art troublesome."],"id":970,"m":"Now you're being a nuisance."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Are you so brave? I'll have you talked with anon."],"id":971,"m":"Are you so bold? I'll have someone deal with you soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a third SERVANT. The first meets him.","id":972},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["What fellow's this?"],"id":973,"m":"Who's this fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["A strange one as ever I looked on: I cannot get him","out o' the house. Pr'ythee call my master to him."],"id":974,"m":"As strange a one as I've ever seen. I can't get him out of the house. Please call the master to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["What have you to do here, fellow? Pray you avoid the house."],"id":975,"m":"What are you doing here, fellow? Please leave the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Let me but stand; I will not hurt your hearth."],"id":976,"m":"Just let me stand here. I won't harm your hearth."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["What are you?"],"id":977,"m":"What are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["A gentleman."],"id":978,"m":"A gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["A marvellous poor one."],"id":979,"m":"An amazingly poor one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["True, so I am."],"id":980,"m":"True, I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Pray you, poor gentleman, take up some other station; here's no","place for you. Pray you avoid; come."],"id":981,"m":"Please, poor gentleman, find some other place to stand. There's no place for you here. Please leave. Come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Follow your function, go,","And batten on cold bits."],"id":982,"m":"Go about your job, go on, and fatten yourself on leftovers."},{"t":"sd","text":"Pushes him away.","id":983},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["What, you will not?—Pr'ythee, tell my master what a strange","guest he has here."],"id":984,"m":"What, you won't go? Please tell the master what a strange guest he has here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["And I shall."],"id":985,"m":"I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":986},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Where dwell'st thou?"],"id":987,"m":"Where do you live?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Under the canopy."],"id":988,"m":"Under the canopy."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Under the canopy?"],"id":989,"m":"Under the canopy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Ay."],"id":990,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Where's that?"],"id":991,"m":"Where's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I' the city of kites and crows."],"id":992,"m":"In the city of kites and crows."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["I' the city of kites and crows!—What an ass it is!—Then thou","dwell'st with daws too?"],"id":993,"m":"In the city of kites and crows! What an ass! Then you live with jackdaws too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["No, I serve not thy master."],"id":994,"m":"No, I don't serve your master."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["How, sir! Do you meddle with my master?"],"id":995,"m":"What, sir! Are you meddling with my master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Ay; 'tis an honester service than to meddle with thy mistress.","Thou prat'st and prat'st; serve with thy trencher, hence!"],"id":996,"m":"Yes; it's more honest work than meddling with your mistress. You chatter and chatter. Go and wait at table with your plate! Get out!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Beats him away.","id":997},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter AUFIDIUS and the second SERVANT.","id":998},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Where is this fellow?"],"id":999,"m":"Where's this fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Here, sir; I'd have beaten him like a dog, but for","disturbing the lords within."],"id":1000,"m":"Here, sir. I'd have beaten him like a dog, but I didn't want to disturb the lords inside."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Whence com'st thou? what wouldst thou? thy name?","Why speak'st not? speak, man: what's thy name?"],"id":1001,"m":"Where do you come from? What do you want? Your name? Why don't you speak? Speak, man. What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["[Unmuffling] If, Tullus,","Not yet thou know'st me, and, seeing me, dost not","Think me for the man I am, necessity","Commands me name myself."],"id":1002,"m":"(Uncovering his face) If you still don't know me, Tullus, and seeing me, don't take me for the man I am, necessity forces me to name myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["What is thy name?"],"id":1003,"m":"What is your name?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Servants retire.","id":1004},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["A name unmusical to the Volscians' ears,","And harsh in sound to thine."],"id":1005,"m":"A name that sounds unmusical to Volscian ears and harsh to yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Say, what's thy name?","Thou hast a grim appearance, and thy face","Bears a command in't; though thy tackle's torn,","Thou show'st a noble vessel: what's thy name?"],"id":1006,"m":"Say, what's your name? You look grim, and your face has authority in it. Though your rigging is torn, you look like a noble ship. What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Prepare thy brow to frown:—know'st thou me yet?"],"id":1007,"m":"Get ready to frown. Do you know me now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I know thee not:—thy name?"],"id":1008,"m":"I don't know you. Your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["My name is Caius Marcius, who hath done","To thee particularly, and to all the Volsces,","Great hurt and mischief; thereto witness may","My surname, Coriolanus: the painful service,","The extreme dangers, and the drops of blood","Shed for my thankless country, are requited","But with that surname; a good memory,","And witness of the malice and displeasure","Which thou shouldst bear me: only that name remains;","The cruelty and envy of the people,","Permitted by our dastard nobles, who","Have all forsook me, hath devour'd the rest,","And suffer'd me by the voice of slaves to be","Whoop'd out of Rome. Now, this extremity","Hath brought me to thy hearth: not out of hope,","Mistake me not, to save my life; for if","I had fear'd death, of all the men i' the world","I would have 'voided thee; but in mere spite,","To be full quit of those my banishers,","Stand I before thee here. Then if thou hast","A heart of wreak in thee, that wilt revenge","Thine own particular wrongs, and stop those maims","Of shame seen through thy country, speed thee straight","And make my misery serve thy turn: so use it","That my revengeful services may prove","As benefits to thee; for I will fight","Against my canker'd country with the spleen","Of all the under fiends. But if so be","Thou dar'st not this, and that to prove more fortunes","Th'art tir'd, then, in a word, I also am","Longer to live most weary, and present","My throat to thee and to thy ancient malice;","Which not to cut would show thee but a fool,","Since I have ever follow'd thee with hate,","Drawn tuns of blood out of thy country's breast,","And cannot live but to thy shame, unless","It be to do thee service."],"id":1009,"m":"My name is Caius Marcius, who has done great harm and damage to you in particular and to all the Volscians; my surname, Coriolanus, is witness to it. The painful service, the extreme dangers and the drops of blood shed for my ungrateful country are repaid only with that surname, a good reminder and proof of the hatred and anger you ought to feel for me. Only the name remains. The cruelty and spite of the people, allowed by our cowardly nobles, who have all abandoned me, has swallowed up the rest and let me be hooted out of Rome by the voices of slaves. Now this desperate state has brought me to your hearth, not in hope of saving my life, don't mistake me, because if I'd feared death, I'd have avoided you more than any man in the world, but out of pure spite, to get even with those who banished me, I stand here before you. So if you have a heart full of vengeance that will avenge your own personal wrongs and stop those shameful wounds seen all over your country, hurry and make my misery serve your purpose. Use it so that my revengeful services prove a benefit to you, because I'll fight against my rotten country with the fury of all the devils in hell. But if you don't dare to do this and you're too tired to try your luck any more, then, in a word, I'm also very tired of living, and I offer my throat to you and your old hatred. Not to cut it would only show you a fool, since I've always pursued you with hatred, drawn barrels of blood from your country's breast, and can only live to your shame, unless it's to serve you."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["O Marcius, Marcius!","Each word thou hast spoke hath weeded from my heart","A root of ancient envy. If Jupiter","Should from yond cloud speak divine things,","And say ''Tis true,' I'd not believe them more","Than thee, all noble Marcius.—Let me twine","Mine arms about that body, where against","My grained ash an hundred times hath broke","And scar'd the moon with splinters; here I clip","The anvil of my sword, and do contest","As hotly and as nobly with thy love","As ever in ambitious strength I did","Contend against thy valour. Know thou first,","I lov'd the maid I married; never man","Sighed truer breath; but that I see thee here,","Thou noble thing! more dances my rapt heart","Than when I first my wedded mistress saw","Bestride my threshold. Why, thou Mars! I tell thee","We have a power on foot; and I had purpose","Once more to hew thy target from thy brawn,","Or lose mine arm for't: thou hast beat me out","Twelve several times, and I have nightly since","Dreamt of encounters 'twixt thyself and me;","We have been down together in my sleep,","Unbuckling helms, fisting each other's throat,","And wak'd half dead with nothing. Worthy Marcius,","Had we no other quarrel else to Rome, but that","Thou art thence banish'd, we would muster all","From twelve to seventy; and, pouring war","Into the bowels of ungrateful Rome,","Like a bold flood o'erbear. O, come, go in,","And take our friendly senators by the hands;","Who now are here, taking their leaves of me,","Who am prepar'd against your territories,","Though not for Rome itself."],"id":1010,"m":"Oh Marcius, Marcius! Every word you've spoken has rooted out of my heart a root of old hatred. If Jupiter spoke divine things from that cloud and said \"It's true\", I wouldn't believe him more than you, all-noble Marcius. Let me wind my arms around that body against which my ash-wood spear has broken a hundred times and scared the moon with splinters. Here I hug the anvil of my sword, and I compete as hotly and nobly with your love as I ever fought against your courage in ambitious strength. Know this first: I loved the girl I married; no man ever sighed more truly. But seeing you here, you noble thing, makes my enraptured heart dance more than when I first saw my bride step over my threshold. Why, you Mars! I tell you, we have an army ready, and I intended once more to hack your shield from your arm or lose my own arm trying. You've beaten me twelve separate times, and every night since I've dreamt of fights between us. We've been on the ground together in my sleep, unbuckling helmets, grabbing each other's throats, and I've woken half dead from nothing. Worthy Marcius, if we had no other quarrel with Rome but that you're banished from it, we'd call up every man from twelve to seventy and, pouring war into the guts of ungrateful Rome, overwhelm it like a bold flood. Oh, come in and shake hands with our friendly senators, who are here now taking leave of me; I'm ready to march against your lands, though not against Rome itself."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["You bless me, gods!"],"id":1011,"m":"Gods, you bless me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Therefore, most absolute sir, if thou wilt have","The leading of thine own revenges, take","Th' one half of my commission; and set down,—","As best thou art experienc'd, since thou know'st","Thy country's strength and weakness,—thine own ways;","Whether to knock against the gates of Rome,","Or rudely visit them in parts remote,","To fright them, ere destroy. But come in;","Let me commend thee first to those that shall","Say yea to thy desires. A thousand welcomes!","And more a friend than e'er an enemy;","Yet, Marcius, that was much. Your hand: most welcome!"],"id":1012,"m":"So, most perfect sir, if you want to lead your own revenge, take half of my command and decide your own course, as you're most experienced and know your country's strengths and weaknesses: whether to knock at the gates of Rome, or roughly attack its outlying parts to frighten them before destroying them. But come in. Let me introduce you first to those who'll agree to what you want. A thousand welcomes! And more a friend than ever an enemy; and yet, Marcius, that was a great deal. Your hand: most welcome!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CORIOLANUS and AUFIDIUS.","id":1013},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Here's a strange alteration!"],"id":1014,"m":"Here's a strange change!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["By my hand, I had thought to have strucken him with a cudgel; and","yet my mind gave me his clothes made a false report of him."],"id":1015,"m":"I swear I was going to hit him with a club; and yet I had a feeling his clothes were lying about him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["What an arm he has! He turned me about with his finger and his","thumb, as one would set up a top."],"id":1016,"m":"What an arm he has! He spun me round with his finger and thumb like a spinning top."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Nay, I knew by his face that there was something in him; he had,","sir, a kind of face, methought,—I cannot tell how to term it."],"id":1017,"m":"Well, I could tell from his face there was something in him. He had, sir, a kind of face, I thought— I can't say how to put it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["He had so, looking as it were,—would I were hanged, but I","thought there was more in him than I could think."],"id":1018,"m":"He did, looking as if— hang me, but I thought there was more in him than I could imagine."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["So did I, I'll be sworn: he is simply the rarest man i' the","world."],"id":1019,"m":"So did I, I swear. He's simply the most remarkable man in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["I think he is; but a greater soldier than he you wot on."],"id":1020,"m":"I think he is; but there's a greater soldier than him, you know who."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Who, my master?"],"id":1021,"m":"Who, our master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Nay, it's no matter for that."],"id":1022,"m":"No, that doesn't matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Worth six on him."],"id":1023,"m":"Worth six of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Nay, not so neither: but I take him to be the greater soldier."],"id":1024,"m":"No, not that either; but I think he's the greater soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Faith, look you, one cannot tell how to say that: for the defence","of a town our general is excellent."],"id":1025,"m":"Well, look, you can't really say. For defending a town our general is excellent."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Ay, and for an assault too."],"id":1026,"m":"Yes, and for attacking one too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter third SERVANT.","id":1027},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["O slaves, I can tell you news,—news, you rascals!"],"id":1028,"m":"Oh, you slaves, I've got news for you, news, you rascals!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST AND SECOND SERVANT","lines":["What, what, what? let's partake."],"id":1029,"m":"What, what, what? Let's share it."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["I would not be a Roman, of all nations; I had as lief be a","condemned man."],"id":1030,"m":"I wouldn't be a Roman for anything; I'd as soon be a condemned man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST AND SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Wherefore? wherefore?"],"id":1031,"m":"Why? Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Why, here's he that was wont to thwack our general,—Caius","Marcius."],"id":1032,"m":"Why, here's the man who used to thrash our general: Caius Marcius."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Why do you say, thwack our general?"],"id":1033,"m":"Why do you say \"thrash our general\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["I do not say thwack our general; but he was always good enough","for him."],"id":1034,"m":"I don't say \"thrash our general\"; but he was always more than a match for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Come, we are fellows and friends: he was ever too hard for him; I","have heard him say so himself."],"id":1035,"m":"Come on, we're comrades and friends. He was always too strong for him; I've heard him say so himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["He was too hard for him directly, to say the troth on't; before","Corioli he scotched him and notched him like a carbonado."],"id":1036,"m":"To tell the truth, he was flatly too much for him. At Corioli he slashed and scored him like a steak for grilling."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["An he had been cannibally given, he might have broiled and eaten","him too."],"id":1037,"m":"If he'd been a cannibal, he might have grilled and eaten him too."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["But more of thy news?"],"id":1038,"m":"But what else is your news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Why, he is so made on here within as if he were son and heir to","Mars; set at upper end o' the table: no question asked him by any","of the senators but they stand bald before him: our general","himself makes a mistress of him, sanctifies himself with's hand,","and turns up the white o' the eye to his discourse. But the","bottom of the news is, our general is cut i' the middle, and but","one half of what he was yesterday; for the other has half, by the","entreaty and grant of the whole table. He'll go, he says, and","sowl the porter of Rome gates by the ears; he will mow all down","before him, and leave his passage polled."],"id":1039,"m":"Why, they're making as much of him inside as if he were Mars's son and heir. He's seated at the head of the table; none of the senators asks him a question without standing bareheaded before him. Our general himself treats him like a mistress, touches his hand as if it were holy, and rolls his eyes upward at what he says. But the heart of the news is that our general has been cut in half and is only half of what he was yesterday, because the other man has the other half, at the request and with the consent of the whole table. He says he'll go and drag the porter of Rome's gates by the ears; he'll mow down everything in his way and leave his path cleared."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["And he's as like to do't as any man I can imagine."],"id":1040,"m":"And he's as likely to do it as any man I can imagine."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Do't! he will do't; for look you, sir, he has as many friends as","enemies; which friends, sir, as it were, durst not, look you,","sir, show themselves, as we term it, his friends, whilst he's in","dejectitude."],"id":1041,"m":"Do it! He will do it. Because look, sir, he has as many friends as enemies; and those friends, sir, so to speak, didn't dare, look you, sir, show themselves to be, as we say, his friends while he was in \"dejectitude\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Dejectitude! what's that?"],"id":1042,"m":"\"Dejectitude\"! What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["But when they shall see, sir, his crest up again, and the man in","blood, they will out of their burrows, like conies after rain,","and revel all with him."],"id":1043,"m":"But when they see, sir, his crest raised again and the man in full vigour, they'll come out of their burrows like rabbits after rain and all celebrate with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["But when goes this forward?"],"id":1044,"m":"But when is this happening?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["To-morrow; to-day; presently; you shall have the drum struck up","this afternoon: 'tis as it were parcel of their feast, and to be","executed ere they wipe their lips."],"id":1045,"m":"Tomorrow; today; right now. You'll hear the drum beaten this afternoon. It's part of their feast, so to speak, and will be carried out before they wipe their lips."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Why, then we shall have a stirring world again. This peace is","nothing but to rust iron, increase tailors, and breed","ballad-makers."],"id":1046,"m":"Why, then we'll have a lively world again. This peace does nothing but rust iron, multiply tailors and breed ballad-writers."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Let me have war, say I; it exceeds peace as far as day does","night; it's spritely, waking, audible, and full of vent. Peace is","a very apoplexy, lethargy; mulled, deaf, sleepy, insensible; a","getter of more bastard children than war's a destroyer of men."],"id":1047,"m":"Give me war, I say. It beats peace as day beats night. It's lively, wide awake, loud and full of news. Peace is a real paralysis, a coma: flat, deaf, sleepy, senseless; it fathers more bastards than war kills men."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["'Tis so: and as war in some sort, may be said to be a ravisher,","so it cannot be denied but peace is a great maker of cuckolds."],"id":1048,"m":"That's right. And just as war could be called a rapist in a way, there's no denying peace makes plenty of cuckolds."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Ay, and it makes men hate one another."],"id":1049,"m":"Yes, and it makes men hate each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Reason: because they then less need one another. The wars for my","money. I hope to see Romans as cheap as Volscians. They are","rising, they are rising."],"id":1050,"m":"That's because they need each other less then. Give me war. I hope to see Romans as cheap as Volscians. They're getting up from the table, they're getting up."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["In, in, in, in!"],"id":1051,"m":"In, in, in, in!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1052},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"Rome. A public place.","id":1053},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SICINIUS and BRUTUS.","id":1054},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["We hear not of him, neither need we fear him;","His remedies are tame i' the present peace","And quietness of the people, which before","Were in wild hurry. Here do make his friends","Blush that the world goes well; who rather had,","Though they themselves did suffer by't, behold","Dissentious numbers pestering streets than see","Our tradesmen singing in their shops, and going","About their functions friendly."],"id":1055,"m":"We hear nothing of him, and we don't need to fear him. His help is useless now, with the people peaceful and quiet when before they were in wild uproar. This makes his friends blush that everything's going well. They'd rather see quarrelsome crowds plaguing the streets, even if they suffered for it themselves, than see our tradesmen singing in their shops and going about their work in a friendly way."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["We stood to't in good time.—Is this Menenius?"],"id":1056,"m":"We stood firm at the right time. Is this Menenius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["'Tis he, 'tis he. O, he is grown most kind","Of late."],"id":1057,"m":"It's him, it's him. Oh, he has become very friendly lately."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MENENIUS.","id":1058},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Hail, sir!"],"id":1059,"m":"Greetings, sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Hail to you both!"],"id":1060,"m":"Greetings to you both!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Your Coriolanus is not much miss'd","But with his friends: the commonwealth doth stand;","And so would do, were he more angry at it."],"id":1061,"m":"Your Coriolanus isn't missed much except by his friends. The state stands firm, and would still stand if he were even angrier with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["All's well, and might have been much better if","He could have temporiz'd."],"id":1062,"m":"All's well, and it might have been much better if he could have compromised."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Where is he, hear you?"],"id":1063,"m":"Have you heard where he is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Nay, I hear nothing: his mother and his wife","Hear nothing from him."],"id":1064,"m":"No, I hear nothing. His mother and his wife hear nothing from him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter three or four Citizens.","id":1065},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["The gods preserve you both!"],"id":1066,"m":"The gods keep you both!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["God-den, our neighbours."],"id":1067,"m":"Good evening, neighbours."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["God-den to you all, God-den to you all."],"id":1068,"m":"Good evening to you all, good evening to you all."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Ourselves, our wives, and children, on our knees,","Are bound to pray for you both."],"id":1069,"m":"We, our wives and children are bound to pray for you both on our knees."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Live and thrive!"],"id":1070,"m":"Live and prosper!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Farewell, kind neighbours: we wish'd Coriolanus","Had lov'd you as we did."],"id":1071,"m":"Goodbye, kind neighbours. We wish Coriolanus had loved you as we do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Now the gods keep you!"],"id":1072,"m":"Now the gods keep you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["Farewell, farewell."],"id":1073,"m":"Goodbye, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Citizens.","id":1074},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["This is a happier and more comely time","Than when these fellows ran about the streets","Crying confusion."],"id":1075,"m":"This is a happier and more fitting time than when these fellows ran around the streets shouting for chaos."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Caius Marcius was","A worthy officer i' the war; but insolent,","O'ercome with pride, ambitious past all thinking,","Self-loving,—"],"id":1076,"m":"Caius Marcius was a worthy officer in war, but arrogant, swollen with pride, ambitious beyond belief, self-loving—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["And affecting one sole throne,","Without assistance."],"id":1077,"m":"And aiming at sole rule, without anyone to share it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I think not so."],"id":1078,"m":"I don't think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["We should by this, to all our lamentation,","If he had gone forth consul, found it so."],"id":1079,"m":"If he'd become consul, we'd all have found it so by now, to our sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The gods have well prevented it, and Rome","Sits safe and still without him."],"id":1080,"m":"The gods have kindly prevented it, and Rome sits safe and quiet without him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter an AEDILE.","id":1081},{"t":"sp","who":"AEDILE","lines":["Worthy tribunes,","There is a slave, whom we have put in prison,","Reports,—the Volsces with several powers","Are enter'd in the Roman territories,","And with the deepest malice of the war","Destroy what lies before 'em."],"id":1082,"m":"Worthy tribunes, there's a slave we've put in prison who reports that the Volscians, with two separate armies, have entered Roman territory and are destroying everything in their path with the most savage hostility of war."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["'Tis Aufidius,","Who, hearing of our Marcius' banishment,","Thrusts forth his horns again into the world;","Which were inshell'd when Marcius stood for Rome,","And durst not once peep out."],"id":1083,"m":"It's Aufidius, who, hearing of our Marcius's banishment, is poking his horns out into the world again. They were pulled back into his shell while Marcius stood up for Rome, and he didn't dare peep out once."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Come, what talk you of Marcius?"],"id":1084,"m":"Come, why are you talking about Marcius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Go see this rumourer whipp'd.—It cannot be","The Volsces dare break with us."],"id":1085,"m":"Have this rumour-monger whipped. It can't be that the Volscians dare break the treaty with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Cannot be!","We have record that very well it can;","And three examples of the like hath been","Within my age. But reason with the fellow,","Before you punish him, where he heard this;","Lest you shall chance to whip your information","And beat the messenger who bids beware","Of what is to be dreaded."],"id":1086,"m":"Can't be! We have records that show very well it can, and there have been three examples of it in my lifetime. But before you punish the fellow, ask him where he heard this, in case you end up whipping your source of information and beating the messenger who warns you of what you should fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Tell not me:","I know this cannot be."],"id":1087,"m":"Don't tell me. I know this can't be."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Not possible."],"id":1088,"m":"It's not possible."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter A MESSENGER.","id":1089},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The nobles in great earnestness are going","All to the senate-house: some news is come","That turns their countenances."],"id":1090,"m":"The nobles are all going in great seriousness to the senate house. Some news has come that has changed their faces."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["'Tis this slave,—","Go whip him fore the people's eyes:—his raising;","Nothing but his report."],"id":1091,"m":"It's this slave. Go and whip him in front of the people. It's his stirring, nothing but his report."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Yes, worthy sir,","The slave's report is seconded, and more,","More fearful, is deliver'd."],"id":1092,"m":"Yes, worthy sir, the slave's report has been confirmed, and more, more frightening news has been brought."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["What more fearful?"],"id":1093,"m":"What, more frightening?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["It is spoke freely out of many mouths,—","How probable I do not know,—that Marcius,","Join'd with Aufidius, leads a power 'gainst Rome,","And vows revenge as spacious as between","The young'st and oldest thing."],"id":1094,"m":"Many people are openly saying, how likely it is I don't know, that Marcius has joined Aufidius and is leading an army against Rome, and vows a revenge as wide as the gap between the youngest and the oldest thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["This is most likely!"],"id":1095,"m":"That's very likely!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Rais'd only, that the weaker sort may wish","Good Marcius home again."],"id":1096,"m":"Started only so the weaker people will wish good Marcius home again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["The very trick on 't."],"id":1097,"m":"That's exactly the trick."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["This is unlikely:","He and Aufidius can no more atone","Than violentest contrariety."],"id":1098,"m":"This is unlikely. He and Aufidius can no more be reconciled than the most violent opposites."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a second MESSENGER.","id":1099},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MESSENGER","lines":["You are sent for to the senate:","A fearful army, led by Caius Marcius","Associated with Aufidius, rages","Upon our territories; and have already","O'erborne their way, consum'd with fire and took","What lay before them."],"id":1100,"m":"You're called to the senate. A terrifying army led by Caius Marcius, together with Aufidius, is raging through our territory. They've already forced their way through, burned and taken everything in front of them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter COMINIUS.","id":1101},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["O, you have made good work!"],"id":1102,"m":"Oh, you've done fine work!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What news? what news?"],"id":1103,"m":"What news? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["You have holp to ravish your own daughters, and","To melt the city leads upon your pates;","To see your wives dishonour'd to your noses,—"],"id":1104,"m":"You've helped to rape your own daughters and to melt the city's lead roofs onto your heads, to see your wives disgraced in front of you—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What's the news? what's the news?"],"id":1105,"m":"What's the news? What's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Your temples burned in their cement; and","Your franchises, whereon you stood, confin'd","Into an auger's bore."],"id":1106,"m":"Your temples burned to their foundations, and your rights, which you stood on so firmly, squeezed into a hole the size of a drill bit."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Pray now, your news?—","You have made fair work, I fear me.—Pray, your news.","If Marcius should be join'd wi' the Volscians,—"],"id":1107,"m":"Please, now, your news? You've done fine work, I'm afraid. Please, your news. If Marcius has joined the Volscians—"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["If!","He is their god: he leads them like a thing","Made by some other deity than nature,","That shapes man better; and they follow him,","Against us brats, with no less confidence","Than boys pursuing summer butterflies,","Or butchers killing flies."],"id":1108,"m":"If! He's their god. He leads them like a being made by some other god than nature, one who shapes men better; and they follow him against us brats as confidently as boys chasing summer butterflies or butchers swatting flies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You have made good work,","You and your apron men; you that stood so much","Upon the voice of occupation and","The breath of garlic-eaters!"],"id":1109,"m":"You've done fine work, you and your men in aprons, you who made so much of the votes of workmen and the breath of garlic-eaters!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["He'll shake","Your Rome about your ears."],"id":1110,"m":"He'll shake your Rome down around your ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["As Hercules","Did shake down mellow fruit.—You have made fair work!"],"id":1111,"m":"As Hercules shook down ripe fruit. You've done fine work!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["But is this true, sir?"],"id":1112,"m":"But is this true, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Ay; and you'll look pale","Before you find it other. All the regions","Do smilingly revolt; and who resists","Are mock'd for valiant ignorance,","And perish constant fools. Who is't can blame him?","Your enemies and his find something in him."],"id":1113,"m":"Yes, and you'll turn pale before you find it otherwise. All the regions are gladly going over to him, and those who resist are mocked for their brave ignorance and die loyal fools. Who can blame him? Your enemies and his see something in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["We are all undone unless","The noble man have mercy."],"id":1114,"m":"We're all ruined unless the noble man shows mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Who shall ask it?","The tribunes cannot do't for shame; the people","Deserve such pity of him as the wolf","Does of the shepherds: for his best friends, if they","Should say 'Be good to Rome,' they charg'd him even","As those should do that had deserv'd his hate,","And therein show'd like enemies."],"id":1115,"m":"Who will ask him for it? The tribunes can't, for shame. The people deserve as much pity from him as the wolf gets from shepherds. As for his best friends, if they said \"Be good to Rome\", they'd be asking it of him just as those who'd earned his hatred would, and so would look like enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["'Tis true:","If he were putting to my house the brand","That should consume it, I have not the face","To say 'Beseech you, cease.'—You have made fair hands,","You and your crafts! You have crafted fair!"],"id":1116,"m":"That's true. If he were putting the torch to my house to burn it down, I wouldn't have the nerve to say \"Please, stop.\" (To the Tribunes) You've done a fine job, you and your craftsmen! You've crafted it beautifully!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["You have brought","A trembling upon Rome, such as was never","So incapable of help."],"id":1117,"m":"You've brought a trembling on Rome that was never so far beyond help."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH TRIBUNES","lines":["Say not, we brought it."],"id":1118,"m":"Don't say we brought it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["How! Was it we? we lov'd him, but, like beasts,","And cowardly nobles, gave way unto your clusters,","Who did hoot him out o' the city."],"id":1119,"m":"What! Was it us? We loved him, but, like beasts and cowardly nobles, we gave way to your mobs, who hooted him out of the city."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["But I fear","They'll roar him in again. Tullus Aufidius,","The second name of men, obeys his points","As if he were his officer:—desperation","Is all the policy, strength, and defence,","That Rome can make against them."],"id":1120,"m":"But I'm afraid they'll roar him back in again. Tullus Aufidius, the second-best of men, obeys his orders as if he were his officer. Desperation is all the strategy, strength and defence Rome can put up against them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a troop of citizens.","id":1121},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Here comes the clusters.—","And is Aufidius with him?—You are they","That made the air unwholesome, when you cast","Your stinking greasy caps in hooting at","Coriolanus' exile. Now he's coming;","And not a hair upon a soldier's head","Which will not prove a whip: as many coxcombs","As you threw caps up will he tumble down,","And pay you for your voices. 'Tis no matter;","If he could burn us all into one coal","We have deserv'd it."],"id":1122,"m":"Here come the mobs. And is Aufidius with him? You're the ones who poisoned the air when you threw up your stinking greasy caps, hooting at Coriolanus's exile. Now he's coming, and there isn't a hair on any soldier's head that won't be a whip. He'll knock down as many fools' heads as you threw up caps, and pay you back for your votes. It doesn't matter. If he burned us all into one cinder, we'd have deserved it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Faith, we hear fearful news."],"id":1123,"m":"Honestly, we're hearing terrible news."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["For mine own part,","When I said banish him, I said 'twas pity."],"id":1124,"m":"For my part, when I said \"banish him\", I said it was a pity."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["And so did I."],"id":1125,"m":"And so did I."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["And so did I; and, to say the truth, so did very many of us. That","we did, we did for the best; and though we willingly consented to","his banishment, yet it was against our will."],"id":1126,"m":"And so did I; and, to tell the truth, so did very many of us. What we did, we did for the best; and though we willingly agreed to his banishment, it was against our will."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["You are goodly things, you voices!"],"id":1127,"m":"You're fine creatures, you voters!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You have made","Good work, you and your cry!—Shall's to the Capitol?"],"id":1128,"m":"You've done good work, you and your pack! Shall we go to the Capitol?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["O, ay; what else?"],"id":1129,"m":"Oh yes; what else?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt COMINIUS and MENENIUS.","id":1130},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Go, masters, get you home; be not dismay'd;","These are a side that would be glad to have","This true which they so seem to fear. Go home,","And show no sign of fear."],"id":1131,"m":"Go home, friends; don't be alarmed. These men are a faction who'd be glad for this to be true, though they seem so afraid of it. Go home and show no sign of fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["The gods be good to us!—Come, masters, let's home. I","ever said we were i' the wrong when we banished him."],"id":1132,"m":"The gods be good to us! Come, friends, let's go home. I always said we were wrong to banish him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["So did we all. But come, let's home."],"id":1133,"m":"So did we all. But come, let's go home."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Citizens.","id":1134},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I do not like this news."],"id":1135,"m":"I don't like this news."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Nor I."],"id":1136,"m":"Nor do I."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Let's to the Capitol:—would half my wealth","Would buy this for a lie!"],"id":1137,"m":"Let's go to the Capitol. I'd give half my wealth to make this a lie!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Pray let's go."],"id":1138,"m":"Please, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1139},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VII","loc":"A camp at a short distance from Rome.","id":1140},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter AUFIDIUS and his LIEUTENANT.","id":1141},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Do they still fly to the Roman?"],"id":1142,"m":"Are they still flocking to the Roman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LIEUTENANT","lines":["I do not know what witchcraft's in him, but","Your soldiers use him as the grace 'fore meat,","Their talk at table, and their thanks at end;","And you are darken'd in this action, sir,","Even by your own."],"id":1143,"m":"I don't know what witchcraft is in him, but your soldiers use him as their grace before meat, their talk at dinner, and their thanks at the end. And you're overshadowed in this campaign, sir, even by your own men."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I cannot help it now,","Unless by using means, I lame the foot","Of our design. He bears himself more proudlier,","Even to my person, than I thought he would","When first I did embrace him: yet his nature","In that's no changeling; and I must excuse","What cannot be amended."],"id":1144,"m":"I can't help it now, unless I cripple our whole plan by the means I use. He behaves more proudly, even towards me, than I thought he would when I first embraced him. But his nature hasn't changed in that, and I must excuse what can't be put right."},{"t":"sp","who":"LIEUTENANT","lines":["Yet I wish, sir,—","I mean, for your particular,—you had not","Join'd in commission with him; but either","Had borne the action of yourself, or else","To him had left it solely."],"id":1145,"m":"Still, I wish, sir, for your own sake, that you hadn't shared the command with him, but had either led the campaign yourself or left it entirely to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I understand thee well; and be thou sure,","When he shall come to his account, he knows not","What I can urge against him. Although it seems,","And so he thinks, and is no less apparent","To the vulgar eye, that he bears all things fairly,","And shows good husbandry for the Volscian state,","Fights dragon-like, and does achieve as soon","As draw his sword: yet he hath left undone","That which shall break his neck or hazard mine","Whene'er we come to our account."],"id":1146,"m":"I understand you well. And be sure of this: when he comes to account for himself, he doesn't know what I can charge him with. Although it seems, and he thinks so, and it's no less obvious to ordinary eyes, that he conducts everything fairly, manages well for the Volscian state, fights like a dragon and wins as soon as he draws his sword, still he has left something undone that will break his neck or risk mine whenever we come to settle our accounts."},{"t":"sp","who":"LIEUTENANT","lines":["Sir, I beseech you, think you he'll carry Rome?"],"id":1147,"m":"Sir, please tell me, do you think he'll take Rome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["All places yield to him ere he sits down;","And the nobility of Rome are his;","The senators and patricians love him too:","The tribunes are no soldiers; and their people","Will be as rash in the repeal as hasty","To expel him thence. I think he'll be to Rome","As is the osprey to the fish, who takes it","By sovereignty of nature. First he was","A noble servant to them; but he could not","Carry his honours even: whether 'twas pride,","Which out of daily fortune ever taints","The happy man; whether defect of judgment,","To fail in the disposing of those chances","Which he was lord of; or whether nature,","Not to be other than one thing, not moving","From the casque to the cushion, but commanding peace","Even with the same austerity and garb","As he controll'd the war; but one of these,—","As he hath spices of them all, not all,","For I dare so far free him,—made him fear'd,","So hated, and so banish'd: but he has a merit","To choke it in the utterance. So our virtues","Lie in the interpretation of the time:","And power, unto itself most commendable,","Hath not a tomb so evident as a cheer","To extol what it hath done.","One fire drives out one fire; one nail, one nail;","Rights by rights falter, strengths by strengths do fail.","Come, let's away. When, Caius, Rome is thine,","Thou art poor'st of all; then shortly art thou mine."],"id":1148,"m":"Every place surrenders to him before he lays siege. The nobility of Rome are on his side, and the senators and patricians love him too. The tribunes are no soldiers, and their people will be as rash in recalling him as they were hasty in throwing him out. I think he'll be to Rome what the osprey is to the fish, which it takes by natural supremacy. At first he was a noble servant to them, but he couldn't carry his honours steadily. Whether it was pride, which always infects the lucky man who succeeds every day; whether a lack of judgment, failing to make the most of chances he controlled; or whether his nature, not to be anything but one thing, not changing from the helmet to the senate cushion, but ruling in peace with the same harshness and manner as he commanded in war: one of these (he has traces of them all, though not all completely, I'll clear him that far) made him feared, so hated, and so banished. But he has merit enough to choke the charges as they're spoken. So our virtues depend on how the times interpret them, and power, however praiseworthy in itself, has no tomb so certain as a public platform that praises what it has done. One fire drives out another, one nail another; rights give way to rights, strengths fail by strengths. Come, let's go. When Rome is yours, Caius, you'll be the poorest of all; and then you'll soon be mine."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1149},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A public place.","id":1150},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MENENIUS, COMINIUS, SICINIUS and BRUTUS, and others.","id":1151},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["No, I'll not go: you hear what he hath said","Which was sometime his general; who lov'd him","In a most dear particular. He call'd me father:","But what o' that? Go, you that banish'd him;","A mile before his tent fall down, and knee","The way into his mercy: nay, if he coy'd","To hear Cominius speak, I'll keep at home."],"id":1152,"m":"No, I won't go. You heard what he said to the man who was once his general and who loved him with a very special affection. He called me father; but what of it? You go, you who banished him. Fall down a mile before his tent and crawl on your knees into his mercy. No, if he was reluctant to hear Cominius speak, I'll stay at home."},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["He would not seem to know me."],"id":1153,"m":"He pretended not to know me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Do you hear?"],"id":1154,"m":"Do you hear that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["Yet one time he did call me by my name:","I urged our old acquaintance, and the drops","That we have bled together. Coriolanus","He would not answer to: forbad all names;","He was a kind of nothing, titleless,","Till he had forg'd himself a name i' the fire","Of burning Rome."],"id":1155,"m":"Still, once he did call me by my name. I pressed our old friendship and the blood we've shed together. He wouldn't answer to \"Coriolanus\"; he rejected every name. He was a kind of nothing, without a title, until he had forged himself a name in the fire of burning Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Why, so!—you have made good work!","A pair of tribunes that have rack'd for Rome,","To make coals cheap,—a noble memory!"],"id":1156,"m":"Why, there you are! You've done good work! A pair of tribunes who've worked so hard for Rome to make coal cheap: a noble memorial!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I minded him how royal 'twas to pardon","When it was less expected: he replied,","It was a bare petition of a state","To one whom they had punish'd."],"id":1157,"m":"I reminded him how royal it is to pardon when it's least expected. He replied that it was a feeble petition from a state to a man they had punished."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Very well:","Could he say less?"],"id":1158,"m":"Very well. Could he say less?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I offer'd to awaken his regard","For's private friends: his answer to me was,","He could not stay to pick them in a pile","Of noisome musty chaff: he said 'twas folly,","For one poor grain or two, to leave unburnt","And still to nose the offence."],"id":1159,"m":"I tried to stir his concern for his personal friends. His answer to me was that he couldn't stop to pick them out of a pile of stinking, mouldy chaff. He said it was foolish, for the sake of one or two poor grains, to leave the rest unburnt and go on smelling the stink."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["For one poor grain","Or two! I am one of those; his mother, wife,","His child, and this brave fellow too- we are the grains:","You are the musty chaff; and you are smelt","Above the moon: we must be burnt for you."],"id":1160,"m":"For one or two poor grains! I'm one of those; his mother, wife, child, and this brave fellow too, we are the grains. You are the mouldy chaff, and you stink higher than the moon. We must be burnt because of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Nay, pray be patient: if you refuse your aid","In this so never-needed help, yet do not","Upbraid's with our distress. But, sure, if you","Would be your country's pleader, your good tongue,","More than the instant army we can make,","Might stop our countryman."],"id":1161,"m":"No, please be patient. If you refuse your help when it was never needed more, at least don't reproach us in our trouble. But surely if you'd plead for your country, your good tongue might stop our countryman better than any army we could raise right now."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["No; I'll not meddle."],"id":1162,"m":"No, I won't get involved."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Pray you, go to him."],"id":1163,"m":"Please, go to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["What should I do?"],"id":1164,"m":"What should I do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Only make trial what your love can do","For Rome, towards Marcius."],"id":1165,"m":"Only try what your love for Marcius can do for Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Well, and say that Marcius","Return me, as Cominius is return'd,","Unheard; what then?","But as a discontented friend, grief-shot","With his unkindness? Say't be so?"],"id":1166,"m":"Well, and suppose Marcius sends me back unheard, as Cominius was sent back? What then? Just a rejected friend, wounded with grief by his unkindness? Suppose that happens?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Yet your good-will","Must have that thanks from Rome, after the measure","As you intended well."],"id":1167,"m":"Still, Rome must thank you for your good will, in proportion to your good intentions."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I'll undertake't;","I think he'll hear me. Yet to bite his lip","And hum at good Cominius much unhearts me.","He was not taken well: he had not din'd;","The veins unfill'd, our blood is cold, and then","We pout upon the morning, are unapt","To give or to forgive; but when we have stuff'd","These pipes and these conveyances of our blood","With wine and feeding, we have suppler souls","Than in our priest-like fasts. Therefore I'll watch him","Till he be dieted to my request,","And then I'll set upon him."],"id":1168,"m":"I'll take it on. I think he'll listen to me. Still, his biting his lip and grumbling at good Cominius discourages me a lot. He didn't catch him at the right moment; he hadn't eaten. When our veins aren't filled, our blood is cold, and then we sulk at the morning and aren't inclined to give or forgive. But when we've stuffed these pipes and channels of our blood with wine and food, our spirits are more flexible than when we fast like priests. So I'll wait until he's been fed enough for my request, and then I'll go at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You know the very road into his kindness","And cannot lose your way."],"id":1169,"m":"You know the very road into his kindness and can't lose your way."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Good faith, I'll prove him,","Speed how it will. I shall ere long have knowledge","Of my success."],"id":1170,"m":"Honestly, I'll try him, however it turns out. I'll soon know whether I've succeeded."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1171},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["He'll never hear him."],"id":1172,"m":"He'll never listen to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Not?"],"id":1173,"m":"No?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COMINIUS","lines":["I tell you he does sit in gold, his eye","Red as 'twould burn Rome: and his injury","The gaoler to his pity. I kneel'd before him;","'Twas very faintly he said 'Rise'; dismissed me","Thus, with his speechless hand: what he would do,","He sent in writing after me; what he would not,","Bound with an oath to yield to his conditions:","So that all hope is vain,","Unless his noble mother and his wife;","Who, as I hear, mean to solicit him","For mercy to his country. Therefore, let's hence,","And with our fair entreaties haste them on."],"id":1174,"m":"I tell you, he sits on a golden throne, his eye red as if it would burn Rome, and the wrong done to him keeps his pity locked up like a jailer. I knelt before him; he said \"Rise\" very faintly and dismissed me like this, with a silent wave of his hand. He sent after me in writing what he would do, and what he wouldn't, being bound by an oath to keep to his conditions. So all hope is useless, unless his noble mother and his wife, who I hear mean to beg him to have mercy on his country, can do it. So let's go and hurry them on with our earnest pleas."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1175},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"An advanced post of the Volscian camp before Rome.","id":1176},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter to them MENENIUS.","id":1177},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["Stay: whence are you?"],"id":1178,"m":"Stop! Where are you from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["Stand, and go back."],"id":1179,"m":"Stand, and go back."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["You guard like men; 'tis well: but, by your leave,","I am an officer of state, and come","To speak with Coriolanus."],"id":1180,"m":"You guard like real men; that's good. But, if you'll excuse me, I'm an officer of state, and I've come to speak to Coriolanus."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["From whence?"],"id":1181,"m":"From where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["From Rome."],"id":1182,"m":"From Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["You may not pass; you must return: our general","Will no more hear from thence."],"id":1183,"m":"You can't pass; you must go back. Our general will hear nothing more from there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["You'll see your Rome embrac'd with fire before","You'll speak with Coriolanus."],"id":1184,"m":"You'll see your Rome wrapped in fire before you speak to Coriolanus."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Good my friends,","If you have heard your general talk of Rome","And of his friends there, it is lots to blanks","My name hath touch'd your ears: it is Menenius."],"id":1185,"m":"My good friends, if you've heard your general talk about Rome and his friends there, it's a sure bet my name has reached your ears. It's Menenius."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["Be it so; go back: the virtue of your name","Is not here passable."],"id":1186,"m":"So be it. Go back. Your name's power doesn't get you through here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I tell thee, fellow,","Thy general is my lover: I have been","The book of his good acts, whence men have read","His fame unparallel'd, haply amplified;","For I have ever verified my friends,—","Of whom he's chief,—with all the size that verity","Would without lapsing suffer: nay, sometimes,","Like to a bowl upon a subtle ground,","I have tumbled past the throw: and in his praise","Have almost stamp'd the leasing: therefore, fellow,","I must have leave to pass."],"id":1187,"m":"I tell you, fellow, your general is my dear friend. I've been the book of his good deeds, from which men have read his unequalled fame, perhaps exaggerated, because I've always praised my friends, of whom he's the chief, as fully as truth would allow without slipping; indeed, sometimes, like a bowl on tricky ground, I've rolled past the mark, and in praising him I've almost stamped lies as true. So, fellow, I must be allowed to pass."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["Faith, sir, if you had told as many lies in his behalf as you","have uttered words in your own, you should not pass here: no,","though it were as virtuous to lie as to live chastely.","Therefore, go back."],"id":1188,"m":"Honestly, sir, if you'd told as many lies on his behalf as you've spoken words on your own, you still wouldn't pass here; no, not even if lying were as virtuous as living chastely. So go back."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Pr'ythee, fellow, remember my name is Menenius, always","factionary on the party of your general."],"id":1189,"m":"Please, fellow, remember my name is Menenius, always a supporter of your general's party."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["Howsoever you have been his liar,—as you say you have, I am one","that, telling true under him, must say you cannot pass. Therefore","go back."],"id":1190,"m":"However much you've been his liar, as you say you have, I'm someone who tells the truth under his command, and I must say you can't pass. So go back."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Has he dined, canst thou tell? For I would not speak with him","till after dinner."],"id":1191,"m":"Do you know whether he has eaten? Because I wouldn't want to talk to him until after dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["You are a Roman, are you?"],"id":1192,"m":"You're a Roman, are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I am as thy general is."],"id":1193,"m":"I am, as your general is."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["Then you should hate Rome, as he does. Can you, when you have","pushed out your gates the very defender of them, and in a violent","popular ignorance, given your enemy your shield, think to front","his revenges with the easy groans of old women, the virginal","palms of your daughters, or with the palsied intercession of such","a decayed dotant as you seem to be? Can you think to blow out the","intended fire your city is ready to flame in, with such weak","breath as this? No, you are deceived; therefore back to Rome, and","prepare for your execution: you are condemned; our general has","sworn you out of reprieve and pardon."],"id":1194,"m":"Then you should hate Rome, as he does. When you've pushed the very defender of your gates out of them and, in violent mob ignorance, given your shield to your enemy, can you think you'll face his revenge with old women's feeble groans, your daughters' virgin hands raised in prayer, or the shaky pleading of a worn-out old dodderer like you seem to be? Can you think you'll blow out the fire your city's about to blaze in with such weak breath as this? No, you're mistaken. So go back to Rome and get ready to be executed. You're condemned; our general has sworn you beyond reprieve or pardon."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Sirrah, if thy captain knew I were here he would use me with","estimation."],"id":1195,"m":"Fellow, if your captain knew I was here, he'd treat me with respect."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["Come, my captain knows you not."],"id":1196,"m":"Come on, my captain doesn't know you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I mean thy general."],"id":1197,"m":"I mean your general."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["My general cares not for you. Back, I say; go, lest I let forth","your half pint of blood;—back; that's the utmost of your","having:—back."],"id":1198,"m":"My general doesn't care about you. Back, I say; go, or I'll let out your half pint of blood. Back; that's all you'll get. Back."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Nay, but fellow, fellow,—"],"id":1199,"m":"No, but fellow, fellow—"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS with AUFIDIUS.","id":1200},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":1201,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["Now, you companion, I'll say an errand for you; you shall know","now that I am in estimation; you shall perceive that a jack","guardant cannot office me from my son Coriolanus: guess but by my","entertainment with him if thou standest not i' the state of","hanging, or of some death more long in spectatorship and crueller","in suffering; behold now presently, and swoon for what's to come","upon thee.—The glorious gods sit in hourly synod about thy","particular prosperity, and love thee no worse than thy old father","Menenius does! O my son! my son! thou art preparing fire for us;","look thee, here's water to quench it. I was hardly moved to come","to thee; but being assured none but myself could move thee, I","have been blown out of your gates with sighs; and conjure thee to","pardon Rome and thy petitionary countrymen. The good gods assuage","thy wrath, and turn the dregs of it upon this varlet here; this,","who, like a block, hath denied my access to thee."],"id":1202,"m":"Now, you fellow, I'll put in a word for you. You'll see now that I'm respected; you'll find that a guard dog of a soldier can't keep me from my son Coriolanus. Just judge by how he receives me whether you're not in line for hanging, or some death longer to watch and crueller to suffer. Watch now, and faint at what's coming to you. (To Coriolanus) May the glorious gods sit in council every hour about your personal good fortune, and love you no less than your old father Menenius does! Oh my son, my son! You're preparing fire for us; look, here's water to put it out. I was hard to persuade to come to you; but being assured no one but me could move you, I've been blown out of our gates by sighs, and I beg you to pardon Rome and your pleading countrymen. May the good gods soften your anger and turn the dregs of it on this rascal here, this one who, like a block of wood, has refused to let me through to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Away!"],"id":1203,"m":"Go away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["How! away!"],"id":1204,"m":"What! Go away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Wife, mother, child, I know not. My affairs","Are servanted to others: though I owe","My revenge properly, my remission lies","In Volscian breasts. That we have been familiar,","Ingrate forgetfulness shall poison, rather","Than pity note how much.—Therefore be gone.","Mine ears against your suits are stronger than","Your gates against my force. Yet, for I lov'd thee,","Take this along; I writ it for thy sake,"],"id":1205,"m":"Wife, mother, child, I don't know them. My affairs are now in other men's service. Though the revenge is my own, the power to pardon lies in Volscian hearts. Ungrateful forgetfulness shall poison our past friendship rather than pity remembering how close it was. So go. My ears are stronger against your pleas than your gates are against my force. Still, because I loved you, take this. I wrote it for your sake,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives a letter.","id":1206},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["And would have sent it. Another word, Menenius,","I will not hear thee speak.—This man, Aufidius,","Was my beloved in Rome: yet thou behold'st!"],"id":1207,"m":"and I would have sent it. Another word, Menenius, I won't listen to. This man, Aufidius, was dear to me in Rome; and yet you see!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["You keep a constant temper."],"id":1208,"m":"You stay firm."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CORIOLANUS and AUFIDIUS.","id":1209},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["Now, sir, is your name Menenius?"],"id":1210,"m":"Now, sir, is your name Menenius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["'Tis a spell, you see, of much power: you know the way home","again."],"id":1211,"m":"It's a spell of great power, you see. You know your way home."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["Do you hear how we are shent for keeping your greatness back?"],"id":1212,"m":"Do you hear how we've been scolded for keeping your greatness out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["What cause, do you think, I have to swoon?"],"id":1213,"m":"What reason do you think I have to faint?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I neither care for the world nor your general; for such things as","you, I can scarce think there's any, y'are so slight. He that","hath a will to die by himself fears it not from another. Let your","general do his worst. For you, be that you are, long; and your","misery increase with your age! I say to you, as I was said to,","away!"],"id":1214,"m":"I don't care about the world or your general. As for things like you, I can hardly believe you exist, you're so insignificant. A man who's willing to die by his own hand doesn't fear death from another. Let your general do his worst. As for you, stay what you are for a long time, and may your misery increase as you age! I say to you what was said to me: go away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1215},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GUARD","lines":["A noble fellow, I warrant him."],"id":1216,"m":"A noble fellow, I'll bet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GUARD","lines":["The worthy fellow is our general: he is the rock, the oak not to","be wind-shaken."],"id":1217,"m":"The worthy fellow is our general. He's the rock, the oak no wind can shake."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1218},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The tent of Coriolanus.","id":1219},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS, AUFIDIUS, and others.","id":1220},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["We will before the walls of Rome to-morrow","Set down our host.—My partner in this action,","You must report to the Volscian lords how plainly","I have borne this business."],"id":1221,"m":"Tomorrow we'll set our army down before the walls of Rome. My partner in this campaign, you must tell the Volscian lords how honestly I've conducted this business."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Only their ends","You have respected; stopped your ears against","The general suit of Rome; never admitted","A private whisper, no, not with such friends","That thought them sure of you."],"id":1222,"m":"You've considered only their interests, closed your ears to the whole of Rome's plea, and never allowed even a private whisper, not even with friends who thought they were sure of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["This last old man,","Whom with crack'd heart I have sent to Rome,","Lov'd me above the measure of a father;","Nay, godded me indeed. Their latest refuge","Was to send him; for whose old love I have,—","Though I show'd sourly to him,—once more offer'd","The first conditions, which they did refuse,","And cannot now accept, to grace him only,","That thought he could do more, a very little","I have yielded to: fresh embassies and suits,","Nor from the state nor private friends, hereafter","Will I lend ear to.—"],"id":1223,"m":"This last old man, whom I've sent back to Rome with a broken heart, loved me more than a father; in fact, he made a god of me. Sending him was their last resort. For the sake of my old love for him, though I was sour to him, I offered the first terms again, which they refused and can't accept now. Only to honour him, who thought he could do more, I've given way a very little. I won't listen to any new embassies or pleas, either from the state or from personal friends, from now on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shout within.","id":1224},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["Ha! what shout is this?","Shall I be tempted to infringe my vow","In the same time 'tis made? I will not."],"id":1225,"m":"Ha! What's that shouting? Will I be tempted to break my vow at the very moment I make it? I won't."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, in mourning habits, VIRGILIA, VOLUMNIA, leading YOUNG MARCIUS, VALERIA, and attendants.","id":1226},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["My wife comes foremost; then the honour'd mould","Wherein this trunk was fram'd, and in her hand","The grandchild to her blood. But, out, affection!","All bond and privilege of nature, break!","Let it be virtuous to be obstinate.—","What is that curt'sy worth? or those doves' eyes,","Which can make gods forsworn?—I melt, and am not","Of stronger earth than others.—My mother bows,","As if Olympus to a molehill should","In supplication nod: and my young boy","Hath an aspect of intercession which","Great nature cries 'Deny not.'—Let the Volsces","Plough Rome and harrow Italy: I'll never","Be such a gosling to obey instinct; but stand,","As if a man were author of himself,","And knew no other kin."],"id":1227,"m":"My wife comes first; then the honoured mould this body was formed in, and holding her hand, her grandchild. But away, affection! Let every bond and right of nature break! Let it be virtuous to be stubborn. What's that curtsy worth? Or those dove's eyes, which could make gods break their oaths? I'm melting; I'm not made of stronger earth than other men. My mother bows, as if Mount Olympus bowed in pleading to a molehill; and my young boy has a pleading look to which great nature cries, \"Don't refuse.\" Let the Volscians plough Rome and rake over Italy; I'll never be such a gosling as to obey instinct, but stand as if a man were his own creator and had no other family."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["My lord and husband!"],"id":1228,"m":"My lord and husband!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["These eyes are not the same I wore in Rome."],"id":1229,"m":"These eyes aren't the same ones I had in Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["The sorrow that delivers us thus chang'd","Makes you think so."],"id":1230,"m":"The sorrow that brings us here so changed makes you think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Like a dull actor now,","I have forgot my part and I am out,","Even to a full disgrace. Best of my flesh,","Forgive my tyranny; but do not say,","For that, 'Forgive our Romans.'—O, a kiss","Long as my exile, sweet as my revenge;","Now, by the jealous queen of heaven, that kiss","I carried from thee, dear; and my true lip","Hath virgin'd it e'er since.—You gods! I prate,","And the most noble mother of the world","Leave unsaluted: sink, my knee, i' the earth;"],"id":1231,"m":"Like a bad actor now, I've forgotten my part and lost my lines, to my complete disgrace. Best part of my flesh, forgive my cruelty; but don't say because of that, \"Forgive our Romans.\" Oh, a kiss as long as my exile, as sweet as my revenge! Now, by Juno, jealous queen of heaven, I've carried that kiss from you, dear, and my faithful lip has kept it untouched ever since. You gods! I'm chattering and leaving the most noble mother in the world ungreeted. Sink, my knee, into the earth;"},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels.","id":1232},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["Of thy deep duty more impression show","Than that of common sons."],"id":1233,"m":"show a deeper mark of your duty than common sons do."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["O, stand up bless'd!","Whilst, with no softer cushion than the flint,","I kneel before thee; and unproperly","Show duty, as mistaken all this while","Between the child and parent."],"id":1234,"m":"Oh, stand up, blessed, while I kneel to you with no softer cushion than the flint, and wrongly show the duty that all this time has been mistaken between child and parent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels.","id":1235},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["What is this?","Your knees to me? to your corrected son?","Then let the pebbles on the hungry beach","Fillip the stars; then let the mutinous winds","Strike the proud cedars 'gainst the fiery sun;","Murdering impossibility, to make","What cannot be, slight work."],"id":1236,"m":"What's this? Your knees to me? To your rebuked son? Then let the pebbles on the hungry beach flick at the stars; then let the rebellious winds hurl the proud cedars against the fiery sun, murdering impossibility, making what can't be into easy work."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Thou art my warrior;","I holp to frame thee. Do you know this lady?"],"id":1237,"m":"You're my warrior; I helped make you. Do you know this lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["The noble sister of Publicola,","The moon of Rome; chaste as the icicle","That's curded by the frost from purest snow,","And hangs on Dian's temple:—dear Valeria!"],"id":1238,"m":"The noble sister of Publicola, the moon of Rome, as chaste as the icicle frozen by the frost from the purest snow that hangs on Diana's temple. Dear Valeria!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["This is a poor epitome of yours,","Which, by the interpretation of full time,","May show like all yourself."],"id":1239,"m":"This is a little copy of you, which, when time has filled it out, may look exactly like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["The god of soldiers,","With the consent of supreme Jove, inform","Thy thoughts with nobleness; that thou mayst prove","To shame unvulnerable, and stick i' the wars","Like a great sea-mark, standing every flaw,","And saving those that eye thee!"],"id":1240,"m":"May Mars, the god of soldiers, with the consent of supreme Jove, fill your thoughts with nobility, so you prove proof against shame and stand out in the wars like a great landmark for sailors, withstanding every gust and saving those who look to you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Your knee, sirrah."],"id":1241,"m":"Your knee, young man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["That's my brave boy."],"id":1242,"m":"That's my brave boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Even he, your wife, this lady, and myself,","Are suitors to you."],"id":1243,"m":"He, your wife, this lady and I are all here to ask something of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I beseech you, peace:","Or, if you'd ask, remember this before,—","The thing I have forsworn to grant may never","Be held by you denials. Do not bid me","Dismiss my soldiers, or capitulate","Again with Rome's mechanics.—Tell me not","Wherein I seem unnatural: desire not","To allay my rages and revenges with","Your colder reasons."],"id":1244,"m":"I beg you, quiet. Or, if you're going to ask, remember this first: you mustn't take it as a refusal of you if I won't grant what I've sworn not to grant. Don't tell me to dismiss my soldiers or negotiate again with Rome's workmen. Don't tell me how I seem unnatural. Don't try to calm my rage and revenge with your cooler reasoning."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["O, no more, no more!","You have said you will not grant us anything;","For we have nothing else to ask but that","Which you deny already: yet we will ask;","That, if you fail in our request, the blame","May hang upon your hardness; therefore hear us."],"id":1245,"m":"Oh, no more, no more! You've said you won't grant us anything, because we have nothing else to ask except what you've already refused. Still, we will ask, so that if you refuse our request, the blame will rest on your hardness. So hear us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Aufidius, and you Volsces, mark: for we'll","Hear nought from Rome in private.—Your request?"],"id":1246,"m":"Aufidius, and you Volscians, take note, because we'll hear nothing from Rome in private. What is your request?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Should we be silent and not speak, our raiment","And state of bodies would bewray what life","We have led since thy exile. Think with thyself,","How more unfortunate than all living women","Are we come hither: since that thy sight, which should","Make our eyes flow with joy, hearts dance with comforts,","Constrains them weep, and shake with fear and sorrow;","Making the mother, wife, and child, to see","The son, the husband, and the father, tearing","His country's bowels out. And to poor we,","Thine enmity's most capital: thou barr'st us","Our prayers to the gods, which is a comfort","That all but we enjoy; for how can we,","Alas, how can we for our country pray,","Whereto we are bound,—together with thy victory,","Whereto we are bound? alack, or we must lose","The country, our dear nurse, or else thy person,","Our comfort in the country. We must find","An evident calamity, though we had","Our wish, which side should win; for either thou","Must, as a foreign recreant, be led","With manacles through our streets, or else","Triumphantly tread on thy country's ruin,","And bear the palm for having bravely shed","Thy wife and children's blood. For myself, son,","I purpose not to wait on fortune till","These wars determine: if I can not persuade thee","Rather to show a noble grace to both parts","Than seek the end of one, thou shalt no sooner","March to assault thy country than to tread,—","Trust to't, thou shalt not,—on thy mother's womb","That brought thee to this world."],"id":1247,"m":"Even if we kept silent and didn't speak, our clothes and the state of our bodies would show what life we've led since your exile. Think how much more unfortunate than all living women we are in coming here, since the sight of you, which should make our eyes flow with joy and our hearts dance with comfort, forces them to weep and shake with fear and sorrow, making the mother, wife and child see the son, husband and father tearing out his country's guts. And to poor us your hatred is most deadly. You stop us praying to the gods, a comfort everyone but us enjoys; because how can we, alas, how can we pray for our country, as we're bound to, and also for your victory, as we're bound to? Alas, either we must lose our country, our dear nurse, or else you, our comfort in that country. We're bound to meet obvious disaster even if we had our wish about which side should win; because either you must be led in chains through our streets as a foreign traitor, or else tread in triumph on your country's ruins and win the prize for bravely shedding your wife's and children's blood. As for me, son, I don't intend to wait on fortune until these wars are decided. If I can't persuade you to show noble grace to both sides rather than seek the destruction of one, you won't march to attack your country without treading (believe me, you won't) on your mother's womb that brought you into this world."},{"t":"sp","who":"VIRGILIA","lines":["Ay, and mine,","That brought you forth this boy, to keep your name","Living to time."],"id":1248,"m":"Yes, and on mine, which gave you this boy to keep your name alive through time."},{"t":"sp","who":"YOUNG MARCIUS","lines":["'A shall not tread on me;","I'll run away till I am bigger; but then I'll fight."],"id":1249,"m":"He won't tread on me. I'll run away till I'm bigger; but then I'll fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Not of a woman's tenderness to be,","Requires nor child nor woman's face to see.","I have sat too long."],"id":1250,"m":"Not to be softened by a woman's tenderness, one must not look at a child's or a woman's face. I've sat too long."},{"t":"sd","text":"Rising.","id":1251},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIA","lines":["Nay, go not from us thus.","If it were so that our request did tend","To save the Romans, thereby to destroy","The Volsces whom you serve, you might condemn us,","As poisonous of your honour: no; our suit","Is that you reconcile them: while the Volsces","May say 'This mercy we have show'd,' the Romans","'This we receiv'd,' and each in either side","Give the all-hail to thee, and cry, 'Be bless'd","For making up this peace!' Thou know'st, great son,","The end of war's uncertain; but this certain,","That, if thou conquer Rome, the benefit","Which thou shalt thereby reap is such a name","Whose repetition will be dogg'd with curses;","Whose chronicle thus writ:—'The man was noble,","But with his last attempt he wip'd it out;","Destroy'd his country, and his name remains","To the ensuing age abhorr'd.' Speak to me, son:","Thou hast affected the fine strains of honour,","To imitate the graces of the gods,","To tear with thunder the wide cheeks o' the air,","And yet to charge thy sulphur with a bolt","That should but rive an oak. Why dost not speak?","Think'st thou it honourable for a noble man","Still to remember wrongs?—Daughter, speak you:","He cares not for your weeping.—Speak thou, boy:","Perhaps thy childishness will move him more","Than can our reasons.—There's no man in the world","More bound to's mother; yet here he lets me prate","Like one i' the stocks. Thou hast never in thy life","Show'd thy dear mother any courtesy;","When she,—poor hen,—fond of no second brood,","Has cluck'd thee to the wars, and safely home,","Loaden with honour. Say my request's unjust,","And spurn me back: but if it be not so,","Thou art not honest; and the gods will plague thee,","That thou restrain'st from me the duty which","To a mother's part belongs.—He turns away:","Down, ladies: let us shame him with our knees.","To his surname Coriolanus 'longs more pride","Than pity to our prayers. Down: an end;","This is the last.—So we will home to Rome,","And die among our neighbours.—Nay, behold's:","This boy, that cannot tell what he would have","But kneels and holds up hands for fellowship,","Does reason our petition with more strength","Than thou hast to deny't.—Come, let us go:","This fellow had a Volscian to his mother;","His wife is in Corioli, and his child","Like him by chance.—Yet give us our despatch:","I am hush'd until our city be afire,","And then I'll speak a little."],"id":1252,"m":"No, don't leave us like this. If our request were meant to save the Romans by destroying the Volscians you serve, you could condemn us as poisoners of your honour. No; our plea is that you reconcile them, so that the Volscians can say, \"We showed this mercy\", and the Romans, \"We received it\", and both sides hail you and cry, \"Bless you for making this peace!\" You know, great son, the outcome of war is uncertain; but this is certain: if you conquer Rome, the reward you'll reap is a name that will be followed by curses whenever it's repeated, whose history will read: \"The man was noble, but with his last act he wiped it out, destroyed his country, and his name is hated by the generations that followed.\" Speak to me, son. You've aimed at the finest heights of honour, to imitate the graces of the gods, to tear the wide cheeks of the sky with thunder, and yet to load your lightning with a bolt that would only split an oak. Why don't you speak? Do you think it's honourable for a noble man to keep remembering wrongs? Daughter, you speak; he doesn't care about your crying. You speak, boy; maybe your childishness will move him more than our reasons can. There's no man in the world who owes more to his mother, yet here he lets me prattle like someone in the stocks. You've never in your life shown your dear mother any kindness, while she, poor hen, wanting no second brood, has clucked you off to the wars and safely home again, loaded with honour. Say my request is unjust and kick me away; but if it isn't, you're not honest, and the gods will punish you for keeping from me the duty owed to a mother. He turns away. Down, ladies; let's shame him with our knees. His surname Coriolanus has more pride in it than pity for our prayers. Down; let's finish. This is the last. Then we'll go home to Rome and die among our neighbours. No, look at us. This boy, who can't say what he wants but kneels and holds up his hands with us, argues our case with more strength than you have to refuse it. Come, let's go. This fellow had a Volscian for a mother; his wife is in Corioli, and his child looks like him by chance. Still, send us away with an answer. I'll be silent until our city is on fire, and then I'll say a little."},{"t":"sd","text":"After holding VOLUMNIA by the hands, in silence.","id":1254},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","cont":1,"lines":["O mother, mother!","What have you done? Behold, the heavens do ope,","The gods look down, and this unnatural scene","They laugh at. O my mother, mother! O!","You have won a happy victory to Rome;","But for your son,—believe it, O, believe it,","Most dangerously you have with him prevail'd,","If not most mortal to him. But let it come.—","Aufidius, though I cannot make true wars,","I'll frame convenient peace. Now, good Aufidius,","Were you in my stead, would you have heard","A mother less? or granted less, Aufidius?"],"id":1255,"m":"Oh mother, mother! What have you done? Look, the heavens open, the gods look down and laugh at this unnatural scene. Oh my mother, mother! Oh! You've won a happy victory for Rome; but for your son, believe it, oh believe it, you've beaten him in a way that's most dangerous to him, if not deadly. But let it come. Aufidius, though I can't fight this war properly, I'll make a suitable peace. Now, good Aufidius, if you were in my place, would you have listened to a mother less? Or granted less, Aufidius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I was mov'd withal."],"id":1256,"m":"I was moved by it too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["I dare be sworn you were:","And, sir, it is no little thing to make","Mine eyes to sweat compassion. But, good sir,","What peace you'll make, advise me: for my part,","I'll not to Rome, I'll back with you; and, pray you","Stand to me in this cause.—O mother! wife!"],"id":1257,"m":"I'd swear you were. And, sir, it's no small thing to make my eyes sweat with pity. But, good sir, advise me what peace you'll make. For my part, I won't go to Rome; I'll go back with you. And please, support me in this. Oh mother! Wife!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["[Aside] I am glad thou hast set thy mercy and thy honour","At difference in thee; out of that I'll work","Myself a former fortune."],"id":1258,"m":"(Aside) I'm glad you've set your mercy and your honour against each other. Out of that I'll make myself the fortune I had before."},{"t":"sd","text":"The Ladies make signs to CORIOLANUS.","id":1259},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["[To VOLUMNIA, VIRGILIA, &c] Ay, by and by;","But we'll drink together; and you shall bear","A better witness back than words, which we,","On like conditions, will have counter-seal'd.","Come, enter with us. Ladies, you deserve","To have a temple built you: all the swords","In Italy, and her confederate arms,","Could not have made this peace."],"id":1260,"m":"(To Volumnia, Virgilia and the others) Yes, soon; but first we'll drink together, and you'll carry back a better proof than words, a treaty that we'll have signed on the same terms. Come in with us. Ladies, you deserve to have a temple built for you. All the swords in Italy and its allied armies couldn't have made this peace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1261},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Rome. A public place.","id":1262},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter MENENIUS and SICINIUS.","id":1263},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["See you yond coign o' the Capitol,—yond corner-stone?"],"id":1264,"m":"Do you see that corner of the Capitol, that cornerstone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Why, what of that?"],"id":1265,"m":"Why, what about it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["If it be possible for you to displace it with your little","finger, there is some hope the ladies of Rome, especially his","mother, may prevail with him. But I say there is no hope in't:","our throats are sentenced, and stay upon execution."],"id":1266,"m":"If you can shift it with your little finger, there's some hope the ladies of Rome, especially his mother, may win him over. But I say there's no hope in it. Our throats are sentenced and only waiting for the execution."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Is't possible that so short a time can alter the condition of a","man?"],"id":1267,"m":"Is it possible that so short a time can change a man's nature?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["There is differency between a grub and a butterfly; yet your","butterfly was a grub. This Marcius is grown from man to dragon;","he has wings; he's more than a creeping thing."],"id":1268,"m":"There's a difference between a grub and a butterfly, yet your butterfly was once a grub. This Marcius has grown from a man into a dragon. He has wings; he's more than a crawling thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["He loved his mother dearly."],"id":1269,"m":"He loved his mother dearly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["So did he me: and he no more remembers his mother now than an","eight-year-old horse. The tartness of his face sours ripe grapes:","when he walks, he moves like an engine, and the ground shrinks","before his treading: he is able to pierce a corslet with his eye,","talks like a knell, and his hum is a battery. He sits in his","state as a thing made for Alexander. What he bids be done is","finished with his bidding. He wants nothing of a god but","eternity, and a heaven to throne in."],"id":1270,"m":"So he did me; and he remembers his mother now no more than an eight-year-old horse does. The sourness of his face turns ripe grapes sour. When he walks, he moves like a siege engine, and the ground shrinks under his tread. He can pierce armour with his look, he talks like a funeral bell, and his \"hm\" is a cannon blast. He sits in state like a statue made of Alexander the Great. What he orders is done as soon as he orders it. He lacks nothing of a god except eternity and a heaven to sit enthroned in."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Yes, mercy, if you report him truly."],"id":1271,"m":"Yes, and mercy, if you describe him truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["I paint him in the character. Mark what mercy his mother shall","bring from him. There is no more mercy in him than there is","milk in a male tiger; that shall our poor city find: and all this","is 'long of you."],"id":1272,"m":"I paint him true to life. Watch what mercy his mother will bring back from him. There's no more mercy in him than there's milk in a male tiger. Our poor city will find that out, and it's all because of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["The gods be good unto us!"],"id":1273,"m":"The gods be good to us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["No, in such a case the gods will not be good unto us. When we","banished him we respected not them; and, he returning to break","our necks, they respect not us."],"id":1274,"m":"No, in a case like this the gods won't be good to us. When we banished him we didn't respect them; and now he's coming back to break our necks, they don't respect us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a MESSENGER.","id":1275},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Sir, if you'd save your life, fly to your house:","The plebeians have got your fellow-tribune","And hale him up and down; all swearing, if","The Roman ladies bring not comfort home","They'll give him death by inches."],"id":1276,"m":"Sir, if you want to save your life, run to your house. The plebeians have caught your fellow tribune and are dragging him up and down, all swearing that if the Roman ladies don't bring home good news, they'll kill him inch by inch."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a second MESSENGER.","id":1277},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["What's the news?"],"id":1278,"m":"What's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MESSENGER","lines":["Good news, good news;—the ladies have prevail'd,","The Volscians are dislodg'd, and Marcius gone:","A merrier day did never yet greet Rome,","No, not the expulsion of the Tarquins."],"id":1279,"m":"Good news, good news! The ladies have won. The Volscians have broken camp, and Marcius has gone. A happier day never dawned on Rome, no, not even the expulsion of the Tarquins."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["Friend,","Art thou certain this is true? is't most certain?"],"id":1280,"m":"Friend, are you sure this is true? Absolutely sure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MESSENGER","lines":["As certain as I know the sun is fire:","Where have you lurk'd, that you make doubt of it?","Ne'er through an arch so hurried the blown tide","As the recomforted through the gates. Why, hark you!"],"id":1281,"m":"As sure as I know the sun is fire. Where have you been hiding that you doubt it? The swollen tide never rushed through an arch as fast as the comforted people are rushing through the gates. Why, listen!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets and hautboys sounded, drums beaten, and shouting within.","id":1282},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MESSENGER","cont":1,"lines":["The trumpets, sackbuts, psalteries, and fifes,","Tabors and cymbals, and the shouting Romans,","Make the sun dance. Hark you!"],"id":1283,"m":"The trumpets, trombones, harps and fifes, drums and cymbals, and the shouting Romans make the sun dance. Listen!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Shouting within.","id":1284},{"t":"sp","who":"MENENIUS","lines":["This is good news.","I will go meet the ladies. This Volumnia","Is worth of consuls, senators, patricians,","A city full: of tribunes such as you,","A sea and land full. You have pray'd well to-day:","This morning for ten thousand of your throats","I'd not have given a doit. Hark, how they joy!"],"id":1285,"m":"This is good news. I'll go and meet the ladies. This Volumnia is worth a city full of consuls, senators and patricians, and a whole sea and land full of tribunes like you. You've prayed well today. This morning I wouldn't have given a penny for ten thousand of your throats. Listen to how happy they are!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Shouting and music.","id":1286},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["First, the gods bless you for your tidings; next,","Accept my thankfulness."],"id":1287,"m":"First, the gods bless you for your news; next, accept my thanks."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND MESSENGER","lines":["Sir, we have all","Great cause to give great thanks."],"id":1288,"m":"Sir, we all have great reason to give great thanks."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["They are near the city?"],"id":1289,"m":"Are they near the city?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Almost at point to enter."],"id":1290,"m":"Almost about to enter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICINIUS","lines":["We'll meet them,","And help the joy."],"id":1291,"m":"We'll go and meet them and add to the joy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1292},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Rome. A street near the gate.","id":1293},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter VOLUMNIA, VIRGILIA, VALERIA, &c., accompanied by Senators, Patricians, and Citizens.","id":1294},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Behold our patroness, the life of Rome!","Call all your tribes together, praise the gods,","And make triumphant fires; strew flowers before them:","Unshout the noise that banish'd Marcius,","Repeal him with the welcome of his mother;","Cry, 'Welcome, ladies, welcome!'—"],"id":1295,"m":"Look at our protector, the life of Rome! Call all your tribes together, praise the gods and light bonfires of triumph; scatter flowers in front of them. Unshout the noise that banished Marcius; call him back by welcoming his mother. Cry, \"Welcome, ladies, welcome!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Welcome, ladies,","Welcome!"],"id":1296,"m":"Welcome, ladies, welcome!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1297},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VI","loc":"Antium. A public place.","id":1298},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TULLUS AUFIDIUS, with attendants.","id":1299},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Go tell the lords o' the city I am here:","Deliver them this paper; having read it,","Bid them repair to the market-place: where I,","Even in theirs and in the commons' ears,","Will vouch the truth of it. Him I accuse","The city ports by this hath enter'd and","Intends t' appear before the people, hoping","To purge himself with words: despatch."],"id":1300,"m":"Go and tell the lords of the city I'm here. Give them this paper, and when they've read it, tell them to come to the market square, where I'll swear to its truth in their hearing and the common people's. The man I accuse has entered the city gates by now and means to appear before the people, hoping to clear himself with words. Hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt attendants.","id":1301},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter three or four CONSPIRATORS of AUFIDIUS' faction.","id":1302},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Most welcome!"],"id":1303,"m":"You're very welcome!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CONSPIRATOR","lines":["How is it with our general?"],"id":1304,"m":"How are things with our general?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Even so","As with a man by his own alms empoison'd,","And with his charity slain."],"id":1305,"m":"As with a man poisoned by his own charity and killed by his own kindness."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CONSPIRATOR","lines":["Most noble sir,","If you do hold the same intent wherein","You wish'd us parties, we'll deliver you","Of your great danger."],"id":1306,"m":"Most noble sir, if you still have the plan you wanted us to join in, we'll free you from your great danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Sir, I cannot tell:","We must proceed as we do find the people."],"id":1307,"m":"Sir, I can't say. We must act according to how we find the people."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CONSPIRATOR","lines":["The people will remain uncertain whilst","'Twixt you there's difference: but the fall of either","Makes the survivor heir of all."],"id":1308,"m":"The people will stay undecided while there's a quarrel between you; but the fall of either one makes the survivor heir to everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I know it;","And my pretext to strike at him admits","A good construction. I rais'd him, and I pawn'd","Mine honour for his truth: who being so heighten'd,","He water'd his new plants with dews of flattery,","Seducing so my friends; and to this end","He bow'd his nature, never known before","But to be rough, unswayable, and free."],"id":1309,"m":"I know it. And my excuse for striking at him can be presented well. I raised him up, and I pledged my honour for his loyalty. Raised so high, he watered his new plants with the dew of flattery, winning over my friends that way; and to do it he bent his nature, which had never been known to be anything but rough, unbending and outspoken."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CONSPIRATOR","lines":["Sir, his stoutness","When he did stand for consul, which he lost","By lack of stooping,—"],"id":1310,"m":"Sir, his stubbornness when he stood for consul, which he lost because he wouldn't stoop—"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["That I would have spoken of:","Being banish'd for't, he came unto my hearth;","Presented to my knife his throat: I took him;","Made him joint-servant with me; gave him way","In all his own desires; nay, let him choose","Out of my files, his projects to accomplish,","My best and freshest men; serv'd his designments","In mine own person; holp to reap the fame","Which he made all his; and took some pride","To do myself this wrong: till, at the last,","I seem'd his follower, not partner; and","He wag'd me with his countenance as if","I had been mercenary."],"id":1311,"m":"I was going to mention that. Banished for it, he came to my hearth and offered his throat to my knife. I took him in, made him my joint commander, let him have his way in everything he wanted; even let him choose my best and freshest troops from my ranks to carry out his plans. I served his schemes in person, helped to reap the fame he made all his own, and took a sort of pride in doing myself this wrong; until in the end I seemed his follower, not his partner, and he paid me with his patronizing looks as if I'd been a hired soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CONSPIRATOR","lines":["So he did, my lord:","The army marvell'd at it; and, in the last,","When he had carried Rome, and that we look'd","For no less spoil than glory,—"],"id":1312,"m":"So he did, my lord. The army was amazed at it; and in the end, when he could have taken Rome, and we expected no less loot than glory—"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["There was it;—","For which my sinews shall be stretch'd upon him.","At a few drops of women's rheum, which are","As cheap as lies, he sold the blood and labour","Of our great action: therefore shall he die,","And I'll renew me in his fall. But, hark!"],"id":1313,"m":"That was it, the thing for which I'll strain every muscle against him. For a few drops of women's tears, which are as cheap as lies, he sold the blood and toil of our great campaign. So he shall die, and I'll be renewed by his fall. But listen!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Drums and trumpets sound, with great shouts of the people.","id":1314},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CONSPIRATOR","lines":["Your native town you enter'd like a post,","And had no welcomes home; but he returns","Splitting the air with noise."],"id":1315,"m":"You entered your own home town like a messenger, with no welcome home; but he comes back splitting the air with noise."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CONSPIRATOR","lines":["And patient fools,","Whose children he hath slain, their base throats tear","With giving him glory."],"id":1316,"m":"And the long-suffering fools whose children he killed tear their common throats cheering him."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CONSPIRATOR","lines":["Therefore, at your vantage,","Ere he express himself or move the people","With what he would say, let him feel your sword,","Which we will second. When he lies along,","After your way his tale pronounc'd shall bury","His reasons with his body."],"id":1317,"m":"So, at your best moment, before he explains himself or moves the people with what he'd say, let him feel your sword, and we'll back you up. When he's lying flat, your version of his story will bury his reasons along with his body."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Say no more:","Here come the lords."],"id":1318,"m":"Say no more. Here come the lords."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the LORDS of the city.","id":1319},{"t":"sp","who":"LORDS","lines":["You are most welcome home."],"id":1320,"m":"You're very welcome home."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["I have not deserv'd it.","But, worthy lords, have you with heed perus'd","What I have written to you?"],"id":1321,"m":"I haven't deserved it. But, worthy lords, have you carefully read what I wrote to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORDS","lines":["We have."],"id":1322,"m":"We have."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["And grieve to hear't.","What faults he made before the last, I think","Might have found easy fines: but there to end","Where he was to begin, and give away","The benefit of our levies, answering us","With our own charge: making a treaty where","There was a yielding.—This admits no excuse."],"id":1323,"m":"And we're sorry to hear it. The faults he committed before this last one could, I think, have been let off with small fines; but to stop where he should have started, to give away the profit of our war and repay us with nothing but our own costs, to make a treaty where there was a surrender— this can't be excused."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["He approaches: you shall hear him."],"id":1324,"m":"He's coming. You'll hear him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CORIOLANUS, with drum and colours; a crowd of Citizens with him.","id":1325},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Hail, lords! I am return'd your soldier;","No more infected with my country's love","Than when I parted hence, but still subsisting","Under your great command. You are to know","That prosperously I have attempted, and","With bloody passage led your wars even to","The gates of Rome. Our spoils we have brought home","Do more than counterpoise a full third part","The charges of the action. We have made peace","With no less honour to the Antiates","Than shame to the Romans: and we here deliver,","Subscribed by the consuls and patricians,","Together with the seal o' the senate, what","We have compounded on."],"id":1326,"m":"Greetings, lords! I've come back your soldier, no more infected with love of my country than when I left here, but still serving under your great command. You should know that I've succeeded, and led your wars by a bloody path right up to the gates of Rome. The loot we've brought home more than pays a full third of the cost of the campaign. We've made a peace that brings as much honour to the people of Antium as shame to the Romans; and here we hand over, signed by the consuls and patricians and with the senate's seal, the terms we've agreed."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Read it not, noble lords;","But tell the traitor, in the highest degree","He hath abus'd your powers."],"id":1327,"m":"Don't read it, noble lords. Tell the traitor he has abused your power to the highest degree."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Traitor!—How now?"],"id":1328,"m":"Traitor! What's this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Ay, traitor, Marcius."],"id":1329,"m":"Yes, traitor, Marcius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Marcius!"],"id":1330,"m":"Marcius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Ay, Marcius, Caius Marcius! Dost thou think","I'll grace thee with that robbery, thy stol'n name","Coriolanus, in Corioli?—","You lords and heads o' the state, perfidiously","He has betray'd your business, and given up,","For certain drops of salt, your city Rome,—","I say your city,—to his wife and mother;","Breaking his oath and resolution, like","A twist of rotten silk; never admitting","Counsel o' the war; but at his nurse's tears","He whin'd and roar'd away your victory;","That pages blush'd at him, and men of heart","Look'd wondering each at others."],"id":1331,"m":"Yes, Marcius, Caius Marcius! Do you think I'll honour you with that theft, your stolen name Coriolanus, here in Corioli? You lords and leaders of the state, he has treacherously betrayed your cause and given up your city Rome, I say your city, for a few salty drops, to his wife and mother, breaking his oath and his resolve like a strand of rotten silk, never asking the war council's advice. At his nurse's tears he whined and bawled your victory away, so that pageboys blushed at him and brave men looked at each other in amazement."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Hear'st thou, Mars?"],"id":1332,"m":"Do you hear this, Mars?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Name not the god, thou boy of tears,—"],"id":1333,"m":"Don't name the god, you boy of tears—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Ha!"],"id":1334,"m":"Ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["No more."],"id":1335,"m":"No more."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Measureless liar, thou hast made my heart","Too great for what contains it. Boy! O slave!—","Pardon me, lords, 'tis the first time that ever","I was forc'd to scold. Your judgments, my grave lords,","Must give this cur the lie: and his own notion,—","Who wears my stripes impress'd upon him; that must bear","My beating to his grave,—shall join to thrust","The lie unto him."],"id":1336,"m":"Boundless liar, you've made my heart too big for the body that holds it. Boy! Oh, slave! Forgive me, lords; it's the first time I've ever been forced to brawl with words. Your judgment, my serious lords, must call this cur a liar; and his own memory, since he carries my stripes printed on him and will carry my beating to his grave, will join in throwing the lie back at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Peace, both, and hear me speak."],"id":1337,"m":"Quiet, both of you, and hear me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["Cut me to pieces, Volsces; men and lads,","Stain all your edges on me.—Boy! False hound!","If you have writ your annals true, 'tis there,","That, like an eagle in a dove-cote, I","Flutter'd your Volscians in Corioli:","Alone I did it.—Boy!"],"id":1338,"m":"Cut me to pieces, Volscians; men and boys, stain all your blades on me. Boy! Lying dog! If you've written your history truthfully, it's recorded there that, like an eagle in a dovecote, I scattered your Volscians in Corioli. I did it alone. Boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Why, noble lords,","Will you be put in mind of his blind fortune,","Which was your shame, by this unholy braggart,","'Fore your own eyes and ears?"],"id":1339,"m":"Why, noble lords, will you let this godless braggart remind you of his blind luck, which was your shame, in front of your own eyes and ears?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSPIRATORS","lines":["Let him die for't."],"id":1340,"m":"Let him die for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Tear him to pieces, do it presently:—he killed my son; my","daughter; he killed my cousin Marcus; he killed my father,—"],"id":1341,"m":"Tear him to pieces, do it now! He killed my son! My daughter! He killed my cousin Marcus! He killed my father!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Peace, ho!—no outrage;—peace!","The man is noble, and his fame folds in","This orb o' the earth. His last offences to us","Shall have judicious hearing.—Stand, Aufidius,","And trouble not the peace."],"id":1342,"m":"Quiet, there! No violence! Quiet! The man is noble, and his fame wraps around the whole world. His latest offences against us will have a proper hearing. Stand back, Aufidius, and don't disturb the peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORIOLANUS","lines":["O that I had him,","With six Aufidiuses, or more, his tribe,","To use my lawful sword!"],"id":1343,"m":"Oh, if only I had him, and six Aufidiuses or more, his whole tribe, to use my lawful sword on!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["Insolent villain!"],"id":1344,"m":"Insolent villain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONSPIRATORS","lines":["Kill, kill, kill, kill, kill him!"],"id":1345,"m":"Kill, kill, kill, kill, kill him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"AUFIDIUS and the CONSPIRATORS draw, and kill CORIOLANUS, who falls. AUFIDIUS stands on him.","id":1346},{"t":"sp","who":"LORDS","lines":["Hold, hold, hold, hold!"],"id":1347,"m":"Stop, stop, stop, stop!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["My noble masters, hear me speak."],"id":1348,"m":"My noble masters, let me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["O Tullus,—"],"id":1349,"m":"Oh Tullus—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Thou hast done a deed whereat valour will weep."],"id":1350,"m":"You've done a deed that will make courage weep."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["Tread not upon him.—Masters all, be quiet;","Put up your swords."],"id":1351,"m":"Don't stand on him. Everyone, be quiet. Put away your swords."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["My lords, when you shall know,—as in this rage,","Provok'd by him, you cannot,—the great danger","Which this man's life did owe you, you'll rejoice","That he is thus cut off. Please it your honours","To call me to your senate, I'll deliver","Myself your loyal servant, or endure","Your heaviest censure."],"id":1352,"m":"My lords, when you know (as you can't in this rage he provoked) the great danger this man's life held for you, you'll be glad he has been cut off like this. If your honours will summon me to your senate, I'll show myself your loyal servant or accept your harshest sentence."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Bear from hence his body,","And mourn you for him. Let him be regarded","As the most noble corse that ever herald","Did follow to his urn."],"id":1353,"m":"Carry his body away from here, and mourn for him. Let him be honoured as the noblest corpse any herald ever followed to its urn."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["His own impatience","Takes from Aufidius a great part of blame.","Let's make the best of it."],"id":1354,"m":"His own impatience takes a great part of the blame off Aufidius. Let's make the best of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUFIDIUS","lines":["My rage is gone;","And I am struck with sorrow.—Take him up:—","Help, three o' the chiefest soldiers; I'll be one.—","Beat thou the drum, that it speak mournfully;","Trail your steel pikes. Though in this city he","Hath widow'd and unchilded many a one,","Which to this hour bewail the injury,","Yet he shall have a noble memory.—","Assist."],"id":1355,"m":"My rage is gone, and I'm struck with sorrow. Lift him up. Help, three of the leading soldiers; I'll be the fourth. Beat the drum so that it sounds mournful. Trail your steel pikes. Though in this city he has made many widows and childless parents, who still grieve over the injury today, he will have a noble memorial. Help me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt, bearing the body of CORIOLANUS. A dead march sounded.","id":1356}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Caius Marcius Coriolanus","group":"Romans","desc":"Rome's greatest soldier, a patrician raised by his mother to value honour above life. He despises the common people and says so, even while they starve. After taking Corioli almost single-handed he is given the surname Coriolanus and nominated consul, but he cannot bear to beg for the people's votes, and the tribunes provoke him into an outburst that gets him banished. He joins his old enemy Aufidius and marches on Rome. Only his mother's plea makes him spare the city, and the Volscians kill him for it."},{"name":"Volumnia","group":"Romans","desc":"Coriolanus's mother, a Roman matron who rejoices in her son's wounds and says she would rather lose eleven sons nobly than see one live idly. She shaped his courage and his pride. In Act 3 she tells him to act humble for policy's sake; in Act 5 she kneels before him outside Rome and talks him out of destroying it, winning Rome's gratitude and, as he sees, his death."},{"name":"Menenius Agrippa","group":"Romans","desc":"An old patrician, witty, fond of wine, and popular with the people. He calms the rioting citizens with the fable of the belly, mocks the tribunes, and loves Coriolanus like a son. He tries again and again to patch things up, and when Rome is threatened he goes to plead with Coriolanus, who sends him away."},{"name":"Tullus Aufidius","group":"Volscians","desc":"General of the Volscians and Coriolanus's great rival, beaten by him five (later he says twelve) times. He swears to get him by fair means or foul. When the banished Coriolanus arrives at his house he embraces him and shares his command, but grows jealous of his fame. After Coriolanus spares Rome, Aufidius calls him a traitor and a 'boy of tears', and his conspirators kill him."},{"name":"Sicinius Velutus","group":"Tribunes","desc":"One of the two tribunes of the people, newly created at the start of the play. With Brutus he sees Coriolanus as a threat to the people's power, persuades the citizens to withdraw their votes, provokes Coriolanus to rage, and pronounces his banishment. When Coriolanus returns with an army, Sicinius is left helpless and frightened."},{"name":"Junius Brutus","group":"Tribunes","desc":"The other tribune, Sicinius's partner in every move. He plans how to turn the people against Coriolanus, coaches them on what to say, and makes sure they will shout 'It shall be so' on cue. The plebeians later drag him through the streets when it seems Rome will fall."},{"name":"Cominius","group":"Romans","desc":"Consul and general in the war against the Volscians. He gives Coriolanus his new name, delivers the great speech praising his deeds to the Senate, and stands by him at his banishment. He goes first to plead with him outside Rome and is ignored."},{"name":"Virgilia","group":"Romans","desc":"Coriolanus's wife, quiet and anxious, the opposite of Volumnia. She will not leave the house while he is at war and weeps at the thought of his blood. He calls her 'my gracious silence'. She turns on the tribunes after his banishment and kneels with Volumnia outside Rome."},{"name":"Valeria","group":"Romans","desc":"A noble Roman lady, friend of the family, described by Coriolanus as 'the moon of Rome'. She brings news of the war, tells the story of young Marcius tearing a butterfly, and joins the women's embassy to the Volscian camp."},{"name":"Titus Lartius","group":"Romans","desc":"A Roman general who fights beside Marcius at Corioli, holds the captured town, and later reports Aufidius's hatred. Old but eager, he says he would fight on a crutch rather than stay at home."},{"name":"Young Marcius","group":"Romans","desc":"Coriolanus's small son, who is said to chase and tear a butterfly like his father. He kneels with his mother and grandmother in Act 5 and says he will run away until he is bigger, then fight."},{"name":"The Citizens","group":"The people","desc":"The plebeians of Rome, starving at the start and ready to kill Marcius for the price of grain. They are not stupid: the First Citizen argues sharply, the Third Citizen jokes about their many-headed wits. They give Coriolanus their votes, take them back when the tribunes stir them, banish him, and regret it when he returns with an army."},{"name":"Volscian Senators, Lords and Conspirators","group":"Volscians","desc":"The rulers of Corioli and Antium, who welcome Coriolanus as a weapon against Rome. Aufidius's conspirators urge him to kill Coriolanus before he can win the people of Antium over, and the lords mourn him after."},{"name":"Adrian and Nicanor","group":"Volscians","desc":"A Volscian and a Roman spy who meet on the road in 4.3. Their chat shows that Rome is divided and that the Volscians are ready to strike."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Starving citizens of Rome gather to kill Caius Marcius, whom they call the chief enemy of the people, so they can get grain at their own price. Menenius Agrippa calms them with the fable of the belly: the senators are the stomach that feeds the whole body. Marcius arrives and pours contempt on them, reporting that another crowd has been granted five tribunes. News comes that the Volscians are in arms under Tullus Aufidius, Marcius's rival, and Marcius gladly agrees to serve under Cominius. The new tribunes, Sicinius and Brutus, remark on his pride, and Brutus notes that as second in command he will escape blame for failure but take the credit for success.","key":["The play opens with a hunger riot: politics in Rome is about bread and who controls it.","Menenius's fable presents the state as a body with the patricians at its centre; the citizens argue back.","Marcius's first speech is a torrent of insults, and his admiration for Aufidius shows he cares only about worthy enemies."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do the citizens want to kill Marcius at the start?","o":["He has murdered a tribune of the people in the street","They see him as the chief enemy of the people while they starve","He has sided with the Volscians","He has refused to fight"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Menenius compare the senators to in his fable?","o":["The head","The heart","The belly","The hands"],"a":2},{"q":"What have the other rioters been granted?","o":["Free grain from the state stores for a year","Five tribunes to speak for them","Marcius's banishment","Seats in the Senate"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Second Citizen say in Marcius's favour?","o":["He has given grain to the poor in secret","He has done great service for his country","He has always spoken kindly to the people","He has refused to fight in any foreign war"],"a":1},{"q":"Why do the tribunes say Marcius is happy to serve under Cominius?","o":["He is too young to lead an army of his own yet","Cominius is his oldest and dearest friend in Rome","Any failure will fall on Cominius, any fame on him","He wants to learn the art of war from Cominius"],"a":2}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"In Corioli, Aufidius tells the Volscian senators that Rome has learned of their plans and is raising an army under Cominius, Marcius and Titus Lartius. He goes to lead the army in the field while the senators guard the city, vowing that if he meets Marcius they will fight until one can fight no more.","key":["The Volscians are shown as serious rivals, with their own politics and spies.","Aufidius's vow to fight Marcius to the end sets up their personal rivalry."],"quiz":[{"q":"What worries Aufidius in this scene?","o":["His army has mutinied and gone back to Antium","Rome knows the Volscians' plans","The senators of Corioli distrust him","Marcius is dead"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Aufidius sworn about Marcius?","o":["To make peace with him if Rome will treat with the Volscians","To strike until one of them can do no more","To capture him alive and parade him through the streets of Antium","To avoid him in battle at all costs"],"a":1},{"q":"Who stays to guard Corioli?","o":["Aufidius","The Volscian senators","Titus Lartius","The tribunes"],"a":1},{"q":"How has news of the Roman plans reached Corioli?","o":["A Roman prisoner has confessed","Aufidius has letters from Rome","Spies saw the army on the march","Marcius sent a challenge to them"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the senators tell Aufidius to do?","o":["Wait in Corioli until the Romans arrive","Make peace with Rome before it is too late","Take the field while they hold the city","Send a message to Marcius to fight alone"],"a":2}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"At home in Rome, Volumnia and Virgilia sew. Volumnia boasts that she sent her son to war young and rejoiced when he came back a man; she would rather lose eleven sons nobly than have one live in idle pleasure. Virgilia fears for him. Valeria visits, tells how little Marcius tore a butterfly with his teeth, and tries to get Virgilia to go out. Virgilia refuses to leave the house until her husband returns.","key":["Volumnia shows where Marcius's values come from: honour, blood and wounds are beautiful to her.","Virgilia's quiet fear is a contrast to Volumnia's warlike pride.","The butterfly story hints that young Marcius is being shaped like his father."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Volumnia say she would prefer to having one son live idly?","o":["To have no sons at all rather than one who lives idly","To have eleven sons die nobly for their country","To be a soldier herself and fight beside him","To leave Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"What did young Marcius do to the butterfly?","o":["Set it free in the garden","Painted it in bright colours","Tore it with his teeth","Gave it to his mother"],"a":2},{"q":"Why will Virgilia not leave the house?","o":["She is ill with a fever and cannot walk as far as Valeria's","She has vowed to stay in until her husband returns","Volumnia forbids her to go out of the house without her","She dislikes Valeria, her gossip and her idle visits"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Volumnia say she sent Marcius to war so young?","o":["To get him out of the house and away from her","Because his father had ordered it before his death","To win honour, which she valued above all","Because the Senate needed every young man in Rome"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Volumnia say about blood and a man?","o":["It is a sign of weakness and shame in battle","It is something that only cowards fear to see","It becomes a man more than gilt his trophy","It should be washed off before coming home"],"a":2}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"Before Corioli, Marcius and Lartius bet a horse on whether the armies have met. The Volscians sally out and drive the Romans back to their trenches. Marcius curses his men as cowards, rallies them and chases the enemy to the gates, then goes in alone; the gates shut behind him. Lartius mourns him as dead, but Marcius reappears bleeding and fighting, and the Romans rush in to rescue him.","key":["Marcius's courage is superhuman: he enters the enemy city alone.","His contempt for the common soldiers shows on the battlefield as well as in Rome.","Lartius's lament praises him as a soldier 'even to Cato's wish'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do Marcius and Lartius bet?","o":["A sword","A horse","A house","A crown"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens when Marcius follows the Volscians to the gates?","o":["He is captured","He is shut inside the city alone","He makes peace","He is wounded and retreats"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Marcius treat his retreating soldiers?","o":["He forgives them and leads them back in","He curses them as souls of geese","He pays them","He sends them home"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Marcius call his retreating soldiers?","o":["You brave men who have done all that you could","You souls of geese that bear the shapes of men","You cowards who should be sent back to Rome","You fools who did not listen to my orders"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Lartius say of Marcius when the gates shut?","o":["He is safe, he will soon return","He is a fool, he should not have gone","He is lost, a carbuncle entire","He is a traitor, he has joined them"],"a":2}]},"a1s5":{"sum":"Inside the captured city, Roman soldiers loot cheap goods. Marcius scorns them, leaves Lartius to hold Corioli, and, though bleeding, hurries off to fight Aufidius beside Cominius.","key":["Marcius despises looters: he fights for honour, not plunder.","He refuses to rest despite his wounds."],"quiz":[{"q":"What are the soldiers doing when Marcius arrives?","o":["Burying their dead in the ditch","Looting worthless goods","Fighting Aufidius's guard","Praying"],"a":1},{"q":"Who stays to hold Corioli?","o":["Cominius, the general","Titus Lartius","Menenius Agrippa","Marcius himself"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Marcius go next?","o":["Back to Rome","To help Cominius against Aufidius","To the Volscian senate","To a surgeon"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Marcius say about the looters?","o":["They would trade honour for cheap trash","They have earned the goods by their fighting","They should be paid in gold, not in goods","They are tired and deserve to take a rest"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Lartius urge Marcius to do about his wounds?","o":["Show them to the Senate","Rest and have them seen to","Bind them with his scarf","Ignore them and fight on"],"a":1}]},"a1s6":{"sum":"Cominius's army has retreated. A messenger reports that the Romans at Corioli were beaten back, but Marcius arrives covered in blood with news of victory. He begs to be set against Aufidius and calls for volunteers; the soldiers shout, lift him in their arms and wave their swords. He chooses his men and they march.","key":["The soldiers turn Marcius into a living weapon: 'Make you a sword of me?'","His appearance 'as he were flay'd' makes blood his badge."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Marcius look when he reaches Cominius?","o":["Clean and calm, as if he had not fought at all that day","As if he had been skinned, covered in blood","Disguised as a Volscian","Wounded and fainting"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Marcius ask Cominius?","o":["To rest before the next attack on the enemy","To be set directly against Aufidius","To be made consul","To go home"],"a":1},{"q":"How do the soldiers respond to his call?","o":["They refuse to follow him into battle","They lift him up in their arms","They desert to the Volscians","They mock him"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Cominius at first not recognise Marcius?","o":["He wears Volscian armour","He has lost his helmet","He arrives from the wrong side","He is so covered in blood"],"a":3},{"q":"What question does Marcius ask about the enemy?","o":["Where Aufidius and the Antiates stand","How many men the Volscians have left","Whether Corioli has been taken yet","Who leads the Volscian cavalry today"],"a":0}]},"a1s7":{"sum":"Titus Lartius sets a guard on the gates of Corioli and leaves to join Cominius and Marcius.","key":["A short scene that keeps the military plot moving."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lartius order before he leaves Corioli?","o":["The city to be burned","The gates to be guarded","The prisoners freed","A feast"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Lartius going?","o":["Back to Rome with the prisoners","To the Roman camp","To Antium","To the Senate"],"a":1},{"q":"What does he say if they lose the field?","o":["They will win anyway","They cannot keep the town","They will surrender","They will retreat to Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"How many men does Lartius leave to guard the gates?","o":["None, he takes all of them","Only his own small company","Enough to hold the town","Half of the whole Roman army"],"a":2},{"q":"Who does Lartius ask to guide him?","o":["A Volscian prisoner who knows the hills","A scout who has seen Aufidius's army","A messenger who came from Cominius","A guide to lead him to the Roman camp"],"a":3}]},"a1s8":{"sum":"Marcius and Aufidius meet and fight. Aufidius declares he would not let him escape if he were Hector himself. Some Volscians come to help Aufidius, and he angrily says they have shamed him; Marcius drives them all off.","key":["The two rivals finally meet in single combat.","Aufidius is humiliated by his own men's help, which feeds his later bitterness."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does Aufidius say Marcius could be and still not escape?","o":["Achilles","Hector","Hercules","Mars"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Aufidius angry with the Volscians who help him?","o":["They are late to the fight","Their help shames him","They kill Marcius","They run away"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Marcius say about the blood on him?","o":["It is his own","It is not his own","It is from the looters","It is paint"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Marcius call Aufidius when they meet?","o":["The finest soldier in all of the Volscian army","A coward who hides behind his own soldiers","The man he hates worse than a promise-breaker","An old friend whom he is sorry to have to fight"],"a":2},{"q":"How does the fight between Marcius and Aufidius end?","o":["Volscians help Aufidius and are driven off","Aufidius is badly wounded and surrenders","Marcius is wounded and carried away","Both men agree to stop and fight again later"],"a":0}]},"a1s9":{"sum":"After the victory Cominius praises Marcius before the army. Marcius refuses praise and refuses a tenth of the spoils, saying he will not take a bribe for his sword. Cominius gives him his own horse and names him Caius Marcius Coriolanus. Coriolanus asks freedom for a poor man of Corioli who once sheltered him, but cannot remember his name.","key":["Coriolanus hates praise as much as reward: he wants honour without being seen to want it.","The new name ties his identity to a conquered city.","He forgets the poor man's name, a small sign of how little the poor matter to him even when he means well."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Marcius refuse after the battle?","o":["The new name Coriolanus","A tenth of the spoils","Cominius's own horse","Medical help for his wounds"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is he called Coriolanus?","o":["It was his father's name","For what he did at Corioli","Because the people chose it","It means 'proud'"],"a":1},{"q":"What goes wrong with his request for the poor man?","o":["Cominius refuses him","He forgets the man's name","The man is dead","Aufidius kills him"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Marcius not want praise?","o":["He thinks the others deserve more of it","He fears it will make the people jealous","It hurts him to hear himself praised","He does not believe that he won the battle"],"a":2},{"q":"What do the soldiers do when Cominius names him?","o":["Lift him onto their shoulders and cheer","Shout 'Caius Marcius Coriolanus'","Throw their spears into the air in joy","Kneel and swear loyalty to him alone"],"a":1}]},"a1s10":{"sum":"Aufidius, defeated, says Corioli will be handed back on terms. He admits Marcius has beaten him five times and vows that, since he cannot win fairly, he will get him by anger or by cunning, even breaking every law of sanctuary and hospitality.","key":["Aufidius's honour is poisoned by repeated defeat; he gives up fair fighting.","His vow to kill Marcius even at his own hearth is ironic: Coriolanus will come to that hearth."],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times has Marcius beaten Aufidius, according to this scene?","o":["Twice","Three times","Five times","Twelve times"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Aufidius now intend?","o":["To make a lasting peace with Rome and its consuls","To kill Marcius by any means, fair or foul","To retire","To join Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Aufidius say he would kill Marcius, against all custom?","o":["In a temple or at his brother's house","Only on the battlefield, as a soldier should","In Rome's Senate house","Nowhere"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Aufidius say his hatred has changed?","o":["He has learned to love Marcius as a friend","He has decided to make peace with Rome now","His honour is lost, so he will use any craft","He wants to fight Marcius only in fair fight"],"a":2},{"q":"Where does Aufidius ask a soldier to bring him news?","o":["At the cypress grove south of the city","At the gates of Corioli in the morning","At his tent in the Volscian camp at night","At the temple of Mars in the market place"],"a":0}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"In Rome, Menenius trades insults with the tribunes, calling them proud and foolish. Volumnia, Virgilia and Valeria bring news that Marcius is coming home with victory; Volumnia and Menenius count his wounds with pleasure. Coriolanus enters in triumph with his oak garland and kneels to his mother, greets his 'gracious silence', Virgilia, and goes to the Capitol. Volumnia hints that one thing is still missing: the consulship. Left alone, the tribunes plan to remind the people of his hatred for them, and hear that he is likely to be made consul.","key":["Menenius's comic duel with the tribunes shows class tension in peacetime.","Volumnia and Menenius count wounds as if they were medals: wounds are political capital.","The tribunes plan to provoke Coriolanus into showing his contempt."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Volumnia thank the gods for?","o":["That her son has come home unhurt","That her son is wounded","That the war with the Volscians is over","That Aufidius is dead"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus call Virgilia?","o":["My dove","My gracious silence","My moon","My lady"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the tribunes plan?","o":["To make him consul at once and win back the people's trust","To stir the people by reminding them of his hatred","To leave Rome","To join him"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Menenius say the tribunes cannot judge others?","o":["They cannot see their own faults","They are too young for any office","They have never been to war","They were not elected properly"],"a":0},{"q":"Why do the tribunes think Coriolanus will lose the people?","o":["The Senate will refuse to make him consul","His wounds will not heal in time for it","His pride will make him scorn them","His mother will speak against him in public"],"a":2}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Officers laying cushions in the Capitol discuss whether Coriolanus's contempt for the people is honest or as bad as flattery. In the Senate, Cominius gives a great speech on Coriolanus's deeds from the age of sixteen to Corioli. Coriolanus leaves rather than hear himself praised. He is named consul but begs to skip the custom of showing his wounds to the people in the gown of humility; the tribunes insist.","key":["Cominius's speech builds Coriolanus into a war machine, 'a thing of blood'.","Coriolanus walks out rather than hear his praise.","The custom of begging the people's votes becomes the crisis point."],"quiz":[{"q":"At what age did Coriolanus first fight, according to Cominius?","o":["Twelve","Sixteen","Seventeen","Thirty"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Coriolanus leave the Senate?","o":["He is angry with the tribunes for doubting him before the people","He would rather not hear his deeds praised","He is called away to the war in the field","He is ill from his wounds and must rest"],"a":1},{"q":"What custom does Coriolanus ask to skip?","o":["Swearing an oath to the gods","Standing in the gown of humility and showing his wounds to beg votes","Making a speech to the Senate","Giving grain to the poor"],"a":1},{"q":"What does one officer say of Coriolanus's contempt?","o":["It is an honest pride that does him credit","It will win him the votes of the Senate","Seeking their hate is as bad as flattering them","It is only for show and he does not mean it"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Cominius say Coriolanus did at Corioli?","o":["He waited at the gates for his soldiers","He made peace with Aufidius in the city","He took the town with no blood being shed","He struck Corioli like a planet, alone"],"a":3}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Citizens discuss whether they can refuse their votes. Coriolanus, in the gown of humility, mocks the custom with Menenius, then asks the citizens for their 'voices' with barely hidden scorn, refusing to show his wounds. They give him their votes. Once he has gone, the tribunes ask why they voted for a man who mocked them and coach them to withdraw their votes and blame the tribunes for persuading them.","key":["Coriolanus's 'Your voices!' is half begging, half sneering.","The citizens are reasonable and willing; it is the tribunes who turn them.","The tribunes' plan relies on making Coriolanus angry in public."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Coriolanus refuse to do as he asks for votes?","o":["Wear the gown","Show his wounds","Speak to the citizens","Give his name"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the tribunes tell the citizens to say?","o":["That the patricians forced them to give their votes against their will","That they voted for him because the tribunes urged them to","That they love Coriolanus","Nothing"],"a":1},{"q":"How do the citizens first react?","o":["They refuse him to his face in the market","They give him their votes","They attack him with stones and staves","They leave"],"a":1},{"q":"What does a citizen say about ingratitude?","o":["If the people are ungrateful they become monsters","Ingratitude is the worst crime of the Senate","The patricians are the most ungrateful of men","The gods punish all those who are ungrateful"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Coriolanus describe the gown of humility?","o":["A noble robe","A wolvish toge","A soldier's coat","A sad gown"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Lartius reports that Aufidius raised a new army before the peace and now waits in Antium, hating Coriolanus above all men. As Coriolanus goes to be confirmed as consul, the tribunes stop him: the people have withdrawn their votes. Coriolanus rages against the tribunes and the free grain, and says the people's power should be abolished. The tribunes call him a traitor and try to arrest him; there is a riot. They sentence him to be thrown from the Tarpeian rock. Coriolanus draws his sword, the patricians drive the people off, and he is hurried home. Menenius persuades the tribunes to let him answer the charges in the market place.","key":["Coriolanus's speeches against 'Hydra' and the 'multitudinous tongue' are political dynamite.","'What is the city but the people?' sets the tribunes' view against his.","Menenius says his nature is 'too noble for the world': he cannot flatter even Jove."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Coriolanus say should be done to the tribunes' power?","o":["Increase it for the sake of peace","Throw it in the dust","Share it with the Senate","Leave it alone"],"a":1},{"q":"What punishment do the tribunes first demand?","o":["A fine","Banishment","Death from the Tarpeian rock","Prison"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Menenius say about Coriolanus's nature?","o":["It is too noble for the world","It is wicked and cruel to the poor","It is changeable","It is cowardly"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Coriolanus say about giving grain freely?","o":["It is a kindness to the poor","It will win the people's love","It nourishes disobedience","It should have been done long ago"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Menenius say the tribunes should not kill Coriolanus?","o":["He is the only man who can beat the Volscians","The Senate will rise up against the tribunes","He has served Rome and should be tried by law","The gods will punish Rome for such a crime"],"a":2}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"At home, Coriolanus is surprised that his mother does not approve of his defiance. Volumnia argues that honour and policy can go together in peace as in war, and urges him to go back and speak mildly. When he refuses, she tells him to do as he likes: his courage came from her, but his pride is his own. He gives in and promises to answer 'mildly'.","key":["Volumnia argues for strategic deception, the opposite of her son's absolute honesty.","Coriolanus fears acting a part will teach his mind 'a most inherent baseness'.","He obeys his mother even against his nature, which foreshadows Act 5."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Volumnia want Coriolanus to do?","o":["Leave Rome at once for his own safety and hers","Go back and speak mildly to the people","Fight the tribunes","Resign"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia compare honour and policy to?","o":["Enemies who can never be reconciled","Inseparable friends in war","Brothers born of one mother","Husband and wife"],"a":1},{"q":"What word does Coriolanus repeat at the end?","o":["Honour","Mildly","Rome","Mother"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia say he should have done first?","o":["Asked the Senate for its help and advice","Killed the tribunes when he had the chance","Left Rome with his army and gone to war","Been consul before showing his true self"],"a":3},{"q":"How does Volumnia want him to hold his cap?","o":["On his head, as a sign of rank","On his sword, raised above him","In his hand, bowing to the people","Under his arm, as if in a hurry"],"a":2}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"The tribunes arrange for the people to shout whatever sentence they call out. They plan to accuse Coriolanus of seeking tyrannical power, knowing it will anger him. He tries to stay calm, but when Sicinius calls him a traitor he explodes. The tribunes banish him on pain of death, and the people chant 'It shall be so'. Coriolanus turns on them: 'I banish you!' and 'There is a world elsewhere'.","key":["The tribunes stage-manage the crowd like a play.","One word, 'traitor', breaks Coriolanus's promise.","'I banish you!' reverses the sentence: he cannot accept being acted on."],"quiz":[{"q":"What charge makes Coriolanus lose his temper?","o":["Coward","Traitor","Liar","Thief"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the sentence?","o":["Death on the rock","Banishment","A fine","Prison"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus say as he leaves?","o":["'Farewell, Rome, my mother city'","'There is a world elsewhere'","'I will return with an army'","'Forgive me'"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus call the people when banished?","o":["A crowd of honest men","A flock of sheep","A common cry of curs","A herd of oxen"],"a":2},{"q":"What do the tribunes plan to do when he loses his temper?","o":["Arrest him and throw him off the rock","Have the people shout their sentence","Send for his mother to calm him down","Call the Senate to judge his words"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"At the city gate Coriolanus says goodbye to his mother, wife, Menenius and Cominius. He comforts them with his mother's own lessons about bearing misfortune, refuses Cominius's offer to go with him, and promises they will hear nothing of him but what is like his former self. He compares himself to a lonely dragon.","key":["Coriolanus is calmer and more tender in exile than in power.","The 'lonely dragon' image prepares for his transformation."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Coriolanus compare himself to?","o":["A lonely dragon","A wolf alone in the hills","An eagle","A lion"],"a":0},{"q":"Who offers to go with him for a month?","o":["Menenius","Cominius","Volumnia","Lartius"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia curse?","o":["The gods","All trades in Rome","The Volscians","Herself"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus tell Volumnia to remember?","o":["The day he was born and named by his father","The victory he won for Rome at Corioli","Her own sayings about bearing misfortune","The promise she made him when he was young"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Coriolanus say about his future?","o":["He will return to Rome as soon as the people forgive him","He will live quietly in the country with his family","He will go to Greece and serve its kings in war","He will exceed the common or fall to cautelous baits"],"a":3}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"The tribunes try to slip away from Volumnia, but she and Virgilia confront them, curse them and boast that her son is worth more than all of them. Volumnia says she feeds on anger.","key":["Volumnia's fury shows her strength; the tribunes call her mad.","Even Virgilia, usually silent, attacks."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the tribunes say about Volumnia?","o":["She is wise and her grief is just","They say she is mad","She is kind","She is a Volscian"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia say she feeds on?","o":["Grief","Anger","Hope","Wine"],"a":1},{"q":"Who also turns on the tribunes?","o":["Valeria","Virgilia","Menenius","Cominius"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia say she wishes the gods would do?","o":["Bring her son home again to Rome","Make the people sorry for this","Make her curses work on the tribunes","Strike the city with a plague"],"a":2},{"q":"How does Volumnia say she will eat?","o":["She will not eat again","She eats with the poor","She dines with Menenius","She sups on herself"],"a":3}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"On the road between Rome and Antium, a Roman spy, Nicanor, meets a Volscian, Adrian, and tells him that Coriolanus has been banished and Rome is divided. The Volscians have an army ready and hope to strike now.","key":["Rome's divisions are being sold to the enemy.","The scene shows the Volscians poised to attack."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is Nicanor?","o":["A Volscian general of great fame in Antium","A Roman working for the Volscians","A tribune","A servant"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does he bring?","o":["Coriolanus is dead in a street fight","Coriolanus has been banished","Rome has surrendered to the Volscians","The tribunes have fled"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the Volscians have ready?","o":["Gold","An army","A treaty","Ships"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Adrian not know Nicanor at first?","o":["He has never met Nicanor before this day","His face is changed, but his voice gives him away","Nicanor wears a Volscian soldier's armour","Nicanor is hiding his face behind his cloak"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Nicanor say the Volscians should strike now?","o":["Rome is starving and weak","Rome has no army left","Rome has lost its best soldier","Rome is sick with plague"],"a":2}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Coriolanus arrives in Antium in poor clothes and disguise. In a soliloquy he reflects on how quickly friends become enemies and enemies friends, and decides to offer himself to Aufidius: if he kills him, it is just; if not, he will serve the Volscians.","key":["His only soliloquy shows a man who has given up his birthplace.","'O world, thy slippery turns!' is about the fickleness he hated in the people."],"quiz":[{"q":"How is Coriolanus dressed?","o":["In armour","In poor clothes, disguised","In his consul's robe","In the gown of humility"],"a":1},{"q":"What does he say he now hates?","o":["Antium and its people","His birthplace","Aufidius","His mother"],"a":1},{"q":"What two outcomes does he accept?","o":["Aufidius kills him, or he serves the Volscians","He returns home to Rome in triumph, or dies","He rules Antium in Aufidius's place, or Rome","None"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Coriolanus describe the world's friendships?","o":["Constant bonds that last as long as a life","Simple debts that are paid back in kind","Rare jewels that are only found in Rome","Slippery turns: friends become foes overnight"],"a":3},{"q":"Whose house does Coriolanus ask the citizen about?","o":["Menenius's","Aufidius's","Cominius's","The Senate's"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"Aufidius's servants try to throw out the shabby stranger, and he beats one. Aufidius comes, and Coriolanus reveals himself, offering either his service against Rome or his throat. Aufidius embraces him with joy, gives him half his command, and plans an attack on Rome. The servants, amazed, gossip that Coriolanus is the better soldier and look forward to war.","key":["Aufidius's welcome is passionate, almost like a lover's.","Coriolanus offers to fight 'against my canker'd country'.","The servants' praise of war over peace adds comedy and a warning."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Coriolanus offer Aufidius?","o":["A chest of gold from Rome for the Volscian army","His service or his throat","All of Rome's secrets, but nothing more","A lasting peace"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aufidius give him?","o":["Nothing","Half his command","His house","His daughter"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the servants prefer to peace?","o":["Feasting","War","Travel","Sleep"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the servant say Coriolanus lives?","o":["In the palace","By the river","In the city","Under the canopy"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Aufidius say he dreamed of?","o":["Fighting Marcius, unbuckling helms","Marching into Rome with his army","Marrying a lady from Antium","Being made king of the Volscians"],"a":0}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"Rome is quiet and the tribunes congratulate themselves. Then news comes that the Volscians are attacking, led by Coriolanus with Aufidius. Cominius and Menenius blame the tribunes. The citizens claim they always said banishing him was wrong.","key":["The tribunes' triumph collapses in one scene.","The citizens change their minds again, proving Coriolanus's charge of fickleness."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the tribunes first do with the report of the Volscian attack?","o":["Believe it and arm the city","Order the messenger whipped","Flee","Arm the people"],"a":1},{"q":"Who leads the Volscian army?","o":["Aufidius alone, with all his men","Coriolanus with Aufidius","Nicanor, the Roman spy","Lartius"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the citizens say now?","o":["They are glad he is gone and would banish him again","They always said banishing him was a pity","They will fight him at the walls to the last man","They blame the gods and the tribunes alike"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the tribunes say about Rome when it is quiet?","o":["It is sad without its great soldier","It is in danger from the Volscians","It is better without Coriolanus","It is waiting for its consul to return"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Cominius say the tribunes have helped to do?","o":["Save Rome from a cruel and proud tyrant at last","Melt the city leads upon their own heads","Keep the peace and feed the people well all year","Bring the Volscians to the peace table at once"],"a":1}]},"a4s7":{"sum":"Aufidius's lieutenant complains that the Volscian soldiers worship Coriolanus and Aufidius is overshadowed. Aufidius says he has a charge ready to break Coriolanus's neck when they come to account. He analyses why Coriolanus was banished: pride, poor judgment, or a nature that could not change from war to peace.","key":["Aufidius's jealousy is now a plan.","His speech is the play's most balanced analysis of Coriolanus."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the lieutenant complain about?","o":["The weather","That the soldiers prefer Coriolanus","That Rome is too strong","That pay is late"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aufidius say will happen when Rome is Coriolanus's?","o":["He will be the richest man in all of Italy","He will be poorest of all, and then Aufidius's","He will be king","He will return home"],"a":1},{"q":"Which reason does Aufidius NOT give for the banishment?","o":["Pride","Lack of judgment","A nature that cannot change","Cowardice"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Aufidius say Coriolanus has left undone?","o":["Something that will break his neck","Nothing at all: he is a perfect soldier","The taking of Corioli back from the Romans","A promise he made to the Volscian lords"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Aufidius say about Rome?","o":["Rome will be too strong for the Volscians to take","Rome will surrender when it sees Coriolanus","Coriolanus will take it as the osprey takes fish","Rome is already lost to the plebeians"],"a":2}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Cominius reports that Coriolanus would not even answer to his name and called his friends a few grains in a pile of chaff. Menenius blames the tribunes but agrees to try, thinking Coriolanus may be kinder after dinner. Cominius doubts it and says only his mother and wife can help.","key":["Coriolanus has become 'a kind of nothing, titleless'.","Menenius's comic theory about dinner shows his hopefulness."],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Coriolanus refuse when Cominius pleaded?","o":["Food and drink from his old friend","Any name, even Coriolanus","To meet him","To fight"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Menenius think will help?","o":["Gifts of gold and land from the Senate","Catching him after dinner","Tears from his wife and mother","Threats of the gods' anger"],"a":1},{"q":"What image does Coriolanus use for his friends?","o":["Grains in a pile of chaff","Stars shining in the dark winter sky","Sheep in a fold that has lost its shepherd","Flowers"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did Coriolanus refuse a name?","o":["He said that only his mother may call him by any name","He has taken the name of Aufidius as his own now","He said he was titleless till he forged one in Rome's fire","He has forgotten his name since he left the city"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Cominius say Coriolanus sat in?","o":["A chair of iron","Gold, his eye red","The Senate seat","A Volscian tent"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Menenius tries to pass the Volscian guards, boasting of his friendship with Coriolanus. When Coriolanus appears, Menenius pleads with him, but Coriolanus says he knows no wife, mother or child, gives him a letter and sends him away. The guards mock Menenius, who leaves in despair.","key":["Coriolanus's rejection of Menenius is the cruellest proof of his change.","He shows Aufidius his firmness, a performance for his new ally."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Coriolanus give Menenius?","o":["A sword from his own side","A letter","A ring","Nothing at all"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus say about wife, mother and child?","o":["He loves them","He knows them not","They are safe","They must leave Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"How do the guards treat Menenius after?","o":["They apologise","They mock him","They arrest him","They follow him"],"a":1},{"q":"Who does Menenius boast that he is?","o":["A senator of Rome with power over the guards","The father of Coriolanus's wife Virgilia","Coriolanus's friend and his book of good acts","A friend of Aufidius from long ago in Antium"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Coriolanus say about Menenius's love?","o":["He loved him, and so he sends him away","He never loved him and never will","He will love him more after this","He wants him to come with the army"],"a":0}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Coriolanus tells Aufidius he will hear no more pleas. Then Volumnia, Virgilia, Valeria and young Marcius arrive in mourning. He tries to stand 'as if a man were author of himself'. Volumnia kneels and argues that conquering Rome would curse his name forever; he rises to leave, but she stops him, reminds him what he owes her and finally makes to go home in disgust. He holds her hand in silence, then gives in, knowing it may cost his life. He agrees to make peace, and Aufidius privately sees his chance.","key":["The climax: the play's greatest speech is a mother's argument.","'O mother, mother! What have you done?' He knows the victory is deadly to him.","Aufidius's aside shows the trap closing."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who kneels first in the scene?","o":["Volumnia kneels before Coriolanus","Coriolanus kneels to his mother","Aufidius kneels","Virgilia kneels"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia say conquering Rome would bring him?","o":["Glory to last a thousand years","A name dogged with curses","Wealth","Peace"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus do before he gives in?","o":["Leaves the tent without a word to anyone","Holds her hand in silence","Calls the guards to take her away","Draws his sword on Aufidius"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Coriolanus say he must not see his family?","o":["They have betrayed him to the tribunes","Their pleas would break his vow","Aufidius has forbidden it to him","They are dressed as Volscian enemies"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Volumnia say as she turns to go home?","o":["He must have had a Volscian for a mother","The Volscians have ordered her out of the camp","Her grandson is cold and tired from the journey","She fears that Aufidius will have her killed"],"a":0}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Menenius tells Sicinius there is no hope: Coriolanus has become a dragon without mercy. A messenger reports the plebeians are dragging Brutus through the streets. Then news comes that the women have succeeded and the Volscians have gone; Rome erupts in joy.","key":["Menenius's description makes Coriolanus a god or a machine.","Rome turns from terror to joy in a moment."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Menenius say there is no more of in Coriolanus than milk in a male tiger?","o":["Pride","Mercy","Fear","Love"],"a":1},{"q":"What are the plebeians doing to Brutus?","o":["Praising him in the market as the saviour of Rome","Dragging him up and down the streets","Making him consul in place of Coriolanus","Hiding him"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the news?","o":["Rome has fallen to the Volscian army","The ladies have prevailed","Coriolanus is dead at the gates","Aufidius is coming"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Menenius compare Coriolanus's change to?","o":["A grub that grew into a butterfly, a man into a dragon","A lamb that grew into a lion, a friend into an enemy for life","A boy that grew into a man, a soldier into a great consul","A seed that grew into an oak, a servant into a master"],"a":0},{"q":"How is the news celebrated?","o":["A feast in the Senate house","Bells ringing in all churches","Fires lit along the walls","Trumpets, drums and shouting"],"a":3}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"Volumnia and the women return to Rome in triumph. A senator calls her the life of Rome and orders the people to welcome the ladies.","key":["Volumnia is hailed as Rome's saviour while her son goes to his death.","The welcome 'unshouts' the noise that banished him."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the senator call Volumnia?","o":["The life of Rome","A traitor","A mother of wolves","The consul"],"a":0},{"q":"What does he tell the people to do?","o":["Arm themselves for war against Antium","Light fires and strew flowers","Banish the tribunes from the city for ever","Pray in silence"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is not with the returning women?","o":["Valeria","Virgilia","Coriolanus","Young Marcius"],"a":2},{"q":"What does the senator call for?","o":["Bring Coriolanus home to Rome tomorrow","Unshout the noise that banished Marcius","Make Volumnia the consul of Rome now","Punish the tribunes for their crime"],"a":1},{"q":"Where are the ladies welcomed?","o":["Into Rome","Into the Senate","Into Antium","Into the temple"],"a":0}]},"a5s6":{"sum":"In Antium, Aufidius prepares his accusation with his conspirators. Coriolanus returns to cheers and presents the treaty. Aufidius calls him a traitor who gave away the victory for his mother's tears, and calls him 'boy'. Coriolanus rages, boasting that he alone beat the Volscians at Corioli. The crowd shouts that he killed their families; the conspirators stab him. Aufidius stands on his body, then, his rage gone, orders him carried with honour.","key":["'Boy of tears' is the one insult Coriolanus cannot bear.","'Alone I did it' is his last boast and his death sentence.","Aufidius's sudden sorrow mirrors his sudden welcome in 4.5."],"quiz":[{"q":"What word of Aufidius's most enrages Coriolanus?","o":["Traitor","Boy","Roman","Coward"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Coriolanus boast about?","o":["His mother and how much she loves and honours him","Beating the Volscians alone at Corioli","His new treaty with Rome and its terms for Antium","His wealth and the spoils of war"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aufidius do at the end?","o":["Leaves the body in the street for the people to see","Orders him carried with honour","Burns it","Sends it to Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Aufidius say Coriolanus did with his tears?","o":["Wept for his mother in front of all of the army","Sold their victory for a few drops of women's rheum","Hid them from the soldiers and from all the Volscians","Gave them to Rome as a sign of his true repentance"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the lords say at the end?","o":["Throw his body into the river","Send his body back to Rome","Let him be buried with honour","Leave him in the market place"],"a":2}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens in the middle of a riot. Shakespeare wrote it around 1608, soon after the Midlands Rising of 1607, when hungry peasants tore down enclosure hedges.","11":"The First Citizen's argument is sharp: the rich get richer from the poor's suffering, and 'our sufferance is a gain to them'. The people are not shown as fools.","17":"The First Citizen already names the two sources of Marcius's deeds: his mother and his pride. The rest of the play proves him right.","29":"Menenius blames the famine on the gods, not the patricians: a classic way for rulers to avoid responsibility.","33":"The fable of the belly was an old political story (told by Livy and Plutarch, and by writers in Shakespeare's time). The state is a body; the senators are its stomach.","36":"The citizen interrupts with his own version of the body: the head, eye, heart, arm and leg. He imagines the people as the useful working parts.","47":"The moral: everything good comes 'from them to you'. Menenius then calls the citizen 'the great toe', the lowest part of the body.","54":"Marcius's first speech is almost all insult: curs, hares, geese, coal on ice. He attacks the people's fickleness, a theme that returns in Act 4.","60":"The tribunes were real officials of the Roman Republic, created in 494 BC to protect the plebeians. Here Shakespeare places their creation at the same moment as the war with Corioli.","70":"Marcius admires only one man, his enemy. 'Were I anything but what I am, I would wish me only he.'","92":"The tribunes' first judgement: he is 'too proud to be so valiant'. Their whole campaign starts from this.","118":"Volumnia's speech shows how she raised her son: sending him to danger young, measuring love in wounds. 'Man-child' and 'man' are her key words.","126":"Volumnia says blood suits a man better than gold suits a monument. Hecuba nursing Hector links breast-milk and blood, mother and war.","137":"The butterfly story: young Marcius catches, releases, chases and tears the butterfly. It is charming to Valeria and Volumnia but sinister to an audience.","150":"Penelope waited twenty years for Ulysses, spinning and unpicking her weaving. Valeria jokes that all she produced was moths.","190":"Marcius's curse on his own soldiers is as violent as anything he says to the enemy.","204":"Cato the Elder was the model of the stern Roman soldier. The reference is an anachronism: he lived about 300 years after Coriolanus.","255":"'O, me alone! Make you a sword of me?' The soldiers' joy turns him into a weapon, an object, not a man.","284":"Marcius will not be paid: taking spoils would make his courage a trade. Compare his hatred of 'begging' votes.","287":"The new name is given by acclamation, like an election by the army. He will later lose the right to it.","298":"He cannot remember the poor man's name. A sympathetic moment, or a sign that ordinary people do not stick in his memory?","305":"Aufidius gives up fighting fair. This is the first step to the murder in 5.6.","307":"Aufidius imagines killing Marcius at a hearth, under hospitality. In 4.5 Marcius walks to his hearth of his own will.","338":"Menenius describes himself honestly: a joker who loves wine. His prose style is part of his charm with the people.","356":"Volumnia's 'I thank the gods for't' is one of the play's most startling lines: she is glad her son is wounded.","368":"Wounds are counted like votes. In Rome a candidate for consul showed his scars to prove his service.","374":"Volumnia's rhyming couplet describes her son as death itself in human form.","384":"'My gracious silence' is Coriolanus's tender name for Virgilia, who says almost nothing in the play.","397":"Brutus's description of the crowds at the triumph is a picture of celebrity: everyone wants to see the hero.","403":"The 'napless vesture of humility' is the threadbare gown candidates wore to ask for votes. Coriolanus swore never to wear it.","409":"The tribunes plan to provoke him. Notice they do not deny his merit; they fear what he would do with power.","444":"Cominius's speech is the play's great portrait of Coriolanus in war. Compare the language of machines and planets: 'a thing of blood', 'like a planet'.","455":"Coriolanus asks to skip the custom of showing his wounds. He says he cannot 'stand naked'.","474":"The Third Citizen jokes about the 'many-headed multitude'. The people are self-aware and funny.","485":"Coriolanus rehearses his begging and cannot do it without insulting: 'some certain of your brethren roar'd and ran'.","514":"His speech is full of irony: he promises to flatter, to 'counterfeit', and calls the people his 'sworn brother'.","520":"A soliloquy-like speech in rhyme, the only rhyming passage he speaks. 'Wolvish toge' compares the gown to a sheep's clothing on a wolf.","553":"The Third Citizen imitates Coriolanus's mocking voice: 'Your most sweet voices'. They have noticed the scorn.","566":"Brutus lists Coriolanus's noble ancestors, including King Ancus Marcius. Another anachronism: some of them came after him.","630":"'Hydra' was the many-headed monster killed by Hercules. Coriolanus calls Sicinius a 'Triton of the minnows', a sea-god among tiny fish.","636":"He argues the free grain rewarded cowards and will teach the people that the senate fears them.","639":"His 'double worship' speech argues that divided government cannot work: one side must rule.","667":"'What is the city but the people?' is the tribunes' political creed, against Coriolanus's view of the city as its noble defenders.","675":"The Tarpeian rock was a cliff on the Capitoline Hill from which traitors were thrown.","704":"Menenius's verdict: 'His nature is too noble for the world.' The whole play tests whether this is praise or criticism.","728":"Menenius's medical images (a limb with a disease, gangrene) answer the tribunes' 'disease that must be cut away'.","766":"Volumnia argues that deceit is honourable in war, so why not in peace? This is the play's debate about acting and sincerity.","771":"She wants him to use words 'rooted in your tongue' only, not his heart.","785":"Coriolanus imagines acting humble as becoming a harlot, a eunuch, a beggar: he sees it as unmanning.","786":"'Thy valiantness was mine, thou suck'dst it from me, But owe thy pride thyself.' Volumnia separates the courage she gave from the pride he made.","839":"Sicinius's 'traitor' undoes all Volumnia's coaching in one word.","858":"'I banish you!' Coriolanus turns the sentence round. 'There is a world elsewhere' is his exit line from Rome.","872":"He comforts his mother with her own lessons, and compares himself to 'a lonely dragon'.","907":"Volumnia calls the plebeians 'cats', unable to judge her son's worth.","953":"His only full soliloquy. Friends turn to enemies and enemies to friends 'on a dissension of a doit': the fickleness he hated in the people is now his own.","1009":"Coriolanus offers his throat to Aufidius as much as his service. He wants revenge 'in mere spite'.","1010":"Aufidius's welcome compares his joy to his wedding night. Many critics read the rivals' bond as intense and erotic.","1047":"The servants praise war over peace: peace 'a getter of more bastard children than war's a destroyer of men'. Comic, but it echoes Coriolanus's values.","1148":"Aufidius's judgement on why Coriolanus fell: pride, poor judgement, or a nature that could not move 'from the casque to the cushion' (from helmet to senate seat).","1155":"Coriolanus refuses every name, 'a kind of nothing, titleless'. Having lost Rome, he has lost his identity.","1168":"Menenius's theory that men are kinder after dinner is comic, and ironic: food began the play's conflict.","1205":"'Wife, mother, child, I know not.' He denies the bonds that will soon defeat him.","1227":"He tries to stand 'as if a man were author of himself', but the sight of his family already moves him.","1236":"Volumnia kneeling to her son is unnatural, he says, as if pebbles could hit the stars.","1252":"Volumnia's great speech. She argues, shames, threatens and finally pretends to give up. Notice how she uses Volscian and Roman, honour and name.","1255":"The stage direction 'holds her by the hand, silent' is one of the most famous in Shakespeare. 'O mother, mother! What have you done?'","1258":"Aufidius's aside: he will use the mercy against him.","1270":"Menenius describes Coriolanus as a machine of war: 'he moves like an engine'.","1295":"Rome welcomes Volumnia as its saviour. The ladies' triumph mirrors Coriolanus's triumph in 2.1.","1311":"Aufidius's grievance: he has been treated like a hired man in his own army.","1331":"Aufidius calls him by his old name, Marcius, takes away 'Coriolanus', and calls him a 'boy of tears'.","1338":"'Alone I did it.' Boasting of killing Volscians in a Volscian city is suicidal.","1355":"Aufidius's last speech honours the man he has just killed. The play ends with a dead march."},"gloss":{"famish":"starve","patricians":"Roman nobles","surfeits":"overeats","superfluity":"surplus, leftovers","inventory":"list","particularize":"spell out in detail","sufferance":"suffering","pikes":"long spears","commonalty":"common people","altitude":"height","barren":"empty, short of","prating":"chattering","Capitol":"temple and centre of government in Rome","inkling":"hint","dearth":"famine","helms":"steering, rulers","usury":"lending money at interest","stale":"make stale, repeat","fob off":"put off","viand":"food","cupboarding":"storing away","cormorant":"greedy (like the seabird)","muniments":"defences","incorporate":"forming one body","cranks":"winding paths","audit":"account","weal":"welfare, state","bale":"harm, ruin","dissentious":"quarrelsome","rogues":"rascals","curs":"dogs","quarry":"heap of dead","conjectural":"guessed at","emulation":"rivalry","vulgar":"common","insurrection":"rebellion","garners":"granaries","gird":"mock","demerits":"merits","singularity":"oddness","pretences":"plans","circumvention":"means of blocking","hie":"hurry","comfortable":"cheerful","comeliness":"good looks","cicatrices":"scars","mail'd":"armoured","Hecuba":"queen of Troy, Hector's mother","fell":"cruel","housekeepers":"stay-at-homes","mammocked":"tore to shreds","huswife":"housewife","cambric":"fine linen","Penelope":"Ulysses' faithful wife","drum":"army drum","colours":"flags","parley":"talk with the enemy","pound us up":"pen us in","cloven":"split","contagion":"plague","agued":"shivering with fever","doit":"tiny coin","drachma":"Greek coin","doublets":"jackets","physical":"healthy, like bloodletting","Antiates":"men of Antium","vaward":"front line","target":"shield","caparison":"horse's trappings","traducement":"slander","tent":"probe and clean (a wound)","hyperbolical":"exaggerated","debile":"feeble","addition":"title","condition":"terms","potch":"stab, thrust","embarquements":"obstacles","fane":"temple","augurer":"fortune-teller","censured":"judged","Lycurguses":"wise lawgivers","bisson":"blind, purblind","conspectuities":"powers of sight","botcher":"tailor who mends","Deucalion":"survivor of the Flood in Greek myth","empiricutic":"quackish","Galen":"famous Greek doctor","pow, wow":"nonsense","malkin":"kitchen maid","lockram":"coarse linen","reechy":"grimy","flamens":"priests","gawded":"made up","napless":"threadbare","vesture":"clothing","provand":"food","sennet":"trumpet call","lictors":"officers who walk before magistrates","convented":"summoned","bencher":"senator","giber":"joker","fosset-seller":"seller of taps","Amazonian":"beardless, like the Amazons","bestrid":"stood over","lurch'd":"robbed","stem":"prow of a ship","reeking":"steaming","toge":"gown, toga","Hob and Dick":"any common men","vouches":"approvals","plebeii":"plebeians","portance":"behaviour","Censorinus":"a later Marcius, twice censor","muniment":"defence","voices":"votes","palt'ring":"trickery","rub":"obstacle","measles":"scabs, lepers","tetter":"cover with scabs","Triton":"sea-god","minnows":"tiny fish","Hydra":"many-headed monster","peremptory":"absolute","vail":"lower, bow","cockle":"weed","sedition":"rebellion","navel":"centre","debase":"lower","Tarpeian":"the rock from which traitors were thrown","aediles":"officers of the tribunes","rascal":"low fellow","tag":"rabble","kam":"crooked, wrong","bolted":"sifted, refined","woollen":"wearing coarse wool","ordinance":"rank","unbarb'd":"bare, uncovered","sconce":"head","mountebank":"win like a quack","cog":"cheat","Juno":"queen of the gods","stocks":"wooden frame for punishing offenders","cicatrice":"scar","ostler":"stable boy","enforce":"press, urge","prerogative":"right","cry":"pack of hounds","reek":"stink","fens":"swamps","cautelous":"crafty","wot":"know","fond":"foolish","exposture":"exposure","mankind":"mannish, fierce","foxship":"foxlike cunning","posterity":"descendants","baited":"tormented","puling":"whining","Adrian":"a Volscian","favour":"face","billeted":"quartered","edifices":"buildings","spits":"roasting spikes","doit's":"a penny's","batten":"grow fat","trencher":"wooden plate","daws":"jackdaws","kites":"birds of prey","grained":"straight-grained","clip":"embrace","brawn":"arm","target's":"shield's","carbonado":"meat scored for grilling","dejectitude":"servant's mistake for dejection","sowl":"drag","polled":"cleared, stripped","coin":"corner","dragon":"winged monster","engine":"war machine, siege engine","corslet":"breastplate","knell":"funeral bell","battery":"bombardment","Alexander":"Alexander the Great","sackbuts":"trombones","psalteries":"stringed instruments","tabors":"small drums","hautboys":"oboes","doublet":"jacket","shent":"scolded","Dian's":"Diana's, goddess of chastity","Publicola":"a Roman consul","epitome":"miniature version","sea-mark":"landmark for sailors","flaw":"gust of wind","capitulate":"negotiate terms","mechanics":"workmen","bewray":"reveal","recreant":"traitor","rive":"split","sulphur":"lightning","fillip":"flick","prate":"chatter","counter-seal'd":"signed by both sides","post":"messenger","rheum":"tears","compounded":"agreed","perfidiously":"treacherously","twist":"strand","stripes":"marks of a beating","dove-cote":"dovecote","Mars":"god of war","corse":"corpse","urn":"burial urn"}};
const PARTS={"29":[[0,3,"I tell you, friends, the patricians look after you with the kindest care. As for your needs and your suffering in this famine, you might as well hit the sky with your sticks as raise them against the Roman state."],[4,9,"The state will go on its way, snapping ten thousand chains far stronger than any you could put in its path. As for the famine, the gods make it, not the patricians, and you need to kneel to the gods, not take up arms."],[10,13,"Oh dear, your misery is carrying you towards worse trouble, and you slander the men who steer the state, who care for you like fathers, when you curse them as enemies."]],"33":[[0,4,"Once upon a time all the parts of the body rebelled against the belly and accused it like this: that it just sat like a pit in the middle of the body, idle and doing nothing, always storing away the food,"],[5,9,"never doing its share of the work like the rest, while the other organs saw and heard, planned, taught, walked, felt, and, working together, served the desires and needs of the whole body. The belly answered—"]],"43":[[0,2,"Take note of this, my good friend. Your very serious belly took its time, unlike its rash accusers, and answered like this:"],[3,7,"\"It's true, my friends who make up one body with me,\" it said, \"that I take in all the food first, which you live on, and that's right, because I'm the storehouse and the workshop of the whole body."],[8,14,"But remember, I send it through the rivers of your blood all the way to the court, which is the heart, and to the throne, which is the brain; and through all the winding passages and rooms of the body, the strongest sinews and smallest veins get from me the natural supply they live on. And though all of you at once, my good friends\"—this is the belly speaking, listen to me—"]],"54":[[0,6,"Anyone who gives you kind words is a disgusting flatterer. What do you want, you dogs, who like neither peace nor war? War frightens you, peace makes you arrogant. Whoever trusts you finds hares where he wanted lions, geese where he wanted foxes. You're no more reliable than hot coals on ice or a hailstone in the sun."],[7,14,"Your great skill is to honour a criminal brought down by his crime and curse the justice that did it. Whoever deserves greatness deserves your hatred, and your desires are like a sick man's appetite, wanting most what will make him worse. Anyone who depends on your favour is swimming with lead fins and chopping down oaks with reeds. Hang you! Trust you!"],[15,21,"You change your minds every minute, calling a man noble who was just now hateful to you, and calling the man you crowned with garlands vile. What's the matter, that all over the city you're shouting against the noble senate, who, under the gods, keep you in check, when otherwise you'd eat each other? What are they after?"]],"56":[[0,6,"Hang them! \"They say\"! They sit by the fire and claim to know what goes on in the Capitol: who's likely to rise, who's thriving and who's in decline. They take sides, gossip about who'll marry whom, build up one party and run down those they don't like until they're lower than their patched shoes. \"They say there's grain enough\"!"],[7,10,"If the nobility would put aside their pity and let me use my sword, I'd make a heap of these chopped-up slaves as high as I could throw my lance."]],"58":[[0,4,"They've broken up, hang them! They said they were hungry and sighed out proverbs: that hunger breaks through stone walls, that dogs must eat, that food was made for mouths, that the gods didn't send grain for the rich alone."],[5,10,"With these scraps they let out their complaints. When they were answered and given what they asked, a strange demand fit to break the heart of the nobility and make power go pale, they threw their caps up as if to hang them on the horns of the moon, shouting to outdo each other."]],"118":[[0,3,"Please, daughter, sing, or at least cheer up a bit. If my son were my husband, I'd be happier about him being away winning honour than about him embracing me in bed, where he'd show most love."],[4,9,"When he was still a soft-bodied boy, my only son, when his youth and good looks drew every eye to him, when a mother wouldn't let him out of her sight for an hour even if kings begged her for a whole day, I thought how honour would suit a man like him, that without fame to bring him to life he'd be no better than a picture hanging on the wall. So I was glad to let him look for danger where he'd find fame."],[10,13,"I sent him to a cruel war, and he came back with an oak-leaf wreath on his brow. I tell you, daughter, I didn't leap for joy more when I first heard I'd given birth to a boy than I did when I first saw he'd proved himself a man."]],"124":[[0,4,"You certainly won't. I think I can hear your husband's drum from here. I can see him dragging Aufidius down by the hair, the Volscians running from him like children from a bear. I can see him stamping like this and shouting like this:"],[5,9,"\"Come on, you cowards! You were conceived in fear, even if you were born in Rome!\" Then, wiping his bloody brow with his armoured hand, he goes forward like a harvester who has to mow the whole field or lose his pay."]],"190":[[0,6,"May all the diseases of the south fall on you, you disgraces to Rome! You herd of— May boils and plagues cover you so people loathe you before they even see you, and you infect each other a mile upwind! You souls of geese in the shape of men, how you've run from slaves that apes could beat! Pluto and hell!"],[7,12,"All wounded in the back, backs red and faces white with running and shivering fear! Pull yourselves together and charge hard, or by the fires of heaven I'll leave the enemy and make war on you. Watch out! Come on: if you stand firm, we'll beat them back to their wives, as they beat us back to our trenches."]],"204":[[0,3,"Oh noble man! He can feel, yet he's bolder than his unfeeling sword, and he stands firm when it bends! You're lost, Marcius. A flawless ruby as big as you would not be so rich a jewel."],[4,9,"You were a soldier just as Cato would have wished, not only fierce and terrible in your blows, but with your grim looks and the thunderous crash of your voice you made your enemies shake as if the world had a fever and trembled."]],"216":[[0,4,"Look at these looters, who value their time at a cracked penny! Cushions, lead spoons, iron scraps worth a farthing, jackets a hangman would bury along with the men who wore them: these low slaves are packing up before the fight is even over. Cut them down!"],[5,10,"And listen to the noise the general's making! Go to him! There's the man my soul hates, Aufidius, cutting through our Romans. So, brave Titus, take enough men to hold the city, while I hurry with those who have the spirit to help Cominius."]],"253":[[0,4,"The best men are the most willing. If there are any here (and it would be a sin to doubt it) who love this paint you see me smeared with, if any fear for their bodies less than for a bad reputation,"],[5,9,"if any think a brave death outweighs a shameful life and their country is dearer than themselves, let him alone, or all who think the same, wave like this (waving his hand) to show their feelings, and follow Marcius."]],"255":[[0,3,"Oh, me alone! Are you making a sword of me? If this isn't just for show, which of you isn't worth four Volscians? Every one of you can hold up a shield against the great Aufidius as hard as his own."],[4,9,"Thanking you all, I must still choose a certain number; the rest will do their part in some other fight, as duty requires. Please march on, and four of you will quickly pick out the men best suited for my command."]],"277":[[0,4,"If I told you all over again what you did today, you wouldn't believe your own deeds. But I'll report it where senators will mix tears with smiles, where great patricians will listen, shrug, and finally admire; where ladies will be frightened and, happily trembling, ask to hear more;"],[5,8,"where the dull tribunes, who along with the mouldy plebeians hate your honours, will say against their will, \"We thank the gods that our Rome has such a soldier.\""],[9,10,"And yet you came to this feast for just a bite, having already eaten your fill."]],"286":[[0,5,"May these same instruments that you're abusing never sound again! When drums and trumpets on the battlefield turn into flatterers, then courts and cities may as well be made entirely of two-faced smooth talk. When steel grows as soft as a flatterer's silk, let silk be made into armour for war."],[6,11,"No more, I say! Just because I haven't washed my bleeding nose or have knocked down some feeble wretch, which plenty of others here have done without being noticed, you cheer me with exaggerated shouts, as if I liked my small deeds to be fed on praise flavoured with lies."]],"287":[[0,4,"You're too modest, more cruel to your own reputation than grateful to us who are being truthful about you. With your permission, if you're angry with yourself, we'll put you in handcuffs like a man who means to harm himself, and then argue with you safely."],[5,13,"So let it be known to the whole world, as it is to us, that Caius Marcius wins this war's garland. As a token of it I give him my noble horse, known throughout the camp, with all its trappings; and from now on, for what he did at Corioli, call him, with all the army's applause and cheering, \"Caius Marcius Coriolanus.\" Wear that title nobly forever!"]],"305":[[0,3,"Terms! I wish I were a Roman, because as a Volscian I can't be what I really am. Terms? What good terms can a treaty give the side that's at the other's mercy?"],[4,6,"Five times, Marcius, I've fought you, and every time you've beaten me, and I think you would even if we met as often as we eat."],[7,13,"By the elements, if I ever meet him face to face again, either he's mine or I'm his. My rivalry has lost the honour it had, because where I once meant to crush him in a fair fight, sword against sword, now I'll stab at him somehow; either anger or cunning will get him."]],"307":[[0,6,"Bolder, though not so cunning. My courage is poisoned just by being stained with defeat by him; it will abandon its own nature to get him. Neither sleep nor holy refuge, nor his being unarmed or sick, nor temple nor Capitol, nor priests' prayers or times of sacrifice, all of which hold back fury, shall raise their worn-out rights and customs against my hatred of Marcius."],[7,12,"Wherever I find him, even at home under my brother's protection, there, against the laws of hospitality, I'd wash my fierce hand in his heart. Go to the city; find out how it's being held and who must be given as hostages to Rome."]],"338":[[0,5,"I'm known as a whimsical patrician who loves a cup of hot wine without a drop of Tiber water to dilute it; said to be rather at fault for siding with whoever complains first, quick to flare up like tinder over trifles; one who keeps company with the backside of the night more than the brow of the morning. I say what I think, and use up my spite in talking."],[6,9,"When I meet two statesmen like you (I can't call you great lawgivers like Lycurgus), if the drink you give me tastes bad, I pull a face at it. I can't say your worships have argued well when I find most of what you say is asinine;"],[10,15,"and though I have to put up with people who call you serious, respectable men, those who tell you you have good faces are lying through their teeth. If you can read this in the map of my little world, does it follow that I'm well known too? What harm can your blinkered sight pick out of this description, if I'm well known too?"]],"340":[[0,3,"You know neither me, nor yourselves, nor anything. You're greedy for poor wretches to doff their caps and bow to you. You waste a whole good morning hearing a case between an orange-seller and a tap-maker, and then adjourn the threepenny dispute to a second day of hearing."],[4,10,"When you're hearing a case between two parties, if you happen to get a stomach ache, you pull faces like clowns, raise the red flag of war against all patience, and, bellowing for a chamber pot, dismiss the case still bleeding, more tangled than before you heard it. The only peace you make between them is calling both sides scoundrels. You're a strange pair."]],"342":[[0,3,"Even our priests would have to turn mockers if they met such ridiculous creatures as you. When you speak most to the point, it isn't worth the wagging of your beards, and your beards don't deserve so honourable a grave as stuffing a tailor's cushion or being buried in a donkey's saddle."],[4,10,"Yet you have to keep saying Marcius is proud, a man who, at the lowest estimate, is worth all your ancestors since the Flood, though maybe some of the best of them were hangmen by family trade. Good evening to your worships. Any more of your conversation would infect my brain, since you're the herdsmen of those beastly plebeians. I'll make so bold as to take my leave of you."]],"397":[[0,4,"Everyone's talking about him, and people with bleary eyes put on spectacles to see him. The chattering nurse lets her baby scream itself into fits while she gossips about him. The kitchen maid pins her best linen round her grimy neck and climbs the walls to look at him."],[5,9,"Stalls, shop fronts and windows are packed, roofs filled and rooftops straddled by all sorts of people, all equally eager to see him. Priests who rarely show themselves push into the crowds and puff and pant to get a place among the common people."],[10,16,"Our veiled ladies let the war of white and red in their carefully made-up cheeks be ruined by the sun's burning kisses. Such a fuss, as if whatever god guides him had slipped secretly into his human body and given him grace."]],"409":[[0,4,"It has to turn out that way, for him or for our authority. To that end, we must remind the people how much he has always hated them: that if he'd had the power he'd have made them mules, silenced their spokesmen and stripped them of their freedoms;"],[5,10,"thinking of them, as human beings, as having no more soul or use in the world than camels in war, who get their food only for carrying loads and get hard blows when they sink under them."]],"427":[[0,3,"Having settled what to do about the Volscians and to send for Titus Lartius, it remains, as the main point of this further meeting, to reward the noble service of the man who has stood up for his country in this way."],[4,11,"So may it please you, most reverend and serious elders, to ask the present consul, our general in these well-won victories, to tell us a little of the worthy work done by Caius Marcius Coriolanus, whom we've met here both to thank and to honour as he deserves."]],"444":[[0,4,"I won't have voice enough; the deeds of Coriolanus shouldn't be told feebly. It's said that courage is the greatest virtue and brings most honour to whoever has it. If so, the man I'm speaking of has no equal in the world."],[5,15,"At sixteen, when Tarquin raised an army against Rome, he fought better than anyone. Our dictator then, whom I name with all praise, saw him fight, when with his beardless, Amazon-like chin he drove bearded men before him. He stood over a Roman who'd been knocked down and, in the consul's sight, killed three attackers. He met Tarquin himself and struck him to his knee. In that day's deeds, when he was young enough to play a woman's part on stage, he proved the best man on the field, and as his reward he was crowned with oak leaves."],[16,18,"Having entered manhood like that in his boyhood, he grew like a rising sea, and in the shock of seventeen battles since then he has robbed every other sword of the garland."],[19,32,"As for this last one, at and in Corioli, let me say I can't praise him enough. He stopped the men who were running away and by his rare example made cowards treat terror as a game. Like weeds before a ship under sail, men gave way and fell beneath his prow. His sword, death's seal, took whatever it marked. From head to foot he was a thing of blood, every movement keeping time with dying cries. Alone he entered the deadly city gate, which he painted with the blood of men who couldn't escape their fate; he got out without help, and with sudden reinforcements struck Corioli like a planet's evil influence. Now it's all his."],[33,40,"Then, when the noise of battle reached his alert ears, his doubled spirit revived his tired body, and he came to the battle, where he ran steaming over men's lives as if it would be endless slaughter. Until we could call both field and city ours, he never stopped to let his chest rest from panting."]],"520":[[0,2,"Sweetest voices! It's better to die, better to starve, than beg for the reward we've already earned."],[3,8,"Why should I stand here in this wolfish gown begging every Tom, Dick and Harry who comes along for their unnecessary approval? Custom makes me do it. If we did whatever custom wanted in everything, the dust of ancient times would lie unswept, and mountains of error would pile up too high for truth to see over them."],[9,13,"Rather than play the fool like this, let the high office and the honour go to someone who would. I'm halfway through; I've suffered one half, I'll do the other. Here come more voices."]],"555":[[0,8,"Couldn't you have told him, as you'd been taught, that when he had no power and was only a minor servant of the state, he was your enemy, always speaking against your liberties and the rights you hold in the commonwealth; and that now he's reaching a position of power and control in the state, if he stayed spitefully your firm enemy, your votes might turn into curses on yourselves?"],[9,14,"You should have said that, just as his worthy deeds deserved nothing less than what he was asking for, so his gracious nature should remember you for your votes and turn his malice towards you into love, as your friendly lord."]],"556":[[0,4,"Saying that, as you'd been advised, would have tested his spirit and tried his intentions. It would have dragged out of him either a gracious promise, which you could have held him to when the need came,"],[5,9,"or else it would have irritated his surly nature, which can't easily bear any condition that binds him. Then, having put him in a rage, you could have taken advantage of his anger and left him unelected."]],"563":[[0,4,"Let them assemble and, on second thoughts, all cancel your ignorant choice. Stress his pride and his old hatred of you. And don't forget how scornfully he wore the humble gown, how he despised you while asking."],[5,9,"But your love, thinking of his services, stopped you noticing how he was behaving now, which he shaped, in the most mocking and frivolous way, out of his deep-rooted hatred of you."]],"566":[[0,5,"Yes, don't spare us. Say we lectured you on how young he was when he started serving his country, how long he has continued, and what family he comes from: the noble house of the Marcii, which produced that Ancus Marcius, grandson of Numa, who was king here after great Hostilius;"],[6,10,"Publius and Quintus, who brought our best water here by aqueducts, were of the same house; and Censorinus, the people's darling, so nobly named because he was censor twice, was his great ancestor."]],"619":[[0,3,"Now, as I live, I will. My nobler friends, I ask your pardon. As for the fickle, foul-smelling mob, let them look at me, since I don't flatter, and see themselves in what I say."],[4,10,"I say again: by humouring them we're growing the weeds of rebellion, insolence and riot against our senate, which we ourselves ploughed for, sowed and scattered by mixing them with us, the honoured few, who lack no virtue, no, nor power either, except what we've given away to beggars."]],"630":[[0,6,"\"Will\"! Oh good but most unwise patricians! Why, you serious but careless senators, have you let the many-headed Hydra choose an official who, with his imperious \"will\", though he's only the horn and noise of the monster, has the nerve to say he'll divert your stream into a ditch and make your channel his own?"],[7,13,"If he has power, then bow down in your ignorance; if he has none, wake up from your dangerous softness. If you're wise, don't act like common fools; if you're not, let them sit on the senate cushions beside you. You're plebeians if they're senators, and they're no less than senators when, with both your voices mixed, the strongest flavour is theirs."],[14,22,"They choose their own magistrate, and one like this, who sets his \"will\", his mob's \"will\", against a more dignified senate than ever frowned in Greece. By Jove himself, it makes the consuls worthless, and it pains my soul to know that when two authorities are set up and neither is supreme, chaos can very soon slip into the gap between them and use one to destroy the other."]],"636":[[0,5,"I'll give my reasons, which are worth more than their votes. They know the grain wasn't a reward from us, since they're well aware they never did any service for it. When they were called up for war, even when the state was struck at its heart, they wouldn't go out through the gates. That kind of service didn't deserve free grain."],[6,10,"In the war, their mutinies and revolts, where they showed most courage, didn't speak well for them. The accusations they've often made against the senate, completely groundless, could never have been the reason for our so generous gift."],[11,20,"Well, what then? How will this blind mob take the senate's kindness? Let their actions show what they're likely to say: \"We demanded it; we're the majority, and they gave us what we wanted out of real fear.\" This is how we cheapen the nature of our office and let the rabble call our care fear, which will in time break open the senate's locks and let in the crows to peck at the eagles."]],"639":[[0,8,"No, take more. May everything one can swear by, divine and human, confirm what I end with! This divided rule, where one side scorns with reason and the other insults without any reason at all, where birth, title and wisdom can't decide anything without the yes and no of common ignorance, must neglect real needs and give way to fickle trifles. With purpose blocked like that, it follows that nothing purposeful gets done."],[9,14,"So I beg you, you who'd rather be wise than fearful, who love the foundations of the state more than you fear changing them, who prefer a noble life to a long one, and would risk a dangerous medicine on a body that will certainly die without it:"],[15,21,"pull out the mob's tongue at once. Don't let them lick the sweet that is their poison. Your dishonour mangles true judgment and robs the state of the integrity that should belong to it, leaving it without the power to do the good it wants because of the evil that controls it."]],"771":[[0,5,"Because now it's your duty to speak to the people, not as you'd choose yourself or with what your heart prompts, but with words that are rooted only in your tongue, illegitimate syllables that your heart's truth doesn't stand behind."],[6,9,"Now this dishonours you no more than capturing a town with gentle words, which otherwise would force you to trust to luck and risk much bloodshed."],[10,17,"I'd disguise my true nature if my fortunes and my friends were at stake and honour required it. I speak in this for your wife, your son, these senators, the nobles; and you'd rather show our common oafs how you can frown than give them one fawning look to win their love and protect what lacking it could ruin."]],"773":[[0,7,"Please now, my son, go to them with this cap in your hand, holding it out this far, like this, be with them; your knee kissing the stones (because in such business action is eloquence, and the eyes of ignorant people are more learned than their ears), nodding your head, and so often correcting your proud heart, which is now as humble as the ripest mulberry that falls apart when it's handled."],[8,14,"Or tell them you're their soldier, and being brought up in fighting you don't have the gentle manner which, you admit, it would be right for you to use and for them to expect when you ask for their love; but that from now on you'll make yourself theirs, indeed, as far as you have power and person."]],"785":[[0,4,"Well, I have to do it. Away with my true nature, and let some prostitute's spirit possess me! Let my war-trained throat, which sang in chorus with my drum, turn into a pipe as thin as a eunuch's or the girl's voice that lulls babies to sleep!"],[5,9,"Let villains' smiles camp in my cheeks, and schoolboys' tears fill my eyes! Let a beggar's tongue move between my lips, and my armoured knees, which have only bent in my stirrup, bow like a man who's been given charity!"],[10,13,"I won't do it, in case I stop honouring my own integrity and, by what my body does, teach my mind a deep-rooted baseness."]],"850":[[0,5,"Because he has, as far as he could, shown hatred against the people time and again, looking for ways to take away their power, and now at last has struck hostile blows, and not just in the presence of feared justice but against the officers who administer it:"],[6,12,"in the name of the people and by the power of us, the tribunes, we banish him from our city from this moment, on pain of being thrown off the Tarpeian rock, never to enter Rome's gates again. In the people's name, I say it shall be so."]],"858":[[0,4,"You common pack of curs, whose breath I hate like the stink of rotten swamps, whose love I value like the dead bodies of unburied men that poison my air: I banish you! Stay here with your fickleness!"],[5,7,"May every feeble rumour make your hearts shake! May your enemies, nodding their plumed helmets, fan you into despair! Keep the power to banish your defenders,"],[8,12,"until at last your ignorance, which understands nothing until it feels it, keeps only yourselves, always your own enemies, and hands you over as the most humbled prisoners to some nation that conquered you without a blow!"],[13,15,"Despising the city because of you, I turn my back on it like this. There is a world elsewhere."]],"868":[[0,5,"Come, stop crying; a short goodbye. The beast with many heads butts me out. No, mother, where's your old courage? You used to say extreme trouble tests the spirit; that ordinary men can bear ordinary luck; that when the sea is calm, all boats look equally skilled at floating;"],[6,10,"that when fortune's blows strike deepest, staying gentle under the wound takes noble skill. You used to load me with rules that would make the heart that learned them unbeatable."]],"872":[[0,4,"What, what, what! They'll love me when they miss me. No, mother, take up again the spirit you had when you used to say that if you'd been Hercules's wife, you'd have done six of his labours and saved your husband all that sweat."],[5,8,"Cominius, don't be downcast. Goodbye. Farewell, my wife, my mother. I'll do well yet. You old and faithful Menenius, your tears are saltier than a younger man's and poison to your eyes."],[9,12,"My former general, I've seen you stern, and you've often seen sights that harden the heart. Tell these sad women it's as foolish to wail at blows that can't be avoided as it is to laugh at them."],[13,19,"Mother, you know well my dangers have always been your comfort; and believe me, though I go alone, like a solitary dragon whose swamp is feared and talked about more than seen, your son will either outdo ordinary men or be caught by crafty bait and trickery."]],"876":[[0,3,"Farewell. You're old, and you've had too much of war to go wandering with someone who's still unbruised. Just see me out of the gate."],[4,9,"Come, my sweet wife, my dearest mother, and my noble friends. When I'm outside, say goodbye to me and smile. Please, come. While I'm still above ground, you'll keep hearing from me, and never anything about me but what's like what I've been before."]],"953":[[0,5,"Oh world, how slippery your turns are! Friends sworn true now, whose two breasts seem to hold one heart, who always spend their hours, their bed, their meals and their exercise together, who are like twins in their inseparable love, will within the hour break out into the bitterest hatred over a quarrel about a penny."],[6,9,"In the same way, the deadliest enemies, whose passions and schemes to catch each other have kept them awake, will by some chance, some trick not worth an egg, become dear friends and join their children in marriage."],[10,14,"So it is with me. I hate my birthplace, and my love has turned to this enemy town. I'll go in. If he kills me, he does fair justice; if he takes me in, I'll serve his country."]],"1009":[[0,7,"My name is Caius Marcius, who has done great harm and damage to you in particular and to all the Volscians; my surname, Coriolanus, is witness to it. The painful service, the extreme dangers and the drops of blood shed for my ungrateful country are repaid only with that surname, a good reminder and proof of the hatred and anger you ought to feel for me."],[8,12,"Only the name remains. The cruelty and spite of the people, allowed by our cowardly nobles, who have all abandoned me, has swallowed up the rest and let me be hooted out of Rome by the voices of slaves."],[13,18,"Now this desperate state has brought me to your hearth, not in hope of saving my life, don't mistake me, because if I'd feared death, I'd have avoided you more than any man in the world, but out of pure spite, to get even with those who banished me, I stand here before you."],[19,26,"So if you have a heart full of vengeance that will avenge your own personal wrongs and stop those shameful wounds seen all over your country, hurry and make my misery serve your purpose. Use it so that my revengeful services prove a benefit to you, because I'll fight against my rotten country with the fury of all the devils in hell."],[27,36,"But if you don't dare to do this and you're too tired to try your luck any more, then, in a word, I'm also very tired of living, and I offer my throat to you and your old hatred. Not to cut it would only show you a fool, since I've always pursued you with hatred, drawn barrels of blood from your country's breast, and can only live to your shame, unless it's to serve you."]],"1010":[[0,4,"Oh Marcius, Marcius! Every word you've spoken has rooted out of my heart a root of old hatred. If Jupiter spoke divine things from that cloud and said \"It's true\", I wouldn't believe him more than you, all-noble Marcius."],[5,11,"Let me wind my arms around that body against which my ash-wood spear has broken a hundred times and scared the moon with splinters. Here I hug the anvil of my sword, and I compete as hotly and nobly with your love as I ever fought against your courage in ambitious strength."],[12,16,"Know this first: I loved the girl I married; no man ever sighed more truly. But seeing you here, you noble thing, makes my enraptured heart dance more than when I first saw my bride step over my threshold."],[17,24,"Why, you Mars! I tell you, we have an army ready, and I intended once more to hack your shield from your arm or lose my own arm trying. You've beaten me twelve separate times, and every night since I've dreamt of fights between us. We've been on the ground together in my sleep, unbuckling helmets, grabbing each other's throats, and I've woken half dead from nothing."],[25,34,"Worthy Marcius, if we had no other quarrel with Rome but that you're banished from it, we'd call up every man from twelve to seventy and, pouring war into the guts of ungrateful Rome, overwhelm it like a bold flood. Oh, come in and shake hands with our friendly senators, who are here now taking leave of me; I'm ready to march against your lands, though not against Rome itself."]],"1012":[[0,6,"So, most perfect sir, if you want to lead your own revenge, take half of my command and decide your own course, as you're most experienced and know your country's strengths and weaknesses: whether to knock at the gates of Rome, or roughly attack its outlying parts to frighten them before destroying them."],[7,11,"But come in. Let me introduce you first to those who'll agree to what you want. A thousand welcomes! And more a friend than ever an enemy; and yet, Marcius, that was a great deal. Your hand: most welcome!"]],"1039":[[0,3,"Why, they're making as much of him inside as if he were Mars's son and heir. He's seated at the head of the table; none of the senators asks him a question without standing bareheaded before him. Our general himself treats him like a mistress, touches his hand as if it were holy, and rolls his eyes upward at what he says."],[4,9,"But the heart of the news is that our general has been cut in half and is only half of what he was yesterday, because the other man has the other half, at the request and with the consent of the whole table. He says he'll go and drag the porter of Rome's gates by the ears; he'll mow down everything in his way and leave his path cleared."]],"1122":[[0,3,"Here come the mobs. And is Aufidius with him? You're the ones who poisoned the air when you threw up your stinking greasy caps, hooting at Coriolanus's exile."],[4,10,"Now he's coming, and there isn't a hair on any soldier's head that won't be a whip. He'll knock down as many fools' heads as you threw up caps, and pay you back for your votes. It doesn't matter. If he burned us all into one cinder, we'd have deserved it."]],"1146":[[0,4,"I understand you well. And be sure of this: when he comes to account for himself, he doesn't know what I can charge him with. Although it seems, and he thinks so, and it's no less obvious to ordinary eyes, that he conducts everything fairly,"],[5,9,"manages well for the Volscian state, fights like a dragon and wins as soon as he draws his sword, still he has left something undone that will break his neck or risk mine whenever we come to settle our accounts."]],"1148":[[0,6,"Every place surrenders to him before he lays siege. The nobility of Rome are on his side, and the senators and patricians love him too. The tribunes are no soldiers, and their people will be as rash in recalling him as they were hasty in throwing him out. I think he'll be to Rome what the osprey is to the fish, which it takes by natural supremacy."],[7,19,"At first he was a noble servant to them, but he couldn't carry his honours steadily. Whether it was pride, which always infects the lucky man who succeeds every day; whether a lack of judgment, failing to make the most of chances he controlled; or whether his nature, not to be anything but one thing, not changing from the helmet to the senate cushion, but ruling in peace with the same harshness and manner as he commanded in war: one of these (he has traces of them all, though not all completely, I'll clear him that far) made him feared, so hated, and so banished."],[20,25,"But he has merit enough to choke the charges as they're spoken. So our virtues depend on how the times interpret them, and power, however praiseworthy in itself, has no tomb so certain as a public platform that praises what it has done."],[26,29,"One fire drives out another, one nail another; rights give way to rights, strengths fail by strengths. Come, let's go. When Rome is yours, Caius, you'll be the poorest of all; and then you'll soon be mine."]],"1168":[[0,5,"I'll take it on. I think he'll listen to me. Still, his biting his lip and grumbling at good Cominius discourages me a lot. He didn't catch him at the right moment; he hadn't eaten. When our veins aren't filled, our blood is cold, and then we sulk at the morning and aren't inclined to give or forgive."],[6,11,"But when we've stuffed these pipes and channels of our blood with wine and food, our spirits are more flexible than when we fast like priests. So I'll wait until he's been fed enough for my request, and then I'll go at him."]],"1174":[[0,7,"I tell you, he sits on a golden throne, his eye red as if it would burn Rome, and the wrong done to him keeps his pity locked up like a jailer. I knelt before him; he said \"Rise\" very faintly and dismissed me like this, with a silent wave of his hand. He sent after me in writing what he would do, and what he wouldn't, being bound by an oath to keep to his conditions. So all hope is useless,"],[8,11,"unless his noble mother and his wife, who I hear mean to beg him to have mercy on his country, can do it. So let's go and hurry them on with our earnest pleas."]],"1187":[[0,5,"I tell you, fellow, your general is my dear friend. I've been the book of his good deeds, from which men have read his unequalled fame, perhaps exaggerated, because I've always praised my friends, of whom he's the chief, as fully as truth would allow without slipping;"],[6,10,"indeed, sometimes, like a bowl on tricky ground, I've rolled past the mark, and in praising him I've almost stamped lies as true. So, fellow, I must be allowed to pass."]],"1194":[[0,4,"Then you should hate Rome, as he does. When you've pushed the very defender of your gates out of them and, in violent mob ignorance, given your shield to your enemy, can you think you'll face his revenge with old women's feeble groans, your daughters' virgin hands raised in prayer, or the shaky pleading of a worn-out old dodderer like you seem to be?"],[5,9,"Can you think you'll blow out the fire your city's about to blaze in with such weak breath as this? No, you're mistaken. So go back to Rome and get ready to be executed. You're condemned; our general has sworn you beyond reprieve or pardon."]],"1202":[[0,5,"Now, you fellow, I'll put in a word for you. You'll see now that I'm respected; you'll find that a guard dog of a soldier can't keep me from my son Coriolanus. Just judge by how he receives me whether you're not in line for hanging, or some death longer to watch and crueller to suffer. Watch now, and faint at what's coming to you."],[6,10,"(To Coriolanus) May the glorious gods sit in council every hour about your personal good fortune, and love you no less than your old father Menenius does! Oh my son, my son! You're preparing fire for us; look, here's water to put it out. I was hard to persuade to come to you; but being assured no one but me could move you, I've been blown out of our gates by sighs,"],[11,14,"and I beg you to pardon Rome and your pleading countrymen. May the good gods soften your anger and turn the dregs of it on this rascal here, this one who, like a block of wood, has refused to let me through to you."]],"1223":[[0,3,"This last old man, whom I've sent back to Rome with a broken heart, loved me more than a father; in fact, he made a god of me. Sending him was their last resort."],[4,11,"For the sake of my old love for him, though I was sour to him, I offered the first terms again, which they refused and can't accept now. Only to honour him, who thought he could do more, I've given way a very little. I won't listen to any new embassies or pleas, either from the state or from personal friends, from now on."]],"1227":[[0,4,"My wife comes first; then the honoured mould this body was formed in, and holding her hand, her grandchild. But away, affection! Let every bond and right of nature break! Let it be virtuous to be stubborn."],[5,10,"What's that curtsy worth? Or those dove's eyes, which could make gods break their oaths? I'm melting; I'm not made of stronger earth than other men. My mother bows, as if Mount Olympus bowed in pleading to a molehill; and my young boy has a pleading look to which great nature cries, \"Don't refuse.\""],[11,15,"Let the Volscians plough Rome and rake over Italy; I'll never be such a gosling as to obey instinct, but stand as if a man were his own creator and had no other family."]],"1231":[[0,3,"Like a bad actor now, I've forgotten my part and lost my lines, to my complete disgrace. Best part of my flesh, forgive my cruelty; but don't say because of that, \"Forgive our Romans.\""],[4,10,"Oh, a kiss as long as my exile, as sweet as my revenge! Now, by Juno, jealous queen of heaven, I've carried that kiss from you, dear, and my faithful lip has kept it untouched ever since. You gods! I'm chattering and leaving the most noble mother in the world ungreeted. Sink, my knee, into the earth;"]],"1247":[[0,8,"Even if we kept silent and didn't speak, our clothes and the state of our bodies would show what life we've led since your exile. Think how much more unfortunate than all living women we are in coming here, since the sight of you, which should make our eyes flow with joy and our hearts dance with comfort, forces them to weep and shake with fear and sorrow, making the mother, wife and child see the son, husband and father tearing out his country's guts."],[9,16,"And to poor us your hatred is most deadly. You stop us praying to the gods, a comfort everyone but us enjoys; because how can we, alas, how can we pray for our country, as we're bound to, and also for your victory, as we're bound to? Alas, either we must lose our country, our dear nurse, or else you, our comfort in that country."],[17,23,"We're bound to meet obvious disaster even if we had our wish about which side should win; because either you must be led in chains through our streets as a foreign traitor, or else tread in triumph on your country's ruins and win the prize for bravely shedding your wife's and children's blood."],[24,31,"As for me, son, I don't intend to wait on fortune until these wars are decided. If I can't persuade you to show noble grace to both sides rather than seek the destruction of one, you won't march to attack your country without treading (believe me, you won't) on your mother's womb that brought you into this world."]],"1252":[[0,8,"No, don't leave us like this. If our request were meant to save the Romans by destroying the Volscians you serve, you could condemn us as poisoners of your honour. No; our plea is that you reconcile them, so that the Volscians can say, \"We showed this mercy\", and the Romans, \"We received it\", and both sides hail you and cry, \"Bless you for making this peace!\""],[9,16,"You know, great son, the outcome of war is uncertain; but this is certain: if you conquer Rome, the reward you'll reap is a name that will be followed by curses whenever it's repeated, whose history will read: \"The man was noble, but with his last act he wiped it out, destroyed his country, and his name is hated by the generations that followed.\""],[17,21,"Speak to me, son. You've aimed at the finest heights of honour, to imitate the graces of the gods, to tear the wide cheeks of the sky with thunder, and yet to load your lightning with a bolt that would only split an oak."],[22,28,"Why don't you speak? Do you think it's honourable for a noble man to keep remembering wrongs? Daughter, you speak; he doesn't care about your crying. You speak, boy; maybe your childishness will move him more than our reasons can. There's no man in the world who owes more to his mother, yet here he lets me prattle like someone in the stocks."],[29,36,"You've never in your life shown your dear mother any kindness, while she, poor hen, wanting no second brood, has clucked you off to the wars and safely home again, loaded with honour. Say my request is unjust and kick me away; but if it isn't, you're not honest, and the gods will punish you for keeping from me the duty owed to a mother."],[37,41,"He turns away. Down, ladies; let's shame him with our knees. His surname Coriolanus has more pride in it than pity for our prayers. Down; let's finish. This is the last. Then we'll go home to Rome and die among our neighbours."],[42,45,"No, look at us. This boy, who can't say what he wants but kneels and holds up his hands with us, argues our case with more strength than you have to refuse it."],[46,51,"Come, let's go. This fellow had a Volscian for a mother; his wife is in Corioli, and his child looks like him by chance. Still, send us away with an answer. I'll be silent until our city is on fire, and then I'll say a little."]],"1255":[[0,7,"Oh mother, mother! What have you done? Look, the heavens open, the gods look down and laugh at this unnatural scene. Oh my mother, mother! Oh! You've won a happy victory for Rome; but for your son, believe it, oh believe it, you've beaten him in a way that's most dangerous to him, if not deadly. But let it come."],[8,11,"Aufidius, though I can't fight this war properly, I'll make a suitable peace. Now, good Aufidius, if you were in my place, would you have listened to a mother less? Or granted less, Aufidius?"]],"1311":[[0,5,"I was going to mention that. Banished for it, he came to my hearth and offered his throat to my knife. I took him in, made him my joint commander, let him have his way in everything he wanted; even let him choose my best and freshest troops from my ranks to carry out his plans."],[6,12,"I served his schemes in person, helped to reap the fame he made all his own, and took a sort of pride in doing myself this wrong; until in the end I seemed his follower, not his partner, and he paid me with his patronizing looks as if I'd been a hired soldier."]],"1326":[[0,5,"Greetings, lords! I've come back your soldier, no more infected with love of my country than when I left here, but still serving under your great command. You should know that I've succeeded, and led your wars by a bloody path right up to the gates of Rome."],[6,13,"The loot we've brought home more than pays a full third of the cost of the campaign. We've made a peace that brings as much honour to the people of Antium as shame to the Romans; and here we hand over, signed by the consuls and patricians and with the senate's seal, the terms we've agreed."]],"1331":[[0,2,"Yes, Marcius, Caius Marcius! Do you think I'll honour you with that theft, your stolen name Coriolanus, here in Corioli?"],[3,7,"You lords and leaders of the state, he has treacherously betrayed your cause and given up your city Rome, I say your city, for a few salty drops, to his wife and mother, breaking his oath and his resolve like a strand of rotten silk,"],[8,12,"never asking the war council's advice. At his nurse's tears he whined and bawled your victory away, so that pageboys blushed at him and brave men looked at each other in amazement."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"pride":["Pride and honour","Coriolanus's pride, his hatred of praise and his idea of what a noble man owes no one."],"people":["The people and power","Plebeians, patricians and tribunes: who rules Rome, and who counts as the city."],"mother":["Mothers and sons","Volumnia's shaping of her son, and the bond that saves Rome and kills him."],"war":["War, wounds and blood","Battle, scars, blood and the soldier's body as the measure of worth."],"names":["Names and identity","Marcius, Coriolanus, 'a kind of nothing': who he is when his names are taken."],"body":["Body, food and hunger","The belly fable, famine, grain and the state as a body that can sicken."],"acting":["Words, acting and flattery","Voices, flattery, performing humility and the refusal to play a part."],"rivals":["Rivals and enemies","Coriolanus and Aufidius, hatred and love between enemies."]},"quotes":[{"id":"11","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","text":"our sufferance is a gain to them.","themes":["people","body"],"note":"A sharp case against the rich."},{"id":"11","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","text":"I speak this in hunger for bread, not in thirst for revenge.","themes":["body","people"],"note":"The citizen insists his motive is need, not malice."},{"id":"17","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","text":"he did it to please his mother, and to be partly proud","themes":["mother","pride"],"note":"The people see through the hero from the start."},{"id":"29","who":"MENENIUS","text":"The gods, not the patricians, make it","themes":["people","body"],"note":"Menenius blames heaven for the famine."},{"id":"30","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","text":"If the wars eat us not up, they will; and there's all the love they bear us.","themes":["people","body"],"note":"The rich devour the poor."},{"id":"33","who":"MENENIUS","text":"There was a time when all the body's members Rebell'd against the belly","themes":["body","people"],"note":"The famous fable begins."},{"id":"43","who":"MENENIUS","text":"Because I am the storehouse and the shop Of the whole body","themes":["body"],"note":"The belly's defence of the senate."},{"id":"47","who":"MENENIUS","text":"The senators of Rome are this good belly, And you the mutinous members","themes":["body","people"],"note":"The moral of the fable."},{"id":"52","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"What's the matter, you dissentious rogues That, rubbing the poor itch of your opinion, Make yourselves scabs?","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"His first words to the people are insults."},{"id":"54","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Where he should find you lions, finds you hares; Where foxes, geese","themes":["people"],"note":"Animal insults for the crowd."},{"id":"54","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Who deserves greatness Deserves your hate","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"His view of the people's envy."},{"id":"56","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I'd make a quarry With thousands of these quarter'd slaves","themes":["people","war"],"note":"He would cut the people down."},{"id":"60","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"The rabble should have first unroof'd the city Ere so prevail'd with me","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"His fury at the tribunes' creation."},{"id":"67","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"then we shall ha' means to vent Our musty superfluity.","themes":["war","people"],"note":"War as a way to get rid of the poor."},{"id":"70","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I sin in envying his nobility;","themes":["rivals"],"note":"He admires only his enemy."},{"id":"72","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"he is a lion That I am proud to hunt.","themes":["rivals","pride"],"note":"His enemy is a worthy prey."},{"id":"85","who":"SICINIUS","text":"Was ever man so proud as is this Marcius?","themes":["pride","people"],"note":"The tribunes' first verdict."},{"id":"92","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Too proud to be so valiant.","themes":["pride","war"],"note":"Pride outweighs courage."},{"id":"109","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"'Tis sworn between us we shall ever strike Till one can do no more.","themes":["rivals","war"],"note":"Aufidius's vow."},{"id":"118","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"if my son were my husband, I should freelier rejoice in that absence wherein he won honour","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"Volumnia's love is ambition."},{"id":"118","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"To a cruel war I sent him","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"She sent him to war as a boy."},{"id":"118","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"than now in first seeing he had proved himself a man.","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"Manhood means war."},{"id":"120","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"I had rather had eleven die nobly for their country than one voluptuously surfeit out of action.","themes":["mother","war","pride"],"note":"Honour above life."},{"id":"125","who":"VIRGILIA","text":"His bloody brow! O Jupiter, no blood!","themes":["war","mother"],"note":"The wife's fear against the mother's pride."},{"id":"126","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"It more becomes a man Than gilt his trophy","themes":["war","mother"],"note":"Blood is better than gold."},{"id":"126","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"the breasts of Hecuba, When she did suckle Hector, looked not lovelier Than Hector's forehead when it spit forth blood","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"Milk and blood."},{"id":"129","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"He'll beat Aufidius' head below his knee, And tread upon his neck.","themes":["rivals","mother"],"note":"Volumnia's violent fantasy."},{"id":"137","who":"VALERIA","text":"O, I warrant, how he mammocked it!","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"The boy tears the butterfly."},{"id":"190","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"You souls of geese That bear the shapes of men","themes":["war","people"],"note":"Rage at fleeing soldiers."},{"id":"204","who":"LARTIUS","text":"Thou wast a soldier Even to Cato's wish","themes":["war"],"note":"Lartius's lament for him."},{"id":"218","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"The blood I drop is rather physical Than dangerous to me","themes":["war","body"],"note":"Wounds as healthy bloodletting."},{"id":"251","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Set me against Aufidius and his Antiates","themes":["rivals","war"],"note":"He wants only his rival."},{"id":"255","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"O, me alone! Make you a sword of me?","themes":["war","names"],"note":"He becomes a weapon."},{"id":"266","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I'll fight with none but thee, for I do hate thee Worse than a promise-breaker.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Hatred as a kind of loyalty."},{"id":"267","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"Not Afric owns a serpent I abhor More than thy fame and envy.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Aufidius hates his fame."},{"id":"273","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"you have sham'd me In your condemned seconds.","themes":["rivals","pride"],"note":"Help as humiliation."},{"id":"280","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"When she does praise me grieves me.","themes":["mother","pride"],"note":"He cannot bear praise, even hers."},{"id":"284","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"A bribe to pay my sword","themes":["pride","war"],"note":"He refuses reward."},{"id":"286","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"As if I loved my little should be dieted In praises sauc'd with lies.","themes":["pride","acting","body"],"note":"Praise as food."},{"id":"287","who":"COMINIUS","text":"'Caius Marcius Coriolanus.'","themes":["names"],"note":"The new name."},{"id":"305","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"I would I were a Roman; for I cannot, Being a Volsce, be that I am.","themes":["rivals","names"],"note":"Defeat has cost Aufidius his self."},{"id":"305","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"Or wrath or craft may get him.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Honour gives way to cunning."},{"id":"356","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"O, he is wounded, I thank the gods for't.","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"Shocking joy."},{"id":"371","who":"MENENIUS","text":"every gash was an enemy's grave.","themes":["war"],"note":"Wounds as graves."},{"id":"374","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"before him He carries noise, and behind him he leaves tears","themes":["war","mother"],"note":"Her son as death."},{"id":"384","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"My gracious silence, hail!","themes":["names"],"note":"His name for his wife."},{"id":"394","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I had rather be their servant in my way Than sway with them in theirs.","themes":["pride","people"],"note":"He will not rule on their terms."},{"id":"397","who":"BRUTUS","text":"All tongues speak of him and the bleared sights Are spectacled to see him","themes":["people"],"note":"Celebrity in Rome."},{"id":"403","who":"BRUTUS","text":"The napless vesture of humility","themes":["acting","people"],"note":"The gown of humility."},{"id":"410","who":"SICINIUS","text":"will be his fire To kindle their dry stubble","themes":["people"],"note":"The tribunes' plan."},{"id":"422","who":"SECOND OFFICER","text":"if they love they know not why, they hate upon no better a ground","themes":["people"],"note":"The fickleness of popular love."},{"id":"437","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I had rather have my wounds to heal again Than hear say how I got them.","themes":["pride","war"],"note":"He hates praise."},{"id":"441","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"To hear my nothings monster'd.","themes":["pride","acting"],"note":"Praise as exaggeration."},{"id":"444","who":"COMINIUS","text":"valour is the chiefest virtue","themes":["war","pride"],"note":"Rome's highest value."},{"id":"444","who":"COMINIUS","text":"He was a thing of blood, whose every motion Was timed with dying cries","themes":["war"],"note":"A war machine."},{"id":"455","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Put on the gown, stand naked, and entreat them","themes":["acting","war"],"note":"He cannot strip for votes."},{"id":"458","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"It is a part That I shall blush in acting","themes":["acting"],"note":"Politics as theatre."},{"id":"473","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","text":"the many-headed multitude","themes":["people"],"note":"The people quote his insult."},{"id":"485","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I cannot bring My tongue to such a pace.","themes":["acting"],"note":"He cannot beg."},{"id":"514","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I will counterfeit the bewitchment of some popular man","themes":["acting","people"],"note":"Mock-flattery."},{"id":"520","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Better it is to die, better to starve, Than crave the hire which first we do deserve.","themes":["pride","acting"],"note":"His creed."},{"id":"520","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Why in this wolvish toge should I stand here","themes":["acting"],"note":"The gown as a disguise."},{"id":"522","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Your voices: for your voices I have fought;","themes":["people","acting","war"],"note":"The mocking repetition."},{"id":"553","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","text":"was not this mockery?","themes":["people","acting"],"note":"The citizens noticed."},{"id":"557","who":"BRUTUS","text":"He did solicit you in free contempt","themes":["people","acting"],"note":"The tribune turns the vote."},{"id":"585","who":"LARTIUS","text":"That of all things upon the earth he hated Your person most","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Aufidius's hatred reported."},{"id":"602","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"You being their mouths, why rule you not their teeth?","themes":["people","body"],"note":"Mouths and teeth."},{"id":"613","who":"SICINIUS","text":"You show too much of that For which the people stir","themes":["pride","people"],"note":"Pride is the issue."},{"id":"619","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"For the mutable, rank-scented many","themes":["people"],"note":"The stink of the crowd."},{"id":"623","who":"BRUTUS","text":"As if you were a god, to punish, not A man of their infirmity.","themes":["pride","people"],"note":"The tribunes' charge."},{"id":"626","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Were I as patient as the midnight sleep, By Jove, 'twould be my mind!","themes":["pride"],"note":"He will not change his mind."},{"id":"628","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Hear you this Triton of the minnows?","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"Contempt for Sicinius."},{"id":"630","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"have you thus Given Hydra leave to choose an officer","themes":["people"],"note":"The many-headed monster."},{"id":"636","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"The crows to peck the eagles.","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"His fear of the people's power."},{"id":"639","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"at once pluck out The multitudinous tongue","themes":["people","acting"],"note":"Abolish the tribunes."},{"id":"639","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"prefer A noble life before a long","themes":["pride","war"],"note":"Honour over survival."},{"id":"667","who":"SICINIUS","text":"What is the city but the people?","themes":["people"],"note":"The tribunes' creed."},{"id":"682","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"No; I'll die here.","themes":["pride","war"],"note":"He draws his sword."},{"id":"704","who":"MENENIUS","text":"His nature is too noble for the world:","themes":["pride"],"note":"Menenius's verdict."},{"id":"704","who":"MENENIUS","text":"He would not flatter Neptune for his trident, Or Jove for's power to thunder.","themes":["pride","acting"],"note":"No flattery, even to gods."},{"id":"710","who":"SICINIUS","text":"Where is this viper That would depopulate the city and Be every man himself?","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"The tribune's view of tyranny."},{"id":"727","who":"SICINIUS","text":"He's a disease that must be cut away.","themes":["body","people"],"note":"The state as a body."},{"id":"728","who":"MENENIUS","text":"O, he's a limb that has but a disease;","themes":["body"],"note":"Menenius answers."},{"id":"750","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Would you have me False to my nature? Rather say, I play The man I am.","themes":["acting","pride","mother"],"note":"Being and playing."},{"id":"751","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"I would have had you put your power well on Before you had worn it out.","themes":["mother","acting"],"note":"Volumnia's politics."},{"id":"759","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"I have a heart as little apt as yours, But yet a brain that leads my use of anger To better vantage.","themes":["mother","acting"],"note":"Policy over passion."},{"id":"766","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Honour and policy, like unsever'd friends, I' the war do grow together","themes":["mother","acting","war"],"note":"Deceit as honourable."},{"id":"771","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"such words that are but rooted in Your tongue","themes":["acting","mother"],"note":"Words not from the heart."},{"id":"773","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Action is eloquence, and the eyes of the ignorant More learned than the ears","themes":["acting","people"],"note":"Theatre for the crowd."},{"id":"775","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Follow thine enemy in a fiery gulf Than flatter him in a bower.","themes":["mother","pride","acting"],"note":"She knows her son."},{"id":"782","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"You have put me now to such a part which never I shall discharge to the life.","themes":["acting"],"note":"He cannot play the part."},{"id":"785","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"possess me Some harlot's spirit!","themes":["acting","pride"],"note":"Acting as degradation."},{"id":"785","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"And by my body's action teach my mind A most inherent baseness.","themes":["acting","body"],"note":"Acting changes the actor."},{"id":"786","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Thy valiantness was mine, thou suck'dst it from me; But owe thy pride thyself.","themes":["mother","pride"],"note":"Volumnia's famous rebuke."},{"id":"787","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I'll mountebank their loves, Cog their hearts from them","themes":["acting","people"],"note":"He promises to cheat them."},{"id":"793","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Well, mildly be it then; mildly.","themes":["acting"],"note":"The promise he cannot keep."},{"id":"839","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"The fires i' the lowest hell fold in the people!","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"His explosion."},{"id":"849","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I would not buy Their mercy at the price of one fair word","themes":["pride","acting"],"note":"No compromise."},{"id":"858","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"You common cry of curs!","themes":["people"],"note":"His last words to Rome's people."},{"id":"858","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I banish you;","themes":["people","pride","names"],"note":"He reverses the sentence."},{"id":"858","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"There is a world elsewhere.","themes":["pride","names"],"note":"His exit from Rome."},{"id":"868","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"the beast With many heads butts me away.","themes":["people"],"note":"The many-headed beast."},{"id":"872","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I shall be lov'd when I am lack'd.","themes":["people","pride"],"note":"He predicts the people's change of heart."},{"id":"872","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Like to a lonely dragon, that his fen Makes fear'd and talk'd of more than seen","themes":["names","war"],"note":"The lonely dragon."},{"id":"898","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Hadst thou foxship To banish him that struck more blows for Rome Than thou hast spoken words?","themes":["mother","people"],"note":"Volumnia attacks."},{"id":"916","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Anger's my meat; I sup upon myself, And so shall starve with feeding.","themes":["mother","body"],"note":"Anger as food."},{"id":"953","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"O world, thy slippery turns!","themes":["names","rivals"],"note":"His only soliloquy."},{"id":"953","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"My birthplace hate I, and my love's upon This enemy town.","themes":["names","rivals"],"note":"The turn against Rome."},{"id":"992","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"I' the city of kites and crows.","themes":["names","war"],"note":"A city of scavengers."},{"id":"1009","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"My name is Caius Marcius","themes":["names"],"note":"He names himself to his enemy."},{"id":"1009","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"only that name remains;","themes":["names"],"note":"All he has left."},{"id":"1009","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Against my canker'd country with the spleen Of all the under fiends.","themes":["rivals","war"],"note":"Revenge on Rome."},{"id":"1010","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"here I clip The anvil of my sword","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Aufidius embraces his enemy."},{"id":"1010","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"Than when I first my wedded mistress saw Bestride my threshold.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Love language between rivals."},{"id":"1047","who":"FIRST SERVANT","text":"Let me have war, say I; it exceeds peace as far as day does night","themes":["war"],"note":"Comic praise of war."},{"id":"1108","who":"COMINIUS","text":"He is their god","themes":["war","names"],"note":"The Volscians worship him."},{"id":"1126","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","text":"though we willingly consented to his banishment, yet it was against our will.","themes":["people"],"note":"The people's confusion."},{"id":"1148","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"As is the osprey to the fish, who takes it By sovereignty of nature.","themes":["rivals","war"],"note":"Coriolanus as a bird of prey."},{"id":"1148","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"Not to be other than one thing","themes":["pride","names"],"note":"His single nature."},{"id":"1148","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"So our virtues Lie in the interpretation of the time","themes":["pride","people"],"note":"Virtue depends on the times."},{"id":"1148","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"When, Caius, Rome is thine, Thou art poor'st of all; then shortly art thou mine.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Aufidius's plan."},{"id":"1155","who":"COMINIUS","text":"He was a kind of nothing, titleless,","themes":["names"],"note":"No name at all."},{"id":"1155","who":"COMINIUS","text":"Till he had forg'd himself a name i' the fire Of burning Rome.","themes":["names","war"],"note":"A name made from destruction."},{"id":"1168","who":"MENENIUS","text":"He was not taken well: he had not din'd;","themes":["body"],"note":"Menenius's comic hope."},{"id":"1205","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Wife, mother, child, I know not.","themes":["mother","names"],"note":"He denies his family."},{"id":"1217","who":"SECOND GUARD","text":"he is the rock, the oak not to be wind-shaken.","themes":["pride"],"note":"The guards' view of him."},{"id":"1227","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Let it be virtuous to be obstinate.","themes":["pride","mother"],"note":"He tries to stand firm."},{"id":"1227","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"As if a man were author of himself, And knew no other kin.","themes":["names","mother","pride"],"note":"The impossible dream of self-creation."},{"id":"1231","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Like a dull actor now, I have forgot my part","themes":["acting","mother"],"note":"He loses his lines."},{"id":"1231","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"O, a kiss Long as my exile, sweet as my revenge","themes":["mother","rivals"],"note":"Tenderness and revenge."},{"id":"1236","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Then let the pebbles on the hungry beach Fillip the stars","themes":["mother"],"note":"A mother kneeling is unnatural."},{"id":"1237","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Thou art my warrior; I holp to frame thee.","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"She made him."},{"id":"1249","who":"YOUNG MARCIUS","text":"I'll run away till I am bigger; but then I'll fight.","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"The next generation."},{"id":"1252","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"such a name Whose repetition will be dogg'd with curses","themes":["names","mother"],"note":"Volumnia's strongest argument."},{"id":"1252","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"'The man was noble, But with his last attempt he wip'd it out","themes":["names","pride"],"note":"The epitaph she threatens."},{"id":"1252","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"Think'st thou it honourable for a noble man Still to remember wrongs?","themes":["pride","mother"],"note":"Honour and mercy."},{"id":"1252","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"When she,—poor hen,—fond of no second brood, Has cluck'd thee to the wars","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"The mother hen."},{"id":"1252","who":"VOLUMNIA","text":"This fellow had a Volscian to his mother;","themes":["mother","names"],"note":"Her last shaming."},{"id":"1255","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"O mother, mother! What have you done?","themes":["mother"],"note":"The turning point."},{"id":"1255","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"Most dangerously you have with him prevail'd, If not most mortal to him.","themes":["mother"],"note":"He knows the cost."},{"id":"1258","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"out of that I'll work Myself a former fortune.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Aufidius's aside."},{"id":"1268","who":"MENENIUS","text":"This Marcius is grown from man to dragon","themes":["names","war"],"note":"Transformation."},{"id":"1272","who":"MENENIUS","text":"There is no more mercy in him than there is milk in a male tiger","themes":["mother","war"],"note":"No mercy."},{"id":"1295","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"Behold our patroness, the life of Rome!","themes":["mother","people"],"note":"Volumnia's triumph."},{"id":"1313","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"At a few drops of women's rheum, which are As cheap as lies","themes":["rivals","mother"],"note":"Contempt for tears."},{"id":"1331","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"thy stol'n name Coriolanus, in Corioli?","themes":["names","rivals"],"note":"The name taken away."},{"id":"1331","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"at his nurse's tears He whin'd and roar'd away your victory","themes":["mother","rivals"],"note":"Aufidius's charge."},{"id":"1333","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"Name not the god, thou boy of tears,","themes":["names","rivals","mother"],"note":"The fatal insult."},{"id":"1338","who":"CORIOLANUS","text":"like an eagle in a dove-cote, I Flutter'd your Volscians in Corioli: Alone I did it.","themes":["pride","war","names"],"note":"His last boast."},{"id":"1342","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"The man is noble, and his fame folds in This orb o' the earth.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Praise from the enemy."},{"id":"1355","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"My rage is gone; And I am struck with sorrow.","themes":["rivals"],"note":"Aufidius's turn."},{"id":"1355","who":"AUFIDIUS","text":"Yet he shall have a noble memory.","themes":["names","rivals"],"note":"The last line about him."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Rome is starving, and its citizens blame Caius Marcius, a proud patrician soldier who openly despises them. To calm a riot the Senate grants the people five tribunes, among them Sicinius and Brutus. When the Volscians attack under Tullus Aufidius, Marcius fights his way alone into the enemy city of Corioli, wins the war, and is given the surname Coriolanus.","Back in Rome, his mother Volumnia and the Senate want him made consul. By custom he must show his wounds to the people and ask for their votes. He does it with open scorn; the tribunes persuade the people to take their votes back, provoke him into calling for their office to be abolished, and have him banished. 'I banish you!' he answers, and leaves.","He goes to his old enemy Aufidius, who welcomes him and gives him half his army. Together they march on Rome. Cominius and then Menenius beg him to spare the city and are turned away. Finally Volumnia, his wife Virgilia and his son kneel before him; Volumnia's plea breaks him and he makes peace. Rome rejoices. In Antium, Aufidius accuses him of treachery and calls him a 'boy of tears'; Coriolanus boasts that he alone defeated the Volscians, and Aufidius's men kill him."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Starving citizens plan to kill Caius Marcius","id":6},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Menenius tells the fable of the belly","id":33},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Marcius insults the people; five tribunes are granted","id":60},{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Volscians are in arms under Aufidius","id":70},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Volumnia rejoices in her son's wounds and honour","id":118},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Marcius enters Corioli alone","id":192},{"s":"a1s6","t":"The soldiers lift him up: 'Make you a sword of me?'","id":255},{"s":"a1s8","t":"Marcius and Aufidius fight","id":266},{"s":"a1s9","t":"He refuses the spoils and is named Coriolanus","id":287},{"s":"a1s10","t":"Aufidius vows to kill him by any means","id":307},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Triumphant return; Volumnia hopes for the consulship","id":393},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The tribunes plot to provoke him","id":409},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Cominius praises him; he is named consul","id":444},{"s":"a2s3","t":"In the gown of humility he begs for votes","id":522},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The tribunes turn the people against him","id":557},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The people withdraw their votes; he attacks the tribunes","id":639},{"s":"a3s1","t":"'What is the city but the people?' A riot","id":667},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Volumnia persuades him to answer 'mildly'","id":786},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Called a traitor, he explodes and is banished","id":850},{"s":"a3s3","t":"'I banish you! There is a world elsewhere'","id":858},{"s":"a4s1","t":"He says farewell at the gates like a lonely dragon","id":872},{"s":"a4s4","t":"He arrives in Antium in disguise","id":953},{"s":"a4s5","t":"He offers himself to Aufidius, who embraces him","id":1010},{"s":"a4s6","t":"Rome learns he is marching on the city","id":1108},{"s":"a4s7","t":"Aufidius plans his downfall","id":1148},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Cominius has been rejected; Menenius agrees to try","id":1155},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Menenius is sent away","id":1205},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Volumnia kneels and pleads","id":1252},{"s":"a5s3","t":"'O mother, mother! What have you done?'","id":1255},{"s":"a5s6","t":"Called 'boy', he boasts and is killed","id":1338}],"irony":{"17":"The citizen's guess that he fights to please his mother is proved exactly right in Act 5, when he gives up his revenge to please her.","70":"He says that if he were not himself he would wish to be Aufidius. In Act 4 he almost becomes him, a Volscian general.","120":"Volumnia would rather lose eleven sons nobly than see one live idly. In the end her plea costs her only son his life.","307":"Aufidius vows to kill Marcius even at his own hearth. Marcius later walks to that hearth and offers his throat.","393":"Volumnia says only the consulship is missing. Seeking it is what ruins him.","394":"He says he would rather be their servant in his own way. He ends up serving their enemies.","520":"He calls the custom unnecessary. Ignoring it, he loses everything.","588":"He wishes he had a reason to seek Aufidius in Antium. Banishment soon gives him one.","787":"He promises to come home 'belov'd of all the trades in Rome'. He comes home banished.","858":"He banishes the people, but he is the one who leaves.","872":"'I shall be lov'd when I am lack'd.' He is right: the people regret banishing him when he returns as an enemy.","1080":"Brutus says Rome sits safe without him, just before news of the Volscian army arrives.","1168":"Menenius thinks dinner will soften Coriolanus. Food began the play's quarrel.","1227":"He vows to stand as if he had no kin, at the very moment his family wins.","1295":"Rome hails Volumnia as its life; her victory is her son's death.","1313":"Aufidius sneers at women's tears 'as cheap as lies', yet he was moved by them himself in 5.3.","1338":"'Alone I did it' is a boast of killing the people he is standing among.","1355":"Aufidius promises a noble memory for the man he has just murdered."},"devices":{"body":"The body and disease","animals":"Animals and beasts","food":"Food, hunger and eating","theatre":"Acting and roles","war":"War, blood and machines","myth":"Gods, myths and Rome"},"dev":{"11":[["food","Thin bodies as a list of the rich's plenty.","is as an inventory to particularize their abundance"]],"33":[["body","The state as a body with a belly at its centre.","all the body's members Rebell'd against the belly"]],"36":[["body","The citizen's counter-image of the body.","The kingly crowned head, the vigilant eye"]],"43":[["food","The belly as storehouse and shop.","I am the storehouse and the shop"]],"52":[["body","Opinion as an itch that turns men into scabs.","Make yourselves scabs?"]],"54":[["animals","Hares, geese, curs: the people as cowardly animals.","you curs"]],"83":[["animals","The people as rats.","take these rats thither"]],"126":[["war","Blood as more beautiful than gold.","Than gilt his trophy"],["myth","Hecuba nursing Hector.","the breasts of Hecuba"]],"190":[["animals","Souls of geese in men's shapes.","You souls of geese"]],"218":[["body","Bleeding as healthy medicine.","The blood I drop is rather physical"]],"255":[["war","A man turned into a weapon.","Make you a sword of me?"]],"286":[["food","Praise as food seasoned with lies.","In praises sauc'd with lies"]],"374":[["war","Death lying in his arm.","Death, that dark spirit, in's nervy arm doth lie"]],"444":[["war","A thing of blood, a planet striking.","He was a thing of blood"],["myth","Cosmic force.","struck Corioli like a planet"]],"458":[["theatre","Politics as a part to act.","It is a part That I shall blush in acting"]],"520":[["animals","A wolf in a humble gown.","wolvish toge"]],"602":[["body","Tribunes as mouths, people as teeth.","why rule you not their teeth?"]],"622":[["body","The people as measles and scabs.","those measles"]],"628":[["myth","A sea-god among tiny fish.","Triton of the minnows"]],"630":[["myth","The many-headed Hydra.","Given Hydra leave"]],"636":[["animals","Crows pecking eagles.","The crows to peck the eagles"]],"639":[["body","Pulling out the many-headed tongue.","The multitudinous tongue"]],"727":[["body","Coriolanus as a disease to cut out.","He's a disease that must be cut away"]],"728":[["body","A diseased limb that can be cured.","he's a limb that has but a disease"]],"782":[["theatre","A part he cannot play to the life.","such a part which never I shall discharge to the life"]],"785":[["theatre","Acting as becoming a harlot or a eunuch.","Some harlot's spirit!"]],"858":[["animals","A pack of curs.","You common cry of curs!"]],"872":[["animals","The lonely dragon in its fen.","Like to a lonely dragon"]],"916":[["food","Feeding on her own anger.","Anger's my meat"]],"1148":[["animals","The osprey taking fish.","As is the osprey to the fish"]],"1231":[["theatre","A dull actor forgetting his part.","Like a dull actor now"]],"1270":[["war","A man become a war machine.","he moves like an engine"]],"1338":[["animals","An eagle in a dovecote.","like an eagle in a dove-cote"]]},"stage":{"street":"A street in Rome","capitol":"The Capitol, where the Senate sits","house":"A room in Coriolanus's house","senate":"The Volscian senate house in Corioli","walls":"Before the walls and gates of Corioli","battle":"A field of battle","camp":"The Roman or Volscian camp","road":"A highway between Rome and Antium","hall":"Aufidius's house in Antium","tent":"Coriolanus's tent before Rome"},"stagerules":[["senate house","senate"],["capitol","capitol"],["house","house"],["apartment","house"],["within corioli","street"],["before corioli","walls"],["gates","walls"],["field of battle","battle"],["camp","camp"],["post","camp"],["highway","road"],["aufidius's house","hall"],["hall","hall"],["tent","tent"],["forum","street"],["public place","street"],["street","street"]],"place":{"a1s1":"A street in Rome","a1s2":"The senate house in Corioli","a1s3":"Marcius's house in Rome","a1s4":"Before the walls of Corioli","a1s5":"Inside Corioli","a1s6":"Near Cominius's camp","a1s7":"The gates of Corioli","a1s8":"A battlefield","a1s9":"The Roman camp","a1s10":"The Volscian camp","a2s1":"A public place in Rome","a2s2":"The Capitol","a2s3":"The Forum","a3s1":"A street in Rome","a3s2":"Coriolanus's house","a3s3":"The Forum","a4s1":"A gate of Rome","a4s2":"A street near the gate","a4s3":"A road between Rome and Antium","a4s4":"Before Aufidius's house in Antium","a4s5":"A hall in Aufidius's house","a4s6":"A public place in Rome","a4s7":"The Volscian camp near Rome","a5s1":"A public place in Rome","a5s2":"A Volscian outpost before Rome","a5s3":"Coriolanus's tent","a5s4":"A public place in Rome","a5s5":"A street near the gate of Rome","a5s6":"A public place in Antium"},"arc":{"Coriolanus's standing":[4,5,5,7,7,8,7,8,9,9,9,10,7,4,4,2,4,3,4,5,6,7,7,8,8,8,6,5,2],"Tribunes' power":[6,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,4,4,5,4,6,8,7,9,10,10,9,9,4,3,2,2,2,1,4,4,4]},"arcnote":"Coriolanus's standing in Rome rises through Act 1 as he wins Corioli and his new name, and peaks with his triumph and nomination as consul in Act 2. It drops as he begs for votes and collapses in Act 3, when the tribunes have him banished. In Act 4 his power climbs again as Aufidius's ally and Rome's terror, before his mother's plea and his death in Antium bring it down. The tribunes' power is the mirror image: new and uncertain at first, it grows as they turn the people in Act 2, reaches its height at the banishment, and crashes when he returns with an army and the plebeians turn on Brutus.","context":[["Date","Probably written in 1608, one of Shakespeare's last tragedies, after Antony and Cleopatra. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623, with unusually detailed stage directions."],["Source","Plutarch's Life of Caius Martius Coriolanus, in Sir Thomas North's English translation (1579). Livy's history of Rome, which also tells the fable of the belly, may have been a second source. Shakespeare follows it closely, sometimes almost word for word in Volumnia's plea, but builds up Menenius and Aufidius and invents the comic citizens."],["The Midlands Rising","In 1607 hungry peasants in Northamptonshire, Warwickshire and Leicestershire rioted against enclosures and food shortages. Shakespeare's opening hunger riot, which Plutarch gives as a protest over debt, would have looked very current."],["The Roman Republic","The play is set around 493 BC, soon after the last king, Tarquin, was driven out. Rome is ruled by two consuls and a Senate of patricians; the plebeians win their own officers, the tribunes, in this period."],["The body politic","The fable of the belly was a common political image, found in Livy, Plutarch, Camden and others. Elizabethan and Jacobean writers used the state-as-body to argue that every class had its natural place."],["Tribunes and the people","Jacobean England had no voting public like Rome's, but James I's quarrels with Parliament made the question of who speaks for the people a live one."],["Honour and the Roman ideal","Roman virtue (virtus) meant manly courage above all. Cominius says 'valour is the chiefest virtue'. The play tests what happens when one man embodies it completely."],["Mothers in Rome","Roman matrons like Volumnia were honoured for raising soldier sons. Plutarch says Coriolanus did everything to please his mother, which the play makes central."],["Staging","The play was probably first acted around 1609 or 1610, at the Blackfriars or the Globe; the date is uncertain. It is full of crowds, battles and processions, and silence: the stage direction where he holds his mother's hand in silence is unusual in its detail."],["Reputation","Coriolanus has often been read politically. In 1934 a Paris production caused riots; it has been claimed by both the right, as a hero against the mob, and the left, as a portrait of the people's fight against an elite. Brecht adapted it."],["Later productions","Laurence Olivier played him hanging upside down at his death (1959). Ralph Fiennes filmed it in modern dress in 2011, with television news and street riots."]],"critics":{"people":[["A. C. Bradley, 'Coriolanus' (1912)","Paraphrased: Bradley argued Shakespeare shows the citizens as good-natured but foolish, and the tribunes as small men, while giving his hero greatness of spirit."],["Bertolt Brecht, notes on adapting Coriolanus (1950s)","Paraphrased: Brecht wanted the play to show that the people do not need a single irreplaceable hero, and that the city can defend itself."]],"mother":[["Janet Adelman, 'Anger's My Meat' (1978)","Paraphrased: Adelman argued that images of feeding and hunger tie Coriolanus's rage to his dependence on a mother who gave him wounds and courage instead of nourishment."],["Coppelia Kahn, Roman Shakespeare (1997)","Paraphrased: Kahn showed how Roman manhood in the play is made by mothers, so that Coriolanus's independence is always borrowed from Volumnia."]],"pride":[["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom saw Coriolanus as a hero who cannot grow or reflect, almost a war machine, unlike Hamlet or Lear."],["Stanley Cavell, 'Who does the wolf love?' (1983)","Paraphrased: Cavell read the play as about hunger and the wish to need nothing; Coriolanus's refusal of praise is a refusal to be fed by others."]],"acting":[["Anne Barton, 'Livy, Machiavelli and Coriolanus' (1985)","Paraphrased: Barton argued the play is about a republic in which compromise and speaking to the people are necessary, and Coriolanus's refusal to perform them is a political failure."],["Kenneth Burke, 'Coriolanus and the Delights of Faction' (1966)","Paraphrased: Burke read the hero as a scapegoat whose excesses let audiences enjoy and then purge the tensions between classes."]],"body":[["Zvi Jagendorf, 'Coriolanus: Body Politic and Private Parts' (1990)","Paraphrased: Jagendorf showed how the body fable keeps returning, in wounds, mouths, voices and disease, to make politics physical."]],"rivals":[["Jonathan Goldberg and others on Aufidius","Paraphrased: Several critics have read the language between Coriolanus and Aufidius, especially the wedding-night image in 4.5, as erotic, showing how rivalry and desire overlap among Roman and Volscian men."]]},"symbols":{"Voices":"\\bvoices?\\b","Blood":"\\bblood\\w*","Wounds":"\\bwounds?\\b|\\bscars?\\b","Proud":"\\bproud\\w*|\\bpride\\b","Mother":"\\bmother\\w*","Name":"\\bnames?\\b|\\bnam'd\\b","Corn":"\\bcorn\\b|\\bgrain\\b|\\bbread\\b"},"symbols2":[["The belly","Menenius's fable makes the Senate the belly that feeds Rome; the whole play is about who feeds whom."],["The gown of humility","The threadbare gown candidates wore to beg for votes; Coriolanus calls it a 'wolvish toge'."],["Wounds","Badges of honour for Volumnia, political capital for the custom of election, and private for Coriolanus, who will not show them."],["Corn","The grain the people demand and the free handout Coriolanus attacks: hunger is the root of the conflict."],["The name 'Coriolanus'","Won at Corioli, renounced in exile, stripped away by Aufidius at the end."],["The butterfly","Young Marcius tearing it shows the family's violence passed down a generation."]],"solo":{"520":1,"953":1},"iago":[["a1s1","Marcius insults the starving people, and the Senate grants them five tribunes to defend them."],["a1s1","The tribunes note his pride and resolve to watch him."],["a1s9","He wins Corioli alone and is named Coriolanus, making him the obvious candidate for consul."],["a2s1","The tribunes plot to remind the people of his hatred and provoke his temper."],["a2s2","He asks to skip the custom of begging for votes; the tribunes insist."],["a2s3","He begs the people's votes with scorn; the tribunes persuade them to withdraw them."],["a3s1","He calls for the tribunes' power to be thrown down; they call him a traitor and demand his death."],["a3s2","Volumnia persuades him to go back and speak mildly."],["a3s3","The tribunes call him a traitor; he explodes and they banish him."],["a4s1","He leaves Rome vowing to be like 'a lonely dragon'."],["a4s4","He turns against his birthplace and comes to Antium."],["a4s5","He offers his revenge to Aufidius and is given half the Volscian army."],["a4s6","The Volscians, led by Coriolanus, burn their way towards Rome."],["a4s7","Aufidius, jealous, prepares a charge to break his neck."],["a5s2","He turns away Cominius and then Menenius."],["a5s3","Volumnia's plea makes him spare Rome and make peace."],["a5s6","Aufidius calls him traitor and boy; he is killed in Antium."]],"rel":{"1":[["CORIOLANUS","FIRST CITIZEN","hate"],["MENENIUS","FIRST CITIZEN","trust"],["CORIOLANUS","AUFIDIUS","hate"],["SICINIUS","CORIOLANUS","hate"],["BRUTUS","CORIOLANUS","hate"],["VOLUMNIA","CORIOLANUS","love"],["VIRGILIA","CORIOLANUS","love"],["COMINIUS","CORIOLANUS","trust"]],"2":[["SICINIUS","CORIOLANUS","deceive"],["BRUTUS","CORIOLANUS","deceive"],["MENENIUS","SICINIUS","hate"],["VOLUMNIA","CORIOLANUS","love"],["CORIOLANUS","FIRST CITIZEN","hate"],["COMINIUS","CORIOLANUS","love"],["MENENIUS","CORIOLANUS","love"]],"3":[["CORIOLANUS","SICINIUS","hate"],["SICINIUS","CORIOLANUS","hate"],["VOLUMNIA","CORIOLANUS","trust"],["MENENIUS","CORIOLANUS","love"],["BRUTUS","CORIOLANUS","deceive"],["COMINIUS","CORIOLANUS","love"]],"4":[["CORIOLANUS","AUFIDIUS","trust"],["AUFIDIUS","CORIOLANUS","love"],["AUFIDIUS","CORIOLANUS","deceive"],["VOLUMNIA","SICINIUS","hate"],["VIRGILIA","BRUTUS","hate"],["MENENIUS","SICINIUS","hate"]],"5":[["CORIOLANUS","MENENIUS","hate"],["VOLUMNIA","CORIOLANUS","love"],["CORIOLANUS","VOLUMNIA","love"],["AUFIDIUS","CORIOLANUS","deceive"],["AUFIDIUS","CORIOLANUS","hate"],["CORIOLANUS","VIRGILIA","love"]]},"why":["Shows the people's complaint is reasoned, not mindless.","Hunger, not hatred, drives the revolt: a challenge to the patricians' view.","The first diagnosis of the hero: mother and pride.","The rulers' habit of blaming the gods for a famine.","A blunt summary of how the poor see their rulers.","Opens the play's central image of the state as a body.","The patricians' defence: they store and share the food.","The fable's political moral, which the people do not fully accept.","His first words: insult and disease imagery.","Animal imagery for the people's cowardice.","His belief that the people hate whatever is great.","His violent fantasy of killing the poor.","Shows his fury at any share of power for the people.","War as a way to rid Rome of its 'surplus' poor.","He can admire only an equal: his enemy.","Rivalry as a hunt between two lions.","The tribunes' first and constant charge.","Pride and courage set against each other.","The rivals' vow, which only death can end.","Volumnia's love is ambition for her son's honour.","She chose danger for him while he was a boy.","For her, manhood means proving yourself in battle.","Her most shocking statement of Roman values.","Virgilia's fear contrasts with Volumnia's pride.","Blood as beauty: the family creed.","Nursing and wounding brought together.","Volumnia's own violence, imagined in her son.","The next generation already tears and destroys.","His contempt for common soldiers on the battlefield.","Lartius's lament makes him the ideal Roman soldier.","He treats his wounds as nothing.","He wants only to face his rival.","He becomes a weapon in his soldiers' hands.","Hatred as the strongest bond.","Aufidius's hatred is really envy of fame.","Help as humiliation: Aufidius's pride is hurt.","He cannot bear praise, even from his mother.","Reward would make his courage a trade.","Praise as food he refuses.","The name that makes and later unmakes him.","Defeat has taken away Aufidius's sense of self.","Honour gives way to cunning.","Volumnia's joy at his wounds startles even Menenius.","Wounds turned into graves of enemies.","Volumnia's son as death walking.","His tender name for his silent wife.","He will not rule on the people's terms.","The crowds' adoration of a celebrity.","The gown that symbolises begging.","The tribunes' plan to set the people alight.","The fickleness of popular love explained.","He would rather bleed than be praised.","Praise turns trifles into monsters.","Rome's highest value stated by the consul.","A man turned into a machine of war.","He refuses to strip himself for votes.","Politics as acting, the play's key metaphor.","The people repeat his insult against him.","He literally cannot make his tongue beg.","His mocking promise to flatter.","His creed: honour over need.","The gown as a disguise: a wolf in sheep's clothing.","His scornful repetition of 'voices'.","The citizens see through him.","Brutus turns the vote with this charge.","Aufidius's hatred reported back to Rome.","Mouths and teeth: government as a body that bites.","Sicinius names the issue: his pride.","The stink of the crowd.","The tribunes' charge that he thinks himself a god.","He will not change his mind for anyone.","His contempt for a petty official.","The many-headed monster of the people.","His fear that the people will destroy the nobles.","His call to abolish the tribunes: political suicide.","Honour over a long life.","The tribunes' creed: the people are Rome.","He would rather die than yield.","Menenius's verdict, praise and criticism at once.","He cannot flatter even the gods.","Sicinius accuses him of tyranny.","The state as a body that must lose a limb.","Menenius's medical answer.","He asks to be allowed to be himself.","Volumnia's political realism.","She advises policy over passion.","Her case for honourable deceit.","She wants words without heart.","Theatre for an ignorant audience.","She knows his nature better than anyone.","He cannot play the part convincingly.","Acting humble as becoming a prostitute.","He fears acting will change who he is.","Volumnia separates the courage she gave from his pride.","He promises to cheat the people of their love.","The word he cannot keep.","His explosion at the word 'traitor'.","No compromise, even to save his life.","His final insult to the Roman people.","He reverses the sentence.","His exit line: exile as freedom.","The many-headed beast pushes him out.","He predicts their change of heart.","His self-image in exile.","Volumnia's fury at the tribunes.","Anger as her food: she feeds on herself.","His only soliloquy on the fickleness of the world.","He turns against his birthplace.","Homeless: he lives among scavengers.","He names himself to his enemy.","Only the name is left of all he won.","He offers to fight Rome with the fury of devils.","Aufidius embraces the anvil of his own sword.","Love language between enemies.","The servants' comic praise of war.","The Volscians make him a god.","The citizens' confused regret.","Aufidius's image of natural power.","Aufidius's analysis of his single nature.","Virtue depends on how the times read it.","Aufidius's plan in one couplet.","He has become a nobody without a name.","A name to be forged in fire.","Menenius's comic hope.","He denies his own family.","The guards see him as unshakeable.","He tries to make stubbornness a virtue.","His impossible dream of being self-made.","He forgets his lines like a bad actor.","Love and revenge in one image.","A mother kneeling is against nature.","She claims to have made him.","The boy will be like his father.","His name will be cursed forever.","The epitaph she threatens him with.","Honour and forgiveness.","The mother hen who sent him to war.","Her final shaming.","The turning point of the play.","He knows the cost of giving in.","Aufidius sees his opportunity.","From man to dragon.","No mercy left in him.","Volumnia as Rome's saviour.","Aufidius's contempt for tears.","The name taken away.","Aufidius's charge of weakness.","The insult that destroys him.","His last fatal boast.","Praise from the enemy's lord.","Aufidius's sudden sorrow.","The last word on his fame."],"insults":[{"id":"13","f":"he's a very dog to the commonalty","who":"First Citizen","n":"The people call him a dog before he calls them dogs."},{"id":"52","f":"you dissentious rogues","who":"Coriolanus","n":"His first words to the people."},{"id":"54","f":"you curs","who":"Coriolanus","n":"Dogs: his favourite insult for the people."},{"id":"62","f":"you fragments!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"The people as scraps."},{"id":"83","f":"Worshipful mutineers","who":"Coriolanus","n":"Mock respect."},{"id":"190","f":"You shames of Rome!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"He curses his own retreating soldiers."},{"id":"190","f":"You souls of geese","who":"Coriolanus","n":"Cowardly birds in men's shapes."},{"id":"336","f":"alias fools","who":"Menenius","n":"Menenius mocks the tribunes."},{"id":"342","f":"the beastly plebeians","who":"Menenius","n":"Even the people's friend despises them."},{"id":"489","f":"Bid them wash their faces","who":"Coriolanus","n":"Contempt for the poor's bodies."},{"id":"619","f":"rank-scented many","who":"Coriolanus","n":"The crowd's smell."},{"id":"628","f":"Triton of the minnows","who":"Coriolanus","n":"A god among little fish."},{"id":"642","f":"Thou wretch","who":"Coriolanus","n":"He abuses Sicinius to his face."},{"id":"647","f":"Hence, old goat!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"Animal abuse for a tribune."},{"id":"650","f":"Hence, rotten thing!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"Sicinius as rotting flesh."},{"id":"710","f":"Where is this viper","who":"Sicinius","n":"The tribune answers with a snake."},{"id":"839","f":"Thou injurious tribune!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"His outburst at the trial."},{"id":"858","f":"You common cry of curs!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"His last words to the Roman people."},{"id":"898","f":"Ay, fool; is that a shame?","who":"Volumnia","n":"Volumnia's scorn for the tribune."},{"id":"907","f":"Cats, that can judge as fitly of his worth","who":"Volumnia","n":"The people as cats."},{"id":"1109","f":"The breath of garlic-eaters!","who":"Menenius","n":"The voters' bad breath."},{"id":"1333","f":"thou boy of tears","who":"Aufidius","n":"The insult that kills him."},{"id":"1338","f":"False hound!","who":"Coriolanus","n":"His last insult, to Aufidius."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"241","f":"O! let me clip ye","n":"Coriolanus uses 'ye' to his general in a warm embrace."},{"id":"750","f":"Why did you wish me milder?","n":"He uses the respectful 'you' to his mother."},{"id":"786","f":"Thy valiantness was mine, thou suck'dst it from me","n":"Volumnia shifts to 'thou' as she casts him off: intimate and angry."},{"id":"839","f":"Thou liest unto thee","n":"Coriolanus uses 'thou' to Sicinius as an insult."},{"id":"1006","f":"Thou hast a grim appearance","n":"Aufidius uses 'thou' to the stranger, as to an inferior."},{"id":"1010","f":"O Marcius, Marcius!","n":"Aufidius's 'thou' becomes intimate as he embraces his enemy."},{"id":"1237","f":"Thou art my warrior","n":"Volumnia's 'thou' claims him as her own creation."},{"id":"1255","f":"What have you done?","n":"He returns to the formal 'you' to his mother in his defeat."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["The citizens kill Marcius","Menenius calms them and Marcius goes to war"],"a1s2":["The Volscians surrender","Aufidius vows to meet Marcius in battle"],"a1s3":["Virgilia goes out with Valeria","She stays at home until he returns"],"a1s4":["Marcius is killed inside Corioli","He fights his way out and the Romans take the town"],"a1s5":["He rests from his wounds","He rushes on to fight Aufidius"],"a1s6":["Cominius retreats to Rome","Marcius leads volunteers against Aufidius"],"a1s7":["Lartius burns Corioli","He guards it and goes to join the army"],"a1s8":["Marcius kills Aufidius","Aufidius is rescued by his men, to his shame"],"a1s9":["Marcius takes the spoils","He refuses them and is named Coriolanus"],"a1s10":["Aufidius admires Marcius","He vows to kill him by any means"],"a2s1":["The tribunes welcome him","They plot to bring him down"],"a2s2":["He agrees to beg for votes","He asks to skip the custom"],"a2s3":["The people refuse him","They vote for him, then the tribunes turn them"],"a3s1":["He is confirmed as consul","He attacks the tribunes and starts a riot"],"a3s2":["He refuses to go back","His mother persuades him"],"a3s3":["He stays mild and is pardoned","He explodes and is banished"],"a4s1":["His family go into exile with him","He leaves alone"],"a4s2":["Volumnia forgives the tribunes","She curses them"],"a4s3":["Rome is at peace","The Volscians prepare to attack a divided Rome"],"a4s4":["He goes to Aufidius to fight him","He goes to join him"],"a4s5":["Aufidius kills him","Aufidius embraces him and shares his command"],"a4s6":["Rome stays safe","News comes that he leads the Volscians against Rome"],"a4s7":["Aufidius is loyal to him","Aufidius plans his fall"],"a5s1":["Menenius refuses to go","He agrees to plead with him"],"a5s2":["Coriolanus forgives Menenius","He sends Menenius away"],"a5s3":["He burns Rome","His mother's plea makes him spare it"],"a5s4":["Rome falls","The ladies' success is announced"],"a5s5":["The women are blamed","They are welcomed as Rome's saviours"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Coriolanus and Aufidius","pts":["Both are great soldiers who define themselves by their rivalry.","Coriolanus cannot lie or plot; Aufidius turns to craft after defeat.","Coriolanus is killed for his mercy; Aufidius kills him and then mourns him."],"ids":[266,1148]},{"t":"Volumnia and Virgilia","pts":["Both love Coriolanus and kneel to him in Act 5.","Volumnia glories in wounds; Virgilia fears blood.","Volumnia speaks at length and persuades; Virgilia is his 'gracious silence'."],"ids":[126,125]},{"t":"Menenius and the tribunes","pts":["All three are civilians who deal with the people.","Menenius uses jokes and fables to calm them; the tribunes use rumours to rouse them.","Menenius loves Coriolanus; the tribunes fear him."],"ids":[33,409]},{"t":"Coriolanus and Volumnia","pts":["Both are proud and absolute.","He cannot pretend; she believes in policy and acting.","She shaped his courage; he says his pride is his own."],"ids":[750,766]},{"t":"The citizens and the tribunes","pts":["The citizens are willing to give their votes and argue fairly.","The tribunes coach and stage-manage them.","When things go wrong, the citizens blame themselves; the tribunes are frightened."],"ids":[472,557]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Tragedy of Coriolanus"],["Written","About 1608; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Tragedy (in the First Folio's Tragedies); a Roman tragedy and political play"],["Setting","Rome, Corioli and Antium, in the early Roman Republic, around 493 BC"],["Protagonist","Caius Marcius Coriolanus"],["Antagonists","The tribunes Sicinius and Brutus; Tullus Aufidius"],["Inciting incident","The hungry citizens riot and Marcius pours scorn on them; the people are given tribunes (1.1)"],["Turning point","The tribunes provoke him and he is banished: 'I banish you!' (3.3)"],["Climax","Volumnia kneels and pleads, and he spares Rome: 'O mother, mother! What have you done?' (5.3)"],["Time span","A few weeks in Act 1 and 2; months pass during the exile and the Volscian campaign"],["Major conflicts","Patricians against plebeians; Rome against the Volscians; Coriolanus against the tribunes and against Aufidius; his pride against his mother's will"],["Themes","Pride and honour, the people and power, mothers and sons, war and wounds, names and identity, the body politic and hunger, acting and flattery, rivalry"]],"analysis":{"CORIOLANUS":["Caius Marcius is the perfect Roman soldier and a disastrous citizen. He fights alone in Corioli, refuses reward and praise, and cannot bear to be thanked. His courage and his contempt come from the same source: a sense that he needs nothing from anyone.","His hatred of the people is total and open. He calls them curs, rats, geese and scabs, and attacks their new tribunes and their free grain. He will not show his wounds or ask for their 'voices' sincerely, because he thinks his deeds should speak for him.","Volumnia raised him to value honour above life, and he obeys her even when it goes against his nature: he agrees to answer 'mildly' in Act 3, and spares Rome in Act 5. He sees that this obedience may kill him, and it does.","He has almost no inner life on stage: one soliloquy, and few moments of reflection. Critics argue whether he is a tragic hero who learns nothing, a boy who never grew up, or a man too honest for politics. His last cry, 'Alone I did it', sums up both his greatness and his blindness."],"VOLUMNIA":["Volumnia is the play's most powerful speaker. She rejoices in her son's wounds and says she would rather lose eleven sons nobly than see one live idly. She made him what he is: 'Thy valiantness was mine.'","Yet she is also a politician. In Act 3 she tells him that honour and policy go together, and that he must speak words 'rooted in your tongue' to win the people. She understands that power needs performance.","In Act 5 she saves Rome with a speech that argues, shames, flatters and threatens. She never acknowledges what her victory costs him. Rome hails her as 'the life of Rome' while her son goes to his death."],"MENENIUS":["Menenius is a genial old patrician who likes wine and jokes, and the people like him. His fable of the belly calms the riot and states the patricians' view that the state is a body with them at the centre.","He loves Coriolanus like a father and works constantly to patch up his quarrels, but he is no democrat: he mocks the tribunes and calls the plebeians 'beastly'.","His humiliation at the Volscian camp is painful: the guards mock him and Coriolanus rejects him. It shows how far the hero has cut himself off from everyone who loved him."],"AUFIDIUS":["Aufidius is Coriolanus's mirror: a great soldier who is beaten again and again by his rival. After Corioli he gives up honourable fighting: 'wrath or craft may get him.'","When Coriolanus comes to him in exile, his welcome is joyful and passionate, compared to his wedding night. But he soon resents being overshadowed by his own guest.","He gives the play's most balanced judgement on why Coriolanus fell, then uses his mercy in Act 5 to destroy him, calling him 'boy of tears'. After the murder he is suddenly 'struck with sorrow' and promises him a noble memory."],"SICINIUS":["Sicinius is the more aggressive of the two tribunes. He calls Coriolanus a traitor, demands his death, and pronounces his banishment.","His line 'What is the city but the people?' states a real political principle, even if he uses it for personal ends.","When Coriolanus returns with an army, Sicinius is reduced to hoping the women will succeed, and is relieved and grateful when they do."],"BRUTUS":["Brutus is the planner. He predicts that Coriolanus's temper will destroy him and works out how to provoke it.","He coaches the citizens to withdraw their votes and to blame the tribunes for their first choice, and tells them to keep shouting the sentence.","The plebeians later turn on him, dragging him through the streets: the power he used against Coriolanus can be used against him."],"COMINIUS":["Cominius is the consul and general who names Coriolanus and praises him to the Senate in the play's great war speech.","He stands by Coriolanus at the banishment and offers to go with him into exile.","He is the first to plead for Rome and is rejected; he reports that Coriolanus has become 'a kind of nothing, titleless'."]},"motifs":[["The body politic","Menenius's fable makes the state a body; later the tribunes call Coriolanus a disease to be cut out and Menenius calls him a diseased limb.",[5,6,7,81,82]],["Voices and votes","'Voices' means votes: the people's power lies in their mouths, and Coriolanus mocks it by repeating the word.",[57,62,63,66,74]],["Wounds and blood","Wounds are honour for Volumnia, votes for the custom, and private for Coriolanus.",[24,25,42,43,51]],["Hunger and food","The play begins with a hunger riot; praise is food Coriolanus refuses; Volumnia feeds on anger.",[0,1,4,38,105]],["Acting and roles","Coriolanus refuses to play a part; Volumnia urges him to; in Act 5 he forgets his lines.",[56,83,90,91,128]],["Animals","Curs, rats, geese, hares and crows for the people; lions, eagles, dragons and ospreys for the hero.",[9,15,28,103,117]]],"bigq":[["Is Coriolanus a hero or a villain?",["He is brave, honest and refuses reward","He despises the starving people","He betrays Rome for revenge","He spares Rome at the cost of his life"],[29,37,111,139]],["Are the people treated fairly by the play?",["The citizens argue sensibly and are funny","They change their minds easily","They are manipulated by the tribunes","They are starving and have real grievances"],[0,1,50,116]],["Are the tribunes villains or defenders of the people?",["They protect the people against a man who hates them","They manipulate the people for their own power","'What is the city but the people?' states a real principle","They panic when the danger comes"],[16,49,64,76]],["Who is responsible for Coriolanus's fate?",["Volumnia, who raised him to value honour above all","The tribunes, who provoked him","Aufidius, who plotted his death","Coriolanus himself, who could not compromise"],[22,93,120,97]],["Why does Coriolanus give in to his mother?",["He cannot escape what she made him","Her arguments about his name convince him","He cannot be 'author of himself'","He loves his family more than he admits"],[127,133,134,138]],["Is the play anti-democratic?",["Coriolanus's contempt for the people is shown as dangerous","The people's fickleness proves his point","The tribunes are petty but not wrong","The play has been used by both left and right"],[72,73,76,102]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Coriolanus's pride?",[8,36,60,78,97,148]],["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between Coriolanus and his mother?",[2,19,22,93,131,138]],["How does Shakespeare present the people of Rome?",[0,10,50,57,63,76]],["How does Shakespeare present politics and power?",[16,49,72,74,86,119]],["How does Shakespeare present the rivalry between Coriolanus and Aufidius?",[14,33,40,112,120,147]],["How does Shakespeare present ideas about identity and names?",[39,100,109,121,127,145]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (imagery, soliloquy, repetition, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of the people, the tribunes and Volumnia","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[93,"Shakespeare presents Volumnia as the source of her son's courage, but not of his pride.","'Thy valiantness was mine, thou suck'dst it from me' links fighting to breast-feeding: courage is a kind of milk.","The switch to 'thou' is intimate and angry at once.","'But owe thy pride thyself' makes his pride something he made alone, and so something she can disown.","In Act 5 her influence wins again, which suggests his independence was never real."],[62,"Shakespeare shows Coriolanus mocking the custom of election even as he obeys it.","The repetition of 'voices' turns the word into a sneer.","He lists his deeds as if they were payments for votes, which is the bargain he despises.","The citizens' later imitation of 'your most sweet voices' shows they heard the scorn.","The scene makes the audience ask whether a man so honest can survive in politics."],[100,"Shakespeare gives Coriolanus a defiant exit line that turns banishment into freedom.","'There is a world elsewhere' suggests he does not need Rome at all.","Yet the 'world elsewhere' turns out to be Rome's enemy, and he can only define himself against Rome.","Just before it, 'I banish you' reverses the sentence: he will not be acted upon.","The line is admired as heroic and questioned as self-deceiving."],[127,"Shakespeare shows Coriolanus trying, and failing, to be self-made.","'As if a man were author of himself' admits that no man is.","The phrase 'knew no other kin' denies the family standing before him.","The verse breaks down soon after, when he says 'Like a dull actor now'.","The image prepares for his surrender: he cannot write himself without his mother."],[138,"Shakespeare makes the climax a moment of silence followed by a cry.","The stage direction has him hold his mother's hand in silence before he speaks.","'O mother, mother!' repeats the word as if he is a child again.","'What have you done?' recognises that her victory is his defeat.","Aufidius's aside immediately after shows the cost."],[147,"Shakespeare has Aufidius destroy Coriolanus with a single word.","'Boy of tears' attacks both his manhood and his obedience to his mother.","Taking away 'Coriolanus' and calling him 'Marcius' strips his honour.","Coriolanus answers with 'Alone I did it', a boast that seals his death.","The insult works because it is partly true: he gave in to his mother like a child."]],"lang":{"un":[["11","our sufferance is a gain to them.","Our suffering is their profit."],["520","Better it is to die, better to starve, Than crave the hire which first we do deserve.","It's better to die or starve than beg for a reward we've already earned."],["858","There is a world elsewhere.","There are other places in the world for me."],["1205","Wife, mother, child, I know not.","I don't recognise my wife, mother or child."]],"pun":[["54","He that will give good words to thee will flatter","good words","double meaning","The citizen means 'praise'; Marcius means 'flattery'."],["495","Mine own desert.","desert","pun","'Merit', but the citizen hears 'desire'; he plays on it in the next line."],["522","Your voices: for your voices I have fought;","voices","double meaning","Votes, and the noise of the crowd."],["628","His absolute 'shall'?","shall","emphasis","He seizes on a tribune's command word."],["953","On a dissension of a doit","doit","play on value","A doit was a tiny coin: a quarrel over nothing."],["1333","thou boy of tears","boy","insult","Both 'child' and 'servant'."]],"st":[["704","His nature is too noble for the world:",[["nature","his inborn character"],["noble","both 'of high birth' and 'morally fine'"]]],["766","Honour and policy, like unsever'd friends,",[["policy","cunning, strategy"],["unsever'd","inseparable"]]],["1255","O mother, mother! What have you done?",[["mother","repeated as a child might"],["done","her victory, his defeat"]]]]},"dir":[["522","Your voices: for your voices I have fought;","How should Coriolanus ask for votes?",[["With open mockery","He cannot hide his contempt."],["Through gritted teeth","He is trying, and failing, to be polite."],["Almost sincerely","He does want the office, and resents having to beg."]]],["858","You common cry of curs!","How should he deliver his farewell?",[["Shouting at the crowd","His rage overflows."],["Coldly, slowly","He has already left them in his mind."],["To the audience","He turns on the theatre crowd too."]]],["953","O world, thy slippery turns!","How should his soliloquy sound?",[["Bitter","He is disgusted by the world."],["Wondering","He is surprised at himself."],["Calm and decided","He has made his choice."]]],["1010","O Marcius, Marcius!","How should Aufidius greet him?",[["With overwhelming joy","He has found his equal at last."],["With calculation","He sees a weapon against Rome."],["With desire","The language of love and marriage takes over."]]],["1255","O mother, mother!","How long is the silence before he speaks?",[["Very long","The audience should feel his struggle."],["Short","He breaks suddenly."],["Broken by tears","He weeps before he speaks."]]],["1338","Alone I did it.","How should he say his last boast?",[["Proudly, to the lords","He believes it will save him."],["Wildly, to the crowd","He has lost control."],["Quietly, to himself","He knows it is the truth that will kill him."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["1338","444"],["47","1252","1295"],["11","30","47"],["444","630","29"],["33","47","727"],["60","602","522"],["444","305","129"],["129","751","786"],["384","1227","1252"],["54","60","630"],["1338","374"]],"cr":{"people":[["17","54","522"],["667","602","54"]],"mother":[["129","356","916"],["129","751","1252"]],"pride":[["92","286","444"],["284","437","441"]],"acting":[["750","771","785"],["1338","1300","17"]],"body":[["47","33","727"]],"rivals":[["109","305","1010"]]}}};
